[Term]
id: goldbook:08003
name: analytical chemistry
def: "Scientific discipline that develops and applies strategies\, instruments\, and procedures to obtain information on the composition and nature of matter in space and time."
comment: "The definition was coined by the Working Party on Analytical Chemistry (WPAC) of the Federation of European Chemical Societies (FECS) and is known as the "Edinburgh Definition"."
comment: "The term "analytical science" was coined in 1998 to emphasize the impact of informatics on analytical chemistry."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08004

[Term]
id: goldbook:08004
name: chemical analysis
def: "Application of analytical chemistry."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08003

[Term]
id: goldbook:08005
name: additive matrix effect
def: "Matrix effect that is independent of the measured quantity value of the measurand."
comment: "An additive matrix effect affects the intercept, not the slope, of a linear calibration curve."
comment: "The effect is sometimes termed "translational matrix effect" or "background interference"."
synonym: "background interference" []
synonym: "translational matrix effect" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08008

[Term]
id: goldbook:08006
name: analytical run
def: "Set of measurements of the same quantity performed under repeatability conditions of measurement."
comment: "An analytical run may comprise measurements on one or more reference materials, blank materials, quality control materials, and analytical samples."
synonym: "run" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08010
consider: goldbook:08040
consider: goldbook:08107

[Term]
id: goldbook:08007
name: background indication
def: "Indication obtained from a phenomenon\, body\, or substance similar to the one under investigation but for which a quantity of interest is supposed not to be present or is not contributing to the indication."
comment: "In VIM 4.2, this concept is termed "blank indication" with "background indication" as an alternate term. However, since blank indication refers to the explicit use of a blank material in analytical chemistry, it should be distinguished from the concept "background indication"."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08003
consider: goldbook:08009
consider: goldbook:08010

[Term]
id: goldbook:08008
name: blank correction
def: "Step in a measurement procedure in which the effect of a blank indication is removed from an indication."
comment: "A blank indication may be subtracted from an indication, or, in the case of a transmittance measurement, the indication is divided by the blank indication."
comment: "The blank indication and the indication must be of the same kind of quantity."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08009
consider: goldbook:08028

[Term]
id: goldbook:08009
name: blank indication
def: "Indication obtained from a sample of blank material under the measurement conditions for the measurand."
comment: "In VIM 4.2, "background indication" is given as an alternate term for "blank indication". However, in analytical chemistry these terms refer to different concepts."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08010

[Term]
id: goldbook:08010
name: blank material
def: "Material which contains no\, or as little as possible\, of the analyte of interest used in measurement to establish a blank indication."
comment: "A blank material to which a relevant component has been added is often termed "spiked blank" or "fortified blank". Compare spike, internal standard, and measurement procedure with standard addition."
comment: "If the term "blank" is used, for example, to denote blank indication or the related blank value or blank correction, this must be clarified by the context."
comment: "Terms such as "solvent blank", "reagent blank", or "matrix blank" are often used to specify the type of blank material."
comment: "Testing, processing, and measurement of blank materials are nearly always an essential part of chemical analysis and may be part of quality assurance and quality control."
comment: "The concept may be extended to more than one analyte."
comment: "The term "procedure (or procedural) blank" is often used to denote a material that is carried through the entire measurement procedure. Terms such as "field blank", "calibration blank", and "instrument blank" refer to materials handled in specific parts of the measurement procedure."
synonym: "blank" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08004
consider: goldbook:08008
consider: goldbook:08009
consider: goldbook:08028
consider: goldbook:08030
consider: goldbook:08047
consider: goldbook:08049

[Term]
id: goldbook:08011
name: calibration certificate
def: "Document issued by a technically competent organization providing information about a calibration."
comment: "Calibration certificates are often valid for a stated period of time, although this is not stipulated in ISO/IEC 17025."
comment: "The authority of the organization issuing the document comes from its demonstrated technical competence."
comment: "The document may include a statement describing the calibration procedure and the calibrators (calibrants) applied along with the calibration curve, calibration diagram, or other presentations of the calibration."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08065
consider: goldbook:08091

[Term]
id: goldbook:08012
name: calibration interval
def: "Time period between calibrations over which the performance of the measuring system may be expected to meet specified requirements."
comment: "Guidelines for the determination of calibration intervals of measuring systems are given in ILAC-G24."
comment: "ISO 14532 refers to a period "between routine calibrations"."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08122

[Term]
id: goldbook:08013
name: certified property value
def: "Property value of a reference material\, accompanied by statements of associated uncertainty and traceability and identified as a certified property value in the reference material certificate."
comment: "In this definition, "traceability" covers both metrological traceability of a measurement result and examination traceability but not object traceability. Similarly, "uncertainty" covers measurement uncertainty and examination uncertainty."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08036
consider: goldbook:08117

[Term]
id: goldbook:08014
name: chemical purity
def: "Mass \(amount of substance or number of entities\) of a specified component divided by the mass \(amount of substance or number of entities\, respectively\) of the system."
comment: "Purity can be estimated as \(1 - \sum_{j=1}^{j=N} f_{j}\), where \(f_{j}\) denotes fractions of the same type (mass fraction, amount of substance fraction or number fraction) of all other components \(j = 1, \dots, N\). If the contributions are expressed as mass fractions, this estimation is sometimes termed "mass balance"."
comment: "Purity is usually related to a major component. The other components are termed "impurities"."
comment: "The numerical quantity value of purity is often expressed as per cent or per mille."
comment: "The quantity, component, and system must be specified."
synonym: "purity" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08015
name: consensus property value
def: "Property value derived from a collection of results."
comment: "A consensus property value could, through appropriate actions, become a certified property value; this is in analogy to the certification of a reference material."
comment: "A consensus property value is an example of assigned value."
comment: "A consensus property value may be obtained in a material-certification study or by agreement between appropriate organizations or experts."
comment: "The consensus property value, when it is a value of a unitary quantity, may be expressed as a mean, median, or mode, and is then termed "consensus mean", "consensus median", or "consensus mode", respectively. The mode and the median also apply for ordinal quantities and the mode applies for nominal properties."
comment: "The term "consensus value" is typically used to describe estimates of a measure of location, such as mean value, median or mode, and dispersion derived from results reported by participants in a proficiency testing round (see participant in an interlaboratory comparison), but may also be used to refer to values derived from results of a specified subset of such results or, for example, from a number of laboratories chosen for their expertise for the particular analysis. See performance score and standard deviation for proficiency assessment."
synonym: "consensus value" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08013
consider: goldbook:08091
consider: goldbook:08093
consider: goldbook:08095
consider: goldbook:08118
consider: goldbook:08130

[Term]
id: goldbook:08016
name: conventional quantity value
def: "Quantity value attributed by agreement to a quantity for a given purpose."
comment: "A conventional quantity value is generally accepted as being associated with a suitably small measurement uncertainty, which might be zero."
comment: "In quality assurance and quality control in chemistry, a conventional quantity value, which may be a consensus property value, is often termed "assigned value"."
comment: "Sometimes a conventional quantity value is an estimate of a true quantity value."
synonym: "conventional value" []
synonym: "conventional value of a quantity" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
