[Term]
id: goldbook:08030
name: measurement procedure with standard addition
def: "Measurement procedure in which indications are obtained for an analytical sample as well as for analytical samples with addition\(s\) of a measurement standard."
comment: "A measurement procedure with standard addition provides an unbiased measurement result if there is a multiplicative matrix effect."
synonym: "standard addition" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08028
consider: goldbook:08035

[Term]
id: goldbook:08031
name: measurement reproducibility
def: "Measurement precision under reproducibility conditions of measurement."
comment: "Relevant statistical terms are given in ISO 5725-1 and ISO 5725-2."
comment: "The term "interlaboratory precision" or "between-laboratory precision" is sometimes used as a synonym of measurement reproducibility."
synonym: "reproducibility" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08032
name: metrological compatibility of measurement results
def: "Property of a set of measurement results for a specified measurand\, such that the absolute value of the difference of any pair of measured quantity values from two different measurement results is smaller than some chosen multiple of the standard measurement uncertainty of that difference."
comment: "As required by the Mutual Recognition Arrangement (MRA) of the International Committee for Weights and Measures (CIPM), through which national metrology institutes demonstrate the international equivalence of their measurement standards, the concept "degree of equivalence" is applied in special interlaboratory comparisons termed "key comparisons". The degree of equivalence of each national measurement standard is expressed quantitatively by two terms: its deviation from the reference quantity value of the key comparison and the measurement uncertainty of this deviation (at a level of confidence of approximately 95 %). See also: metrological equivalence of measurement results."
comment: "Correlation between the measurements influences metrological compatibility of measurement results. If the measurements are completely uncorrelated, the standard measurement uncertainty of their difference is equal to the root square sum of their standard measurement uncertainties, while it is lower for positive covariance or higher for negative covariance."
comment: "Metrological compatibility of measurement results replaces the traditional concept of "staying within the error", as it represents the criterion for deciding whether two measurement results refer to the same measurand or not. If in a set of measurements of a measurand, thought to be constant, a measurement result is not compatible with the others, either the measurement was not correct (for example, its measurement uncertainty, was assessed as being too small), or the measured quantity changed between measurements."
synonym: "metrological compatibility" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08033
consider: goldbook:08081

[Term]
id: goldbook:08033
name: metrological equivalence of measurement results
def: "Property of two or more measurement results for a given measurand which have metrological compatibility of measurement results\, so that they are each acceptable for the same specified intended use."
comment: "Measurement results are either metrologically equivalent or they are not."
synonym: "equivalence of measurement results" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08032

[Term]
id: goldbook:08034
name: minimum sample size
def: "Lower limit of sample size stipulated in documentation taken for chemical analysis."
comment: "Examples of documentation include a measurement procedure, product information sheets (see reference material), and reference material certificates."
comment: "Values associated with performance characteristics of a measurement procedure and the property values stated in documentation are rendered invalid if the minimum sample size is not taken."
synonym: "minimum sample intake" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08004
consider: goldbook:08028
consider: goldbook:08094
consider: goldbook:08117

[Term]
id: goldbook:08035
name: multiplicative matrix effect
def: "Matrix effect that is proportional to the measured quantity value of the measurand."
comment: "A multiplicative matrix effect affects the slope, not the intercept, of a linear calibration curve."
comment: "A multiplicative matrix effect can be compensated for by following a measurement procedure with standard addition."
comment: "A multiplicative matrix effect may originate from non-analyte components of the measurement standard if these contribute to the signal attributed to the analyte."
comment: "The effect is sometimes termed "rotational matrix effect" or "proportional interference"."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08030

[Term]
id: goldbook:08036
name: non-certified property value
def: "Property value that is provided for information only but is not certified by a reference material producer."
comment: "A non-certified property value cannot be used as reference in a metrological traceability chain."
comment: "A non-certified property value may be included in the reference material certificate or supplied in other form."
comment: "In ISO Guide 30, "indicative value", "information value", and "informative value" are admitted terms."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08117
consider: goldbook:08120

[Term]
id: goldbook:08037
name: object traceability
def: "Ability to trace the history\, application\, or location of an object."
comment: "In, the term defined is "traceability". However, because of the potential confusion with metrological traceability, it is recommended to use the full term if there is ambiguity."
comment: "When considering a product or a service, traceability can relate to: the origin of materials and parts, the processing history, or the distribution and location of the product or service after delivery."
synonym: "traceability (of an object)" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08038
name: property value assignment
def: "Determination of property values obtained in the course of the production of a reference material."
synonym: "value assignment" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08018
consider: goldbook:08057

[Term]
id: goldbook:08039
name: recovered quantity value ratio
def: "Measured quantity value relating a component to a system divided by a reference value."
comment: "Recovered quantity value ratio may be estimated from the measured change of the quantity value of the component of interest upon the addition of a known amount of substance or mass of the component. The added material containing the component is often termed "spike". See also: blank material Note 5, measurement procedure with standard addition, and spike."
comment: "Recovery quantity value ratio is used in procedure validation to evaluate and correct for the measurement procedure bias."
comment: "The definition can be symbolized by \(R_{\rm{B}} = Q_{\rm{B,measured}}/Q_{\rm{B,reference}}\), where \(R\) denotes the recovered ratio of the quantity values \(Q\), and \(\ce{B}\) identifies the component."
comment: "The quantities involved are rational unitary quantities and of the same kind of quantity, usually either a concentration or content."
comment: "The respective measurement procedure must be specified."
comment: "The term "recovery" is also used to describe material recovery. Therefore, the term "recovery" should not be used without qualification unless the meaning is clear from the context."
synonym: "analytical recovery" []
synonym: "recovery" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
