consider: goldbook:08067
consider: goldbook:08129
consider: goldbook:08136

[Term]
id: goldbook:08136
name: tolerance limit
def: "Specified upper or lower bound of permissible quantity values of a property."
synonym: "specification limit" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08067

[Term]
id: goldbook:08137
name: total quality management
def: "Organization-wide efforts to install a permanent climate in which an organization continuously improves its ability to deliver high-quality products and services to customers."
synonym: "TQM" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08091
consider: goldbook:08106

[Term]
id: goldbook:08138
name: validation
def: "Confirmation\, through the provision of objective evidence\, that the requirements for a specific intended use or application have been fulfilled."
comment: "In the VIM, "validation" is defined as "verification, where the specified requirements are adequate for an intended use"."
comment: "The objective evidence needed for a validation is the result of a test or other form of determination such as performing alternative calculations or reviewing documents."
comment: "The term "validated" is used to designate the corresponding status."
comment: "The use conditions for validation can be real or simulated."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08018
consider: goldbook:08049
consider: goldbook:08097
consider: goldbook:08122

[Term]
id: goldbook:08139
name: verification
def: "Confirmation\, through the provision of objective evidence\, that specified requirements have been fulfilled."
comment: "In the VIM, "verification" is defined as "provision of objective evidence that a given item fulfils specified requirements"."
comment: "The activities carried out for verification are sometimes termed "qualification process"."
comment: "The objective evidence needed for verification can be the result of an inspection or of other forms of determination such as performing alternative calculations or reviewing documents."
comment: "The term "verified" is used to designate the corresponding status."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08018
consider: goldbook:08080
consider: goldbook:08122

[Term]
id: goldbook:08140
name: Youden plot
def: "Graphical technique for evaluating an interlaboratory comparison when each participant has made two measurements on the same sample or one measurement on each of two different samples.Figure 1\: Youden plot of the \\\(z\\\) scores of samples #9 and #10 of Round 5 of U39 of the proficiency testing program held by the RCPA Australia for the analysis of creatinine in urine by 218 laboratories. \(Reproduced with permission\)."
comment: "The Youden plot is a simple but effective method for comparing both within-laboratory variability and between-laboratory variability."
comment: "The coordinates of each point may be the measured quantity values themselves or any transformation thereof such as a performance score, for example, a \(z\) score (see Fig. 1)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08081
consider: goldbook:08095
consider: goldbook:08101

[Term]
id: goldbook:08141
name: activated state
def: "In theories of unimolecular reactions an energized chemical species\, often characterized by the superscript \\\(^\{\\ddagger\}\\\)\, where the excitation is specific and the molecule is poised for reaction."
comment: "Often used as a synonym for activated complex or transition state, but not restricted to transition-state theory."
comment: "This is distinct from an energized molecule, often characterized by the superscript \(^{\ast}\), in which excitation energy is dispersed among internal degrees of freedom."
comment: "This is not a complex according to the definition in this Glossary."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08142
name: ambiphilic
def: "Both nucleophilic and electrophilic."
comment: "The distinction between ambi (Latin: both) and amphi (Greek: both) and the application to hydrophilic and lipophilic or to nucleophilic and electrophilic is arbitrary."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08143
name: α
def: "1\) Designation applied to the carbon to which a functional group is attached. 2\) In carbohydrate nomenclature\, a stereochemical designation of the configuration at the anomeric carbon. 3\) Parameter in a Brønsted relation expressing the sensitivity of the rate of protonation to acidity. 4\) Parameter in Leffler’s relation expressing the sensitivity of changes in Gibbs activation energy to changes in overall Gibbs energy for an elementary reaction."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08183

[Term]
id: goldbook:08144
name: bidentate
def: "Feature of a ligand with two potential binding sites."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08145
name: bifurcation
def: "Feature on a potential-energy surface whereby a minimum-energy reaction path \(MERP\) emanating from a saddle point \(corresponding to a transition structure\) splits in two and leads to alternative products without intervening minima or secondary barriers to overcome. A bifurcation arises when the curvature of the surface in a direction perpendicular to the MERP becomes zero and then negative; it implies the existence of a lower-energy transition structure with a transition vector orthogonal to the original MERP."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08146
name: bond-dissociation enthalpy
def: "Standard molar enthalpy required to break a given bond of some specific molecular entity by homolysis."
comment: "Although \({\rm{D}}H^{\circ}\) is commonly used, \(\Delta_{\rm{diss}} H^{\circ}\) is more consistent with the notation for other thermodynamic quantities."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08147
name: bond enthalpy
def: "Average value of the gas-phase bond-dissociation enthalpies \(usually at a temperature of \\\(\\pu\{298 K\}\\\)\) for all bonds of the same type within the same chemical species."
comment: "More commonly, tabulated mean bond energies (which are really enthalpies) are values of bond enthalpies averaged over a number of selected chemical species containing that type of bond, such as \(\pu{414 kJ mol-1}\) for \(\ce{C-H}\) bonds in a group of \(\ce{R3CH}\) (\(\ce{R = H}\), alkyl)."
synonym: "mean bond energy" []
synonym: "mean bond enthalpy" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08146

[Term]
id: goldbook:08148
name: bond-stretch isomers
def: "Two \(or more\) molecules with the same spin multiplicity but with different lengths for one or more bonds."
comment: "This feature arises because the potential-energy surface, which describes how the energy of the molecule depends on geometry, shows two (or more) minima that are not merely symmetry-related."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08149
name: <i>β</i>\, <i>β</i><sub>nuc</sub>\, <i>β</i><sub>lg</sub>
def: "Parameter in a Brønsted relation expressing the sensitivity of the rate of deprotonation to basicity."
comment: "\(\beta_{\rm{nuc}}\) and \(\beta_{\lg}\) are used to correlate nucleophilic reactivity and leaving-group ability, respectively."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08150
name: β-elimination
def: "Transformation of the general type \\\[\\ce\{XABY -> A=B + XY \(or X + Y\, or X^\{+\} + Y^\{-\}\)\}\\\] where the central atoms \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\) are commonly\, but not necessarily\, carbon."
synonym: "1,2-elimination" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08151
name: Catalán solvent parameters
def: "Quantitative measure of solvent polarity\, based on the solvent’s hydrogen-bond-donor ability\, hydrogen-bond-acceptor ability\, polarizability\, and dipolarity."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08152
name: catalytic antibody
def: "Monoclonal antibody with enzymatic activity."
comment: "A catalytic antibody acts by binding its antigen and catalyzing a chemical reaction that converts the antigen into desired products. Despite the existence of natural catalytic antibodies, most of them were specifically designed to catalyze desired chemical reactions."
comment: "Catalytic antibodies are produced through immunization against a transition-state analogue for the reaction of interest. The resulting antibodies bind strongly and specifically the transition-state analogue, so that they become catalysts for the desired reaction."
comment: "The concept of catalytic antibodies and the strategy for obtaining them were advanced by W. P. Jencks. The first catalytic antibodies were finally produced in 1986."
synonym: "abzyme" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08153
name: catalytic cycle
def: "Sequence of reaction steps in the form of a loop. One step is binding of a reactant to the active catalyst \(sometimes formed from a precatalyst\)\, and another step is the release of product and regeneration of the catalyst."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08154
name: cation-π interaction
def: "Noncovalent attractive force between a positive ion \(metal cation\, protonated Brønsted base\, etc.\) and a \\\(\\ce\{\\uppi\\ electron\}\\\) system."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08155
name: chiral feature
def: "Structural characteristic rendering a molecule chiral."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08156
name: chiral recognition
def: "Attraction between molecules through noncovalent interactions that exhibit complementarity only between partners with specific chirality."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08187

[Term]
id: goldbook:08157
name: coarctate
def: "Feature of a concerted transformation in which the primary changes in bonding occur within a cyclic array of atoms but in which two nonbonding atomic orbitals on an atom interchange roles with two bonding orbitals."
comment: "Because the atomic orbitals that interchange roles are orthogonal, such a reaction does not proceed through a fully conjugated transition state and is thus not a pericyclic reaction. It is therefore not governed by the rules that express orbital symmetry restrictions applicable to pericyclic reactions."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08158
name: condensed formula
def: "Linear representation of the structure of a molecular entity in which bonds are omitted."
comment: "This term is sometimes also called a line formula, because it can be written on a single line, but the line formula explicitly shows all bonds."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08160
name: curly arrows
def: "Symbols for depicting the flow of electrons in a chemical reaction or to generate additional resonance forms."
comment: "Single-headed curly arrows are used to depict the flow of unpaired electrons."
comment: "The tail of the curly arrow shows where an electron pair originates, and the head of the curly arrow shows where the electron pair goes."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08166
consider: goldbook:08205

[Term]
id: goldbook:08161
name: dendrimer
def: "Large molecule constructed from a central core with repetitive branching and multiple functional groups at the periphery."
comment: "The name comes from the Greek δενδρον (dendron), which translates to "tree". Synonymous terms include arborols and cascade molecules."
synonym: "arborol" []
synonym: "cascade molecule" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08163
name: distortion interaction model
def: "Method for analyzing activation energy as the sum of the energies to distort the reactants into the geometries they have in transition states plus the energy of interaction between the two distorted reactants."
synonym: "activation strain model" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08164
name: donicity
def: "Quantitative experimental measure of the Lewis basicity of a molecule \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\)\, expressed as the negative enthalpy of the formation of the 1\:1 complex \\\(\\ce\{B.SbCl5\}\\\)."
comment: "Donicity is often used as a measure of a solvent’s basicity."
synonym: "donor number DN (misnomer)" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08165
name: dynamic NMR
def: "NMR spectroscopy of samples undergoing chemical reactions."
comment: "Customarily, this does not apply to samples where the composition of the sample, and thus its NMR spectrum, changes with time, but rather to samples at equilibrium, without any net reaction. The occurrence of chemical reactions is manifested by features of the NMR line-shape or by magnetization transfer."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08166
name: electron pushing
def: "Method using curly arrows for showing the formal movement of an electron pair \(from a lone pair or a \\\(\\upsigma\\\) or \\\(\\ce\{\\uppi\\ bond\}\\\)\) or of an unpaired electron\, in order to generate additional resonance forms or to denote a chemical reaction."
comment: "The electron movement may be intramolecular or intermolecular."
comment: "When a single electron is transferred, a single-headed curly arrow or "fish-hook" is used, but the electron movement in the opposite direction is redundant and sometimes omitted."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08160
consider: goldbook:08205

[Term]
id: goldbook:08167
name: electrophilic substitution
def: "Heterolytic reaction in which the entering group adds to a nucleophile and in which the leaving group\, or electrofuge\, relinquishes both electrons to its reaction partner\, whereupon it becomes another potential electrophile."
comment: "It is arbitrary to emphasize the electrophile and ignore the feature that this is also a nucleophilic substitution, but the distinction depends on the electrophilic nature of the reactant that is considered to react with the substrate."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08168
name: NMR exchange spectroscopy
def: "Two-dimensional NMR technique producing cross peaks corresponding to site-to site chemical exchange. The cross-peak amplitudes carry information about exchange rates."
synonym: "EXSY" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08169
name: formal charge
def: "Quantity \(omitted if zero\) attached to each atom in a Lewis structure according to \\\[Z_\{\\rm\{formal\}\} = N_\{\\rm\{valence\}\} - N_\{\\rm\{lonepairs\}\} - ½N_\{\\rm\{bonds\}\}\\\]"
comment: "This formalism assumes that electrons in bonds are shared equally, regardless of electronegativity."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08170
name: frustrated Lewis acid-base pair
def: "Acid and base for which adduct formation is prevented by steric hindrance."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08171
name: Gaussian orbital
def: "Function centered on an atom of the form \\\(\\phi\(r\) \\propto x^\{i\} y^\{j\} z^\{k\} \\exp \(-\\zeta r^\{2\}\)\\\)\, used to approximate atomic orbitals in the LCAO-MO method."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08172
name: graphene
def: "Allotrope of carbon\, whose structure is a one-atom-thick planar sheet of \\\(\\ce\{sp^\{2\}\{-\}bonded\}\\\) carbon atoms in a honeycomb \(hexagonal\) crystal lattice."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08173
name: halogen bond
def: "Association between a Lewis-acidic halogen atom in a molecular entity and a Lewis-basic region in another\, or the same\, molecular entity\, which acts as an electron-pair donor\, such that the balance of forces of attraction and repulsion results in net stabilization."
comment: "The interaction provides a stabilization of a few \(\pu{kJ mol-1}\)."
comment: "This is analogous to a hydrogen bond, in which the \(\ce{H}\) is the acidic atom."
comment: "Typical halogen-bond donors are \(\ce{I2}\), \(\ce{Br2}\), \(\ce{ICN}\), and \(\ce{IC#CH}\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08174
name: hapticity
def: "Topological description for the number of contiguous atoms of a ligand that are bonded to a central metal atom."
comment: "The hapticity is indicated as a superscript following the Greek letter \(\upeta\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08175
name: HBA solvent
def: "Strongly or weakly basic solvent capable of acting as a hydrogen-bond acceptor \(HBA\) and forming strong intermolecular solute-solvent hydrogen bonds."
comment: "A solvent that is not capable of acting as a hydrogen-bond acceptor is called a non-HBA solvent."
synonym: "hydrogen-bond acceptor solvent" []
synonym: "protophilic solvent" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08176
name: HBD solvent
def: "Solvent with a sizable permanent dipole moment that bears suitably acidic hydrogen atoms to form strong intermolecular solvent-solute hydrogen bonds."
comment: "A solvent that is not capable of acting as hydrogen-bond donor is called a non-HBD solvent (formerly aprotic solvent)."
synonym: "dipolar HBD solvent" []
synonym: "hydrogen-bond donating solvent" []
synonym: "protic solvent" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08177
name: high-throughput screening
def: "Automated method to quickly assay large libraries of chemical species for the affinity of small organic molecules toward a target of interest."
comment: "Currently more than \(\pu{E5}\) different compounds can be tested per day."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08178
name: Huisgen reaction
def: "Cycloaddition of 1\,3 dipolar compounds."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08179
name: hydronation
def: "Attachment of the ion \\\(\\ce\{H^\{+\}\}\\\) either in natural abundance or where it is not desired to distinguish between the isotopes."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08180
name: ionic liquid
def: "Liquid that consists exclusively or almost exclusively of equivalent amounts of oppositely charged ions."
comment: "In practice, the ions are monocations and monoanions."
comment: "Ionic liquids that are liquid at or around room temperature are called room-temperature ionic liquids (RTILs)."
comment: "The term ionic liquid has been often restricted to those water-free liquids that have melting points (or glass-transition temperatures) below \(\pu{100^{\circ}C}\), following a definition given by Walden, who prepared the first ionic liquid, ethylammonium nitrate, \(\ce{CH3CH2NH3^{+}NO3^{-}}\), mp. \(\pu{13\!-\! 14^{\circ}C}\), for conductivity measurements."
comment: "The terminology for ionic liquids is not yet settled, as stated by Welton. Room-temperature ionic liquid, non-aqueous ionic liquid, molten salt, liquid organic salt, and fused salt are often synonymous."
synonym: "ionic solvent" []
synonym: "molten salt" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08181
name: keto-enol tautomerization
def: "Interconversion of ketone and enol tautomers by hydron migration\, accelerated by acid or base catalysis."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08213

[Term]
id: goldbook:08182
name: Laurence solvent parameters
def: "Quantitative measures of solvent polarity\, based on the solvent’s dispersion/induction interactions\, electrostatic interactions between permanent multipoles\, solute Lewis base/solvent Lewis acid interactions\, and solute HBD/solvent HBA interactions."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08183
name: Leffler’s relation
def: "In a series of elementary reactions\, the changes in Gibbs activation energies are often found to be proportional to the changes in Gibbs energies for the overall reaction.\\\[\\updelta \\Delta^\{\\ddagger\} G = \\alpha \\Delta_\{\\rm\{r\}\} G^\{\\circ\}\\\] This relation was interpreted in terms of the simple assumption that a small change in any transition-state property \\\(P_\{\\ddagger\}\\\) is a linear combination of changes in reactant- and product-state properties\, \\\(P_\{\\ce\{R\}\}\\\) and \\\(P_\{\\ce\{P\}\}\\\). \\\[\\updelta P_\{\\ddagger\} = \\alpha \\updelta P_\{\\ce\{P\}\} + \(1-\\alpha\)\\updelta P_\{\\ce\{R\}\}\\\] Within the limits of this assumption\, the parameter \\\(\\alpha\\\) is an approximate measure of the fractional displacement of the transition state along the minimum-energy reaction path from reactants to products."
comment: "There are many exceptions to the validity of Leffler’s assumption that \(\alpha\) is a measure of the position of the transition state."
synonym: "Leffler’s assumption" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08184
name: mass action\, law of
def: "Statement that the velocity of a reaction depends on the active mass\, i.e.\, the concentrations of the reactants."
comment: "First recognized in 1864 as the kinetic law of mass action by Guldberg and Waage, who first introduced the concept of dynamic equilibrium, but incorrectly assumed that the rates could be deduced from the stoichiometric equation. Only after the work of Horstmann and van’t Hoff a mathematical derivation of the reaction rates considering the order of the reaction involved was correctly made."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08185
name: Mayr–Patz equation
def: "Rate constants for the reactions of \\\(\\ce\{sp^\{2\}\{-\}hybridized\}\\\) electrophiles with nucleophiles can be expressed by the correlation \\\[\\lg \[k/\(\\pu\{dm3 mol-1 s-1\}\)\] = s_\{\\rm\{N\}\}\(E + N\)\\\] where \\\(E\\\) is the nucleophile-independent electrophilicity parameter\, \\\(N\\\) is the electrophile-independent nucleophilicity parameter\, and \\\(s_\{\\rm\{N\}\}\\\) is the electrophile-independent nucleophile-specific susceptibility parameter."
comment: "Applications to \(\rm{S}_{\rm{N}}2\mbox{-}type\) reactions are possible if an electrophile-specific susceptibility parameter is introduced."
comment: "As the \(E\) parameters of the reference electrophiles are defined as solvent-independent, all solvent effects are shifted into the parameters \(N\) and \(s_{\rm{N}}\)."
comment: "The Mayr scale is available at https://www.cup.lmu.de/oc/mayr/DBintro.html."
comment: "The correlation should not be applied to reactions with bulky electrophiles, where steric effects cannot be neglected. Because of the way of parametrization, the correlation is applicable only if one or both reaction centers are carbon."
comment: "The equation transforms into the Ritchie equation for \(s_{\rm{N}} = 1\)."
comment: "This equation is equivalent to the conventional linear Gibbs energy relationship \(\lg k = Nu + s_{\rm{N}}E\), where \(Nu = s_{\rm{N}}N\). The use of \(N\) is preferred, because it provides an approximate ranking of relative reactivities of nucleophiles."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08203

[Term]
id: goldbook:08186
name: minimum structural change\, principle of
def: "Principle that a chemical reaction is expected to occur with a minimum of bond changes or with a minimum redistribution of electrons \(although more complex reaction cascades are also possible\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08187
name: molecular recognition
def: "Attraction between specific molecules through noncovalent interactions that often exhibit electrostatic and stereochemical complementarity between the partners."
comment: "The partners are usually designated as host and guest, where the host recognizes and binds the guest with high selectivity over other molecules of similar size and shape."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08188
name: nanomaterial
def: "Substance whose particles are in the size range of 1 to \\\(\\pu\{100 nm\}\\\)."
comment: "This may have chemical properties different from those of the corresponding bulk material."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08190
name: non-Kekulé structure
def: "Compound with unpaired electrons for which no Lewis structures are possible with all bonding electrons paired in single or double bonds."
comment: "The isomer shown on the right (valence tautomer methylidenecyclopropane) is a Kekulé structure."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08191
name: nucleophilic substitution
def: "Heterolytic reaction in which an entering group adds to the electrophilic part of the substrate and in which the leaving group\, or nucleofuge\, retains both electrons of the bond that is broken\, whereupon it becomes another potential nucleophile."
comment: "It is arbitrary to emphasize the nucleophile and ignore the feature that this is also an electrophilic substitution, but the distinction depends on the nucleophilic nature of the reactant that is considered to react with the substrate."
comment: "Nucleophilic substitution reactions are designated as \(\rm{S}_{\rm{N}}1\) or \(\rm{S}_{\rm{N}}2\), depending on whether they are unimolecular or bimolecular, respectively. Mechanistically, these correspond to two-step and one-step processes, respectively. \(\rm{S}_{\rm{N}}1\) reactions follow first-order kinetics but \(\rm{S}_{\rm{N}}2\) reactions do not always follow second-order kinetics."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08192
name: octanol-water partition ratio
def: "Equilibrium concentration of a substance in octan-1-ol divided by its equilibrium concentration in water."
comment: "This is a measure of the lipophilicity of a substance. It is used in pharmacological studies and in the assessment of environmental fate and transport of organic chemicals."
synonym: "partition ratio" []
synonym: "water/octanol partition coefficient" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08193
name: organocatalysis
def: "Catalysis by small organic molecules\, as distinguished from catalysis by \(transition\) metals or enzymes."
comment: "Frequently used organocatalysts are secondary amines (covalent catalysis by generation of enamines or of iminium ions as reactive intermediates) and thioureas (hydrogen-bonding catalysis)."
comment: "Organocatalysts are often employed for enantioselectivity."
comment: "The mechanisms employed by organocatalysts are examples of general acid catalysis, general base catalysis, nucleophilic catalysis, specific acid catalysis, specific base catalysis."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08194
name: parallel effect
def: "Change of the position of the transition state upon stabilization or destabilization of a structure \(or structures\) along the assumed minimum-energy reaction path."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08195
name: polarity
def: "Characteristic of a bond between atoms of different electronegativity\, such that the electrons in that bond are not shared equally."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08196
name: potential of mean force
def: "Free energy as a function of a set of coordinates\, the negative gradient of which gives the average force acting on that configuration averaged over all other coordinates and momenta within a statistical distribution. If the averaging is performed within a canonical ensemble \(constant volume\, temperature\, and number of particles\)\, the PMF is equivalent to the Helmholtz energy\, but if it is performed within an isobaric-isothermal ensemble \(constant pressure\, temperature\, and number of particles\)\, the PMF is equivalent to the Gibbs energy."
comment: "Commonly, the PMF acting upon a selected geometric variable and averaged over the coordinates and momenta of all other geometric variables is evaluated for a succession of constrained values of the selected variable, thereby generating (generically) a free-energy profile with respect to the selected reaction coordinate (e.g., a bond distance or angle or a combination of internal coordinates); specifically, this is either a Helmholtz-energy or a Gibbs-energy profile, depending upon the choice of ensemble for the statistical averaging within a computational simulation."
comment: "Molecular simulations often yield Helmholtz energies, not Gibbs energies, but for condensed phases the difference is usually neglected."
comment: "Selection of two geometric variables as reaction coordinates allows a free-energy surface to be computed as a two-dimensional PMF."
synonym: "PMF" RELATED OMO:0003012 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08197
name: protofugality
def: "Special case of nucleofugality\, describing the relative rates of transfer of a proton \(more generally\: hydron\) from a series of Brønsted acids \\\(\\ce\{H-X\}\\\) to a common Brønsted base."
comment: "This term has the advantage over the commonly used "kinetic acidity" that philicity and fugality are associated with kinetics, while acidity and basicity are associated with thermodynamics."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08199

[Term]
id: goldbook:08198
name: protonation
def: "Attachment of the ion \\\(\\ce\{^\{1\}H^\{+\}\}\\\) of relative isotopic mass approximately equal to 1."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08179

[Term]
id: goldbook:08199
name: protophilicity
def: "Special case of nucleophilicity\, describing the relative rates of reactions of a series of Lewis bases with a common Brønsted acid. This term has the advantage over the commonly used 'kinetic basicity' that philicity and fugality are associated with kinetics\, while acidity and basicity are associated with thermodynamics."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08197

[Term]
id: goldbook:08200
name: push-pull conjugation
def: "Feature of an extended conjugated \\\(\\ce\{\\uppi\\ system\}\\\) bearing an electron donor group at one end and an electron acceptor group at the other end."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08201
name: π-electron donor
def: "Substituent capable of electron donation by resonance \(e.g.\, \\\(\\ce\{OCH3\}\\\)\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08202
name: quantitative structure–property relationship
def: "Regression model to correlate chemical properties such as boiling point or chromatographic retention time with predictor parameters based on measured or calculated features of molecular structure."
synonym: "QSPR" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08205
name: resonance form
def: "One of at least two Lewis structures\, with fixed single\, double\, and triple bonds\, that is a contributing structure to the valence-bond wavefunction of a molecule that cannot be described by a single Lewis structure."
comment: "Although the valence-bond wavefunction is a linear combination of the wavefunctions of the individual resonance forms, the coefficients and the relative contributions of the various resonance forms are usually kept qualitative. For example, the major resonance forms for the conjugate base of acetone are \(\ce{CH2=C(CH3)-O^{-}}\) and \(\ce{H2C^{-}-C(CH3)=O}\), with the former contributing more."
comment: "Resonance forms are connected by a double-headed arrow (\(\leftrightarrow\)). This must not be confused with the double arrow connecting species in equilibrium (\(\ce{}\))."
synonym: "canonical form" []
synonym: "contributing structure" []
synonym: "resonance structure" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08206
name: self-assembly
def: "Process whereby a system of single-molecule components spontaneously forms an organized structure\, owing to molecular recognition."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08187

[Term]
id: goldbook:08207
name: shift reagent
def: "Paramagnetic substance that induces an additional change of the NMR resonance frequency of a nucleus near any site in a molecule to which the substance binds."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08208
name: skeletal formula
def: "Two-dimensional representation of a molecular entity in which bonds are indicated as lines between vertices representing octet carbon atoms with attached hydrogens omitted and in which other atoms are represented by their chemical symbols."
synonym: "bond-line formula" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08209
name: solvatomers
def: "Isomers that differ in their solvation environment."
comment: "Because the solvation environment fluctuates rapidly, solvatomers interconvert rapidly."
comment: "Species that differ in the type of solvent molecules should not be called solvatomers because they are not isomers."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08210
name: stereogenic center
def: "Atom within a molecule bearing groups such that interchanging any two of them leads to a stereoisomer of the original molecule."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08212
name: superjacent orbital
def: "Second Lowest Unoccupied Molecular Orbital \(SLUMO\)."
comment: "Subjacent and superjacent orbitals sometimes play an important role in the interpretation of molecular interactions according to the frontier orbital approach."
synonym: "SLUMO" RELATED OMO:0003012 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08213
name: tautomers
def: "Constitutional isomers that can interconvert more or less rapidly\, often by migration of a hydron."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08214
name: through-conjugation
def: "Phenomenon whereby electrons can be delocalized from any of three \(or more\) groups to any other."
comment: "In Hammett-type correlations (linear Gibbs-energy relationships), this situation can lead to exalted substituent constants \(\sigma^{+}\) or \(\sigma^{-}\), as in solvolysis of \(\ce{p-CH3OC6H4C(CH3)2Cl}\) or acidity of \(\ce{p-nitrophenol}\), respectively."
comment: "This may be contrasted with cross-conjugation."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08215
name: zero-point energy
def: "Extent\, in consequence of Heisenberg’s Uncertainty Principle\, by which a particle or a set of particles has an energy greater than that of the minimum on the potential-energy surface."
comment: "A molecular entity with zero-point energy may even adopt a geometry with a potential energy greater than its total energy, a possibility that permits tunnelling."
comment: "Because of zero-point energy, a molecular entity has a nonzero probability of adopting a geometry whose energy is greater than that of the energy minimum."
comment: "Because the magnitude of zero-point energy increases with decreasing mass, it is significant in the context of isotope effects, especially of hydrogen isotopes."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1010
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08216
name: atomic spectroscopy
def: "Measurement principle of spectroscopy for the study of electromagnetic radiation absorbed and emitted by atoms."
comment: "Atomic spectra may be emission or absorption spectra."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0203
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08242

[Term]
id: goldbook:08217
name: molecular spectroscopy
def: "Measurement principle of spectroscopy for the study of rotational\, vibrational\, and electronic transitions of molecules."
comment: "Molecular spectra may be emission or absorption spectra."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0203
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08245

[Term]
id: goldbook:08218
name: nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy
def: "Measurement principle of spectroscopy to measure the precession of magnetic moments placed in a magnetic induction based on absorption of electromagnetic radiation of a specific frequency by an atomic nucleus."
comment: "Nuclei having a suitable magnetic moment include \(\ce{^{1}H}\), \(\ce{^{13}C}\), \(\ce{^{15}N}\), \(\ce{^{19}F}\), \(\ce{^{31}P}\)."
comment: "The technique is used as a method of determining structure of organic molecules, or as a mechanism for quantification."
synonym: "NMR" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0203
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08242

[Term]
id: goldbook:08220
name: absorption index
def: "Imaginary part of the complex refractive index describing absorption of electromagnetic radiation."
synonym: "imaginary refractive index" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0203
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08242

[Term]
id: goldbook:08221
name: accidental degeneracy
def: "Condition that occurs when two or more normal transitions have the same energy as a matter of coincidence\, as opposed to because of symmetry conditions."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0203
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08222
name: angle of incidence
def: "Angle between a beam of electromagnetic radiation and a line normal to a surface."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0203
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08242

[Term]
id: goldbook:08223
name: anisotropic
def: "Having properties that depend on direction."
synonym: "nonisotropic" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0203
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08224
name: apodization
def: "Technical term for changing the shape of a mathematical function\, electrical signal\, or optical transmission to remove or smooth a discontinuity at the edges\, literally meaning 'removing the foot'."
comment: "Apodization is also used for manipulating FIDs (free induction decay) to enhance specific components of the signal, for example to improve signal-to-noise or resolution, and is achieved by application of specific shapes to the FID, e.g., in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy: exponential, Gaussian or sine-bell; in infrared spectroscopy: Boxcar, Happ-Genzel, Norton-Beer, cosine."
comment: "Originally, 'apodization' described the procedure by which Fourier-transform infrared spectra are corrected for the side lobes that appear in the wings of spectral bands when the interferogram is not zero at its limits, by multiplying the interferogram prior to Fourier transformation by a weighting function that is zero at its limits (see Fourier-transform spectroscopy). Its use has broadened in recent years to include weighting functions that are not zero at their limits."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0203
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08218
consider: goldbook:08249
consider: goldbook:08282
consider: goldbook:08285
consider: goldbook:08618

[Term]
id: goldbook:08225
name: apodization function
def: "Mathematical function that effects apodization."
synonym: "window function" []
