[Term]
id: goldbook:08361
name: HN\(CA\)CO
def: "Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with nitrogen\, carbon and hydrogen dimensions."
comment: "Each \(\ce{NH}\) group will show correlations to both their own and adjacent carbonyls group."
comment: "the magnetization is transferred from \(\ce{^{1}H}\) to \(\ce{^{15}N}\) and then via the \(\ce{N-C\upalpha\ J{-}coupling}\) to the \(\ce{^{13}C\upalpha}\). From there it is transferred to the \(\ce{^{13}CO}\) via the \(\ce{^{13}C\upalpha\mbox{-}\!^{13}CO J\mbox{-}coupling}\). For detection the magnetization is transferred back the same way: from \(\ce{^{13}CO}\) to \(\ce{^{13}C\upalpha}\), \(\ce{^{15}N}\) and finally \(\ce{^{1}H}\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0203
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08432

[Term]
id: goldbook:08362
name: HN\(CO\)CA
def: "Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with nitrogen\, carbon and hydrogen dimensions."
comment: "The magnetization is passed from \(\ce{^{1}H}\) to \(\ce{^{15}N}\) and then to \(\ce{^{13}CO}\). From here it is transferred to \(\ce{^{13}C\upalpha}\) and the chemical shift is evolved. The magnetization is then transferred back via \(\ce{^{13}CO}\) to \(\ce{^{15}N}\) and \(\ce{^{1}H}\) for detection. This is similar to the HNCA, but is selective for the \(\ce{C\upalpha}\) of the preceding residue."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0203
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08432

[Term]
id: goldbook:08363
name: HNCA
def: "Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with nitrogen\, carbon and hydrogen dimensions."
comment: "The magnetization is passed from \(\ce{^{1}H}\) to \(\ce{^{15}N}\) and then via the \(\ce{N-C\upalpha}\) \(\rm{J}\mbox{-}\rm{coupling}\) to the \(\ce{^{13}C\upalpha}\) and then back again to \(\ce{^{15}N}\) and \(\ce{^{1}H}\) hydrogen for detection. Each \(\ce{NH}\) group will show correlations to both their own and the previous residue’s \(\ce{^{13}C\upalpha}\)."
