[Term]
id: goldbook:08693
name: symmetric top
def: "Molecule that has two equal principal moments of inertia with the third one different."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0203
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08571

[Term]
id: goldbook:08694
name: symmetry coordinate
def: "Each \\\(S_\{i\}\\\) is a linear combination of general coordinates\, usually internal coordinates\, constructed to take advantage of the molecular symmetry. The \\\(S_\{i\}\\\) are elements of the vector \\\(\\boldsymbol\{S\}\\\). They have the same units as the general coordinates from which they were constructed."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0203
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08695
name: symmorphic space group
def: "Space group that does not contain screw-axes and glide planes."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0203
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08696
name: thermal diffusion depth
def: "In photoacoustic spectroscopy\, \\\(L = \\sqrt\{\(D/\\pi f\)\}\\\)\, where \\\(D\\\) is thermal diffusivity and \\\(f\\\) is modulation frequency of the electromagnetic radiation."
comment: "SI unit: \(\pu{m}\). Common unit: \(\pu{\upmu m}\)."
comment: "\(\pu{63 \%}\) of the PAS signal originates within the thermal diffusion depth."
synonym: "thermal wave decay length" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0203
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:08242
consider: goldbook:08697

[Term]
id: goldbook:08697
name: thermal diffusivity
def: "Thermal conductivity divided by the product of the specific heat and the density."
comment: "SI unit: \(\pu{m2 s-1}\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0203
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08698
name: thermal wave decay coefficient
def: "Reciprocal of the thermal diffusion depth \(\\\(L\\\)\). \\\(a_\{\\rm\{s\}\} = 1/L\\\)."
comment: "SI unit: \(\pu{m-1}\)."
