[Term]
id: goldbook:08959
name: initiating species
def: "Species to which monomer adds or becomes inserted to start a chain polymerization."
comment: "An initiating species may be formed from an initiator or be the initiator itself."
comment: "In radical polymerization, initiating species formed directly from an initiator are called primary radicals."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2020-1211
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:08960
name: initiator efficiency
def: "Number of growing chains initiated divided by the number of active-centers generated from initiator molecules."
comment: "In RDP the efficiency of conversion of a starting initiator into a macroinitiator may be called the initiator efficiency (see initialization efficiency)."
comment: "In a radical polymerization, the frequency of production of propagating radicals from an initiator that provides two similar radicals is \(2k_{\rm{d}} f_{\rm{i}}\), where \(k_{\rm{d}}\) is the rate coefficient for initiator decomposition."
comment: "The initiator efficiency for radical generation from initiators such as dialkyl diazenes and organic peroxides (common symbol \(f\), recommended symbol \(f_{\rm{g}}\) to distinguish from \(f_{\rm{i}}\)) is defined as the fraction of radicals that escape the cage (see cage effect) and are potentially available for reaction with a substrate (e.g., monomer). The value of \(f_{\rm{i}}\) will usually be less than \(f_{\rm{g}}\) as depending on the monomer and the monomer concentration, initiator-derived radicals may react to form products other than initiating radicals."
comment: "The number of growing chains initiated is the number of initiating radicals formed. The number of active centers generated is the number of active centers that could formed be in the absence of any cage effect."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2020-1211
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
