[Term]
id: goldbook:09088
name: glassy carbon electrode
def: "Electrode made of glassy carbon material with an intertwined graphitic ribbon structure\, formed by pyrolysis of a resol precursor at temperatures up to \\\(\\pu\{3000^\{\\circ\}C\}\\\)."
comment: "Glassy carbon combines glass-like mechanical characteristics with physical properties similar to graphite, owing to its “graphene ribbon” structure. It is extremely resistant to many chemicals, impermeable to gases and liquids, and has good electrical conductivity, high hardness and strength, and biocompatibility. It is one of the most frequently used working electrode materials."
synonym: "GCE" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
synonym: "vitreous carbon electrode" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-0109
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09060

[Term]
id: goldbook:09089
name: highly oriented pyrolytic graphite electrode
def: "Graphite electrode of material produced by high temperature deposition of carbon on a ceramic surface and subject to graphitization at \\\(\\pu\{3000^\{\\circ\}C\}\\\)"
comment: "HOPGE is employed as edge plane pyrolytic graphite electrode (EPPG, graphene sheets perpendicular to the electrode surface) or as basal plane pyrolytic graphite electrode (BPPG, graphene sheets parallel to the electrode surface). The properties of these electrodes are different."
synonym: "HOPGE" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-0109
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09090
name: paraffin impregnated graphite electrode
def: "Graphite electrode based on a graphitic material impregnated with paraffin having a high melting point."
comment: "Paraffin impregnation results in decreased porosity and lower charging current and memory effects."
synonym: "PIGE" RELATED OMO:0003012 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-0109
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09091
name: boron-doped diamond electrode
def: "Electrode composed of a boron-doped diamond film on an electron-conducting substrate."
comment: "Boron is the most widely used dopant to produce conducting diamond electrodes. A BDDE is characterized by a wide working potential range, low noise, a low and stable background current, chemical and mechanical stability, good biocompatibility, and resistance to passivation. BDDEs can be used as substrate materials for various chemical modifications."
synonym: "BDDE" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-0109
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09060
consider: goldbook:09082

[Term]
id: goldbook:09092
name: biorecognition
def: "Detection principle for a chemically modified electrode \(CME\) in which the electrode is modified by a biological recognition element."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-0109
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09093
name: change of mass
def: "Detection principle for a chemically modified electrode \(CME\) in which the signal originates from accumulation and/or release of an analyte or reagent as well as other electrochemical processes which can result in changes in the mass of the CME."
comment: "Simultaneous electrochemistry and microgravimetry can be accomplished by means of an electrochemical quartz crystal microbalance (EQCM, see electrochemical piezoelectric microgravimetry). For use with an EQCM, a CME is fabricated by covering one face of a metallized quartz resonator with a modifying film."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-0109
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09162

[Term]
id: goldbook:09094
name: electrocatalysis
def: "Detection principle for a chemically modified electrode \(CME\) in which a charge transfer reaction between a CME and analyte proceeds at a lower overpotential than at the bare electrode. Usually\, the effect of electrocatalysis is also an increase of faradaic current."
comment: "Electrocatalysis at a CME must be distinguished from redox mediation."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-0109
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09095

[Term]
id: goldbook:09095
name: redox mediation
def: "Detection principle for a chemically modified electrode \(CME\) in which an immobilized redox couple can shuttle electrons between the electrode and an analyte\, for instance\, one with difficult access to the electrode surface because of steric reasons."
comment: "Electrocatalysis is accomplished by charge mediation but not all mediation results in electrocatalysis."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-0109
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09096
name: carbon paste electrode
def: "Electrode of a composite of carbon powder and a pasting liquid \(including mineral oil\, Nujol\, bromoform\, bromonaphthalene\)."
comment: "The CPE was first described by Adams in 1958. A CPE is typically housed in a Teflon holder, contacted by a conductive wire (occasionally a piston is used to renew the surface by extrusion of the used paste). Problems are long-term stability of the paste and that the binder can influence its properties."
comment: "These electrodes can easily be chemically modified by the incorporation of reagents (electrocatalysts, redox mediators, complexation agents, enzymes, etc.) and by the attachment of micro- or nanoparticles to prepare electrochemical sensors for analytical applications. See for an example using rabbit anti-mouse immunoglobulin in an ELISA assay using a carbon ink electrode."
synonym: "CPE" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-0109
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09060
consider: goldbook:09072

[Term]
id: goldbook:09097
name: carbon ink electrode
def: "Development of a carbon paste electrode that is screen printed using a carbon/polymer mixture of suitable composition."
comment: "These electrodes can easily be chemically modified by the incorporation of reagents (electrocatalysts, redox mediators, complexation agents, enzymes, etc.) and by the attachment of micro- or nanoparticles to prepare electrochemical sensors for analytical applications. See for an example using rabbit anti-mouse immunoglobulin in an ELISA assay using a carbon ink electrode."
comment: "Typical composition of a carbon ink expressed as mass fractions is graphite powder \(\pu{60\%}\) and polystyrene \(\pu{40\%}\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-0109
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09072
consider: goldbook:09096

[Term]
id: goldbook:09098
name: solid amalgam electrode
def: "Electrode made of a solid amalgam of an appropriate metal."
comment: "Liquid and paste amalgams can also be used."
comment: "Solid amalgam electrodes are electrochemically similar to the hanging mercury drop electrode. Their main advantages are high hydrogen overpotential (similar to liquid mercury); a wide working potential range; the simple mechanical, chemical, and electrochemical regeneration of the surface; a long lifetime; low toxicity of material (comparable with that of dental amalgams); applicability for field measurements; compatibility with flow-through systems; simple construction, without mobile parts; and easy miniaturization."
synonym: "SAE" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-0109
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09060
consider: goldbook:09082
consider: goldbook:09103

[Term]
id: goldbook:09099
name: gold electrode
def: "Foil\, wire or disc electrode made of gold which is easily fabricated into a variety of electrode geometries."
comment: "At positive potentials, \(\ce{Au}\) forms an oxide and/or chemisorbed oxygen layer, while in the presence of complexing anions, such as chloride or cyanide, it readily undergoes oxidation and dissolution, limiting its working potential range and its applications."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-0109
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09060
consider: goldbook:09082

[Term]
id: goldbook:09100
name: platinum electrode
def: "Foil\, wire\, disc\, or mesh electrode made of platinum\, which is the most commonly used metallic working electrode in electrochemistry."
comment: "\(\ce{Pt}\) has a very small overpotential for hydrogen evolution, which determines the negative potential limit in protic solvents. \(\ce{Pt}\) adsorbs hydrogen, resulting in hydrogen adsorption/desorption waves. However, the positive potential limit in polar aprotic solvents free of oxygen and water is higher than that for any other commonly used electrode materials. In the presence of water and/or oxygen, \(\ce{Pt}\) forms a film of oxide (and/or chemisorbed oxygen), resulting in potentially interfering waves or peaks."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-0109
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09060

[Term]
id: goldbook:09101
name: silver electrode
def: "Electrode in the form of foil\, mesh\, wire\, rod\, tube\, powder\, pellets\, or single crystal of silver."
comment: "\(\ce{Ag}\) electrodes were first used in potentiometry for the determination of \(\ce{Cl-}\), \(\ce{Br-}\), \(\ce{I-}\), as well as for \(\ce{Ag+}\) ions, to which they are sensitive."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-0109
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09060
consider: goldbook:09127

[Term]
id: goldbook:09102
name: mercury electrode
def: "Liquid metal electrode used in polarography."
comment: "A mercury film electrode (MFE) or thin mercury film electrode (TMFE) is formed by coating a conducting substrate, usually glassy carbon, with a layer of mercury (thin film, amalgam, or array of microdrops), often by electrodeposition from a solution containing \(\ce{Hg(II)}\). The electrode area is normally in the range of \(\pu{0.1 to\, 0.5 cm2}\) and mercury film thickness typically ranges from \(\pu{10 to 1000 nm}\), producing a film of large surface area-to-volume ratio that results in a high analyte pre-concentration during the deposition step of anodic stripping voltammetry. Compared to an HMDE, the MFE provides high sensitivity and resolution; however, it is somewhat less reproducible and more prone to interferences from intermetallic formation and surface-active substances."
comment: "Liquid mercury is an ideal electrode material for negative potentials because of its high overpotential for hydrogen evolution (electrochemical reduction of hydroxonium cations from solution). However, mercury is readily oxidized, particularly in the presence of anions that form complexes or that precipitate with \(\ce{Hg(I)}\) or \(\ce{Hg(II)}\) ions, and thus it is not suitable for use at positive potentials. The use of liquid mercury has largely been discontinued because of concerns about the toxicity of the element and its compounds."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-0109
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09060
consider: goldbook:09103
consider: goldbook:09135
consider: goldbook:09152

[Term]
id: goldbook:09103
name: dropping mercury electrode
def: "Mercury electrode formed by sequence of mercury drops falling from a small aperture."
comment: "Other kinds of mercury drop electrodes are the static mercury drop electrode (SMDE), in which a drop is held at constant radius before being mechanically knocked off, and the hanging mercury drop electrode (HMDE), in which the whole experiment is performed on a single drop of mercury."
comment: "The DME was introduced by Jaroslav Heyrovský (1890–1967). A drop is renewed every few seconds. Many metals amalgamate with mercury so that in the analysis of metal ions in aqueous solution the DME maintains a fresh surface throughout."
synonym: "DME" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-0109
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09102

[Term]
id: goldbook:09104
name: bimetallic electrode
def: "Electrode containing two different metals \(e.g. platinum and ruthenium\) on its surface \(e.g. to modify its electrocatalytic properties\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-0109
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09060

[Term]
id: goldbook:09105
name: chemically-sensitive field effect transistor
def: "Non-faradaic electrode system in which electric field variations in the semiconductor gate region control the magnitude of the source-drain current."
synonym: "ChemFET" RELATED OMO:0003012 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-0109
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09106
name: chemically-modified electrode
def: "Electrode coated with a thin layer of a substance that exhibits particular chemical\, electrochemical\, and/or optical properties."
comment: "A CME is prepared by the deliberate modification of the surface or bulk matrix material of the electrode with a selected reagent (monomeric or polymeric) that governs its electrochemical properties. Such manipulation of the molecular composition of the electrode aims to improve sensitivity, selectivity, and/or stability, allowing for the tailoring of its response to meet analytical needs."
comment: "For electroanalytical purposes, a CME is designed for use as a predominantly voltammetric or amperometric device, but examples of potentiometric, impedimetric, or microgravimetric sensing devices are known."
comment: "The layer may be monomolecular, multimolecular, ionic, or polymeric in structure."
comment: "The range of electrode surface properties sought is broader than that of ion-selective electrodes (ISEs), which also involve the rational design of the phase-boundary, partition, and transport properties of membranes on or between electrodes. CMEs also differ from ISEs in that they are generally used voltammetrically or amperometrically, a faradaic (charge transfer) reaction being the basis of measurement, whereas ISEs are generally used in potentiometric mode, the measured quantity being a phase/boundary potential (interfacial potential difference)."
comment: "The substance is termed a chemical modifier."
synonym: "CME" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-0109
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09060
consider: goldbook:09092
consider: goldbook:09094
consider: goldbook:09095
consider: goldbook:11539

[Term]
id: goldbook:09107
name: enzyme-modified electrode
def: "Chemically-modified electrode in which the products of a reaction between enzymes in an immobilized enzyme layer and the enzyme reaction substrate are electrochemically active and are measured usually by amperometry or potentiometry.Schematic of the mediated oxidation of glucose to gluconolactone by glucose oxidase. The current passed to re-oxidise the mediator is proportional to the amount concentration of glucose."
synonym: "enzyme electrode" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-0109
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
