[Term]
id: goldbook:09211
name: total reflection X-ray fluorescence spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method in which an X-ray spectrometer is used to measure the energy distribution of fluorescence X-rays emitted from a surface irradiated by primary X-rays under the condition of total reflection."
synonym: "TXRF" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09440

[Term]
id: goldbook:09212
name: X-ray absorption fine structure spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method to measure the absorption of X-rays at energies near and above \(typically several hundred \\\(\\pu\{eV\}\\\) greater\) an absorption edge\, over which fine structure \(modulation of the X-ray absorption coefficient\) can be detected."
comment: "Usefully sharp absorption edges are commonly observed in X-ray absorption spectra, although broader increases can be observed for some inner-shell excitations with short lifetimes."
comment: "XAFS includes both extended X-ray absorption fine structure spectroscopy and X-ray absorption near-edge spectroscopy. It involves transitions from a core-level to an unoccupied orbital or band and mainly reflects the local atomic structure and bonding (SEXAFS) and the density of the unoccupied electronic states (XANES)."
comment: "XAFS measurements usually start some \(\pu{10 eV}\) before the core-level binding energy (the absorption edge) of the emitting atoms, because in many cases pre-edge features are used to identify chemical bonds [example: \(\uppi^{\ast}\) resonances (excitation into lowest unoccupied molecular orbitals) in C K-edge spectra of polymer samples]."
comment: "XAFS spectra are best recorded when a highly intense beam of X-rays from a synchrotron is used along with a high resolution double crystal or curved crystal spectrometer. Detectors include ionization chambers, scintillation counters, and solid state detectors."
synonym: "XAFS" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09198
consider: goldbook:09213

[Term]
id: goldbook:09213
name: X-ray absorption near-edge spectroscopy
def: "X-ray absorption fine structure spectroscopy in the energy region near \(within approximately \\\(\\pu\{100 eV\}\\\)\) an absorption edge."
comment: "Usefully sharp absorption edges are commonly observed in X-ray absorption spectra, although broader increases can be observed for some inner-shell excitations with short lifetimes."
comment: "XANES measurements usually start some \(\pu{10 eV}\) before the core-level binding energy (the absorption edge) of the emitting atoms, because in many cases pre-edge features are used to identify chemical bonds, e.g. \(\uppi^{\ast}\) resonances (excitation into lowest unoccupied molecular orbitals) in C K-edge spectra of polymer samples."
comment: "XANES results from interference in multiple scattering of low-energy photoelectrons by surrounding atoms. It provides information on the local coordination environment, oxidation state, molecular orbitals, hybridization, and band structure (unoccupied electronic states)."
synonym: "XANES" RELATED OMO:0003012 []
synonym: "near-edge extended X-ray absorption fine structure spectroscopy (NEXAFS)" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09212

[Term]
id: goldbook:09214
name: X-ray reflectometry
def: "Measurement method for surface and interface roughness and density\, layer thickness of thin layers\, and electron density profile achieved by reflecting X-rays from a flat sample and analysing the reflected intensity as a function of angle."
comment: "Monochromatic X-rays of low divergence are required. Many layers can be modelled, with thicknesses between about \(\pu{1 nm}\) and a few \(\pu{\upmu m}\), and (subject to conditions) fitted to the data. The surface needs to be flat over the beam footprint of typically \(\pu{10 mm}\) to \(\pu{40 mm}\)."
synonym: "XRR" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09204
consider: goldbook:09436

[Term]
id: goldbook:09215
name: X-ray standing waves
def: "Measurement method based on the interference of X-rays that occurs near the Bragg condition at the surface of a crystalline solid\, which leads to intensity maxima in yields of photoelectrons as the Bragg condition is scanned by tilting the sample."
comment: "For defect-rich substrates, such as metal single crystals, a normal-incidence or back-reflection geometry may be used. Instead of rocking the crystal in space, the energy of the incident beam is tuned through the Bragg condition."
comment: "Intensity nodes are scanned from one lattice plane to the next as the sample is tilted. Atomic positions in the crystalline lattice can be determined from comparisons of measured scans of photoelectron intensity versus tilt angle with calculated scans from dynamical scattering theory."
synonym: "XSW" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09216
name: absolute elemental sensitivity factor
def: "Coefficient for an element by which the measured intensity for that element is divided to yield the atomic concentration or atomic fraction of the element present in the sample."
comment: "Sensitivity factors depend on parameters of the excitation source, the spectrometer, and the orientation of the sample to these parts of the instrument. Sensitivity factors also depend on the matrix being analysed. Sensitivity factors also depend on the matrix being analysed. In secondary ion mass spectrometry this has a dominating influence."
comment: "The choice of atomic concentration or atomic fraction should be made clear."
comment: "The source of sensitivity factors should be given to ensure that the correct matrix factors or other parameters are used."
comment: "The type of sensitivity factor utilized should be appropriate for the formulae used in the quantification process and for the type of sample analysed, e.g. for homogeneous samples or segregated layers."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09192
consider: goldbook:09263

[Term]
id: goldbook:09217
name: altered layer
def: "Surface region of a material under particle bombardment where the chemical state or physical structure is modified by the effects of the bombardment."
comment: "For silicon bombarded by \(\pu{4 keV}\) \(\ce{O2+}\) at near-normal incidence, after sputtering for a sufficient time to reach a steady state, the surface is converted to stoichiometric \(\ce{SiO2}\) to a depth of around \(\pu{15 nm}\), with lower oxygen concentrations at greater depths. At \(\pu{2 keV}\), this is reduced to \(\pu{7 nm}\), these thicknesses being approximately twice the projected range."
comment: "The observed interface width (depth resolution) in secondary-ion mass spectrometry can be greater or smaller than the altered-layer thickness, depending on the analyte and bombarding-ion species."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09192
consider: goldbook:09245
consider: goldbook:09271

[Term]
id: goldbook:09218
name: analysis area
def: "Two-dimensional region of a sample surface measured in the plane of that surface\, from which the entire analytical signal or a specified fraction of that signal is detected."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09219
name: analysis area
def: "Two-dimensional region of a sample surface at the analytical point\, but set in the plane at right angles to the spectrometer axis\, from which the entire analytical signal or a specified fraction of that signal is detected."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09220
name: angle lapping
def: "Sample preparation in which a sample is mechanically polished at an angle to the original surface."
comment: "This angle can often be less than \(\pu{1\!^{\circ}}\), so that depth information with respect to the original surface is transformed to lateral information."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09272

[Term]
id: goldbook:09221
name: angle of emission
def: "Angle between the trajectory of a particle or photon\, as it leaves a surface\, and the local or average surface normal."
comment: "In XPS and other techniques, the take-off angle, which is referred to the sample surface, is quoted."
comment: "The particular surface normal needs to be specified."
synonym: "emission angle" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09296

[Term]
id: goldbook:09222
name: areic dose rate
def: "Areic dose introduced into a solid in time interval \\\(t\\\) divided by \\\(t\\\)."
comment: "For a stationary parallel beam, the areic dose rate equals the flux times \(\cos\ \theta\), where \(\theta\) is the angle of incidence of the beam."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09223

[Term]
id: goldbook:09223
name: areic dose
def: "Number\, \\\(N\\\)\, of energetic particles of a specified type introduced into a solid through a surface area \\\(A\\\) divided by \\\(A\\\). \\\(D = N/A\\\)."
comment: "For a discussion of areic dose in relation to ion-implanted reference materials."
comment: "For a stationary parallel beam, the areic dose equals the fluence times \(cos\ \theta\), where \(\theta\) is the angle of incidence of the beam to the surface normal. Therefore, the use of ‘fluence’ for ‘dose’ is not recommended."
comment: "In some texts, the term dose density is used, but the term dose (more correctly areic dose) is more widespread. The term dose has been defined very differently in the fields of radiation and the medical sciences. Some have taken the total amount of particle radiation impacting the surface to be the dose, while others consider the amount divided by the area of the surface to be the dose. Here, dose is taken to be the latter. Dose density and dose, where they occur, are to be taken as the areic dose."
comment: "The energetic particles are atoms or atom clusters, which can be electrically charged or neutral, and the surface area \(A\) is the geometric surface area."
synonym: "dose" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09224
name: areic surface energy
def: "Energy required to increase a surface area at thermodynamic equilibrium divided by that increase in area."
comment: "SI unit \(\pu{J m^{–2}}\)."
comment: "This term has no relation to surface energy approximation, used in energetic-ion analysis and Rutherford backscattering spectrometry."
synonym: "surface energy" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09178

[Term]
id: goldbook:09225
name: atomic mixing
def: "Migration of sample atoms due to energy transfer with incident particles in the surface region."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09232
consider: goldbook:09238

[Term]
id: goldbook:09226
name: Auger neutralization
def: "Process in which an electron\, tunnelling from the conduction band of a solid\, neutralizes an incoming ion and an electron is ejected from a surface atom."
comment: "The ejected electron can be emitted into the vacuum."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09227
name: average beam current
def: "Electric charge of specified polarity in a beam passing in time interval \\\(t\\\)\, divided by \\\(t\\\)."
comment: "For beams in which the instantaneous current varies periodically with time, the time interval \(t\) is an integral number of periods."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09228
name: backscattering energy
def: "Energy of a particle from the primary beam after it has undergone a backscattering collision and escaped from the sample."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09229
name: ball cratering
def: "Procedure in which the sample is abraded by a sphere in order to expose compositional changes in layers below the original surface\, with the intent that the depth of those layers can be related to the lateral position in the crater created by the abrasion."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09272

[Term]
id: goldbook:09230
name: binary elastic scattering
def: "Collision between a moving particle and a second particle in which total kinetic energy and total momentum are conserved."
comment: "In elastic scattering interactions, the moving particle can be deflected through angles of up to \(\pu{180\!^{\circ}}\)."
synonym: "elastic scattering" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09231
name: Bragg’s rule
def: "Empirical rule formulated by W.H. Bragg and R. Kleeman that states that the stopping cross section of a compound sample is equal to the sum of the products of the elemental stopping cross sections for each constituent and its atomic fraction. \\\[S_\{\\ce\{AB\}\}\(\\varepsilon\) = xS_\{\\ce\{A\}\}\(\\varepsilon\) + yS_\{\\ce\{B\}\}\(\\varepsilon\)\\\] where \\\(S_\{\\ce\{AB\}\}\(\\varepsilon\)\\\) is the stopping cross section of the compound \\\(\\ce\{A\}_\{x\}\\ce\{B\}_\{y\}\\\)\, and \\\(S_\{\\ce\{A\}\}\(\\varepsilon\)\\\) and \\\(S_\{\\ce\{B\}\}\(\\varepsilon\)\\\) are the stopping cross sections of elements \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\)\, respectively."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09387

[Term]
id: goldbook:09232
name: cascade mixing
def: "Diffusion-like process in which atoms of material are moved randomly by energy deposited by incident particles slowing down in the sample surface region."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09225
consider: goldbook:09238

[Term]
id: goldbook:09233
name: channeling
def: "Preferential motion of energetic particles along the crystal axes of a crystalline solid as the particles move through the sample."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09234
name: charge neutralization
def: "Maintenance at a fixed potential\, usually near neutrality\, of the surface of a non-conducting or poorly conducting sample material under bombardment by primary particles or photons."
comment: "Charge neutralization can be accomplished by bombarding the surface with electrons or, more rarely, ions or photons."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09235
name: charging potential
def: "Electric potential of the surface region of an insulating sample\, caused by irradiation."
comment: "Different charging potentials can occur on different areas or at different depths in a sample, arising from sample inhomogeneities or non-uniform intensity of the incident flux of radiation."
comment: "The surface and bulk potentials can differ, for example as a result of band bending, interface dipoles, and charge centres."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09251

[Term]
id: goldbook:09236
name: chemical map
def: "Surface map using signals proportional to the amount of an element in a particular chemical state in the sample."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
