consider: goldbook:09260

[Term]
id: goldbook:09286
name: sputtering rate
def: "Amount\, mass\, or number of entities of sample material removed\, as a result of particle bombardment\, per unit time."
comment: "SI unit: \(\pu{mol s^{-1}}\), \(\pu{kg s^{-1}}\), \(\pu{s^{-1}}\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09249

[Term]
id: goldbook:09287
name: sputtering yield
def: "Number of atoms and ions sputtered from a sample divided by the total number of incident primary particles."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09288
name: standard vacuum level
def: "Electric potential \\\(\\pu\{4.500 eV\}\\\) above the Fermi level."
comment: "The Fermi level is an absolute level to which electron kinetic energies can be accurately referenced. Historically, in Auger electron spectroscopy, the electron energies have not been referenced to the Fermi level but, instead, have been referenced to the instrument vacuum level. This level varies from instrument to instrument and does not provide a consistent reference level. However, most reported Auger electron kinetic energies have been referenced to the vacuum level, and most analysts are familiar with the variations that occur from one instrument to another for energies referenced in this way. By convention, the standard vacuum level is defined, as above, to be a consistent reference level close to the value for typical instrument vacuum levels. Energies referenced to the standard vacuum level are consistent and are within approximately \(\pu{1 eV}\) of those referenced to individual instrument vacuum levels."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09172
consider: goldbook:09300

[Term]
id: goldbook:09289
name: steady-state sputtering
def: "State of the sputtering process in which important operational and analytical parameters are unchanging over a meaningful timescale."
comment: "Generally, steady state and stoichiometric sputtering are equivalent but, in profiling dilute delta layers in semiconductors, for example, the sputtering can be at a steady state while the constituents being studied are not being sputtered in their stoichiometric ratio."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09243
consider: goldbook:09291

[Term]
id: goldbook:09290
name: steady-state surface composition sputtering
def: "Steady-state surface composition produced by sputtering a homogeneous sample under non-varying conditions."
synonym: "equilibrium surface composition sputtering" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09291
name: stoichiometric sputtering
def: "State of the sputtering process in which the relative amounts of the elemental components sputtered from a sample are equal to their stoichiometry within the sample."
comment: "For most homogeneous materials, stoichiometric sputtering is attained after the sputter removal of a few nanometres from the surface."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09289

[Term]
id: goldbook:09292
name: surface excess amount
def: "For an interface\, the difference between the amount of component actually present in the system and that which would be present \(in a reference system\) if the bulk concentration in the adjoining phases were maintained up to a chosen geometrical dividing surface \(Gibbs dividing surface\)."
comment: "For a solid/liquid interface in which no component of the liquid phase penetrates into the solid, the surface excess (or adsorption) of component \(i\) is defined as; \(n_{i}^{\upsigma} = n_{i} - V^{\rm{l}} {c}_{i}^{\rm{l}}\), where \(n_{i}\) is the total amount of \(i\) in the system, \(V^{\rm{l}}\) is the volume of an arbitrarily chosen amount of bulk liquid (in the framework of the so-called algebraic method), and \({c}_{i}^{\rm{l}}\) is its bulk concentration in the liquid."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09293
name: surface map
def: "Two- or three-dimensional representation of the sample surface where the information at each point in the representation\, given by a brightness or colour or as a length in a third dimension\, is related to the output signal from a detector or processed intensity information from the available software."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09236
consider: goldbook:09248

[Term]
id: goldbook:09294
name: surface plasmon
def: "Collective motion of electrons on an interface of two phases which have different permittivity\, of which one must have electrical conductivity to sustain the collective motion."
comment: "Collective motion of electrons is induced by an injection of energetic particles, for example photons, electrons, ions, or alpha particles."
comment: "Metals such as silver and gold are well known examples of materials exhibiting surface plasmon behaviour."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09295
name: surface segregation
def: "Partitioning of a species from the bulk of a material to the surface as a result of kinetic or thermodynamic effects."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09296
name: take-off angle
def: "Angle between the trajectory of a particle as it leaves a surface and the local or average surface plane."
comment: "In the past, ‘take-off angle’ has sometimes been used erroneously to mean angle of emission."
comment: "The particular surface plane needs to be specified."
comment: "The take-off angle is the complement of the angle of emission."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09221

[Term]
id: goldbook:09297
name: thermionic work function
def: "Parameter describing the apparent work function of a solid that controls the electronic current density\, \\\(J\\\)\, emitted from a conductor at temperature \\\(T\\\). \\\(W = -kT\\ln\(J/AT^\{2\}\)\\\)\, where \\\(k\\\) is the Boltzmann constant and \\\(A\\\) is the Richardson constant."
comment: "For polycrystalline solids, the thermionic work function will be close to the minimum work function of the crystallites present."
comment: "Richardson constant, \(A = (4\uppi m_{\rm{e}} k^{2} e)/h^{3} \approx \pu{1.202E6 A m-2 K-2}\) where \(m_{\rm{e}}\) is the mass of the electron, \(e\) the elementary charge, and \(h\) is the Planck constant."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09345

[Term]
id: goldbook:09298
name: topographic contrast
def: "Contrast in a surface map or image arising from the topography of the sample surface."
comment: "Topographic contrast can change after ion sputtering."
comment: "Topographic effects can modify the interaction between the primary beam and the sample, making the interpretation of electron or ion yield data more complex than otherwise."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09293

[Term]
id: goldbook:09299
name: total ion yield
def: "Number of ions of both signs sputtered from a sample divided by the number of incident particles."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09253
consider: goldbook:09265
consider: goldbook:09401
consider: goldbook:09410
consider: goldbook:09411
consider: goldbook:09413

[Term]
id: goldbook:09300
name: vacuum level
def: "Energy level of an electron positioned at rest just outside a solid\, i.e. with zero kinetic energy with respect to the sample surface."
comment: "The vacuum level depends on the nature of the surface; for example, it is different between surface crystal planes \(\ce{Si}[001]\) and \(\ce{Si}[111]\)."
comment: "‘Just outside’ has also been written as “a few nanometers”, and is taken as a distance where there is no effect of image forces but that is sufficient for the electron to experience the full impact of the surface dipole."
synonym: "local vacuum level" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09301
name: vacuum level at infinity
def: "Electric potential of the vacuum at a point in space."
comment: "In electron spectroscopy, the point in space is taken at a sufficiently large distance outside the sample, such that electric fields caused by different work functions of different parts of the surface are zero or extremely small."
comment: "This level is not experimentally accessible."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09300

[Term]
id: goldbook:09302
name: volume yield
def: "Volume sputtered from a sample divided by number of incident particles."
comment: "Volume yield is useful for expressing the amount sputtered in organic layers where molecules can be easily fragmented and values of the molecular sputtering yield can be much more variable between samples than the volume yield."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09253
consider: goldbook:09265
consider: goldbook:09401
consider: goldbook:09410
consider: goldbook:09413

[Term]
id: goldbook:09303
name: X-ray linewidth
def: "Energy width of the principal characteristic X-ray line."
comment: "In X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy the X-ray linewidth usually refers to that of the X-ray source."
comment: "X-ray linewidth contributes to the photoelectron peak widths."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09304
name: X-ray monochromator
def: "Device used to eliminate photons of energies other than those in a narrow energy or wavelength band."
comment: "For X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy using \(\ce{Al}\) X-rays, the monochromator is usually aligned close to the \(\ce{Al}\) \(\ce{K}\upalpha_{1}\) energy."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09305
name: adventitious carbon referencing
def: "In X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy\, measurement of the charging potential of a particular sample from a comparison of the experimentally measured \\\(\\ce\{C 1s\}\\\) binding energy\, arising from adsorbed hydrocarbons on the sample\, with a standard binding energy value."
comment: "A nominal value of \(\pu{285.0 eV}\) is often used for the binding energy of the relevant \(\ce{C 1s}\) peak, although some analysts prefer specific values in the range \(\pu{284.6 eV}\) to \(\pu{285.2 eV}\), depending on the nature of the substrate. This method does not determine the true charging potential, since the true binding energy of the adsorbed hydrocarbons is not known."
comment: "Different sample charging potentials can occur on different areas on the surface, or at different depths, arising, for example, from sample inhomogeneities or non-uniform intensity of the incident-radiation flux."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09235
consider: goldbook:09332

[Term]
id: goldbook:09306
name: asymmetry parameter
def: "In X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy factor which characterizes the intensity distribution\, \\\(L\(\\gamma\)\\\)\, of photoelectrons ejected by X-rays from isolated atoms at an angle \\\(\\gamma\\\) from the incident X-ray direction. In the dipole approximation\, \\\(L\(\\gamma\) = 1 + ½\\beta \[3\(\\sin^\{2\}\\gamma\)/2-1\]\\\)."
comment: "This formula relates to gases and is modified by the effects of elastic scattering when applied to solids. At the magic angle, \(L(\gamma) = 1\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09307
name: Auger electron spectrum
def: "Plot of intensity of Auger electrons as a function of the electron kinetic energy\, usually as part of the energy distribution of detected electrons."
comment: "The Auger electron spectrum can be presented in either direct spectrum or differential spectrum formats."
comment: "When excited by incident electrons, the energy distribution of detected electrons, often measured between \(\pu{0 eV}\) and \(\pu{2500 eV}\), contains Auger electrons, backscattered (primary) electrons, and secondary electrons. The entire distribution is sometimes referred to as an Auger electron spectrum."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09311
consider: goldbook:09319
consider: goldbook:09320

[Term]
id: goldbook:09308
name: Auger parameter
def: "Kinetic energy of a narrow Auger electron peak in a spectrum minus the kinetic energy of the most intense X-ray photoelectron peak from the same element."
comment: "The Auger parameter is sometimes called the final state Auger parameter (see initial state Auger parameter)."
comment: "The Auger parameter is useful for evaluating the relaxation energy of the ionized matrix atom associated with the generation of a core hole for those Auger transitions between core levels, which have similar chemical shifts."
comment: "The Auger parameter is useful for separating chemical states for samples in which charging causes uncertainty in the binding energy measurement or in which the binding energy shift is inadequate to identify the chemical state."
comment: "The value of the Auger parameter depends on the energy of the X-rays, which therefore needs to be specified."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09339
consider: goldbook:09343
consider: goldbook:09350

[Term]
id: goldbook:09309
name: Auger vacancy satellite
def: "Emission of an Auger electron in which additional spectator holes are present in the initial state or the final state\) for the transition."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09333
consider: goldbook:09337
consider: goldbook:09356

[Term]
id: goldbook:09310
name: average matrix relative sensitivity factor
def: "Coefficient\, proportional to the intensity\, calculated for an element in an average matrix\, by which the measured intensity for that element is divided in calculations to yield the atomic concentration or atomic fraction of the element present in the sample."
comment: "The choice of atomic concentration or atomic fraction should be made clear."
synonym: "AMRSF" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09311
name: backscattered electron
def: "Electron\, originating in the incident beam\, which is emitted after interaction with the sample."
comment: "By convention, an electron with energy greater than \(\pu{50 eV}\) can be considered a backscattered electron."
comment: "By convention, the incident beam is often called the primary beam and the backscattered electrons are often referred to as the backscattered primary electrons."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09270

[Term]
id: goldbook:09312
name: backscattering correction factor
def: "In Auger electron spectroscopy\, factor equal to the total Auger-electron current arising from ionizations in the sample caused by both primary electrons and backscattered electrons divided by the Auger-electron current arising directly from the primary electrons."
comment: "‘Backscattering correction factor’ is preferred to the contraction ‘backscattering factor’."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09172
consider: goldbook:09311

[Term]
id: goldbook:09313
name: backscattering fraction
def: "In Auger electron spectroscopy\, Auger-electron current arising from ionizations in the sample caused by backscattered electrons divided by Auger-electron current arising directly from the primary electrons."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09172
consider: goldbook:09311

[Term]
id: goldbook:09314
name: backscattering yield
def: "Number of electrons emitted from the sample with energies greater than \\\(\\pu\{50 eV\}\\\) divided by the number of electrons incident at a given energy and angle of incidence."
synonym: "backscattering coefficient" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09312
consider: goldbook:09359
consider: goldbook:09423

[Term]
id: goldbook:09315
name: characteristic electron energy losses
def: "Inelastic scattering of electrons in solids that produces a non-uniform energy loss spectrum determined by the characteristics of the material."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09316
name: charge referencing
def: "Measurement of charging potential of a sample in order to correct the measured energies so that those energies correspond to a sample with no surface charge."
comment: "Charge referencing is often conducted using adventitious carbon referencing, using internal carbon referencing or by gold decoration, or by referencing to the substrate when grounded."
comment: "Different charging potentials can occur on different areas or at different depths in a sample, arising, for example, from sample inhomogeneities or the non-uniform intensity of the incident flux of radiation."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09235
consider: goldbook:09251
consider: goldbook:09305

[Term]
id: goldbook:09317
name: chemical state
def: "State of an atom arising from its chemical interaction with neighbouring atoms in a molecule\, compound\, solid\, liquid\, or gas that leads to a characteristic energy or feature observable in electron spectroscopy."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09318
name: chemical state plot
def: "In X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy\, plot of the measured kinetic energy of a sharp Auger electron peak versus the binding energy of a photoelectron peak for the same element."
comment: "Plots are usually made for a given element that can exist in different chemical states. Such plots are helpful in defining the state for an unknown sample, where measurements of the binding energy alone are inadequate."
comment: "The binding energy is usually plotted on the abscissa, with values decreasing towards the right."
synonym: "Wagner plot" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09319
name: differential spectrum
def: "Differential of a direct spectrum with respect to energy by an analogue electrode modulation method or by the numerical differentiation of that spectrum."
comment: "The modulation amplitude (for example in unit \(\pu{eV}\)) or the number of points and the type of differentiating function should be given."
comment: "Used in Auger electron spectroscopy and, rarely, in X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09172
consider: goldbook:09320

[Term]
id: goldbook:09320
name: direct spectrum
def: "Spectrum of intensity of electrons transmitted and detected by a spectrometer with a dispersing energy analyser as a function of energy\, \\\(E\\\)."
comment: "By convention, direct spectra in X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy are often presented in constant analyser energy mode, in which the spectrum approximates the true spectrum, whereas, in Auger electron spectroscopy, spectra are often presented in constant retardation ratio mode, in which the spectrum approximates to \(E\) times the true spectrum."
comment: "In retarding field energy analysers, which do not have a dispersing element, the direct spectrum can be obtained from the first differential of the collected current with respect to the retarding energy."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09172

[Term]
id: goldbook:09321
name: dynamic emittance matching
def: "Electron or ion optical method of steering a spectrometer axis to align with the impact area of the primary beam at all points of a raster scan on the sample surface."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09274

[Term]
id: goldbook:09322
name: effective attenuation length
def: "Parameter that\, when introduced in place of the electron inelastic mean free path into an expression derived for Auger electron spectroscopy and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy on the assumption that elastic scattering effects are negligible for a given quantitative application\, will correct that expression for elastic scattering effects."
comment: "Since there are different uses of this term, it is recommended that users specify clearly the particular application and the definition of the parameter for that application (e.g. by giving a formula or by providing a reference to a particular source)."
comment: "The effective attenuation length can have different values for different quantitative applications of AES and XPS. However, the most common use of effective attenuation length is in the determination of overlayer-film thicknesses from the measurement of the changes of overlayer and substrate Auger-electron or photoelectron signal intensities after the deposition of a film or as a function of the emission angle. For emission angles of up to about \(\pu{60\!^{\circ}}\) (with respect to the surface normal), it is often satisfactory to use a single value of this parameter. For greater emission angles, the effective attenuation length can depend on this angle."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09172
consider: goldbook:09221
consider: goldbook:09246
consider: goldbook:09327

[Term]
id: goldbook:09323
name: Einstein photoelectric equation
def: "Equation relating the kinetic energy of an electron\, emitted from a conductor illuminated by monochromatic photons\, and the photon energy and work function of the surface from which the photoelectron is emitted. \\\(½m_\{\\rm\{e\}\}  u_\{\\rm\{max\}\}^\{2\} = h u - \\phi_\{0\}\\\)\, where \\\(m_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\) is the mass of an electron\, \\\( u_\{\\rm\{max\}\}\\\) the maximum velocity of the emitted electron\, \\\(h\\\) the Planck constant\, \\\( u\\\) the frequency of the illuminating photons\, and \\\(\\phi_\{0\}\\\) the photoelectric work function of the surface."
synonym: "Einstein equation" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09345

[Term]
id: goldbook:09324
name: elastic peak
def: "Peak in the electron spectrum\, produced by quasi-elastically scattered electrons detected by an electron spectrometer."
comment: "All electrons that are scattered by atoms can be elastically scattered in the centre-of-mass frame, but energy losses that are typically less than \(\pu{1 eV}\) might be observed in the laboratory frame. These losses are generally significantly less than the measured energy width of the electrons in a primary-electron beam. Historically, and more generally, the scattering has been called “elastic”; however, the term quasi-elastic is now often used if the small change in energy that occurs on scattering is important."
comment: "The energy and the energy broadening of the quasi-elastic peak are influenced by the recoil of the scatterer atoms, the energy distribution of the primary (incident) electrons, the scattering geometry, the acceptance geometry, and the response function of the electron spectrometer. The intensity of the elastic peak depends on the electron differential elastic scattering cross section and on the cross section for inelastic electron scattering at the particular beam energy of the primary-electrons and in the given scattering geometry, including the probability of surface excitations."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09174
consider: goldbook:09246
consider: goldbook:09329
consider: goldbook:09334

[Term]
id: goldbook:09325
name: electron energy analyser
def: "Measuring instrument for measuring the number of electrons\, or an intensity proportional to that number\, as a function of the electron kinetic energy."
comment: "An electron energy analyser is part of an electron spectrometer."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09329

[Term]
id: goldbook:09326
name: electron energy loss spectrum
def: "Energy spectrum of electrons from a nominally monoenergetic source emitted after inelastic interactions with the sample\, often exhibiting peaks due to specific inelastic loss processes \(see characteristic electron energy losses and plasmon\)."
comment: "The electron energy loss spectrum obtained using an incident-electron beam of about the same energy as a peak obtained in Auger electron spectroscopy or X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy approximates to the energy loss spectrum associated with that peak."
comment: "The electron energy loss spectrum, measured with an incident-electron beam, is a function of beam energy, angle of incidence of the beam, angle of emission, and the electronic properties of the sample."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09172
consider: goldbook:09221
consider: goldbook:09268
consider: goldbook:09315

[Term]
id: goldbook:09327
name: electron inelastic mean free path
def: "Average distance that an electron with a given energy travels between successive inelastic collisions."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09328
name: electron retardation
def: "In Auger electron spectroscopy and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy\, measurement method for kinetic energy distribution by retarding the emitted electrons before or within the electron energy analyser."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09172
consider: goldbook:09325

[Term]
id: goldbook:09329
name: electron spectrometer
def: "Measuring system\, the essential part of which is an electron energy analyser."
comment: "The term electron spectrometer can be used either as a synonym for electron energy analyser or to describe a more complex instrument based on an electron energy analyser and additional electron-optical components. Occasionally, the term is used to describe a complete working system with an energy analyser, possible electron-optical components, an electron detector, excitation sources, vacuum pumps, control electronics, and a data-processing system. The meaning will normally be made clear by the context."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09325

[Term]
id: goldbook:09330
name: elemental relative sensitivity factor
def: "In dynamic secondary-ion mass spectrometry\, coefficient for an element by which the measured intensity of a mass peak for that element\, divided by the measured intensity of a mass peak for the matrix\, is multiplied to yield the atomic concentration of the element present in the sample."
comment: "Matrix terms are strong, and the matrix, bombarding species, incident-ion energy, and angle of incidence, as well as the spectrometer operating conditions, all affect relative elemental sensitivity factors significantly."
comment: "The elemental relative sensitivity factor can be obtained by dividing the relative isotopic sensitivity factor by the isotope abundance of the detected isotope ion."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09183
consider: goldbook:09419

[Term]
id: goldbook:09331
name: extrinsic plasmon
def: "Plasmon excited as an electron travels through a condensed medium."
comment: "The probability describing the number of extrinsic plasmons excited by the electron depends on the path length traversed and, for typical applications of Auger-electron spectroscopy and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy of a homogeneous sample, follows a Poisson distribution for the parameter \(s/\lambda\), where \(s\) is path length and \(\lambda\) is electron inelastic mean free path."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09268
consider: goldbook:09327
consider: goldbook:09342

[Term]
id: goldbook:09332
name: Fermi level referencing
def: "Process for establishing the binding energy scale for a particular sample by assigning the kinetic energy corresponding to the Fermi level\, as determined by analysis of the spectrum obtained from X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy or ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy\, as the point of zero binding energy."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09361

[Term]
id: goldbook:09333
name: final energy state
def: "Energy state of an atom after a particular Auger\, X-ray\, or photoemission process."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09337
consider: goldbook:09338

[Term]
id: goldbook:09334
name: inelastic electron scattering background
def: "Intensity distribution in a spectrum for particles originally at one energy but which are emitted at lower energies due to one or more inelastic scattering processes."
comment: "For Auger electron spectroscopy and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy, the inelastic background associated with a particular Auger electron or photoelectron peak has been approximated by a measured electron energy loss spectrum for which the incident-electron energy is close to the energy of the peak. Simple linear backgrounds have also been used, but these are much less accurate, except for the XPS analysis of insulators."
synonym: "inelastic background" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09172
consider: goldbook:09326
consider: goldbook:09335
consider: goldbook:09355
consider: goldbook:09360

[Term]
id: goldbook:09335
name: inelastic electron scattering background subtraction
def: "Subtraction of a chosen inelastic electron scattering background from a measured spectrum in Auger electron spectroscopy and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy."
comment: "The inelastic background associated with a particular Auger electron or photoelectron peak has been approximated by a measured electron energy loss spectrum for which the incident-electron energy is close to the energy of the peak."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
