[Term]
id: goldbook:09416
name: pulse width
def: "Full width at half maximum of the time distribution of the pulse of ions generated by a beam chopper and\, optionally\, the use of beam bunching in secondary ion mass spectrometry."
comment: "The pulse width is usually measured using the \(\ce{H+}\) ion. The velocity of \(\ce{H+}\) ions, for a given extraction field, is larger than that of other ions. The width in time of the \(\ce{H+}\) pulse thus provides a more reliable value for the width of the extraction pulse."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09192
consider: goldbook:09393
consider: goldbook:09398

[Term]
id: goldbook:09417
name: pulsed extraction field
def: "Extraction field around the sample that is pulsed to the working value for extracting ions for the time necessary for operation of a time-of-flight mass spectrometer\, but is otherwise at a low value."
comment: "This is the usual mode in time-of-flight secondary ion mass spectrometry systems, either for studying insulators, where charge neutralization is established while the extraction field is off, or for depth profiling using a second ion beam while the extraction field is off."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09192
consider: goldbook:09244
consider: goldbook:09398

[Term]
id: goldbook:09418
name: reflector voltage
def: "Electric potential set on the reflector electrode of a reflectron mass spectrometer with the zero referenced\, such that an ion emitted from the sample with an energy numerically equal to the reflector voltage would just be on the point of reflection or transmission by the reflector electrode."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:11544

[Term]
id: goldbook:09419
name: relative isotopic sensitivity factor
def: "In dynamic secondary-ion mass spectrometry\, coefficient for an element by which the measured intensity for an isotope of that element\, divided by the measured intensity for a matrix ion\, is multiplied to yield the atomic concentration of that isotope of the element present in the sample."
comment: "Matrix terms are strong, and the matrix, bombarding species, incident-ion energy, and angle of incidence, as well as the spectrometer operating conditions, all affect relative elemental sensitivity factors significantly."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09183

[Term]
id: goldbook:09420
name: relative mass accuracy
def: "Mass accuracy divided by reference mass."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0404
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09406

[Term]
id: goldbook:09422
