property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09495

[Term]
id: goldbook:09468
name: development
def: "Process in which a developer is used to generate an image by removing the soluble portion of the latent pattern of an exposed resist."
comment: "Aqueous-based development is carried out using a \(\pu{0.262 M}\) solution of tetramethylammonium hydroxide in water as developer."
comment: "Solvent development is carried out using a non-polar solvent as a developer."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1215
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09495
consider: goldbook:09564

[Term]
id: goldbook:09469
name: dip-pen nanolithography
def: "Direct write\, tip-based patterning method that uses the tip of an AFM probe to act as a pen and deposit molecules on a surface."
comment: "The ink used in dip-pen nanolithography often consists of thiols that self-assemble on surfaces."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1215
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09515

[Term]
id: goldbook:09470
name: directed self-assembly
def: "Process in which a thin film of block copolymer is formed into a regular nanoscale pattern on chemically or topographically nanopatterned surfaces \(or a combination thereof\)."
synonym: "DSA" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1215
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09539

[Term]
id: goldbook:09471
name: dislocation
def: "Defect\, or irregularity\, within an ordered structure."
comment: "A screw dislocation is a structure in which a helical path is traced around a linear defect (dislocation line) by the atomic planes in the crystal lattice."
comment: "An edge dislocation is a defect where an extra half-plane of atoms is introduced mid-way through the crystal."
comment: "In the context of lithography, a dislocation may be found in a block copolymer pattern."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1215
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09464
consider: goldbook:09503

[Term]
id: goldbook:09472
name: dissolution inhibitor
def: "Compound that\, when included in a resist formulation\, slows the rate of dissolution."
comment: "A typical example of a resist based on a dissolution inhibitor comprises a diazonaphthoquinone within a phenolic resin."
comment: "In lithography, the effectiveness of a dissolution inhibitor is changed after exposure to imaging radiation, which facilitates its application in resist formulations."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1215
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09503
consider: goldbook:09564

[Term]
id: goldbook:09473
name: dissolution promoter
def: "Compound that\, when included in a resist formulation\, increases the rate of dissolution."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1215
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09564

[Term]
id: goldbook:09474
name: dissolution rate
def: "Rate at which resist is removed during development."
comment: "Usually reported in \(\pu{nm s^{-1}}\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1215
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09468
consider: goldbook:09564

[Term]
id: goldbook:09475
name: doctor blading
def: "Coating of solution or slurry drawn to uniform film by use of a knife edge or doctor blade."
comment: "The term has been borrowed from printing and coating technologies. It is believed that it derives from the blades employed in letter press equipment that were used to wipe so-called ductor rolls, the term "ductor" becoming doctor over time."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1215
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09476
name: doping \(p- and n-type\)
def: "Process that increases the thermal-equilibrium concentration of free charge carriers in a material to augment its electrical conductivity using chemical agents or additives \(i.e. dopants\)."
comment: "Typical dopants to organic polymers (compounds) are:Oxidants, giving positively charged (p-doped) polymers that act as hole conductors;Reductants, giving negatively charged (n-doped) polymers that mostly act as electron conductors;Strong Brønsted acids, which are indispensable dopants to polymers such as polyaniline."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1215
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09478
name: double exposure
def: "Lithographic process requiring two exposures to radiation for a single resist layer to form a finished pattern."
