property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09583
name: vector scan
def: "Pattern formation using a directed electron beam or ion beam that skips from region to region requiring exposure but is otherwise turned off."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2018-1215
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09562

[Term]
id: goldbook:09584
name: rotated Fischer projection
def: "Projection formula for macromolecules in which horizontal lines denote polymer backbone bonds. At each individual backbone carbon atom\, horizontal lines represent bonds directed below the projection plane and vertical lines represent bonds directed above that plane."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0409
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09585
name: m diad
def: "Pair of adjacent constitutionally equivalent stereogenic units that are of like configuration."
comment: "Usage of the term meso diad for describing structures with a symmetrically constituted connecting group is no longer acceptable because of the potential for confusion with the similar term used in organic chemistry to describe small molecules."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0409
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09586
name: r diad
def: "Pair of adjacent constitutionally equivalent stereogenic units that are of unlike configuration. Figure 2\: Diagrammatic representations of the configurational repeating unit or stereorepeating unit \(upper\)\, and the diad sequence \(lower\) for isotactic poly\(oxypropylene\)\, both as a rotated Fischer projections \(left\) and as a zigzag projection \(right\). \[image2\]"
comment: "Usage of the term racemo diad for describing structures with a symmetrically-constituted connecting group is no longer acceptable because of the potential for confusion with the similar term used in organic chemistry to describe small molecules."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0409
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09587
name: heterotactic macromolecule
def: "Regular macromolecule having a configurational repeating unit comprising four configurational base units\, the first two stereogenic units of which are identical and enantiomorphic to the last two."
comment: "A heterotactic polypropylene can be depicted by a rotated Fischer Projection (top) and zigzag projection (bottom) as follows. The configurational repeating unit, a mrm tetrad, is shown within brackets. The configurational repeating unit can be equally well defined as a rmr tetrad. "
comment: "A macromolecule consisting only of m and r diads in alternating sequence (i.e. mr and rm triads) is heterotactic. The configurational repeating unit consists of four configurational base units."
comment: "The prefix ht- is recommended to indicate a heterotactic macromolecule."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2019-0409
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09585
consider: goldbook:09586

[Term]
id: goldbook:09588
name: asymmetric enantiomer-differentiating polymerization
def: "Asymmetric polymerization in which\, starting from a mixture of enantiomeric monomer molecules\, only one enantiomer is polymerized."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-1007
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09589

[Term]
id: goldbook:09589
name: asymmetric polymerization
def: "Polymerization that proceeds in an unsymmetrical manner in terms of chirality under the influence of chiral features present in one or more components of the reaction system."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-1007
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09590
name: Bernoullian distribution
def: "The Bernoullian distribution is a discrete distribution having two possible outcomes labelled by \\\(n = 0\\\) and \\\(n = 1\\\)\, in which \\\(n = 1\\\) \('success'\) occurs with probability $p$ and \\\(n = 0\\\) \('failure'\) occurs with probability \\\(q = 1 - p\\\)\, where $0 \\lt p \\lt 1$. It is a special case of a binomial distribution corresponding to a random distribution."
comment: "Since enantiomers have the same reactivity, this statistic applies to units in the case of the polycondensation of mixtures of (\(R\)) and (\(S\)) lactic acid, and to pairs of units in the case of the ring-opening polymerization of mixtures of (\(S,S\)) and (\(R,R\)) lactides."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-1007
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09591
name: bioassimilation
def: "Conversion of a substance into biomass by biochemical processes."
comment: "Lactic acid-based polyesters are not biodegradable, but they can be bioassimilated, since the ultimate degradation by-products are lactic acid metabolites."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-1007
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09592
name: biobased
def: "Composed of or derived from\, in whole or in part\, biological products derived from the biomass \(including plant\, animal\, and marine or forestry materials\)."
comment: "Lactic acids are obtained from the fermentation of natural products, such as corn or sugars, or from oil-based chemistry. Only the lactic acid-based polymers derived from natural sources are biobased."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-1007
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09593
name: biodegradable
def: "Qualifier for a substance or device that undergoes biodegradation."
comment: "As hydrolytic degradation leads to low molar mass end-products that can be biodegraded, excreted via kidneys and lungs after biomineralisation, or biorecycled by living organisms or micro-organisms, lactic acid-based polyesters are bioresorbable."
comment: "High molecular mass lactic acid-based polyesters are not degraded by animal cells or environmental micro-organisms. Therefore, the use of the term biodegradable is inappropriate and misleading, unless biodegradation under particular conditions is proved. The correct qualification is "hydrolytically degradable"."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-1007
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09596

[Term]
id: goldbook:09594
name: bioerosion
def: "Surface degradation resulting from the action of cells."
comment: "High molecular mass lactic acid-based polyesters are not bioerodible."
comment: "Some non-human enzymes like proteinase K can cause erosion of lactic acid-based polyesters due to surface enzymatic degradation of lactic acid-based polyesters."
comment: "When hydrophobic compounds are present in a lactic acid-based polymeric matrix, water uptake can be so low that surface hydrolysis is faster than the bulk hydrolysis and causes erosion that can be confused with enzymatic erosion or bioerosion."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-1007
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09595
name: biorelated
def: "Qualifier for actions or substances that are connected to living systems."
comment: "This qualifier can be used for lactic acid-based polyesters when appropriate according to origin or application."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-1007
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09596
name: bioresorbable
def: "Qualifier used to indicate that a compound or a device is bioresorbed\, i.e.\, totally eliminated or bioassimilated by an animal or a human body."
comment: "Lactic acid-based polyesters are bioresorbable after preliminary hydrolytic degradation."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-1007
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09597
name: composting
def: "Process of biological decomposition of organic matter performed by microorganisms\, mostly bacteria and fungi."
comment: "Lactic acid-based polyesters can be composted industrially provided that they are first hydrolytically degraded at a temperature above the glass transition temperature \(T_{\rm{g}}\), otherwise degradation is very slow."
comment: "Packaging made of semi-crystalline lactic acid-based polyesters may not be compostable in a home compost bin."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-1007
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09598
name: degradable
def: "Qualifier to a substance that can undergo physical and/or chemical deleterious changes of some properties\, especially of integrity\, under stress conditions."
comment: "Lactic acid-based polyesters are degradable polymers and more precisely hydrolytically degradable polymers."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-1007
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09599
name: heterogeneous degradation
def: "Degradation or biodegradation occurring at different rates depending on the location within a matrix."
comment: "Heterogeneous degradation is observed when the soluble degradation by-products that participate in the autocatalysis of the hydrolytic degradation of lactic acid-based polyester matrices are degraded faster than they diffuse outside."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-1007
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09600
name: homogeneous degradation
def: "Degradation that occurs at the same rate regardless of the location within a polymeric item."
comment: "Homogeneous degradation occurs in aqueous media when soluble degradation by-products that participate in the autocatalyzed hydrolysis diffuse faster out of the matrix than they degrade inside. Homogeneous degradation is also observed in air or in a medium that is not a solvent for degradation by-products, because these products cannot diffuse to such outer media."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-1007
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09601
name: stereocopolymer
def: "Copolymer formed of constitutional repeating units that are constitutionally identical but configurationally different."
comment: "In transesterification and hydrolytic degradation reactions of lactic-acid stereocopolymers, the intrachain ester bonds in \(m\), \(r\), and heterochiral short sequences exhibit different reactivity."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-1007
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09602
name: branch constitutional repeating unit
def: "Constitutional repeating unit of connectivity three or more connected at least to three other constitutional repeating units."
comment: "In addition, a branch constitutional repeating unit may also be connected to one or more end-groups."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1217
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09603
name: core unit
def: "Constitutional unit in a dendrimer molecule\, from which the dendron\(s\) emanate\(s\)."
comment: "In a hyperbranched macromolecule obtained by the \({\rm{AB}}_{x}\mbox{-}\rm{type}\) monomer + \({\rm{B}}_{y}\mbox{-}\rm{type}\) monomer polycondensation approach, where \(\rm{A}\) and \(\rm{B}\) refer to mutually reactive groups, the terms core molecule (not acceptable) and core unit refer to the \({\rm{B}}_{y}\mbox{-}\rm{type}\) unit, even though the structure of the macromolecule does not comprise dendrons."
comment: "Occasionally, a dendrimer molecule may consist of two dendrons directly connected together."
comment: "The structure of the core unit may be identical to that of the constitutional repeating units."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1217
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09605
consider: goldbook:09611

[Term]
id: goldbook:09604
name: degree of branching
def: "Parameter characterizing the branched architecture of a hyperbranched polymer\, which is equal to unity for a polymer that contains no linear or semi-dendritic constitutional repeating unit and is equal to 0 for a polymer that contains no dendritic or semi-dendritic constitutional repeating unit."
comment: "Several definitions of the degree of branching have been proposed for hyperbranched polymers obtained by polycondensation of \({\rm{AB}}_2\mbox{-}\rm{type}\) monomers and for those obtained by polycondensation of more complex monomer systems. The definition by Hölter et al. is: \(D_{\rm{br}} = 2 x_{\rm{D}}/(2 x_{\rm{D}} + x_{\rm{L}})\), where \(x_{\rm{D}}\) and \(x_{\rm{L}}\) are the mole fractions of dendritic and linear units, respectively."
comment: "The degree of branching of a dendrimer is equal to one. However, a polymer with the degree of branching equal to one is not necessarily a dendrimer. It can be a hyperbranched polymer, since the degree of branching does not per se take into account the regularity and symmetry of the molecules that compose it."
comment: "The degree of branching of a linear polymer is equal to 0."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1217
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09610
consider: goldbook:09618
consider: goldbook:09622
consider: goldbook:09629

[Term]
id: goldbook:09605
name: dendrimer molecule
def: "Molecule consisting of one or more dendrons emanating from a single constitutional unit."
comment: "An example of a formula of a dendrimer molecule is given in the figure in core unit."
comment: "Occasionally, a dendrimer molecule may consist of two dendrons directly connected together."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1217
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09603
consider: goldbook:09611

[Term]
id: goldbook:09606
name: dendritic block macromolecule
def: "Block macromolecule in which at least one block is a dendron."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1217
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09611

[Term]
id: goldbook:09607
name: dendritic constitutional repeating unit
def: "Constitutional repeating unit of connectivity $c \\ge 3$ connected to $c$ other constitutional repeating units."
comment: "Dendritic constitutional repeating units belong to the class of branch constitutional repeating units."
comment: "Examples of dendritic constitutional repeating units of connectivity $c = 3$ connected to three other constitutional repeating units are given in figures in dendron direction and hyperbranched oligomer."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1217
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
