[Term]
id: goldbook:09828
name: glycosyltransferase
def: "Enzyme that catalyses the transfer of a saccharide from a nucleotide di\(mono\)phosphate-activated sugar to an acceptor molecule."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09807
consider: goldbook:09827
consider: goldbook:09834

[Term]
id: goldbook:09829
name: hemiacetal
def: "Compound having the general formula \\\(\\ce\{RCH\(OH\)\(OR^\{\\prime\}\)\}\\\) \(\\\(\\ce\{R^\{\\prime\}\}  eq \\ce\{H\}\\\)\)."
comment: "A hemiacetal results from the nucleophilic addition of a hydroxy group to a carbonyl functionality of an aldehyde, as in ring closure of an aldose."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09808
consider: goldbook:09830

[Term]
id: goldbook:09830
name: hemiketal
def: "Compound having the general formula \\\(\\ce\{R2C\(OH\)OR^\{\\prime\}\}\\\) \(\\\(\\ce\{R^\{\\prime\}\}  eq \\ce\{H\}\\\)\)."
comment: "A hemiketal results from the nucleophilic addition of a hydroxy group to a carbonyl functionality of a ketone, as in ring closure of a ketose."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09829

[Term]
id: goldbook:09831
name: lectin
def: "Saccharide binding protein of non-immune origin."
comment: "Some lectins participate in immune response, but they are not products of primary immune response. Lectins also do not display catalytic activity towards their ligands; the binding of carbohydrate is reversible and highly specific."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09821

[Term]
id: goldbook:09832
name: lectin microarray
def: "Lectins with distinct saccharide-binding specificity arranged on a microarray platform."
comment: "Lectin microarray is used for the characterization of glycans (see glycan profiling) via specific interactions with labelled glycans."
comment: "The specific lectin–glycan interactions are also used for the characterization of lectins in the opposite set-up (see glycan microarray)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09659
consider: goldbook:09812
consider: goldbook:09813
consider: goldbook:09831

[Term]
id: goldbook:09833
name: proteoglycomics
def: "Systematic study of the structure\, expression\, and function of proteoglycans."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09834
name: sugar
def: "Term frequently applied to monosaccharides and lower oligosaccharides."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09835
name: alkaline degradation
def: "Treatment with alkaline solution \(e.g. hydrazine\, \\\(\\ce\{KOH\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{NaOH\}\\\)\) useful for the cleavage of fatty acids."
comment: "The resulting (partially or fully) O-deacylated and (fully) N-deacylated products enable the determination of the fatty acid substitution positions by mass spectrometry."
synonym: "alkaline hydrolysis" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09836
name: core oligosaccharide
def: "Intermediate structural region between the O-polysaccharide and lipid A moiety of lipopolysaccharides \(LPS\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09840

[Term]
id: goldbook:09837
name: fatty acyls
def: "Diverse group of lipid molecules synthesised by chain elongation of an acetyl-CoA primer with malonyl-CoA \(or methylmalonyl-CoA\) groups."
comment: "The fatty acyl category is further divided into multiple subclasses, including fatty acids and conjugates, icosanoid, fatty alcohols and esters, glycans, and others."
synonym: "FA" RELATED OMO:0003012 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09838
name: glycerolipids
def: "Esters\, ethers\, and other O-derivatives of glycerol with alkyls or alkenyls\, including mono-\, di-\, and tri-substituted glycerols."
synonym: "GL" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09839
name: glycerophospholipids
def: "Lipids with glycerol backbone esterified with one or two fatty acids in position \\\(\\ce\{C-1\}\\\) and/or \\\(\\ce\{C-2\}\\\) and with phosphoric acid at position \\\(\\ce\{C-3\}\\\). The phosphate group is usually esterified with an alcohol \(choline\, ethanolamine\, serine\, inositol\, glycerol\) to form the 'lipid head group'."
comment: "GPs are ubiquitous in nature and are key components of the membrane lipid bilayers of cells."
synonym: "GP" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
synonym: "phospholipids" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09840
name: lipid A
def: "Glycolipid component of lipopolysaccharides \(LPSs\) that is integrated into the bacterial cell wall and is responsible for the beneficial \(immunostimulatory\) or toxic \(immunopathological\) effects of the molecule."
comment: "The archetypical (Enterobacteriaceae) structure of lipid A consists of a \(\beta\)-1,6-linked d-glucosamine (GlcN) or 2,3-diamino-2,3-dideoxy-d-glucose (GlcN3N) disaccharide (or a mixture of them), which is acylated by varying numbers (usually four to seven) of ester- and amide-linked acyl groups (often termed primary and secondary fatty acids). Phosphate groups and other glycosyl substituents may be linked at the \(\ce{C-1}\) and \(\ce{C-4^{\prime}}\) positions. Lipid A is linked to the core oligosaccharide at the \(\ce{C-6^{\prime}}\) position of the distal sugar unit of lipid A."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09841
name: lipid droplets
def: "Round-shaped intracellular inclusions consisting of hydrophobic cores \(mainly triacylglycerols\, sterol esters\, occasionally free fatty acids or squalene\) covered by a phospholipid monolayer containing a specific set of proteins."
comment: "Originally considered as lipid storage compartments, lipid droplets are currently recognised as organelles with high metabolic activity. The defective formation and/or function of lipid droplets is involved in various pathogenic conditions, such as obesity, cardiovascular diseases, type 2 diabetes, or metabolic syndrome."
synonym: "adiposomes" []
synonym: "lipid bodies" []
synonym: "lipid particles" []
synonym: "oil bodies" []
synonym: "oleosomes" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09839

[Term]
id: goldbook:09842
name: lipid extraction
def: "Isolation of lipids in their native state from samples of different origin."
comment: "Usually based on the use of organic solvents (methanol, chloroform) to facilitate lipid partitioning between aqueous and organic phases."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09843
name: lipid profiling
def: "Lipidomics technique conferring unbiased information about total lipids within a particular cell type\, tissue\, or body fluid."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09846

[Term]
id: goldbook:09844
name: lipid remodelling
def: "Changes in the lipid molecules composition after completion of their biosynthesis."
comment: "In glycerophospholipids, remodelling includes changes in the composition of fatty acids (for example by acyl chain exchange catalysed by transacylases) or the introduction of unsaturated double bonds (catalysed by desaturases), or by the synthesis of new lipid species or degradation of existing ones. In phosphoinositides, it can involve phosphorylation/dephosphorylation processes."
comment: "Lipid remodelling is important for the adaptation of biological membranes to changing environmental conditions and physiological demands."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09839

[Term]
id: goldbook:09845
name: lipidome
def: "Complete set of lipid species and their metabolites present within a particular cell type\, tissue\, or body fluid."
comment: "It is estimated that lipidome of a eukaryotic cell may contain over \(\pu{100000}\) individual molecular species of various lipids."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09846
name: lipidomics
def: "Systematic and comprehensive study aimed at the identification\, profiling\, and quantification of lipidome."
comment: "In a broader context, lipidomics includes system-level analysis of subcellular localization and the biological activities of individual lipids."
comment: "Lipidomics is a lipid-targeted metabolomics approach aimed at a comprehensive analysis of lipids in biological systems."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09804
consider: goldbook:09845

[Term]
id: goldbook:09847
name: lipooligosaccharide
def: "Lipopolysaccharide produced by specific wild-type bacteria that give a smooth-colony appearance when cultured on agar plates."
comment: "LOSs are composed of lipid A and a saccharide moiety, limited to an oligosaccharide core only."
comment: "The lack of O-polysaccharide chains in LOS structures is an inherent property of bacteria."
synonym: "LOS" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09840

[Term]
id: goldbook:09848
name: lipopolysaccharidomics
def: "Study of the structure-activity relationship of bacterial lipopolysaccharides."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09849
name: membrane bilayer
def: "Planar structure spontaneously formed by amphipathic lipids in a polar \(aqueous\) environment."
comment: "Formation of a lipid bilayer is driven by the interaction of the polar heads of lipids with water molecules while minimizing the exposure of the hydrophobic parts of lipids to water. Lipid bilayers represent the basic structure of biological membranes."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09850
name: membrane rafts
def: "Dynamic\, nanometre-sized\, highly ordered\, membrane domains enriched in sterols and sphingolipids. They contain specific sets of membrane proteins that are sorted into rafts by their specific physical properties and specific lipid-protein interactions."
comment: "In functional terms, membrane rafts represent highly specialised membrane domains involved in various cellular processes, such as transmembrane transport, membrane trafficking, cell signalling, or virus assembly."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09860
consider: goldbook:09861

[Term]
id: goldbook:09851
name: molecular shape of lipids
def: "Specific structure of lipid molecules \(glycerophospholipids\) in aqueous environments and membranes as a function of the ratio between the cross-sections of their polar heads and hydrophobic tails."
comment: "Lipids’ ability to self-assemble in dynamic macrostructures in an aqueous medium is driven by the amphiphilic nature of lipid molecules, which tend to aggregate so that the hydrophobic (tails) and the hydrophilic (heads) parts of the lipid are well separated and the area of the dividing surface is held by the hydrophobic effect."
comment: "The shape of a membrane lipid depends on the relative size of its polar headgroup and apolar tails. In cases in which the headgroup and lipid backbone have similar cross-sectional areas, the molecule has a cylindrical shape (phosphatidylcholine and phosphatidylserine). Lipids with a small headgroup, such as phosphatidylethanolamine, are conical shaped. By contrast, when the hydrophobic part occupies a relatively smaller surface area, the molecule has the shape of an inverted cone (lysophosphatidylcholine and, to some extent, sphingomyelin)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09839

[Term]
id: goldbook:09852
name: O-polysaccharide
def: "Part of a lipopolysaccharide that consists of a polysaccharide chain extending outwards from the bacterial cell surface."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09853
name: plasma lipoproteins
def: "Assemblies of lipids and proteins in blood plasma transporting lipids between tissues."
comment: "They are structurally very similar to intracellular lipid droplets. They are classified according to their size and buoyant density into five types with different functions: chylomicrons, very low density lipoproteins (VLDL), intermediate lipoproteins (IDL), low density lipoproteins (LDL), and high density lipoproteins (HDL)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09854
name: polyunsaturated fatty acids
def: "Fatty acids with more than one double bond."
comment: "The double bonds usually have cis (Z) configuration and are methylene-interrupted."
synonym: "PUFA" RELATED OMO:0003012 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09855
name: prenol lipids
def: "Lipids containing a linear chain of isoprenoid groups."
comment: "Prenol lipids are synthesised from the five-carbon precursors isopentenyl diphosphate and dimethylallyl diphosphate. Both precursors are produced mainly via the mevalonic acid pathway."
synonym: "PR" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09856
name: proteolipids
def: "Proteins with covalently linked lipids\, e.g. fatty acid or glycophosphatidylinositol \(GPI\)."
comment: "The term ‘lipoprotein’ is also occasionally used to describe such compounds, but to avoid confusion this might be better reserved for the non-covalently linked lipid-protein complexes of the type found in plasma (plasma lipoproteins)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09853

[Term]
id: goldbook:09857
name: saccharolipids
def: "Biomolecules where fatty acids are directly bound to a saccharide backbone\, thus forming structures similar to glycerolipids or glycerophospholipids\, with saccharide substituting for the glycerol."
synonym: "SL" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09838
consider: goldbook:09839

[Term]
id: goldbook:09858
name: shotgun lipidomics
def: "Methodological approach based on the direct analysis of the total lipid extracts from cells or tissues without an online chromatographic separation."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09859
name: signalling lipids
def: "Lipid molecules involved in intracellular signalling and the regulation of cell death\, immune response\, or inflammation."
comment: "Broadly defined term referring to any lipid messenger that binds a protein target, such as a receptor, kinase or phosphatase, which in turn mediates the effects of these lipids on specific cellular responses."
comment: "Signalling lipids include diacylglycerol, phosphatidic acid, phosphatidylinositol-4,5-bisphosphate (the source of polyphosphorylated inositols), arachidonic acid-derived mediators (icosanoids, prostaglandins and leukotrienes), sphingolipid-derived mediators (ceramides, sphingosine, sphingosine-1-phosphate), or cholesterol-derived steroid hormones (androgens, estrogens, progestogens, mineralocorticoids, glucocorticoids)."
synonym: "lipid mediators" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09860
name: sphingolipids
def: "Complex family of lipids that contain a sphingoid base backbone and a fatty acyl chain."
comment: "Together with glycerophospholipids and sterols, sphingolipids are the major lipid components of cellular membranes."
synonym: "SP" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09837
consider: goldbook:09839

[Term]
id: goldbook:09861
name: sterol lipids
def: "Structural derivatives of tetracyclic alcohols with a hydroxyl group in the position \\\(\\ce\{C-3\}\\\) and an aliphatic side chain in the position \\\(\\ce\{C-17\}\\\)."
comment: "This category primarily comprises sterols: polycyclic compounds of isoprenoid origin and their various derivatives, including steroids, bile acids, and sterol esters. Sterols, along with glycerophospholipids and sphingolipids, are important constituents of cellular membranes. In addition, sterol lipids act as hormones (steroids) or fat emulsifiers (bile acids)."
synonym: "ST" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09839
consider: goldbook:09860

[Term]
id: goldbook:09862
name: structural lipids
def: "Lipids \(mainly glycerophospholipids\, sterols and sphingolipids\) involved in the formation of biological membranes."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09839
consider: goldbook:09860
consider: goldbook:09861

[Term]
id: goldbook:09863
name: targeted lipidomics
def: "Analytical technique focusing on the quantification of specific lipids of interest."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09864
name: unsaturation index
def: "Measure of the number of double bonds in fatty acids moieties of phospholipids\, calculated as the fraction of each unsaturated fatty acid multiplied by the number of double bonds within that fatty acid."
comment: "UI is indicative of membrane physical properties (e.g. fluidity, curvature, or flexibility)."
synonym: "UI" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09865
name: capture probe
def: "Specifically-designed single-stranded nucleic acid with a defined \(known\) nucleotide sequence immobilised on a surface."
comment: "An analogous principle, based on immobilised nucleic acid probes, has been applied in studies of the interactions of nucleic acids with proteins or other targets. See also nucleic acid aptamers."
comment: "Capture probes are utilised as a recognition element to test the nucleotide sequence of target deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) or ribonucleic acid (RNA) in the sample solution by using DNA or RNA hybridization."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09709
consider: goldbook:09712
consider: goldbook:09714

[Term]
id: goldbook:09866
name: chromatin immunoprecipitation
def: "Method for studying deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\)–protein and/or protein–protein interactions in the context of chromatin\, suitable for the identification of binding sites of proteins in genome DNA\, etc. This method combines chemical cross-linking\, the isolation of specific protein conjugates by immunoprecipitation\, and polymerase chain reaction \(PCR\) amplification of co-precipitated DNA."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09675
consider: goldbook:09685

[Term]
id: goldbook:09867
name: cross-linking
def: "Formation of a covalent bridge between two biomacromolecules or between two segments of the same biomacromolecule using reactive substances \(e.g. formaldehyde\, bifunctional aldehydes such as glutaraldehyde\, or cisplatin\) or photochemical processes."
comment: "Cross-linking is used to study protein oligomerization, as well as protein–protein and nucleic acid–protein interactions"
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09634
consider: goldbook:09866

[Term]
id: goldbook:09868
name: fluorescent <em>in situ</em> hybridization
def: "Cytogenetic technique used to detect and localise the presence or absence of specific deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) sequences on chromosomes. It uses fluorescent probes that only bind to those parts of the chromosome with which they show a high degree of sequence complementarity."
comment: "FISH is often used for finding specific features in DNA for use in genetic counselling, medicine, and species identification. FISH can also be used to detect and localise specific ribonucleic acid (RNA) targets (messenger ribonucleic acid (mRNA), long non-coding ribonucleic acid (lncRNA) and mitochondrial ribonucleic acid (miRNA)) in cells, circulating tumour cells, and tissue samples. In this context, it can help define the spatial-temporal patterns of gene expression within cells and tissues."
synonym: "FISH" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09869
name: groove binding
def: "Process in which molecules are bound within a minor or major groove of the deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) double helix."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09703

[Term]
id: goldbook:09870
name: hybridization assay
def: "Assay with specifically designed single-stranded deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) probe with a defined \(known\) nucleotide sequence usually immobilised on a surface \(in such a case\, the nucleic acid probe is called the capture probe\)."
comment: "The probe is used as a recognition element to test for the nucleotide sequence within the target DNA in the sample solution. If target DNA contains a sequence complementary to the probe, a hybrid double-stranded DNA (dsDNA) is formed."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09712
consider: goldbook:09865

[Term]
id: goldbook:09871
name: interactome
def: "Virtual map depicting protein–protein interactions."
comment: "The interactome is the most visible outcome of functional proteomics."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09746

[Term]
id: goldbook:09872
name: interactomics
def: "Complex and comprehensive study of the interactions between various proteins and between protein and other molecules within a cell\, such as nucleic acids and metabolites\, as well as the consequences of these interactions."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09873
name: intermolecular interactions
def: "Interactions between \(parts of\) two biomolecule chains \(e.g. base pairing in deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) double helix\, or the interactions between two polypeptide chains\, or between a DNA and a protein molecule\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09633
consider: goldbook:09703

[Term]
id: goldbook:09874
name: intramolecular interactions
def: "Interactions between \(neighbouring or distant\) segments within the same biomolecule chain \(e.g. base pairing in hairpin or monomolecular tetraplex structures of deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) or ribonucleic acid \(RNA\)\, the interactions stabilizing the secondary or tertiary structures of proteins\, etc.\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09633
consider: goldbook:09785

[Term]
id: goldbook:09875
name: non-covalent interactions
def: "Interactions between biomolecules or within a biomolecule \(e.g. hydrogen bonds\, electrostatic interactions\, and van der Waals forces\) taking part in the stabilization of proper biomolecule structure and intermolecular interactions."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09633
consider: goldbook:09873

[Term]
id: goldbook:09876
name: non-specific binding
def: "Binding of a protein to deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) independent of the nucleotide sequence or the conformation of the DNA\, often with a strong contribution of electrostatic interaction between the negatively charged sugar-phosphate backbone of the DNA and basic amino acid residues of the protein."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09712

[Term]
id: goldbook:09877
name: nucleic acid–protein interaction
def: "Formation of specific or non-specific complexes between deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) or ribonucleic acid \(RNA\) and proteins\, mediated and stabilised by a network of non-covalent interactions."
comment: "Nucleic acid–protein interactions are critical for basic cellular functions such as replication, transcription, translation, recombination, DNA repair etc. as well as for fine regulation of them. Proteins may bind more tightly to specific sequences in the nucleic acid or non-specifically, in which case their affinity is independent of sequence."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09661
consider: goldbook:09722
consider: goldbook:09875

[Term]
id: goldbook:09878
name: protein–protein interaction
def: "Association of one protein with one or more other proteins to form either homo- or heteromeric proteins or protein complexes."
comment: "Such associations are common in biological systems and are responsible for the regulation of numerous cellular functions in addition to the mediation of disease morphology, where aberrant interactions play a significant role."
synonym: "PPI" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09879
name: pull-down assay
def: "Approach used for studying protein–protein interactions analogous to immunoprecipitation\, using \(instead of antibody\) one of the interacting proteins \(the 'bait'\) immobilised at a surface to capture the other protein \(the 'prey'\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09675
consider: goldbook:09878

[Term]
id: goldbook:09880
name: reporter probe
def: "Specifically designed single-stranded deoxyribonucleic acid \(ssDNA\) \(alternatively\, ribonucleic acid \(RNA\) or a synthetic nucleic acid analogue such as locked nucleic acid \(LNA\) or peptide nucleic acid \(PNA\)\) with a defined \(known\) nucleotide sequence used to detect complementary DNA or RNA sequence in a sample."
comment: "Reporter probe is usually labelled (radioactively, immunologically, or by fluorescent, electroactive, or other species to produce a specific signal upon hybridization)."
comment: "Similar to capture probes, nucleic acid signalling probes (or nucleic acid aptamers) can be applied in studies of nucleic acid interactions with proteins and other ligands."
synonym: "signalling probe" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09709
consider: goldbook:09712
consider: goldbook:09714

[Term]
id: goldbook:09881
name: specific binding
def: "Binding of proteins to specific sites in deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) based on the precise recognition of nucleotide sequence and/or conformation of the DNA."
comment: "Sequence-specific DNA binding is typical for restriction enzymes, transcription factors and other proteins involved in cellular regulation, etc."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09712

[Term]
id: goldbook:09882
name: stacking interactions
def: "Attractive interaction between planar aromatic rings\, in nucleic acids being one of the critical forces stabilizing double- and multistranded structures."
comment: "Stacking interactions are involved in binding of intercalators to DNA."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09883
name: Tunel test
def: "Method for the determination of strand breaks and DNA fragmentation in cells based on labelling and quantification of free 3′-OH ends of deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\)."
comment: "The abbreviation is for terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase dUTP nick end labelling (TUNEL)."
synonym: "TUNEL" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09696
consider: goldbook:09729

[Term]
id: goldbook:09884
name: two hybrid system
def: "Molecular biology method for studying protein–protein or protein–deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) interactions in cellular \(yeast or bacterial\) milieu."
comment: "This method involves a transcription factor split into two separate domains (DNA binding and transcription activating), one of which is fused with one of the tested proteins (the "bait") and the other with another tested protein (the "prey"), as well as with a reporter gene construct. Only upon physical interaction between bait and prey is the expression of the reporter gene observed."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2016-1120
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09878

[Term]
id: goldbook:09885
name: chromatographic bed
def: "Form in which the stationary phase is used."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09886
name: immobilized stationary phase
def: "Stationary phase which has been immobilized on the support particles or on the inner wall of the column\, e.g. by a physical attraction \(coated stationary phase\)\, by chemical bonding \(bonded stationary phase\)\, or by in situ polymerisation \(cross-linked stationary phase\) after coating."
synonym: "immobilized stationary phase material" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09887
name: countercurrent chromatography
def: "Partition chromatography which uses two immiscible liquid phases and no solid support. One liquid acts as the stationary phase and the other as the mobile phase."
comment: "The method allows continuous elution of the mobile phase and on-line monitoring of the effluent."
comment: "The method employs a variety of systems using either gravity or centrifugal force to retain the stationary phase in the column."
synonym: "CCC" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09888
name: enantioselective chromatography
def: "Separation of enantiomeric species due to the enantioselectivity of their interaction with the chiral selector\(s\) of a chromatographic \(electrophoretic\) system."
synonym: "chiral chromatography" []
synonym: "chiral electrophoresis" []
synonym: "enantioselective electrophoresis" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09889
name: hydrodynamic chromatography
def: "Liquid chromatography for the separation of macromolecules and particles based on the difference in the speed of the mobile phase flow between the column axis and column wall and the specific size-dependent distribution of particles across the column cross-section."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09890
name: hydrophilic-interaction liquid chromatography
def: "Liquid chromatography in which the molecules are partitioned between a water-enriched layer\, which is immobilised on a hydrophilic \(polar\) stationary phase\, and a less-polar mobile phase\, containing water and organic solvent."
synonym: "HILIC" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09891
name: hydrophobic-interaction chromatography
def: "Chromatography in which molecules are separated by interactions between their hydrophobic moieties and hydrophobic sites on the surface of the stationary phase."
comment: "High salt concentrations are used in the mobile phase and separations are effected by changing the salt concentration. Gradients are run by decreasing the salt concentration over time."
synonym: "HIC" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09892
name: ion-exclusion chromatography
def: "Exclusion chromatography in which ionized compounds are excluded from the pores of the support and elute first\, while weakly ionized and non-ionic compounds elute later."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09893
name: ion-pair chromatography
def: "Liquid chromatography in which the mobile liquid phase contains an ionized additive \(an ion-pair reagent\) that can form an ion pair with an analyte molecule."
comment: "Separations occur due to the difference in the ability to form ion pairs and/or in the distribution coefficients of the ion pairs between the mobile and stationary phases."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09894
name: ligand exchange chromatography
def: "Chromatography based on the different ability of substances to form complexes with metal ions."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09895
name: micellar chromatography
def: "Reversed-phase liquid chromatography which uses a mobile phase containing a surfactant above its critical micellar concentration \(cmc\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09896
name: isocratic elution
def: "Chromatography in which the composition of the mobile phase remains constant during elution."
synonym: "isocratic analysis" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09897
name: column diameter
def: "Inner diameter of the column tubing."
comment: "The column radius \(r_{\rm{c}} = d_{\rm{c}}/2\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09898
name: column length
def: "Length of that part of the column tube that contains the stationary phase."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09899
name: cross-sectional area of the column
def: "Cross-sectional area of the empty tube\: \\\(A_\{\\rm\{c\}\} = \\pi r_\{\\rm\{c\}\}^\{2\} = \\pi \(d_\{\\rm\{c\}\}/2\)^\{2\}\\\)\, where \\\(d_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\) is the column diameter."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09897

[Term]
id: goldbook:09900
name: liquid-phase film thickness
def: "Average thickness of the liquid stationary phase film coated on the inside wall of the column tubing of an open-tubular column."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09901
name: monolithic column
def: "Column packed with a single piece of solid material \(monolith\) composed of interconnected skeletons \(forming macropores\) and interconnected flow paths \(mesopores\) as a stationary phase material."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09902
name: asymmetry factor
def: "Width of a peak \(\\\(f + b\\\)\) divided by twice the width of the front of the peak \(\\\(f\\\)\)\, measured at \\\(\\pu\{10\\%\}\\\) of the peak height \(see figure\). \\\[A_\{\\rm\{s\}\} = \\frac\{\(f + b\)\}\{2f\}\\\] Peak asymmetry \(schematic\). \\\(R_\{\\rm\{D\}\}\\\) is the response of the detector.Peak asymmetry \(schematic\). \\\(R_\{\\rm\{D\}\}\\\) is the response of the detector."
comment: "For a symmetrical peak \(A_{\rm{s}} = 1\)."
comment: "Some pharmacopoeia definitions of peak asymmetry measure the value at \(\pu{5\%}\) of the peak height."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09905

[Term]
id: goldbook:09903
name: peak width at base
def: "Peak width at peak base intercepted by tangents to the chromatogram drawn at the inflection points on either side of the peak. Represented by the line AB in the peak height figure."
synonym: "base width" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09904
name: peak width at half height
def: "Peak width at \\\(\\pu\{50\\%\}\\\) of the peak height that terminates at the intersections with the peak."
comment: "The peak width at half height must never be called the "half width" because that has a completely different meaning."
comment: "The peak width at half height of a Gaussian peak is about 2.355 times the standard deviation of the modelled Gaussian. See figure in peak height."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09905
name: tailing factor
def: "Width of the back of a peak \(\\\(b\\\)\) divided by the width of the front \(\\\(f\\\)\) of the peak measured at \\\(\\pu\{10\\%\}\\\) of the peak height. See figure in asymmetry factor."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09902

[Term]
id: goldbook:09906
name: effective plate height
def: "Column length \(\\\(L\\\)\) divided by effective plate number \(\\\(N_\{\\rm\{eff\}\}\\\)\)\: \\\[H_\{\\rm\{eff\}\} = \\frac\{L\}\{N_\{\\rm\{eff\}\}\}\\\]"
synonym: "height equivalent to one effective plate" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09898
consider: goldbook:09907

[Term]
id: goldbook:09907
name: effective plate number
def: "Number indicative of column performance\, calculated from the ratios of measures of adjusted retention and peak width\: \\\[\\eqalign\{& N_\{\\rm\{eff\}\} = \\left\(\\frac\{V_\{\\rm\{R\}\}^\{\\prime\}\}\{\\sigma\} \\right\)^\{2\} = \\left\(\\frac\{t_\{\\rm\{R\}\}^\{\\prime\}\}\{\\sigma\} \\right\)^\{2\} \\cr & N_\{\\rm\{eff\}\} = 16 \\left\(\\frac\{V_\{\\rm\{R\}\}^\{\\prime\}\}\{w_\{\\rm\{b\}\}\} \\right\)^\{2\} = 16 \\left\(\\frac\{t_\{\\rm\{R\}\}^\{\\prime\}\}\{w_\{\\rm\{b\}\}\} \\right\)^\{2\} \\cr & N_\{\\rm\{eff\}\} \\approx 5.545 \\left\(\\frac\{V_\{\\rm\{R\}\}^\{\\prime\}\}\{w_\{\\rm\{h\}\}\} \\right\)^\{2\} \\approx 5.545 \\left\(\\frac\{t_\{\\rm\{R\}\}^\{\\prime\}\}\{w_\{\\rm\{h\}\}\} \\right\)^\{2\}\}\\\]"
comment: "The plate number is related to the effective plate number and retention factor (\(k\)) \(N = N_{\rm{eff}}{\left(\frac{{k + 1}}{k} \right)^2}\)"
comment: "These expressions assume a Gaussian (symmetrical) peak."
comment: "Units for the quantities being divided must be consistent so that their ratio is dimensionless: i.e. if the numerator is a time, then peak width must also be expressed in terms of time"
comment: "\(V_{\rm{R}}^{\prime}\): adjusted retention volume; \(t_{\rm{R}}^{\prime}\): total retention time; \(\sigma\): standard deviation of the Gaussian peak; \(w_{\rm{b}}\): peak width at base; \(w_{\rm{h}}\): peak width at half height; \(\rm{8 \ln 2} ≈ 5.545\)."
synonym: "number of effective plates" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09903
consider: goldbook:09904
consider: goldbook:10039

[Term]
id: goldbook:09908
name: reduced plate height
def: "In liquid chromatography plate height divided by average particle diameter. \\\[h = H/d_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\]"
comment: "For open-tubular columns \(h\) is the plate height divided by the column diameter, \(h = H/d_{\rm{c}}\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09897

[Term]
id: goldbook:09909
name: detector
def: "Sensor that responds to components in the effluent."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09911
name: capillary affinity electrophoresis
def: "Capillary electrophoresis in which the background electrolyte contains substances capable of specific\, often biospecific\, interactions with the analytes."
comment: "Biospecific compounds can be already in the sample (receptor-acceptor formation under applied electric field, or the bioselective compounds may be attached on the capillary inner wall before the analysis starts. They are bound covalently or dynamically on the surface."
synonym: "ACE" []
synonym: "CAE" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
synonym: "affinity capillary electrophoresis" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09913

[Term]
id: goldbook:09912
name: capillary electrochromatography
def: "Liquid chromatography\, where the movement of the mobile phase through a capillary\, filled\, packed\, or coated with a stationary phase\, is achieved by electroosmotic flow \(which may be assisted by pressure\)."
comment: "The retention time is determined by a combination of electrophoretic migration and chromatographic retention."
synonym: "CEC" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09963

[Term]
id: goldbook:09913
name: capillary electrophoresis
def: "Separation method based solely on the differences in the electrophoretic mobilities of charged species in capillaries."
comment: "The background electrolyte may be an aqueous or non-aqueous solution."
comment: "The basic kind of CE is capillary zone electrophoresis in which separation takes place in the homogeneous solution of the background electrolyte on the basis of different electrophoretic mobilities of analytes in a free solution."
comment: "The electrolytes can contain additives, which can interact with the charged species and alter their electrophoretic mobilities."
comment: "The use of the term capillary electrophoresis (CE) as a collective term for all capillary electromigration techniques is not recommended, as some of these techniques involve separation mechanisms other than electrophoresis."
synonym: "CE" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09911

[Term]
id: goldbook:09914
name: capillary gel electrophoresis
def: "Capillary sieving electrophoresis in which the capillary is filled with a gel."
synonym: "CGE" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09917

[Term]
id: goldbook:09915
name: capillary isoelectric focusing
def: "Electrophoretic technique for the separation of analytes according to their isoelectric points by an electric field along a pH gradient formed in a capillary."
synonym: "CIEF" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09916
name: capillary isotachophoresis
def: "Electrophoretic separation technique in a discontinuous buffer system in which analytes migrate according to their electrophoretic mobilities\, forming a chain of adjacent zones moving with equal speed \(velocity\) between two solutions\, leading and terminating electrolytes\, bracketing the mobility range of the analytes."
synonym: "CITP" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09917
name: capillary sieving electrophoresis
def: "Electrophoretic separation technique\, which takes place in a capillary containing a medium in the background electrolyte that retains molecules and particles based on their size and shape."
comment: "The medium may be an entangled polymer network."
synonym: "CSE" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09918
name: effective length of the capillary
def: "Length of the capillary from the sample introduction point to the detection point."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09919
name: electrokinetic chromatography
def: "Separation method based on a combination of electrophoresis and interactions of the analytes with additives \(e.g. surfactants\)\, which form a dispersed phase moving at a different speed \(velocity\). In order to achieve separation\, either the analytes or this secondary phase should be charged."
comment: " This is not totally compatible with the definition of chromatography."
comment: "Surfactants must be at concentrations above their critical micelle concentration for being able to form a dispersed phase – otherwise they will just act as ion-pair reagents."
synonym: "electrokinetic capillary chromatography" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09920
name: electrokinetic injection
def: "Injection of the analytical sample by applying a potential across the sample and capillary and using the electroosmotic flow to draw an aliquot into the end of the capillary tube. The sample is then replaced by the running electrolyte for the separation to take place."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09921
name: electroosmosis
def: "Motion of a liquid through a capillary \(or membrane or plug\) because of the application of an electric field across the capillary \(or membrane or plug\)."
synonym: "electro-osmosis" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09922
name: electroosmotic velocity
def: "Effective length of the capillary \(\\\(L_\{\\rm\{eff\}\}\\\)\) divided by the electroosmotic hold-up time \(\\\(t_\{\\rm\{eo\}\}\\\)\). \\\[\\boldsymbol\{v\}_\{\\rm\{eo\}\} = L_\{\\rm\{eff\}\}/t_\{\\rm\{eo\}\}\\\]"
comment: "In capillary electromigration, this velocity is positive, by convention, in the direction from the sample introduction end of the capillary to the detection end, otherwise negative."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09918
consider: goldbook:09921

[Term]
id: goldbook:09923
name: electroosmotic volume flow rate
def: "Volume flow rate divided by electric field strength through the whole plug or capillary."
synonym: "electroosmotic volume flow" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09924
name: electrostacking
def: "Methods that use differences in migration behaviour in spatially defined regions of the capillary of different field strengths \(conductivities\)."
comment: "Electrostacking methods can be used to concentrate dilute samples."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09925
name: hydrodynamic injection
def: "Introduction of the analytical sample into a capillary by a pressure difference between the ends of the capillary."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09926
name: immunoelectrophoresis
def: "Biochemical methods for separation and characterization of proteins based on electrophoresis and reaction with antibodies."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09927
name: micellar electrokinetic chromatography
def: "Electrokinetic chromatography\, in which the secondary phase is a micellar dispersed phase in the capillary."
synonym: "micellar electrokinetic capillary chromatography" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09919

[Term]
id: goldbook:09928
name: microemulsion electrokinetic chromatography
def: "Electrokinetic chromatography\, where a microemulsion is employed as the dispersed phase."
synonym: "microemulsion electrokinetic capillary chromatography" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09919

[Term]
id: goldbook:09929
name: non-aqueous capillary electrophoresis
def: "Capillary electrophoresis\, which uses water-free organic solutions and modifiers to produce ions in solution."
comment: "Typical modifiers are acetic acid, sodium acetate, ammonium acetate, ammonium chloride and ammonium hydroxide (non aqueous)."
comment: "Typical organic solvents are methanol, ethanol, propanol, and acetonitrile."
synonym: "NACE" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09913

[Term]
id: goldbook:09930
name: sample introduction
def: "In isotachophoresis a sample is introduced between a zone of fast leading electrolyte and a zone of slow terminating electrolyte."
comment: "Usually, the leading and terminating electrolytes have a common counter-ion, but the co-ions are different."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09931
name: asymmetrical flow field-flow fractionation
def: "Flow field-flow fractionation in which only one channel wall is permeable\, meaning there is one channel inlet for the flow during the run and some of the channel flow is directed through the accumulation wall."
comment: "Advantages of AsFlFFF include that the upper channel wall can be made of a transparent material, allowing the accumulation and sample migration to be viewed. Sample focusing can be achieved during relaxation, resulting in a narrower sample band being produced, resulting in lower band broadening."
comment: "In a uniform width channel this would result in the channel flow decreasing dramatically along the channel length. In order to minimize this, a trapezoid shape of the separation channel has been developed for AsFlFFF. In general, the channel geometry significantly affects the separation efficiency in AF4."
comment: "Three different channel designs were evaluated using polystyrene latex nanoparticles. The channel breadth was held constant for one (rectangular) profile, and was reduced either linearly (trapezoid profile) or exponentially (exponential profile) along the length for the other two channels. Com-pared with a rectangular channel, trapezoid and exponential profiles provided better performance of AsFlFFF and allowed band broadening to be reduced."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09935

[Term]
id: goldbook:09932
name: Brownian field-flow fractionation mode
def: "Mechanism of field-flow fractionation of sub-micron particles that relies on Brownian motion."
comment: "BFFF mode requires a specific sample injection procedure described as stop-flow injection or a relaxation step. A relaxation time is needed to equilibrate the sample under the influence of a force field and Brownian diffusion in the absence of flow. The injected sample is forced to concentrate in a thin layer adjacent to the accumulation wall (channel bottom)."
comment: "Two main factors influencing the sample behaviour in BFFF mode are the properties of particles characterized by the diffusion coefficient and the strength of the cross-field applied."
comment: "When the flow is switched on, elution begins with smaller particles, which are distributed further away from the accumulation wall and thus protrude further into the faster flow streams; larger particles elute later."
synonym: "BFFF" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
synonym: "BFFF mode" []
synonym: "NFFF mode" []
synonym: "normal FFF" []
synonym: "normal FFF mode (NFFF)" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09934
consider: goldbook:09943

[Term]
id: goldbook:09933
name: coiled-tube field-flow fractionation
def: "Field-flow fractionation in a long \(usually \\\(\\pu\{10 m\}\\\) or more\) rotating coiled column under the action of asymmetrical centrifugal field."
comment: "CTFFF can be attributed to non-conventional sedimentation field-flow fractionation (SdFFF), which enables the sample mass to be increased to gram levels."
comment: "The complex asymmetrical force field is generated in planetary centrifuges where the column drum rotates around its own axis and at the same time revolves around the central axis of the centrifuge."
synonym: "CTFFF" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09934
consider: goldbook:09938

[Term]
id: goldbook:09934
name: field-flow fractionation
def: "Separation based on the retention and elution of macromolecular\, colloidal\, and particulate matter achieved by the combined action of the non-uniform flow speed \(velocity\) profile of a carrier fluid \(mobile phase\) and a physical force field \(e.g. gravitational\, centrifugal\, electrical\) applied at right angles to a thin \(\\\(\\pu\{0.05 mm\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{0.5 mm\}\\\)\) channel \(see figure\). Basic principle of field-flow fractionation.Basic principle of field-flow fractionation."
comment: "Although similar to liquid chromatography, FFF requires no stationary phase, so the separation channel contains no packing material and only physical interactions are involved in the separation process."
comment: "FFF is applicable to the fractionation and characterization of colloidal and solid particles, polymers, and biological cells in the size range from \(\pu{1 nm}\) to \(\pu{50 \upmu m}\)."
comment: "The sample mass injected is usually less than \(\pu{1 mg}\) to avoid overloading."
synonym: "FFF" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09943

[Term]
id: goldbook:09935
name: flow field-flow fractionation
def: "Field-flow fractionation in which the physical force field is a nonspecific hydrodynamic force field across the channel\, generated by a secondary mobile phase cross-flow in addition to the normal channel flow."
comment: "Like sedimentation field-flow fractionation (SdFFF), FlFFF can be used for submicron particles and macromolecules using the Brownian field-flow fractionation mode or micron size particles utilizing the hyperlayer mode."
comment: "The sample components are driven by the cross-flow toward the accumulation wall, which consists of porous frit covered with a permeable membrane."
comment: "This technique can be applied to the size fractionation of different synthetic polymers, liposomes, proteins, viruses, dissolved organic matter, humic and fulvic acids as well as their complexes with trace metals, diesel soot particles, marine colloids, heterogeneous and arbitrarily shaped natural colloidal particles, gold colloid standards starting from 5 nm."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09932
consider: goldbook:09934
consider: goldbook:09937
consider: goldbook:09938

[Term]
id: goldbook:09936
name: gravitational field-flow fractionation
def: "Field-flow fractionation in which the physical force field is the Earth’s gravitational field."
comment: "GFFF can fractionate particulate matter in the micrometre size range (large microorganisms, blood cells, sediments, etc)."
comment: "Since the Earth’s gravitational field produces relatively weak forces, this mode is only appropriate for micron size particles, hence the separation is an example of hyperlayer focusing field-flow fractionation."
synonym: "GFFF" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09934
consider: goldbook:09937

[Term]
id: goldbook:09937
name: hyperlayer focusing field-flow fractionation
def: "Mechanism of field-flow fractionation of particles greater than \\\(\\pu\{1 \\upmu m\}\\\) in diameter relying on hydrodynamic lift forces near the channel wall to move particles into faster flowing streams."
comment: "Due to the large size of the particles, Brownian diffusion away from the accumulation wall is assumed to be negligible in this mode."
comment: "HyFFF is a preferred alternative to steric field-flow fractionation. The retention of particles larger than \(\pu{1 \upmu m}\) typically increases with flow rate. Particles are driven away from the wall by a hydrody-namic lift force and elevated into faster flow streams. The particles form thin layers at an equilibrium position, where the external field is balanced by the opposing hydrodynamic lift force, as compared to steric mode, where the thin layer of particles is adjacent to the accumulation wall."
synonym: "HyFFF" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
synonym: "hyperlayer field-flow fractionation mode" []
synonym: "lift-hyperlayer field-flow fractionation mode" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09934
consider: goldbook:09940

[Term]
id: goldbook:09938
name: sedimentation field-flow fractionation
def: "Field-flow fractionation in which the physical force field is a centrifugal force field."
comment: "SdFFF can be applied to a wide range of colloidal and micron size particles and is a powerful method for cell separations, including industrial carbon blacks, silica nanoparticles, silver and gold nano-particles, aquatic sediments, aerosols, and latex beads."
comment: "SdFFF can be successfully applied to the fractionation and characterization (sizing) of submicron particles, starting from about \(\pu{5 nm}\), but this limit depends on the particle density, as the separation is based on the buoyant mass of the particles."
comment: "SdFFF can operate in both the Brownian field-flow fractionation (BFFF) and hyperlayer field-flow fractionation (HyFFF) modes and caution must be exercised if samples span the Brownian-hyperlayer transition point of about \(\pu{1 \upmu m}\), as this can lead to separated fractions containing two particle sizes, smaller particles separated by Brownian mode and larger particles separated by hyperlayer mode."
comment: "SdFFF is performed in a circular flat channel inserted inside a centrifugal basket."
synonym: "SdFFF" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09932
consider: goldbook:09934
consider: goldbook:09937

[Term]
id: goldbook:09939
name: split-flow thin-cell fractionation
def: "Separation in which laminar flow conditions are established between a liquid containing suspended sample and a carrier liquid\, with a physical force field applied across the streams."
comment: "A semi-preparative binary separation can be generated with smaller particles eluting from one outlet and larger particles emerging from the other. Careful manipulation of the inlet and outlet flow rates and the field strength enables the cut-off diameter of the particles to be selected."
comment: "Although SPILTT is similar to field-flow fractionation, it should not be referred to as FFF. The separation is achieved by the combined action of controlled flow rates and an applied cross-field."
comment: "Fields that have been tested so far include gravitational, centrifugal and magnetic."
comment: "SPLITT enables the separation of samples of masses of the order of grams."
comment: "The system has two channel inlets and two outlets. Sample suspension is continuously pumped into one inlet and carrier liquid is pumped into the other inlet. Due to the laminar flow conditions, these two streams do not mix."
synonym: "SPLITT" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09934

[Term]
id: goldbook:09940
name: steric field-flow fractionation
def: "Mechanism of field-flow fractionation of particles greater than \\\(\\pu\{1 \\upmu m\}\\\) in diameter relying on proximity to the accumulation wall."
comment: "In StFFF the elution order is opposite to that of Brownian field-flow fractionation mode and larger particles move ahead of smaller ones."
comment: "It was originally considered that particles are in contact with the accumulation wall during the entire migration time and consequently the centre of larger particles are situated closer to the centre of the channel in the faster flow streams. This is never achieved in practice due to hydrodynamic lift forces away from the accumulation wall."
synonym: "StFFF" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09932
consider: goldbook:09934

[Term]
id: goldbook:09941
name: symmetrical flow field-flow fractionation
def: "Flow field-flow fractionation in which the speed of mobile phase is constant along the length of the channel due to constant channel width."
comment: "The width is constant except for triangular sections at each of the ends of the channel."
comment: "Theory and data analysis are simplified by the assumption of constant flow."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09935

[Term]
id: goldbook:09942
name: thermal field-flow fractionation
def: "Field-flow fractionation in which the physical force field is created by a temperature difference across the channel."
comment: "One side of the channel is heated and the other side is cooled. A relatively small temperature difference between the top and bottom walls of the channel provides a high thermal gradient."
comment: "TFFF has been mainly used for the separation of lyophilic synthetic polymers, including ultrahigh molecular weight polymers and microgels. However, some reports of other types of macromolecules and particles, including aqueous solvents, are present in the literature."
synonym: "TFFF" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09934

[Term]
id: goldbook:09943
name: elution mode
def: "Method of introduction of the mobile phase \(upper or lower\) into the rotating coil \(from the head or the tail\). Of four combinations\, only two elution modes should be used in high-speed counter-current chromatography\, i.e. either the lower phase from the head toward the tail or the upper phase from the tail toward the head."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09944

[Term]
id: goldbook:09944
name: high-speed counter-current chromatography
def: "Counter-current chromatography system using a multilayer coil separation column\, which undergoes a type-J synchronous planetary motion \(see figure\).Type-J planetary motion of a multilayer coil separation column. The column holder rotates about its own axis and revolves around the centrifuge axis at the same angular velocity \(ω\) in the same direction. This planetary motion prevents twisting of the bundle of flow tubes\, allowing continuous elution through a rotating column without risk of leakage and contamination."
comment: "The method covers analytical (microgram) to preparative (\(\pu{10 g}\)) scale separations usually in a few hours at a high partition efficiency up to a few thousand theoretical plates (see number of theoretical plates)."
synonym: "HSCCC" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09887

[Term]
id: goldbook:09945
name: mobile-phase volume
def: "Volume of the liquid mobile phase contained in the column in counter-current chromatography \\\[V_\{\\rm\{M\}\} = V_\{\\rm\{C\}\} - V_\{\\rm\{S\}\}\\\] where \\\(V_\{\\rm\{C\}\}\\\) is the total volume of the column and \\\(V_\{\\rm\{S\}\}\\\) is the volume of the liquid stationary phase in the column."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09887

[Term]
id: goldbook:09946
name: multilayer coil
def: "Separation column used for high-speed counter-current chromatography."
comment: "The multilayer coil is prepared from a long piece of polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE) tubing by coaxially winding it around a spool-shaped holder, making multiple coiled layers."
comment: "Usually, three different inner diameter sizes of tubing are used: ca. \(\pu{1 mm}\) for analytical, \(\pu{1.6 mm}\) for semipreparative, and \(\pu{2.6 mm}\) for preparative separations. It provides efficient mixing of the two phases and a high retention of the stationary phase under a high flow rate of the mobile phase."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09944

[Term]
id: goldbook:09947
name: pH-zone-refining counter-current chromatography
def: "Counter-current chromatography that produces a train of fused rectangular solute peaks called pH-zones\, each with its specific pH. Each zone contains highly concentrated solute\, often near saturation level\, and is separated from the neighbouring zone by a narrow strip called a mixing zone\, which contains a mixture of impurities."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09887

[Term]
id: goldbook:09948
name: retainer and eluter
def: "Most important reagents in pH-zone-refining counter-current chromatography. A retainer is added to the stationary organic phase to retain the analytes and the eluter added to the mobile aqueous phase to elute the analytes."
comment: "Trifluoroacetic acid and triethylamine are typical retainers for the separation of acidic and basic compounds, respectively, where the stationary phase typically is the organic phase. The retainer determines the analyte concentration in the stationary phase. NH4OH and HCl are commonly used as the eluter for the separation of acidic and basic compounds, respectively. The eluter always stays in the aqueous mobile phase to serve as a counter ion. pH-zone-refining CCC can also be performed using the organic phase as the mobile phase (normal displacement mode). In this case, the retainer becomes the eluter to elute the analytes, and the eluter becomes the retainer to retain the analytes in the aqueous stationary phase."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09947

[Term]
id: goldbook:09949
name: retention volume of the stationary phase
def: "Volume of the stationary phase\, which stays permanently within the column in counter-current chromatography."
comment: "\(V_{\rm{s}}\) is one of the most important parameters that determines the peak resolution."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09887

[Term]
id: goldbook:09950
name: type-J coil planet centrifuge
def: "Centrifuge that provides type-J synchronous planetary motion to the column holder\, the axis of which is parallel to the centrifuge axis \(see figure in high-speed counter-current chromatography\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09944
consider: goldbook:09951

[Term]
id: goldbook:09951
name: type-J synchronous planetary motion
def: "Unique type of planetary motion \(so named because the flow tubes form a letter J figure\, as shown in the figure in high-speed counter-current chromatography\) of the column holder\, which rotates around its own axis once during one revolution around the central axis of the centrifuge in the same direction."
comment: "The system prevents the flow tubes from twisting during column rotation, and produces an excellent mixing and high retention of the stationary phase in a multilayer coil separation column."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09944

[Term]
id: goldbook:09952
name: chiral additive
def: "Chiral selector that has been added as a component of a mobile phase or electrophoretic medium."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09953
name: comprehensive two-dimensional chromatography
def: "Type of two-dimensional chromatography in which all the effluent from the first separation step is transferred in fractions to a second separation step."
comment: "The multiplier (\(\times\)) is used to denote the two separation steps, e.g. \(GC \times GC\) or \(LC \times SFC\) ."
synonym: "comprehensive chromatography" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09954
name: heart-cut separations
def: "Type of two-dimensional chromatography in which selected fractions from the first separation step are transferred to a second separation step."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09955
name: modulation frequency
def: "Number of modulations in a given time interval."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09956
name: modulation number
def: "Number of modulations for a given first-dimension peak."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09957
name: modulation period
def: "Duration of a complete cycle of modulation in a system for comprehensive two-dimensional chromatography."
comment: "Modulation period is the length of a second-dimension chromatogram, i.e. the time between two successive injections into the second column."
synonym: "modulation time" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09953

[Term]
id: goldbook:09958
name: modulator
def: "Interface device between the two columns in a system for comprehensive two-dimensional chromatography that accumulates or samples narrow bands from the effluent of the first column for fast re-injection into the second column."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09953

[Term]
id: goldbook:09959
name: multi-dimensional chromatography
def: "Chromatography in which parts or all of the separated sample components are subjected to additional separation steps."
comment: "Multi-dimensional chromatography can be done, for example, by conducting a particular fraction eluting from the column into other columns (system) having different separation characteristics."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09960
name: wrap-around
def: "Occurrence of second dimension peaks in subsequent elution sequences\, caused by second-dimension retention times that exceed the modulation time in comprehensive two-dimensional chromatography."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09953
consider: goldbook:09957

[Term]
id: goldbook:09962
name: retention volume of an unretained compound
def: "Retention volume of a sample component the molecules of which are larger than the largest pores of the particles of the stationary phase."
comment: "Ignoring any extra-column volume, \(V_{0}\) is equal to the interparticle volume of the column."
comment: "The retention volume of an unretained compound is related to the retention time of an unretained compound (in size-exclusion chromatography) and the flow rate (\(F_{\rm{c}}\)) by \(V_{0} = t_{0} \times F_{\rm{c}}\)"
comment: "These molecules will be eluted first from the column."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09963
consider: goldbook:10040

[Term]
id: goldbook:09963
name: retention volume
def: "Retention volume of a sample component\, the molecules of which are smaller than the largest pores of the particles of the stationary phase but larger than the smallest pores."
comment: "The corresponding retention time is related to the retention time (in size-exclusion chromatography) (\(t_{\rm{R}}\)) and the flow rate (\(F_{\rm{c}}\)) by \(V_{\rm{R}} = t_{\rm{R}} \times F_{\rm{c}}\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09964
name: total mobile phase volume
def: "Retention volume of a sample component\, the molecules of which are smaller than the smallest pores of the particles of the stationary phase."
comment: "The total mobile phase volume is related to the total mobile phase time (in size-exclusion chromatography) (\(t_{\rm{t}}\)) and the flow rate (\(F_{\rm{c}}\)) by \(V_{\rm{t}} = t_{\rm{t}} \times F_{\rm{c}}\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:09963

[Term]
id: goldbook:09965
name: relative molecular-mass exclusion limit
def: "Maximum value of the relative molecular masses of components in a specific polymer solvent system that can enter into the pores of the porous non-adsorbing material used in size-exclusion chromatography."
comment: "For particles with relative molar mass greater than the exclusion limit the separation effect of the size-exclusion chromatography vanishes."
synonym: "molecular-weight exclusion limit" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09966
name: specific resolution
def: "Peak resolution also considering the molar masses \(\\\(M_\{1\}\\\)\, \\\(M_\{2\}\\\)\) of the two test compounds\: \\\[R_\{\\rm\{sp\}\} = \\left\[\\frac\{V_\{\\rm\{R1\}\} - V_\{\\rm\{R2\}\}\}\{\(w_\{\\rm\{b1\}\} + w_\{\\rm\{b2\}\}\)/2\} \\right\] \\cdot \\left\[ \\frac\{1\}\{\\log \(M_\{2\}/M_\{1\}\)\} \\right\]\\\]"
comment: "In some areas, the symbol \(R_{\rm{s}}\) is used for the specific resolution. Due to the possibility of confusing it with the general resolution term (see peak resolution), the symbol \(R_{\rm{sp}}\) is suggested here."
comment: "The test compounds used for the determination of the specific resolution should have a narrow molecular-mass distribution (the ratio of the mass-average and number-average molecular masses should be equal to or less than about \(\pu{1.1}\)) and differ by a factor of about \(\pu{10}\) in their molar masses."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2017-0111
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09967
name: flavans
def: "Flavans are compounds with a 2-phenyl-3\,4-dihydro-2\\\(H\\\)-1-benzopyran skeleton \(1 in flavonoids\)\, that may be substituted\, and include flavan-3-ols \(compounds derived from 2-phenyl-3\,4-dihydro-2\\\(H\\\)-1-benzopyran-3-ol skeleton 2\) and flavan-4-ones \(compounds derived from 2-phenyl-2\,3-dihydro-4\\\(H\\\)-1-benzopyran-4-one skeleton 3\)\, which are generically designated as flavanols and flavanones\, respectively. Compounds derived from 2-phenyl-3\,4-dihydro-2\\\(H\\\)-1-benzopyran-3\,4-diol skeleton 4 are flavan-3\,4-diols\, designated leukoanthocyanidins because these colourless compounds have their structure derived from that of an anthocyanidin\, specifically they are dehydronated 3\,4-dihydroxy-1\,2\,3\,4-tetrahydroanthocyanidins."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2013-0919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:09968
name: flavones
def: "Flavones and 3-hydroxyflavones \(flavonols\) are compounds with a 2-phenyl-4\\\(H\\\)-1-benzopyran-4-one \(2-phenyl-4\\\(H\\\)-chromen-4-one\) skeleton 5."
comment: "The term "flavonol" is used as a class name for compounds with a 3-hydroxy-2-phenyl-4\(H\)-1-benzopyran-4-one (3-hydroxy-2-phenyl-4\(H\)-chromen-4-one) skeleton 6."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2013-0919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

