property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13271

[Term]
id: goldbook:13299
name: immunologically privileged site
def: "Any of various sites in the body where foreign tissue grafts do not induce an immune reaction."
comment: "Such sites include the eye, brain, testis, and unborn fetus. Although antigens do migrate from these privileged sites, they either induce immunological tolerance or a nondestructive response."
synonym: "privileged site" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-11-06-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13302

[Term]
id: goldbook:13300
name: immunological memory
def: "Ability of the immune system to respond faster and more effectively to subsequent exposures to an antigen following a primary immune response to the same antigen."
comment: "Typically, memory T cells appear five days following initial immunization, whereas memory B cells may take about a month to reach maximum levels. Populations of such cells may persist for the lifetime of the individual."
synonym: "immunological anamnesis" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-11-06-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13271
consider: goldbook:13451
consider: goldbook:13452
consider: goldbook:13601

[Term]
id: goldbook:13301
name: immunological synapse
def: "Contact point between the T cell and antigen-presenting cell \(APC\) that is generated by reorganization and clustering of cell surface molecules in lipid rafts. The synapse facilitates interactions between T-cell receptor \(TCR\) and major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) molecules\, and between adhesion molecules\, thereby potentiating a TCR-mediated activation signal."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-11-06-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:12848
consider: goldbook:12888
consider: goldbook:13390
consider: goldbook:13434
consider: goldbook:13714
consider: goldbook:13724

[Term]
id: goldbook:13302
name: immunological tolerance
def: "Persistent specific immunological unresponsiveness toward a substance that would normally be expected to elicit an immune response\, resulting from previous non-sensitizing exposure to an antigen."
comment: "Tolerance to specific foreign antigens can be induced by the exposure to the foreign antigens during embryonic or neonatal life (depending upon species). In adults, tolerance (usually of shorter duration) can be induced by using particular routes of administration for the antigens or administration of agents that are particularly effective against cells proliferating in response to antigen. Mechanisms may include deletion of potentially reactive lymphocytes or their "inactivation" by immunological suppression."
synonym: "tolerance" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-11-06-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13399
consider: goldbook:13701

[Term]
id: goldbook:13303
name: immunology
def: "Science that deals with the immune system including cell-mediated immunity \(CMI\) and humoral aspects of immunity and immune responses."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-11-06-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
