[Term]
id: goldbook:13360
name: killer activatory receptor
def: "Receptor found on natural killer \(NK\) cells\, and some $\\ce\{\\upgamma\\!\{\:\}\\updelta\\!\}$ and $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\{\:\}\\upbeta\\! T cells\}$."
comment: "KARs recognize major histocompatibility complex (MHC) class I molecules and, like the C-type lectin receptors also found in these cells, can either inhibit or activate the killer cells. If immunoreceptor tyrosine-based inhibitory motif (ITIM) sequences are present in their cytoplasmic domain they are inhibitory."
comment: "Killer cell immunoglobulin-like receptors (KIRs) lacking ITIMs can associate with immunoreceptor tyrosine-based activation motif (ITAM)-containing adaptor molecules in which case they can activate the killer cell."
