consider: goldbook:13847

[Term]
id: goldbook:13851
name: ditopic receptor
def: "Receptor with two binding sites."
comment: "Ditopic receptors can be either homotopic or heterotopic according to whether or not the binding sites are the same (typically, self-complementary binding sites) or different."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-12-03-12
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13852
name: rod-coil copolymer
def: "Copolymer the molecules of which consist of rigid and flexible blocks."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-12-03-12
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13853
name: Scatchard equation
def: "Equation used to calculate the association constant\, \\\(K_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\)\, of a ligand with a protein molecule."
comment: "The Scatchard equation is \[\frac{r}{c} = -K_{\rm{a}} r + K_{\rm{a}} n\] in which \(r\) is the ratio of the concentration of bound ligand to the concentration of available protein binding sites, \(c\) is the concentration of free ligand, and \(n\) is the number of binding sites per protein molecule."
comment: "The association constant, \(K_{\rm{a}}\), has units of reciprocal concentration."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-12-03-12
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13854
name: Scatchard plot
def: "Linear plot of \\\(r/c\\\) against \\\(r\\\) in accordance with the Scatchard equation."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-12-03-12
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13853

[Term]
id: goldbook:13855
name: main-chain self-assembly
def: "Inter- or intra-molecular self-assembly of constitutional units within the backbones of polymer molecules."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-12-03-12
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13856
name: side-chain self-assembly
def: "Inter- or intra-molecular self-assembly of side-chains or constitutional units of polymer molecules."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-12-03-12
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13857
name: shell-crosslinked micelle
def: "Core-shell structure formed by self-assembly of amphiphiles and subsequently covalently crosslinked through functionalities in the shell."
comment: "Such structures are used, e.g., in drug delivery and dye encapsulation."
comment: "The amphiphiles are often amphiphilic block copolymers."
synonym: "knedel" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-12-03-12
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13795
consider: goldbook:13805
consider: goldbook:13806

[Term]
id: goldbook:13858
name: smectic
def: "Pertaining to the arrangement and orientation of molecules in a smectic mesophase."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-12-03-12
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13859

[Term]
id: goldbook:13859
name: smectic mesophase
def: "Mesophase that has the molecules arranged in layers with a well-defined layer spacing or periodicity."
comment: "Although the total number of smectic mesophases cannot be specified, the following types have been defined: \(SmA\), \(SmB\), \(SmC\), \(SmF\), and \(SmI\). The alphabetical order of suffixes merely indicates an order of discovery."
comment: "Smectic mesophases are amongst the class of liquid-crystal mesophases."
comment: "There are several types of smectic mesophases, characterised by a variety of molecular arrangements within the layers."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-12-03-12
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13860
name: spherand
def: "Macrocyclic compound capable of completely enveloping a cation\, having donor atoms \(\\\(\\ce\{O\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{N\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{S\}\\\)\) arranged such that they provide a solvation sphere to the encapsulated cation."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-12-03-12
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13861
name: π-π stacking
def: "Inter- or intra-molecular alignment of aromatic groups within molecules induced by the interactional overlap of their \\\(\\ce\{\\uppi\\mbox\{-\}orbitals\}\\\)."
comment: "\(\ce{\uppi\mbox{-}\uppi}\) Interactions become stronger as the number of \(\ce{\uppi\mbox{-}electrons}\) increases."
synonym: "pi-pi stacking" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-12-03-12
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13862
name: T-stacking
def: "Orthogonal alignment of constitutional units within molecules that lead to favourable interactions."
comment: "T-stacking is often seen in proteins where the partially positively charged hydrogen atom of one aromatic system points to the centre of the aromatic plane of another aromatic system."
synonym: "edge-face interaction" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-12-03-12
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13863
name: static self-assembly
def: "Self-assembly at equilibrium."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-12-03-12
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13864
name: stimuli-responsive polymer
def: "Polymer that responds reversibly to external stimulus or stimuli."
comment: "The external stimuli might be temperature, incident radiation, \(\pu{pH}\), etc., and responses would be manifest as changes in a physical property such as conformation, aggregation, liquid-crystalline state, absorption spectrum, emission spectrum, etc."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-12-03-12
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13794

[Term]
id: goldbook:13865
name: solvophilic
def: "Qualifier indicating an affinity for solvents."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-12-03-12
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13866
name: solvophobic
def: "Qualifier indicating a lack of affinity for solvents."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-12-03-12
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13867
name: supramolecular association
def: "Self-assembly involving reversible\, noncovalent\, directional interactions of molecules that result in a high-molecular-mass entity."
comment: "Use of the term "supramolecular polymerization" is discouraged."
synonym: "Lehn association" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-12-03-12
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13868
name: template polymerization
def: "Polymerization of monomer molecules adsorbed and oriented \(a\) on a surface\, \(b\) in a polymer lattice\, \(c\) on polymer molecules in solution\, or \(d\) round a metal ion whereby the structure of the polymer chains formed is controlled by the orientation of the monomer molecules."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-12-03-12
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13869
name: lipid vesicle
def: "Vesicle constructed from a lipid bilayer."
comment: "Liposomes have potential use in drug delivery."
synonym: "liposome" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-12-03-12
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13870
name: artificial
def: "Qualifier for something that is made by human activity\, rather than occurring naturally."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13871
name: artificial polymer
def: "Man-made polymer that is not a biopolymer."
comment: "Artificial polymer should also be used in the case of chemically modified biopolymers."
comment: "Biochemists are now capable of synthesizing copies of biopolymers that should be named synthetic biopolymers to make a distinction with true biopolymers."
comment: "Genetic engineering is now capable of generating non-natural analogues of biopolymers that should be referred to as artificial biopolymers, e.g., artificial protein, artificial polynucleotide, etc."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13907

[Term]
id: goldbook:13872
name: bioactive
def: "Qualifier for a substance that provokes any response from a living system."
comment: "This definition is derived from "material which has been designed to induce specific biological activity" and is limited to material made bioactive on purpose. However, the concept of bioactivity does not imply beneficial action only, although the term is often used positively, i.e., to reflect a beneficial action."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13873

[Term]
id: goldbook:13873
name: bioactivity
def: "Capability of a substance\, such as a drug or a vaccine\, to provoke a response from a living matter."
comment: "Stealth is often used to reflect the absence of recognition by defense proteins of the complement, and more generally opsonins that serve as binding enhancers for the process of phagocytosis."
comment: "There is no polymer (solid or in solution) that is inert in contact with a living system, because of adsorption and/or physical-chemical interactions with life elements (biopolymers, cells, and tissues)."
synonym: "biological activity" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13945

[Term]
id: goldbook:13874
name: bioadhesion
def: "Adhesion of cells or tissues to the surface of a material."
comment: "Assessing of adhesion is made after washing to eliminate unattached cells."
comment: "Cell adhesion is generally followed by proliferation as a biofilm or as a tissue."
comment: "The use of the term "bioattachment" is discouraged."
synonym: "bioattachment" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13879

[Term]
id: goldbook:13875
name: bioalteration
def: "Cell-mediated chemical modification without main chain scission."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13877
name: biodegradability
def: "Capability of being degraded by biological activity."
comment: "In vitro activity of isolated enzymes cannot be considered as biological activity."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13873
consider: goldbook:13905

[Term]
id: goldbook:13878
name: biodisintegration
def: "Disintegration resulting from the action of cells."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13903

[Term]
id: goldbook:13879
name: biofilm
def: "Aggregate of microorganisms in which cells that are frequently embedded within a self-produced matrix of extracellular polymeric substance \(EPS\) adhere to each other and/or to a surface."
comment: "A biofilm is a fixed system that can be adapted internally to environmental conditions by its inhabitants."
comment: "The self-produced matrix of EPS, which is also referred to as slime, is a polymeric conglomeration generally composed of extracellular biopolymers in various structural forms."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13880
name: biofragmentation
def: "Fragmentation resulting from the action of cells."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13881
name: biomaterial
def: "Material exploited in contact with living tissues\, organisms\, or microorganisms."
comment: "The definition "non-viable material used in a medical device, intended to interact with biological systems" recommended in cannot be extended to the environmental field where people mean "material of natural origin"."
comment: "The notion of exploitation includes utility for applications and for fundamental research to understand reciprocal perturbations as well."
comment: "This general term should not be confused with the terms biopolymer or biomacromolecule. The use of "polymeric biomaterial" is recommended when one deals with polymer or polymer device of therapeutic or biological interest."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13882
name: biomineralization
def: "Mineralization caused by cell-mediated phenomena."
comment: "Biomineralization is a process generally concomitant to biodegradation."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13912

[Term]
id: goldbook:13883
name: biostability
def: "Resistance to the deleterious action of living systems that allows preservation of the initial characteristics of a substance."
comment: "If the substance is involved in an application, the term has to be related to a desired duration of performance, because almost any material ages in contact with living systems and biochemical processes regardless of the domain."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13884
name: biotic
def: "Pertaining to\, or produced by\, living cells or organisms."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13885
name: bulk degradation
def: "Homogeneous degradation affecting the volume of a sample."
comment: "Generally, degradation is faster inside because of autocatalysis by entrapped degradation byproducts or by the presence of a chain-cleaving reagent entrapped within the matrix."
comment: "The molar mass of the whole sample decreases progressively as opposed to the constancy observed in the case of erosion and bioerosion."
comment: "This expression is often used when degradation is faster inside than at the surface. This is not appropriate. Nevertheless, it should be adopted specifically in opposition to erosion. In this case, the molar mass distribution becomes rapidly bimodal."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13890
consider: goldbook:13906

[Term]
id: goldbook:13886
name: controlled delivery
def: "Supply of a substance according to desired rate and amount over time."
comment: "If only a delayed or prolonged release is obtained without matching a desired release profile, the expression sustained delivery is to be used."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13920

[Term]
id: goldbook:13887
name: degradability
def: "Capability of undergoing degradation."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13890

[Term]
id: goldbook:13888
name: degradable macromolecule
def: "Macromolecule that is able to undergo chain scissions under specific conditions\, resulting in a decrease of molar mass."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13889
name: degradable polymer
def: "Polymer in which macromolecules are able to undergo chain scissions\, resulting in a decrease of molar mass."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13890

[Term]
id: goldbook:13890
name: degradation
def: "Progressive loss of the performance or of the characteristics of a substance or a device."
comment: "The process of degradation may be specified by a prefix or an adjective preceding the term "degradation". For example, degradation caused by the action of water is termed "hydrodegradation" or hydrolysis; by visible or ultraviolet light is termed "photodegradation"; by the action of oxygen or by the combined action of light and oxygen is termed "oxidative degradation" or "photooxidative degradation", respectively; by the action of heat or by the combined effect of chemical agents and heat is termed "thermal degradation" or "thermochemical degradation", respectively; by the combined action of heat and oxygen is termed "thermooxidative degradation"."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13891
name: degradation
def: "Degradation that results in desired changes in the values of in-use properties of the material because of macromolecule cleavage and molar mass decrease."
comment: "Adapted where the definition is general. For biorelated polymers, the definition is purposely and specifically limited to the chemical degradation of macromolecules in order to make a clear distinction with the physical degradation of the material."
comment: "Degradation can result from the action of enzymes (see enzymatic degradation), or from the action of cells, organisms, and/or microorganisms."
comment: "In any condition, degradation must be used instead of biodegradation when the mechanism of chain scission is not known or demonstrated as cell-mediated."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13903
consider: goldbook:13905

[Term]
id: goldbook:13892
name: degree of bioassimilation
def: "Mass fraction of a substance that is bioassimilated."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13893
name: degree of biodegradation
def: "Mass fraction of a substance that is biodegraded under specified conditions as measured through specified phenomena or techniques sensitive to mineral and biomass formations."
comment: "Expressions like degree of biodegradability, extent of biodegradability, etc., are improper because a property reflected by the suffix "ity" cannot be quantified contrary to a deed reflected by "tion"."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13877

[Term]
id: goldbook:13894
name: degree of biodisintegration
def: "Mass fraction of a biodisintegrated substance."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13895
name: degree of biofragmentation
def: "Mass fraction of the original material that is biofragmented."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13896
name: degree of biomineralization
def: "Mass fraction of a substance that is biomineralized."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13897
name: degree of degradation
def: "Mass fraction of a polymer that is degraded under specified conditions as measured through a specified phenomenon sensitive to molecular dimensions."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13890

[Term]
id: goldbook:13898
name: degree of disintegration
def: "Mass fraction of the particles of defined size issued from a fragmented substance."
comment: "The size is generally defined by sieving. It is a practical characteristic in composting."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13899
name: degree of fragmentation
def: "Mass fraction of a substance that is fragmented."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13900
name: degree of mineralization
def: "Mass fraction of a substance that is mineralized."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13901
name: depolymerase
def: "Enzyme that is able to catalyse the depolymerization of a biomacromolecule."
comment: "A depolymerase does not necessarily convert all the macromolecules to monomer molecules, occasionally, a specific oligomer is the end-product, depending on the mechanism."
comment: "This term is generally used in the case of macromolecules produced by bacteria, because bacteria have the potential to degrade the biopolymers they synthesized."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13902
name: deterioration
def: "Deleterious alteration of a material in quality\, serviceability\, or vigor."
comment: "Deterioration can result from physical and/or chemical phenomena."
comment: "Deterioration is connected to a loss of performances and thus to the function, whereas degradation is connected with a loss of properties."
comment: "Polymer deterioration is more general than polymer degradation, which reflects loss of properties resulting from chemical cleavage of macromolecules only."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13890

[Term]
id: goldbook:13903
name: disintegration
def: "Fragmentation to particles of a defined size."
comment: "The limiting size is generally defined according to sieving conditions."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13904
name: durability
def: "Ability of a material to retain the values of its properties under specified conditions."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13905
name: enzymatic degradation
def: "Degradation caused by the catalytic action of enzymes."
comment: "Degradation caused by enzymes can be observed under biotic or abiotic conditions but only degradation due to cell bioactivity can be called biodegradation, otherwise enzymatic degradation is to be used, especially in the case of in vitro abiotic conditions."
comment: "Sometimes called enzymic degradation."
synonym: "enzymatic decomposition" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13873
consider: goldbook:13884
consider: goldbook:13890

[Term]
id: goldbook:13906
name: erosion
def: "Degradation that occurs at the surface and progresses from it into the bulk."
comment: "Erosion can also result from chemical degradation when the degrading reagent reacts faster than it diffuses inside. There is a risk of confusion that can be eliminated after careful and detailed investigation of the degradation mechanism. (See bioerosion.)"
comment: "See enzymatic degradation. In the case of polymers, water-soluble enzymes can hardly diffuse into the macromolecular network, except, maybe, in some hydrogels. They adhere to surfaces to cause erosion."
comment: "The wording bulk erosion is incorrect and its use therefore discouraged."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13890
consider: goldbook:13905

[Term]
id: goldbook:13907
name: genetic engineering
def: "Process of inserting new genetic information into existing cells in order to modify a specific organism for the purpose of changing its characteristics."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13908
name: maximum degree of biodegradation
def: "Greater value of the degree of biodegradation that can be reached under selected experimental conditions."
comment: "Not to be confused with ultimate degradation."
comment: "This expression reflects the fact that some biodegradable parts of a biodegradable material may not be accessible to biodegradation."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13923

[Term]
id: goldbook:13909
name: microcapsule
def: "Hollow microparticle composed of a solid shell surrounding a core-forming space available to permanently or temporarily entrapped substances."
comment: "The substances can be drugs, pesticides, dyes, etc."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13910

[Term]
id: goldbook:13910
name: microparticle
def: "Particle with dimensions between \\\(\\pu\{1E-7\}\\\) and \\\(\\pu\{1E-4 m\}\\\)."
comment: "The lower limit between micro- and nano-sizing is still a matter of debate. (See nanoparticle.)"
comment: "To be consistent with the prefix "micro" and the range imposed by the definition, dimensions of microparticles should be expressed in \(\pu{\upmu\!m}\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13911
name: microsphere
def: "Microparticle of spherical shape without membrane or any distinct outer layer."
comment: "The absence of outer layer forming a distinct phase is important to distinguish microspheres from microcapsules because it leads to first-order diffusion phenomena, whereas diffusion is zero order in the case of microcapsules."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13909

[Term]
id: goldbook:13912
name: mineralization
def: "Process through which an organic substance becomes impregnated by or turned into inorganic substances."
comment: "A particular case is the process by which living organisms produce and structure minerals often to harden or stiffen existing tissues. (See biomineralization.)"
comment: "In the case of polymer biodegradation, this term is used to reflect conversion to _015233_ and \(\ce{H2O}\) and other inorganics. \(\ce{CH4}\) can be considered as part of the mineralization process because it comes up in parallel to the minerals in anaerobic composting, also called methanization."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13882

[Term]
id: goldbook:13913
name: nanocapsule
def: "Hollow nanoparticle composed of a solid shell that surrounds a core-forming space available to entrap substances."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13914
name: nanosphere
def: "Nanoparticle of spherical shape without membrane or any distinct outer layer."
comment: "A nanosphere is composed of a matrix where substances can be permanently or temporarily embedded, dissolved, or covalently bound."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13911

[Term]
id: goldbook:13915
name: plastic
def: "Generic term used in the case of polymeric material that may contain other substances to improve performance and/or reduce costs."
comment: "The use of this term instead of polymer is a source of confusion and thus is not recommended."
comment: "This term is used in polymer engineering for materials often compounded that can be processed by flow."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13917
name: solid dispersion
def: "Solid multiphasic mixture with at least one polymer component dominating."
comment: "In pharmaceutical preparations, incompatible polymer-drug mixtures are generally solid dispersions."
comment: "Solid dispersion is commonly prepared by three different methods, namely, solventbased, fusion-melt, and hybrid fusion-solvent methods."
comment: "The dispersed compounds can be in clusters of particles."
comment: "The nonpolymeric components can act as fillers."
synonym: "solid dispersion polymer" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13919
name: stimulus-responsive polymer
def: "Polymer that responds or that is designed to respond to a stimulus like pH\, light\, heat\, etc. change\, and provides a predetermined action."
comment: "The generated action can be unique or cyclic. It generally results from cooperative phenomena."
comment: "The stimulus can affect macromolecules or macromolecule assemblies forming the polymer."
synonym: "smart polymer" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13920
name: sustained delivery
def: "Supply of a substance from a container where it is temporarily entrapped for the sake of achieving a prolonged action."
comment: "In some cases, the container is a polymer processed as implant, film, microparticle, or nanoparticle, auto-assembly of molecules, or a macromolecule."
comment: "In the case of sustained delivery according to required specifications, the use of controlled delivery is recommended."
comment: "The substance can be temporarily embedded, dissolved, or covalently bound."
comment: "The term is relevant to the release of substances like pesticides, dyes, drugs, etc."
synonym: "prolonged delivery" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13886
consider: goldbook:13910
consider: goldbook:13936

[Term]
id: goldbook:13921
name: synthetic biopolymer
def: "Copy of a biopolymer man-made using abiotic chemical routes."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13870

[Term]
id: goldbook:13922
name: theoretical degree of biodegradation
def: "Degree of biodegradation that corresponds to conversion of all the organic matter present in an original polymer-based item to minerals and biomass."
comment: "This expression is used as reference to assess biodegradable components that are not accessible to biodegradation from those that are bioavailable."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13876
consider: goldbook:13893

[Term]
id: goldbook:13923
name: ultimate biodegradation
def: "Complete breakdown of a compound to either fully oxidized or reduced simple molecules \(such as carbon dioxide/methane\, nitrate/ammonium\, and water\)."
comment: "The use of this expression is not recommended."
comment: "This term reflects the end-products of biodegradation. As such, it differs from the theoretical degree of biodegradation, which depends on the presence of non-biodegradable components."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13922

[Term]
id: goldbook:13924
name: artificial organ
def: "Medical device that replaces\, in part or in whole\, the function of one of the organs of the body."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13925
name: biomedical
def: "Qualifier for a domain grouping scientific and practical activities related to therapy."
comment: "The term is relevant to therapy in surgery, medicine, pharmacology, dentistry, etc."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13926
name: bioprosthesis
def: "Implantable prosthesis that consists totally or substantially of nonviable\, treated donor tissue."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13927
name: bioresorbability
def: "Ability to be eliminated from an animal or human body through natural pathways."
comment: "Bioassimilation with formation of novel biomass is a particular means of elimination often combined with the other pathways."
comment: "Natural pathways are kidneys through glomerular filtration and lungs after metabolization."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13928
name: bioresorption
def: "Disappearance of a substance from an organism by processes of metabolism\, secretion\, or excretion."
comment: "Bioresorption is now considered as pertinent and should be used specifically only when foreign material and residues have been shown assimilated or eliminated from the living host, regardless of the followed route, namely, lungs or kidneys or insertion in biochemical processes."
comment: "In the case of polymers or high-molar-mass macromolecules that are retained in parenteral compartments, degradation or biodegradation is necessary prior to bioresorption."
comment: "This concept does not apply to the environment as everything, including degradation by-products issued from outdoor degradation or biodegradation can only be stored or chemically transformed on Earth, so far."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13890

[Term]
id: goldbook:13929
name: bone cement
def: "Synthetic\, self-curing organic or inorganic material used to fill up a cavity or to create a mechanical fixation."
comment: "In dentistry, polymer-based cements are also used as fillers of cavities. They are generally cured photochemically using UV radiation in contrast to bone cements."
comment: "In situ self-curing can be the source of released reagents that can cause local and/or systemic toxicity as in the case of the monomer released from methacrylics-based bone cement used in orthopedic surgery."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13930
name: carcinogenicity
def: "Ability or tendency to produce cancer."
comment: "In general, polymers are not known as carcinogens or mutagens, however, residual monomers or additives can cause genetic mutations."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13931
name: drug carrier
def: "Macromolecule or polymer used to transport a pharmacologically active compound to be released later on due to an abiotic or biotic process."
comment: "A complementary property of a polymeric drug carrier is targeting, which can be obtained by specific interactions with a receptor if bearing a specific ligand or by selection depending on other factors such as permeation through membranes or capillaries."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13884
consider: goldbook:13886
consider: goldbook:13920
consider: goldbook:13941
consider: goldbook:13946

[Term]
id: goldbook:13932
name: foreign body reaction
def: "Cellular response of the inflammatory and wound healing processes following introduction of a foreign object in a human or animal body."
comment: "The foreign body reaction results in more or less intense events such as fibrous tissue formation, macrophage activation, giant cells formation, etc."
comment: "The foreign object can be a medical device, a prosthesis, a particle or any compound introduced accidentally."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13944

[Term]
id: goldbook:13933
name: host response
def: "Reaction of a living system to the presence of a substance or a material."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13934
name: hybrid artificial organ
def: "Artificial organ that is a combination of viable cells and one or more biomaterials."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13881

[Term]
id: goldbook:13935
name: immunogenicity
def: "Ability of a material or substance to elicit a cellular immune response and/or antibody production."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13936
name: implant
def: "Medical device made from one or more biomaterials that is intentionally placed within the body\, either totally or partially buried beneath an epithelial surface."
comment: "There are also other devices implanted that are not medical devices, e.g., for cosmetic, cultural, or aesthetic purposes."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13881

[Term]
id: goldbook:13937
name: macromolecular prodrug
def: "Prodrug in which the temporary chemical entity is a macromolecule."
comment: "In this particular case of prodrug, the macromolecule carries several drug molecules generally. These molecules are linked to the carrier through cleavable bonds."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13943

[Term]
id: goldbook:13938
name: medical device
def: "Instrument\, apparatus\, implement\, machine\, contrivance\, in vitro reagent\, or other similar or related article\, including any component\, part of accessory\, which is intended for use in the diagnosis of disease or other conditions\, or in the cure\, mitigation\, treatment\, or prevention of disease in man."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13939
name: pharmaceutical
def: "Qualifier for substances or systems\, including polymers\, exploited by the pharmaceutical industry."
comment: "A pharmaceutical substance can be exploited for its bioactivity or as an excipient."
comment: "The term "pharmaceutical" is also used as short form for a pharmaceutical substance."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13873

[Term]
id: goldbook:13940
name: pharmacological
def: "Qualifiers for substances\, including macromolecules or polymers\, and actions involved in pharmacology."
comment: "A pharmacological polymer can be bioactive by itself or because it is used as a temporary carrier of a bioactive substance of interest in pharmacology."
synonym: "pharmacologic" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13872
consider: goldbook:13937
consider: goldbook:13942

[Term]
id: goldbook:13941
name: pharmacologically active
def: "Qualifier for a substance that exhibits bioactivity of pharmacological interest."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13873
consider: goldbook:13940

[Term]
id: goldbook:13942
name: pharmacology
def: "Science of drugs including their origin\, composition\, pharmacokinetics\, pharmacodynamics\, therapeutic use\, and toxicology."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13943
name: prodrug
def: "Compound that undergoes biotransformation before exhibiting pharmacological effects."
comment: "Prodrugs can thus be viewed as drugs containing specialized nontoxic protective groups used in a transient manner to alter or to eliminate undesirable properties in the parent molecule."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13940

[Term]
id: goldbook:13944
name: prosthesis
def: "Device that replaces a limb\, organ\, or tissue of the body."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13945
name: stealth
def: "Qualifier for a macromolecule\, a surface\, or a device that is not detected by defense proteins of the complement and the Mononuclear Phagocyte System\, especially macrophages\, after introduction in parental compartments."
comment: "Detection by natural defenses generally leads to the destruction of the device or of the surrounding tissues."
comment: "Surfaces are often decorated by chemical entities aimed at suppressing the activation of the natural defense processes."
synonym: "stealth biomedical polymer" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13946
name: targeting
def: "Exploitation of specific or nonspecific interactions to target a particular part of a living system\, or a particular type of cells."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13947
name: therapeutic polymer
def: "Polymer aimed at helping therapists in treating diseases or trauma."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13948
name: thrombogenicity
def: "Property of a material \(or substance\) that induces and/or promotes the formation of a thrombus."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13949
name: tissue engineering
def: "Use of a combination of cells\, engineering and materials methods\, and suitable biochemical and physico-chemical factors to improve or replace biological functions."
comment: "While most definitions of tissue engineering cover a broad range of applications, in practice the term is closely associated with applications that repair or replace portions of or whole tissues (i.e., bone, cartilage, blood vessels, bladder, skin, etc.)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13950
name: transplant
def: "Complete structure\, such as an organ that is transferred from a site in a donor to a site in a recipient for the purpose of the reconstruction of the recipient site."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13951
name: aerobic biodegradation
def: "Biodegradation in the presence of molecular oxygen."
comment: "Oxygen is generally supplied by the atmosphere."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13952
name: anaerobic biodegradation
def: "Biodegradation in the absence of oxygen."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13912

[Term]
id: goldbook:13953
name: bioplastic
def: "Biobased polymer derived from the biomass or issued from monomers derived from the biomass and which\, at some stage in its processing into finished products\, can be shaped by flow."
comment: "A biobased polymer similar to a petrobased one does not imply any superiority with respect to the environment unless the comparison of respective life cycle assessments is favourable."
comment: "Bioplastic is generally used as the opposite of polymer derived from fossil resources."
comment: "Bioplastic is misleading because it suggests that any polymer derived from the biomass is environmentally friendly."
comment: "The use of the term "bioplastic" is discouraged. Use the expression "biobased polymer"."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13958
consider: goldbook:13960

[Term]
id: goldbook:13954
name: compost
def: "Solid product resulting from the decomposition of organic wastes by fermentation."
comment: "A compost is generally processed in personal composters or industrially to be used as fertilizer. In the latter case, specifications in structure and quality are to be provided."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13955
name: conditioning film
def: "Film that is rapidly formed on the surface of a solid in contact with a biological system \(in the widest sense\) that conditions the surface for subsequent interaction with constituents of the biological system."
comment: "Frequently, the conditioning film consists of proteins that prepare almost any surface for subsequent colonization by microorganisms or cells."
comment: "Not to be confused with biofilm, which implies the presence of cells or microorganisms."
comment: "Not to be confused with conditioning film in packaging."
comment: "The term can be applied to the surface of any material that is in contact with blood or body fluids because the very first event is coverage by more or less denaturized adhering proteins."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-12-04
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
