[Term]
id: goldbook:13986
name: absorbed local spectral photon flux density
def: "Local value of the spectral photon radiance\, or spectral photon flux density\, defined for a three-dimensional space\, often in terms of napierian properties."
comment: "Also called local volumetric rate of photon absorption, LVRPA [either \(e_{\lambda}^{\rm{a}}(x,t)\) for one-dimensional or \(e_{\lambda}^{\rm{a}}(\boldsymbol{x},t)\) for three-dimensional absorbed photon flux densities, respectively] in."
comment: "For a divergent beam, the mathematical definition is: \[L_{\rm{p},\lambda}^{\rm{a}} (\boldsymbol{x},t) = \alpha_{\lambda}(\boldsymbol{x},t) L_{\rm{p},\lambda,o} (\boldsymbol{x},t)\], with \(\boldsymbol{x}\) a position vector in a three-dimensional space. Units are the same as above. In this case: \[E_{\rm{p},\lambda,o} (\boldsymbol{x},t) = \int_{4\uppi} L_{\rm{p},\lambda}(\boldsymbol{x},t) {\rm{d}}\mathit{\Omega}\]. In a three-dimensional space, units of \(L_{\rm{p},\lambda}(\boldsymbol{x},t)\) are \(\pu{mol s-1 m-3 sr-1}\). Thus, the integral over the solid angle \(\mathit{\Omega}\) is needed to obtain \(L_{\rm{p},\lambda}\) in units \(\pu{mol s-1 m-4}\)."
comment: "In practice, for polychromatic radiation, the quantity is integrated in the wavelength range used."
comment: "Mathematical expression for one spatial dimension is \[L_{\rm{p},\lambda}^{\rm{a}} (x,t) = \alpha_{\lambda}(\boldsymbol{x},t) L_{\rm{p},\lambda} (x,t)\] where \(L_{\rm{p},\lambda}^{\rm{a}} (x,t)\) is the time-dependent spectral photon radiance incident from one direction at a point defined by the space coordinate \(x\). \(\alpha_{\lambda}(\boldsymbol{x},t)\) is the linear napierian absorption coefficient. SI unit is \(\pu{s-1 m-4}\) in photon number basis and \(\pu{mol s-1 m-4}\) in chemical amount basis. Common unit is \(\pu{s-1 cm-3 nm-1}\) and \(\pu{einstein s-1 cm-3 nm-1}\), respectively. The above equation is valid for a collimated beam of radiation. In this case, \[E_{\rm{p},\lambda,o} = L_{\rm{p,}\lambda}(\boldsymbol{x},t)\] with the subindex 'o' (the letter o) indicating radiation from all directions."
comment: "Rigorously speaking, the general definition should be written in terms of \(E_{\rm{p},\lambda,o}\). However, for a collimated beam of radiation, it is clearer to write it directly in terms of \(L_{\rm{p},\lambda}\)."
synonym: "local spectral photon density absorption rate" []
synonym: "local volumetric rate of photon absorption" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13987
name: absorbed local spectral radiance
def: "Local value of the spectral radiance\, or energy density absorption rate\, defined for a three-dimensional space\, often in terms of napierian properties."
comment: "Also called local volumetric rate of energy absorption, LVREA, [either \(e_{\lambda}^{\rm{a}}(x,t)\) for one spatial dimension or \(e_{\lambda}^{\rm{a}}(\boldsymbol{x},t)\) for three dimensions]."
comment: "For a divergent beam, the mathematical definition is: \[L_{\lambda}^{\rm{a}} (\boldsymbol{x},t) = \alpha_{\lambda} (\boldsymbol{x},t) E_{\lambda,o} (\boldsymbol{x},t)\]. In this case, \(\boldsymbol{x}\) is a position vector in a three-dimensional space. Units are the same as above."
comment: "In practice, for polychromatic irradiation, the quantity is integrated in the wavelength range used."
comment: "Mathematical expression for one spatial dimension is \[L_{\lambda}^{\rm{a}} (x,t) = \alpha_{\lambda} (\boldsymbol{x},t) L_{\lambda} (x,t)\] where \(L_{\lambda}(x,t)\) is the time-dependent spectral radiance incident from one direction at a point \(x\) defined in the space coordinates, \(\alpha_{\lambda}(\boldsymbol{x},t)\) is the space- and time-dependent linear napierian absorption coefficient. SI unit is \(\pu{W m-4}\). Common unit is \(\pu{W cm-3 nm-1}\).The equation above is valid for a collimated beam of radiation, for which \(E_{\lambda,{\rm{o}}}(\boldsymbol{x},t) = L_{\lambda}(\boldsymbol{x},t)\)."
comment: "Rigorously speaking, the general definition should be written in terms of \(E_{\lambda,o}\). However, for a collimated beam of radiation, it is clearer to write it directly in terms of \(L_{\lambda}\)."
synonym: "local volumetric rate of energy absorption" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13988
name: absorbed radiant density
def: "Monochromatic radiant energy\, per time interval \(spectral radiance\) coming from all directions and absorbed by a system integrated over the whole volume and averaged per volume \\\(V\\\)\, \\\(\\langle L_\{\\lambda\}^\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\left\(t \\right\) \\rangle_\{V\}\\\)."
comment: "In practice, for polychromatic irradiation, the quantity is integrated in the wavelength range used."
comment: "Mathematical expression: \[\langle L_{\lambda}^{\rm{a}} \left(t \right) \rangle_{V} = \frac{1}{V} \int_{V} L_{\lambda}^{\rm{a}} (\boldsymbol{x},t) {\rm{d}}V = \frac{1}{V}\int_{V} \alpha_{\lambda}(\boldsymbol{x},t) E_{\lambda,o} (\boldsymbol{x},t) {\rm{d}}V\] over the absorption volume \(V\), with \(\boldsymbol{x}\) a position vector in a three-dimensional space, \(\alpha (\boldsymbol{x},t)\) the space- and time-dependent linear napierian absorption coefficient (absorption coefficients are always spectral and the subscript \(\lambda\) may be omitted), and \(E_{\lambda,o} (\boldsymbol{x},t)\) the incident from all directions space- and time-dependent spectral fluence rate. SI unit is \(\pu{W m-4}\). Common unit is \(\pu{W cm-3 nm-1}\)."
synonym: "absorbed spectral radiant density" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14092

[Term]
id: goldbook:13989
name: accumulation layer
def: "Subsurface space where the majority carriers accumulate near the surface of a semiconductor because of band bending caused by the surface potential."
comment: "The surface charge is of opposite sign to that of the majority charge carriers in the semiconductor. This leads to accumulation of the majority charge carriers in a subsurface layer."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
