[Term]
id: goldbook:13992
name: area-related parameters
def: "Quantities expressed in terms of 'per area' are denoted as area-related parameters."
comment: "In photocatalysis, the area-related parameters do not characterise the catalyst as they do in heterogeneous dark catalysis. In general, the properties of a photocatalyst are not directly proportional to its area, especially in view of the fact that light may not equally reach every part of the surface of a particle. The light may not enter the pores of a porous surface and in this case only the outer area behaves as catalyst. A similar situation may occur when particles are aggregated."
comment: "When the surface area that is accessed by the light cannot be established, it is recommended to use the total surface area in terms of the \(\rm{BET}(\ce{N2})\) area. This implies that most probably a lower limit of the quantity is obtained, because the surface determined by \(\ce{N2}\) adsorption will be larger than that reached by the radiation."
synonym: "areal parameters" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13997

[Term]
id: goldbook:13993
name: artificial photosynthesis
def: "Photocatalytic production of substances from simple compounds \(e.g.\, \\\(\\ce\{H2\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{O2\}\\\) from water\, \\\(\\ce\{H2\}\\\) from hydrogen sulfide\, etc.\) using ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation absorbed by chromophoric systems\, often included in microheterogeneous media\, mimicking the action of antennae and reaction centres in natural photosynthetic organisms."
comment: "Antennae (also called light-harvesting centres) are molecular structures enhancing the wavelength range and sensitivity of the photosynthetic units, due to an increase in light absorption."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14029

[Term]
id: goldbook:13994
name: band-to-band recombination
def: "Recombination of charge carriers occurring via a one-step electron transition from the conduction band to the valence band in a semiconductor or insulator."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13995
name: black body
def: "Idealised object absorbing all incident radiant power\, regardless of wavelength and transforming it all into heat. No electromagnetic radiation passes through it and none is reflected. A black body is a perfect absorber of radiant energy. Therefore\, it is also a perfect emitter of radiation. The wavelength distribution of the radiation of a black body depends only on its temperature and not on its internal nature or structure."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13996
name: black-body-like photocatalytic reactor
def: "Reactor used to determine quantum yields of photoadsorption and photocatalysed reactions when the catalyst is in the form of a powdered layer \(or powder dispersion\). The incident photons are \(nearly\) all absorbed by the catalyst\, provided that the dispersion layer is sufficiently thick \(or the dispersion is sufficiently concentrated\)."
comment: "To use the black-body-like photocatalytic reactor for the determination of quantum yields, the radiation arriving at the device should be monochromatic (\(\lambda + {\rm{d}}\lambda\)) and the temperature well controlled."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13997
name: Brunauer–Emmett–Teller surface area
def: "Specific surface area \(\\\(\\pu\{m2 kg-1\}\\\)\) determined from the adsorption isotherm of a gas with the help of the Brunauer\, Emmett\, and Teller model using the conventional value for the molecular cross-section."
comment: "The area-related parameters do not characterise the catalyst in photocatalysis as they do in heterogeneous dark catalysis. The properties of a photocatalyst are not directly proportional to their area, in view of the fact that light does not equally reach every part of the surface area or particle."
comment: "The most common gas used is \(\ce{N2}\) with the conventional molecular cross-section of \(\pu{0.162 nm2}\). With nitrogen, the total area is measured; this area includes the area in pores (that may not be reached by light). In this case, the expression \(\rm{BET}(\ce{N2})\) is used."
synonym: "BET" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13992

[Term]
id: goldbook:13998
name: catalyst photoactivation
def: "Generation or alteration of the catalytic activity \(selectivity\) of a substance after its interaction with light."
comment: "Typically, the term refers to catalytic activity (selectivity) occurring in the dark after irradiation has terminated."
synonym: "catalyst photomodification" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:13999
name: catalytic site
def: "Site at which catalysed chemical transformations take place."
comment: "If the assumption can be made that the active sites are distributed over the entire (photo)catalyst surface, then the heterogeneous reactions are described "per area". The generally accepted \(\rm{BET}(\ce{N2})\) surface area is used. In spite of the fact that there is no direct correlation between the number of active sites and the \(\rm{BET}(\ce{N2})\) area, the use of the latter may help when no alternative exists."
comment: "Quantities expressed in terms of "per \(\rm{BET}(\ce{N2})\) area" should be denoted as area-related, which also implies that a lower limit of the quantity is given, because the surface determined by \(\ce{N2}\) adsorption will be larger than that reached by the radiation."
comment: "The area-related parameters do not characterise the catalyst in heterogeneous photocatalysis as they do in heterogeneous dark catalysis, inasmuch as the density and concentration of active sites are often unknown. Thus, the properties of a photocatalyst are not directly proportional to their area, in view of the fact that light does not equally reach every part of the surface or particle. Note also that photocatalysts may include several phases, in which case the overall area is physically not meaningful."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13992
consider: goldbook:13997

[Term]
id: goldbook:14000
name: catalysed photochemical reaction
def: "Enhancement \(alteration\) of efficiency of photochemical reactions upon direct excitation of photoactive reactants via an intermediate interaction of these reactants with certain compounds that act as catalysts \(promoters\) of the appropriate chemical transformations of the reactants."
comment: "Sometimes this process can be identical with photocatalysis."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14001
name: Coekelbergs effect
def: "Adsorption at the surface of a pre-irradiated adsorbent \(the photocatalyst\) occurring in the dark\, after the irradiation in the presence of the photoadsorbate entities is terminated\, i.e.\, after preliminary photoadsorption."
synonym: "Coekelbergs–Solonitsyn effect" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14087

[Term]
id: goldbook:14002
name: colour centres
def: "Lattice defects \(typically vacancies\) that trap one or more electrons or holes in ionic crystals. They constitute a long-lived deep trap with a low efficiency of trapping of a carrier of the opposite sign."
comment: "Colour centres accumulate in irradiated solids and give rise to photoinduced extrinsic absorption bands (photoinduced colour) in semiconductors and insulators. The dominant decay pathway of colour centres upon irradiation at moderate temperatures is photoionisation with the formation of free carriers and empty traps."
comment: "Monitoring the photocolouration of a metal oxide during a surface photochemical reaction probes whether the reaction is photocatalytic."
comment: "The surface F (anion vacancies occupied by electrons) and V centres (trapped holes) in semiconductors and insulators are also colour centres with the distinction that they act as metastable active states of photocatalytic centres."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14003
consider: goldbook:14010
consider: goldbook:14014
consider: goldbook:14043

[Term]
id: goldbook:14003
name: deep energy trap
def: "Trap defined by an energy level \(of trapped carriers\) within the bandgap energy of a semiconductor or insulator with an energy difference between it and the conduction band \(for electrons\) or valence band \(for holes\) much greater than the energy of thermal excitation of the solid at a given temperature."
comment: "Some active states of a photocatalytic centre are called a deep trap (as in solid-state physics)."
comment: "The dominant decay pathway of the trap is either by trapping of carriers of the opposite sign (see recombination centre) or by photoionisation (see colour centres)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13991
consider: goldbook:14002
consider: goldbook:14081

[Term]
id: goldbook:14004
name: depletion layer
def: "Subsurface space where the majority carriers are forced away owing to band bending caused by the surface potential. \(i\) The subsurface layer of an n-type semiconductor becomes depleted of electrons\, which are the majority carriers\, as a consequence of upward band bending. \(ii\) The subsurface layer of a p-type semiconductor becomes enriched by electrons \(depleted of holes\) as a consequence of downward band bending."
comment: "The surface charge sign is identical to the sign of the majority charge carriers. The surface charge (surface potential) in semiconductors is produced as a result of adsorption or contact with the medium (an electrolyte or another surface)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14027

[Term]
id: goldbook:14005
name: direct bandgap semiconductor
def: "Semiconductor or dielectric materials whose first band-to-band transition occurs from the highest level in the valence band to the lowest level in the conduction band possessing the same electron momentum\, i.e.\, \\\(\\boldsymbol\{p\}_\{\\rm\{max\}\} = \\boldsymbol\{p\}_\{\\rm\{min\}\}\\\)\, with \\\(\\boldsymbol\{p\}_\{\\rm\{max\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\boldsymbol\{p\}_\{\\rm\{min\}\}\\\) the momentum vectors of electrons of the highest level in the valence band and the lowest level in the conduction band\, respectively."
comment: "On photoexcitation, the electrons change their energy owing to the absorption of photons and maintain the same momentum."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14022

[Term]
id: goldbook:14006
name: dynamic photoadsorption process
def: "Simultaneous \(or synchronous\) formation of adsorbed species \(adsorbate\) via photoadsorption and detachment by photodesorption to yield the same species\, both processes occurring under photoexcitation of the solid photocatalyst \(adsorbent\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14048

[Term]
id: goldbook:14007
name: effective radiation-catalytic activity
def: "Number of product entities \(atoms\, molecules\, or radicals\) generated by each \\\(\\pu\{100 eV\}\\\) of radiation incident on the system."
comment: "In SI units, it refers to the amount of substance in \(\pu{\upmu mol}\) generated for each joule of radiation incident on the system. SI unit is \(\pu{\upmu mol J-1}\)."
comment: "It is convenient to use the amount of substance \(\ce{B}\) in \(\pu{E-7 mol}\) of \(\ce{B}\), generated for each joule of radiation incident on the system. By choosing this (submultiple of) SI unit, a number almost identical to the _009683_ value (within \(\pu{4\%}\)) in the obsolete unit \(\pu{(100 eV)-1}\) is obtained, as \(\pu{(100 eV)-1} = \pu{1.036E-7 mol J-1}\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14069

[Term]
id: goldbook:14008
name: electrical double layer
def: "Array of charged species and oriented dipoles existing at a solid-solution interface. The solid may be a metal or a semiconductor. Double refers to the fact that the surface charge and its counter charge at the solution side are included."
comment: "Often the solution side is considered to be composed of a thin inner layer of (hydrated) ions in contact with the surface and a diffuse layer further out in solution."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14009
name: exciton absorption
def: "Absorption of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation yielding optical information on the excitons in solids. Exciton absorption bands in a spectrum of a semiconductor or insulator are located on the long wavelength side of the fundamental absorption edge."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14017

[Term]
id: goldbook:14010
name: extrinsic absorption
def: "Absorption of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation in semiconductors and insulators corresponding to optical transitions of electrons from defect energy levels \(within the bandgap energy\) to conduction band levels\, or holes to valence band levels \(photoionisation of defects\)\, or from the ground state level to excited levels of a given intrinsic defect of any sort."
comment: "Doping of a catalyst creates an extrinsic defect."
comment: "Typically, extrinsic absorption bands are a few tenths to a few \(\pu{eV}\) distant from the fundamental absorption edge in semiconductors and insulators. The red limits of photocatalytic reactions are determined by photoexcitation of photocatalysts in the extrinsic absorption bands."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14017
consider: goldbook:14026

[Term]
id: goldbook:14011
name: extrinsic defect
def: "Imperfection in a solid semiconductor \(or insulator\) connected with surface defects of any sort involving extrinsic chemical units in relation to the chemical formula of the solid\, particularly adsorbed species of any sort."
comment: "Doping of a catalyst creates an extrinsic defect."
comment: "These extrinsic surface defects play an essential role in heterogeneous photocatalysis. For example, they can serve as photocatalytic centres (sites). Surface defects, as well as bulk defects located in the near-surface region, can serve as entities that absorb ultraviolet, visible, or infrared radiation and generate free charge carriers. Surface defects can trap charge carriers and serve as recombination centres, thereby influencing the concentration of photocarriers at the surface of photocatalyst particles."
synonym: "extrinsic surface defect" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14018
consider: goldbook:14040
consider: goldbook:14043
consider: goldbook:14081

[Term]
id: goldbook:14012
name: F centres
def: "Anion vacancies occupied by electrons in semiconductors."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14002

[Term]
id: goldbook:14013
name: Fermi level splitting
def: "Splitting of the Fermi level in irradiated semiconductors and insulators into two Fermi quasi-levels for electrons \\\(E_\{\\rm\{F\}\}^\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\) and for holes \\\(E_\{\\rm\{F\}\}^\{\\rm\{h\}\}\\\)\, displaced toward the bottom of the conduction band and toward the top of the valence band\, respectively."
comment: "The larger the radiant power of the radiation, the larger is the Fermi level energy splitting, \(\Delta {\rm{F}} = E_{\rm{F}}^{\rm{e}} - E_{\rm{F}}^{\rm{h}}\)."
comment: "The positions of the Fermi quasi-levels within the bandgap energy determine the concentration of free thermalised photocarriers in irradiated solid photocatalysts."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14040

[Term]
id: goldbook:14014
name: free charge carriers
def: "Applies to free electrons or free holes."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14015

[Term]
id: goldbook:14015
name: free electrons
def: "Photoexcited electrons from the valence band into the conduction band in semiconductors and insulators."
comment: "The expression "free electron carrier" is redundant and not recommended."
comment: "Thermoexcitation or doping with certain impurities also leads to formation of free electrons in the conduction band of semiconductors. In metals and n-type semiconductors free electrons exist originally."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14016
name: fundamental absorption
def: "Absorption of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation in semiconductors and insulators inducing optical transitions of electrons originating exclusively from the valence band to the conduction band to yield free electron-hole pairs and/or exciton absorption bands."
synonym: "intrinsic absorption" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14010

[Term]
id: goldbook:14017
name: fundamental absorption edge
def: "High wavelength limit of the fundamental absorption band in the spectrum of semiconductors and of insulators."
synonym: "fundamental absorption threshold" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14016

[Term]
id: goldbook:14018
name: heterogeneous photocatalysis
def: "Photocatalysis taking place at the interfacial boundary between two phases \(solid/liquid\, solid/gas\, liquid/gas\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14019
name: heterogeneous radiation catalysis
def: "Radiation catalysis taking place in heterogeneous systems."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14067

[Term]
id: goldbook:14020
name: homogeneous photocatalysis
def: "Photocatalysis taking place in homogeneous systems."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14021
name: homogeneous radiation catalysis
def: "Radiation catalysis taking place in homogeneous systems."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14067

[Term]
id: goldbook:14022
name: indirect bandgap semiconductor
def: "Semiconductor or dielectric materials with a first band-to-band transition from the highest level in the valence band to the lowest level in the conduction band requiring the change of electron momentum\, that is \\\(\\boldsymbol\{p\}_\{\\rm\{max\}\}  eq \\boldsymbol\{p\}_\{\\rm\{min\}\}\\\)\, where \\\(\\boldsymbol\{p\}_\{\\rm\{max\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\boldsymbol\{p\}_\{\\rm\{min\}\}\\\) are the momentum vectors of electrons of the highest level in the valence band and the lowest level in the conduction band\, respectively."
comment: "Direct band-to-band electronic transitions between two bands of different momentum (e.g., \(\boldsymbol{p}_{\rm{max}} \neq \boldsymbol{p}_{\rm{min}}\)) are forbidden because momentum must be conserved. Upon excitation, such changes occur owing to interaction of the electronic subsystem with the phonons."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14005

[Term]
id: goldbook:14023
name: in-scattering
def: "Physical phenomenon considering scattered radiative energy from rays in any direction \\\(\\mathit\{\\Omega\}'\\\) into the direction \\\(\\mathit\{\\Omega\}\\\) of the rays considered in the radiative transfer equation \(RTE\)."
comment: "Mathematical definition: \[{\rm{Sc}}_{\rm{in}} = \frac{\sigma_{\lambda} (\boldsymbol{x},t)}{4\pi} \int_{4\pi} B_{\lambda} (\mathit{\Omega}' \rightarrow \mathit{\Omega}) L_{\mathit{\Omega},\lambda} (\boldsymbol{x},t)\ {\rm{d}}\mathit{\Omega}'\] where \(\boldsymbol{x}\) is the spatial coordinate in a three-dimensional space along the propagation of the beam, \(t\) is the time coordinate, \(\sigma_{\lambda} (\boldsymbol{x},t)\) is the space- and time-dependent napierian linear scattering coefficient, \(L_{\mathit{\Omega},\lambda} (\boldsymbol{x},t)\) is the incident time- and space-dependent radiance and \(B_{\lambda}\) is the phase function, i.e., a distribution function acting as a source of photons from any direction \(\mathit{\Omega}'\) into the direction \(\mathit{\Omega}\) under consideration. SI unit is \(\pu{W sr-1 m-4}\). Common unit is \(\pu{W sr-1 cm-3 nm-1}\)."
comment: "Mathematical expression in photon terms: \[{\rm{Sc}}_{\rm{in}} = \frac{\sigma_{\lambda}(\boldsymbol{x},t)}{4\pi} \int_{4\pi} B_{\lambda} (\mathit{\Omega}' \rightarrow \mathit{\Omega}) L_{\rm{p},\mathit{\Omega},\lambda} (\boldsymbol{x},t)\ {\rm{d}}\mathit{\Omega}'\] with \(L_{\rm{p},\mathit{\Omega},\lambda} (\boldsymbol{x},t)\) the incident spectral photon radiance. SI unit is \(\pu{s-1 sr-1 m-4}\) in photon number basis and \(\pu{mol s-1 sr-1 m-4}\) in chemical amount basis. Common unit is \(\pu{s-1 sr-1 cm-3 nm-1}\) in photon number basis and \(\pu{einstein s-1 sr-1 cm-3 nm-1}\) in chemical amount basis."
synonym: "scattering in (into the beam under consideration)" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14030
consider: goldbook:14077

[Term]
id: goldbook:14024
name: interparticle electron transfer
def: "Electron transfer taking place from a photoexcited semiconductor onto another semiconductor \(or insulator\) in its ground state leading to vectorial \(irreversible\) charge transfer."
synonym: "IPET" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14025
name: intrinsic emission
def: "Band-to-band recombination of electrons and holes \(or exciton decay\) in a semiconductor or insulator leading to luminescence."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14026
name: intrinsic defects
def: "Imperfections connected with surface point defects \(zero-dimensional\)\, linear defects \(one-dimensional\)\, or square defects \(two-dimensional\) not including extrinsic chemical units in relation to the chemical formula of the solid."
comment: "Surface vacancies, anionic vacancies (in n-type semiconductors), cationic vacancies (in p-type semiconductors), interstitial cations, edges, corners and kinks are some examples of intrinsic surface defects."
synonym: "intrinsic surface defects" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14011

[Term]
id: goldbook:14027
name: inversion layer
def: "Layer formed when band bending places the Fermi level on the surface close to the band of the bulk minority charge carriers. This leads to a higher concentration of the minority charge carriers on the surface relative to the concentration of the majority charge carriers\, an opposite situation to that in the bulk."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13989
consider: goldbook:14004

[Term]
id: goldbook:14028
name: local differential absorption
def: "Absorption term in the radiative transfer equation \(RTE\). Mathematical expression is \\\[\\alpha_\{\\lambda\} \(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\) L_\{\\mathit\{\\Omega\}\,\\lambda\} \(\\boldsymbol\{x\}/t\)\\\] with \\\(\\alpha_\{\\lambda\} \(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\)\\\) the space- and time-dependent linear napierian absorption coefficient and \\\(L_\{\\mathit\{\\Omega\}\,\\lambda\} \(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\)\\\) the spectral radiance arriving at position \\\(x\\\) and time \\\(t\\\) from every possible angular directions \\\(\\mathit\{\\Omega\}\\\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14077

[Term]
id: goldbook:14029
name: natural photosynthesis
def: "Production of complex organic substances by higher plants\, algae and photosynthesising bacteria from simple compounds \(e.g.\, carbon dioxide and water\, hydrogen sulfide\, etc.\) using ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation energy absorbed by\, e.g.\, chlorophyll\, chlorophyll-protein complexes\, and natural chromoproteins."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13993

[Term]
id: goldbook:14030
name: out-scattering
def: "Physical process by which the incoming electromagnetic radiation is forced to deviate from a straight trajectory by non-uniformities in the medium."
comment: "Mathematical definition: \({\rm{Sc}}_{\rm{out}} = \sigma_{\lambda} (\boldsymbol{x},t) L_{\mathit{\Omega},\lambda} (\boldsymbol{x},t)\) where \(\boldsymbol{x}\) is the coordinate in a three-dimensional space in the path of the beam, \(t\) is the time coordinate, \(\sigma_{\lambda} (\boldsymbol{x},t)\) is the space- and time-dependent linear napierian scattering coefficient, \(L_{\mathit{\Omega},\lambda} (\boldsymbol{x},t)\) is the incident space- and time-dependent radiance in the direction \(\mathit{\Omega}\). SI unit is \(\pu{W sr-1 m-4}\). Common unit is \(\pu{W sr-1 cm-3 nm-1}\)."
comment: "Physical phenomenon taking into account scattered radiative energy from the rays in the direction \(\mathit{\Omega}\) considered in the radiative transfer equation (RTE) into any direction \(\mathit{\Omega}'\)."
comment: "The mathematical definition in photon terms is: \({\rm{Sc}}_{\rm{out}} = \sigma_{\lambda} (\boldsymbol{x},t) L_{\rm{p},\mathit{\Omega},\lambda} (\boldsymbol{x},t)\) with \(L_{\rm{p},\mathit{\Omega},\lambda} (\boldsymbol{x},t)\) the incident space- and time-dependent photon radiance. SI unit is \(\pu{s-1 sr-1 m-4}\) in photon number basis and \(\pu{mol s-1 sr-1 m-4}\) in chemical amount basis. Common unit in photon number basis is \(\pu{s-1 sr-1 cm-3 nm-1}\) and \(\pu{einstein s-1 sr-1 cm-3 nm-1}\) in chemical amount basis."
synonym: "scattering out (out of the beam under consideration)" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14023
consider: goldbook:14077

[Term]
id: goldbook:14031
name: phase function
def: "Distribution function representing the fraction of photons scattered from any direction \\\(\\mathit\{\\Omega\}'\\\) into the direction \\\(\\mathit\{\\Omega\}\\\) under consideration in the radiative transfer equation \(RTE\). Dimension one."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14077

[Term]
id: goldbook:14032
name: photoadsorbate
def: "Entity \(molecule\, atom\, or ion\) bound to the surface of a solid \(semiconductor or insulator\) particle \(typically to a photoadsorption centre\) as the result of photoexcitation of either the solid or the entity \(molecule\, atom\, or ion\)."
comment: "When photoadsorption is a step in a photocatalytic reaction, the photoadsorbed entity may be considered an intermediate in the given reaction."
synonym: "photoadsorbed species" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14033
name: photoadsorption area number
def: "Maximum amount of substance photoadsorbed per area on the uniformly irradiated surface of an adsorbent."
comment: "Mathematical expression, \(\gamma^{\rm{s}} = N_{\rm{max}}/S\), where \(N_{\rm{max}}\) is the maximal number of entities or moles photoadsorbed and \(S\) the total surface area. Experimental values of \(\gamma^{\rm{s}}\) in entities are in the range \(\pu{E10 - E12 cm-2}\)."
comment: "Photoadsorption area number should be preferred to photoadsorption capacity (the original name given to this term), since the latter term is reserved for the fractional number, referring to the number of regular sites available for photoadsorption."
comment: "The quantity \(N_{\rm{max}}\) can be obtained by approximation from photoadsorption kinetics \(N(t)\) on a uniformly irradiated surface provided that(i) the powdered sample is uniformly irradiated. Then, the area of the irradiated surface is \(S = m a^{\rm{s}}\), where \(m\) is the mass of the sample and \(a^{\rm{s}}\) is the specific surface area (\(\pu{m2 kg-1}\)) of a given adsorbent, or(ii) the efficiency of desorption of the adsorbed species (via a physical process) is negligible. Then the number of photocatalytic centres can be estimated from the photoadsorption area number, \(\gamma^{\rm{s}}\), to determine TON and TOF for a given photocatalytic reaction."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14032
consider: goldbook:14034
consider: goldbook:14037

[Term]
id: goldbook:14034
name: photoadsorption capacity
def: "Maximal number of entities \(molecules\, atoms\, or ions\) per regular surface site that can be photoadsorbed at a uniformly irradiated surface of an adsorbent\, \\\(\\theta_\{\\rm\{max\}\} = N_\{\\rm\{max\}\}/N^\{\\rm\{ss\}\}\\\) where \\\(N_\{\\rm\{max\}\}\\\) is the maximal number of entities photoadsorbed and \\\(N^\{\\rm\{ss\}\}\\\) is the number of surface sites \(mainly regular surface sites\, i.e.\, regular lattice ions or atoms; typically\, \\\(N^\{\\rm\{ss\}\}\\\) ca. \\\(\\pu\{E15 cm-2\}\\\)\)."
comment: "This term has been often called maximal photoadsorption capacity. This denomination is redundant, because capacity already refers to the maximal possible value of a property."
comment: "\(\theta_{\rm{max}}\) is a quantity of dimension one (a fractional number). \(\theta_{\rm{max}}\) is about \(\pu{E-5 - E-3}\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14006
consider: goldbook:14032
consider: goldbook:14037
consider: goldbook:14083
consider: goldbook:14101

[Term]
id: goldbook:14035
name: photoadsorption centre
def: "Surface site or defect in its active state after photoexcitation capable of photoadsorbing species by a chemical interaction with the adsorbate entities \(molecules\, atoms\, or ions\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13990

[Term]
id: goldbook:14036
name: photoadsorption efficiency
def: "Ratio of the maximal \(typically the initial\) rate of photoadsorption of entities \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\) \(molecules\, atoms\, or ions\) to the integrated incident \(superscript 0\, zero\) photon flux\, \\\(\\int_\{\\lambda_\{1\}\}^\{\\lambda_\{2\}\} q_\{\\rm\{p\}\,\\lambda\}^\{0\}\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\lambda = q_\{\\rm\{p\}\}^\{0\} = \{\\rm\{d\}\}N_\{\\rm\{p\}\}^\{0\}/\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\\\)\, in the wavelength range \\\(\\lambda_\{1\}\{-\}\\lambda_\{2\}\\\). Both rates should be measured under otherwise identical conditions \(preferably as initial rates\) in order to avoid concentration and/or surface area effects."
comment: "For \(P_{\rm{AE}}\) determined under first-order conditions on photon flux and zero-order on concentration (or pressure), it corresponds to the efficiency of formation of the active photoadsorption centre."
comment: "Mathematical expression: \[P_{\rm{AE}} = \frac{{\rm{d}}N_{\ce{B}}/{\rm{d}}t}{\int_{\lambda_{1}}^{\lambda_{2}} q_{\rm{p},\lambda}^{0}{\rm{d}}\lambda} \ {\rm{for}}\ t \to 0\] where \({\rm{d}}N_{\ce{B}}/{\rm{d}}t\) is the rate of adsorption of entities (the initial quasi-stationary rate of photoadsorption)."
comment: "The initial rate of photoadsorption in SI units is \({\rm{d}}p/{\rm{d}}t\) (\(\pu{Pa s-1}\)) for gas/solid systems, with \(p\) the pressure and in SI-coherent units \({\rm{d}}c/{\rm{d}}t\) (\(\pu{mol L-3 s-1}\)) for liquid/solid systems, with \(c\) the amount concentration of the adsorbate in solution."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14035

[Term]
id: goldbook:14037
name: photoadsorption fraction
def: "Number of entities \(atoms\, molecules or ions\) photoadsorbed per regular surface site at a uniformly irradiated surface of an adsorbent."
comment: "Mathematical expression: \(\theta_{\rm{ph}} = N/N^{\rm{ss}}\) with \(N\) the number of entities photoadsorbed and \(N^{\rm{ss}}\) the number of surface sites (mainly regular surface sites, i.e., regular lattice ions or atoms; typically, \(N^{\rm{ss}}\) ca. \(\pu{E15 cm-2}\)). \(\theta_{\rm{ph}}\) is a quantity of dimension one (it is a fractional number)."
comment: "The photoadsorption fraction, \(\theta_{\rm{ph}}\), is smaller than the photoadsorption capacity, \(\theta_{\rm{max}}\) (i.e., \(\theta_{\rm{ph}} \lt \theta_{\rm{max}}\))."
comment: "\(\theta_{\rm{ph}}\) has been called photoadsorption number; fraction is preferred. It should not be called capacity, since the term capacity should be reserved for the maximal adsorption number."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14032
consider: goldbook:14033
consider: goldbook:14034
consider: goldbook:14083

[Term]
id: goldbook:14038
name: photoadsorption photonic yield
def: "Ratio of the maximal \(typically the initial\) rate of photoadsorption of entities \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\) \(molecules\, atoms\, or ions\) on a surface to the incident \(superscript 0\, zero\) photon flux\, \\\(q_\{\\rm\{p\}\,\\lambda\}^\{0\} = \{\\rm\{d\}\}N_\{\\rm\{p\}\,\\lambda\}^\{0\}/\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\\\) at a given wavelength. Both rates should be measured under otherwise identical conditions \(preferably as initial rates\) in order to avoid concentration and/or surface area effects."
comment: "Mathematical expression: \(\frac{{\rm{d}}N_{\ce{B}}/{\rm{d}}t}{q_{\rm{p},\lambda}^{0}} \ \ {\rm{for}}\ t \to 0\)"
comment: "Yield refers to the use of monochromatic irradiation, whereas efficiency is reserved for irradiation within a wavelength range. Photonic refers to incident radiation."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14036
consider: goldbook:14039

[Term]
id: goldbook:14039
name: photoadsorption quantum yield
def: "Ratio of the number of photoadsorbed entities \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\) \(molecules\, atoms\, or ions\) on a surface to the number of photons of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation \(at a given wavelength \\\(\\lambda\\\)\) absorbed by the system \(typically by the adsorbent\) during the initial stage of irradiation."
comment: "Mathematical expression: \(\mathit{\Phi}_{\rm{ads}} = \frac{{\rm{d}}N_{\ce{B}}/{\rm{d}}t}{{\rm{d}}N_{\rm{p},\lambda}/{\rm{d}}t}\), for \(t \to 0\), i.e., the ratio of the initial rates of photoadsorption and monochromatic photons absorbed, with \(N_{\ce{B}}\) the number of entities adsorbed and \(N_{\rm{p},\lambda}\) the number of photons of wavelength \(\lambda\) absorbed. The photoadsorption quantum yield has been called \(K_{\rm{a}}\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14032
consider: goldbook:14036
consider: goldbook:14038

[Term]
id: goldbook:14040
name: photocarrier
def: "Nonequilibrium free electron or free hole generated in a solid by ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared absorption and occupying energy levels in the conduction band or the valence band\, respectively."
comment: "Electrons occupying energy levels near the bottom of the conduction band and holes occupying energy levels near the top of the valence band (thermalised carriers) are considered as quasi-chemical intermediates. Nonthermalised (hot) carriers can also be involved in photocatalysed reactions."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14041
name: photocatalytic active centre
def: "Surface centre\, i.e.\, either a surface defect or a regular surface site\, at which a chemical transformation takes place after converting the centre into an active state by a photophysical process\, e.g.\, by photoexcitation."
comment: "The number of photoactive centres is very often difficult to establish, particularly on solid surfaces and in suspended particles."
comment: "The photophysical process may include one or several processes such as direct excitation of the photocatalytic centre via absorption of ultraviolet, visible, or infrared radiation, via photocarrier trapping, via interaction with excitons, or via energy and/or electron transfer from a photoexcited reagent or intermediate."
comment: "When the density of the active centres is unknown, the reaction rates may be related to the \(\rm{BET}(\ce{N2})\) area with the understanding that the parameter or property of the system will be a lower limit of the actual value, because the surface determined by \(\ce{N2}\) adsorption will be larger than that reached by the radiation. See Note 2 under the definition of Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13997
consider: goldbook:13999
consider: goldbook:14040

[Term]
id: goldbook:14042
name: photocatalytic activity
def: "Number of entities \(product\) formed in a photocatalytic process \(alternatively the number of reactant entities disappearing\) divided by the number of photons absorbed by the photocatalyst at one single wavelength or within a defined wavelength range. For one wavelength it has the same meaning as quantum yield\, whereas within a defined wavelength range it has the same meaning as quantum efficiency."
comment: "This quantity has a well-defined meaning only when the reaction conditions are also stated and the results reported in terms of the initial conditions."
synonym: "PCA" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14043
name: photocatalytic centre
def: "Surface centre\, i.e.\, a surface defect or a regular surface site\, in an active or electronically excited state at which a chemical transformation takes place."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13999
consider: goldbook:14041

[Term]
id: goldbook:14044
name: photocatalytic efficiency
def: "Number of entities \(product\) formed \(alternatively the number of reactant entities disappearing\) divided by the number of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared photons incident \(prior to absorption\) on the system and in a wavelength range \\\(\\lambda_\{1\}\{-\}\\lambda_\{2\}\\\)."
comment: "Quantity relevant in applications of photocatalysis where the interest is to assess an engineering efficiency of energy out vs. energy in. In this context, the quantity has a well-defined meaning only when the operating conditions are also stated and results reported in terms of the initial conditions."
comment: "When using photon flux (number basis or chemical amount basis) the term photonic efficiency is preferred, as efficiency refers to excitation within a wavelength range, whereas for monochromatic irradiation (and incident photons) the term photonic yield should be used."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14057
consider: goldbook:14058

[Term]
id: goldbook:14045
name: photocatalytic oxidation
def: "Commonly used to describe the oxidation of pollutants by the action of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation and a catalyst."
comment: "Often used to describe the oxidation of organic pollutants at low concentrations by UV-light irradiation of \(\ce{TiO2}\)."
synonym: "PCO" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14046
name: photocorrosion
def: "Decomposition of the photocatalyst or of the electrodes as a result of a photoinduced reaction."
comment: "Photocorrosion is one of the factors limiting the turnover number in heterogeneous photocatalysis."
comment: "Process taking place during photoelectrochemical etching."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14018
consider: goldbook:14101

[Term]
id: goldbook:14047
name: photocurrent
def: "Photogeneration and movement of charge between two electrodes in a photovoltaic cell or a photoelectrochemical cell as a result of photoprocesses induced by light absorption."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14048
name: photodesorption
def: "Desorption induced by the absorption of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation either by the adsorbate or by the adsorbent."
comment: "Photodesorption can be a step in the overall mechanism in heterogeneous photocatalysis."
comment: "Photodesorption is the inverse process of photoadsorption. For a particular system, both processes (or reactions) are induced by ultraviolet, visible, or infrared radiation of the same wavelength region."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14006
consider: goldbook:14018

[Term]
id: goldbook:14049
name: photodesorption quantum yield
def: "Number of entities evolving into the medium from a surface divided by the number of photons absorbed at a given wavelength by a solid or by an adsorbed entity treated as a surface extrinsic defect \(centre\)."
comment: "In practice, for polychromatic irradiation, integration is performed in the wavelength range used and the quantity should be called photodesorption quantum efficiency."
comment: "Mathematical expression of the integral photodesorption quantum yield: \[\mathit{\Phi}_{\rm{des}} = \frac{N_{\rm{des}}}{N_{\rm{p},\lambda}}\] where \(N_{\rm{des}}\) is the number of species desorbed and \(N_{\rm{p},\lambda}\) the number of photons of a particular wavelength absorbed during a given irradiation time."
comment: "Should photoexcitation of a system be due to absorption by pre-adsorbed species (see extrinsic absorption), \(\phi_{\rm{des}}\) would not depend on the concentration of pre-adsorbed species."
comment: "The differential expression is: \[\mathit{\Phi}_{\rm{des}} = \frac{{\rm{d}}N_{\rm{des}}/{\rm{d}}t}{q_{\rm{p},\lambda}}\] where \({\rm{d}}N_{\rm{des}}/{\rm{d}}t\) is the initial (quasistationary) or maximal photodesorption rate and \(q_{\rm{p},\lambda} = {\rm{d}}N_{\rm{p},\lambda}/{\rm{d}}t\) is the spectral photon flux absorbed by a solid or by an adsorbed entity treated as a surface extrinsic defect (centre)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14010
consider: goldbook:14011
consider: goldbook:14048

[Term]
id: goldbook:14050
name: photoelectrocatalysis
def: "Electrochemically assisted photocatalysis. The role of the photocatalyst is played by a photoelectrode\, often a semiconductor."
comment: "In photoelectrocatalytic transformations it is possible to identify an electrode and an electrolyte combination in the system along with an explicit, generally macroscopic, electrical circuit for the charge transfer and/or for applying an additional electric potential to the system. This is not so in photocatalysis by colloids or fine dispersions of semiconductors."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14051

[Term]
id: goldbook:14051
name: photoelectrode
def: "Electrode capable of initiating electrochemical transformations after absorbing ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14052
name: photoelectrosynthesis
def: "Process by which ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation is absorbed by a semiconductor\, electron transfer occurs at the interface between the semiconductor and a liquid and the output is chemical energy\, i.e.\, radiant energy is stored as chemical energy."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14053
name: photogenerated catalysis
def: "Initiation of chemical transformations through the photochemical formation of molecular species\, which \(once formed\) are catalytically active even without the action of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13999

[Term]
id: goldbook:14054
name: photogenerated charge carrier
def: "Charge carrying entity\, especially an electron or a hole\, in a semiconductor\, produced by the absorption of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14055
name: photoinitiator
def: "Agent initiating certain chemical transformations under the action of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation and consumed therewith."
comment: "A photocatalyst can also act as a photoinitiator that, however, will not be consumed."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14056
name: photomineralisation
def: "Complete photodegradation of an organic compound into carbon dioxide and water\, and other simple inorganic compounds \(depending on the hetero-atom in the compound\) upon absorption of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation by the photocatalyst or by the adsorbate."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14057
name: photonic efficiency
def: "Ratio of the rate of the photoreaction measured for a specified time interval \(usually the initial conditions\) to the rate of incident photons within a defined wavelength interval inside the irradiation window of the reactor."
comment: "A quantity called formal quantum efficiency (FQE) is sometimes used as synonymous for photonic efficiency and calculated from the known spectral distribution of the excitation source and the known absorption spectrum of the reaction system. It is calculated as the ratio between the rate of reaction and the incident photon flux."
comment: "Mathematical definition: \[\xi = \frac{{\rm{d}}n/{\rm{d}}t}{\int_{\lambda_{1}}^{\lambda_{2}} q_{n,\rm{p},\lambda}^{0}{\rm{d}}\lambda}\] when using the incident spectral photon flux, chemical amount basis, \(q_{n,\rm{p},\lambda}^{0}\) with \(n\) (mol) the amount of substance produced or consumed, and \[\xi = \frac{{\rm{d}}N/{\rm{d}}t}{\int_{\lambda_{1}}^{\lambda_{2}} q_{\rm{p},\lambda}^{0} {\rm{d}}\lambda}\] when using the incident spectral photon flux, number basis, \(q_{\rm{p},\lambda}^{0}\) with \(N\) the number of entities produced or consumed. The superscript 0 (zero) emphasises the fact that the incident number of photons (prior to absorption) is used."
comment: "When monochromatic radiation is used, formal quantum efficiency is \(\xi\); otherwise formal quantum efficiency \(\neq \xi\)."
synonym: "FQE" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
synonym: "formal quantum efficiency" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14044
consider: goldbook:14058
consider: goldbook:14084

[Term]
id: goldbook:14058
name: photonic yield
def: "Ratio of the rate of the photoreaction measured for a specified time interval \(usually the initial conditions\) to the rate of incident photons of monochromatic light inside the irradiation window of the reactor."
comment: "Mathematical definition: \[\frac{{\rm{d}}n/{\rm{d}}t}{q_{n,\rm{p},\lambda}^{0}}\] when using the incident spectral photon flux, chemical amount basis, \(q_{n,\rm{p},\lambda}^{0}\), with \(n\) (mol) the amount of substance produced or consumed, and \[\frac{{\rm{d}}N/{\rm{d}}t}{q_{\rm{p},\lambda}^{0}}\] when using the incident spectral photon flux, number basis, \(q_{\rm{p},\lambda}^{0}\), with \(N\) the number of entities produced or consumed. The superscript 0 (zero) emphasises the fact that the incident number of photons (prior to absorption) is used."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14057

[Term]
id: goldbook:14059
name: photonic yield spectrum
def: "Plot of the photonic yield of a given photoprocess\, measured at the same photon flux of incident \(prior to absorption\) light\, as a function of a measure of the energy of the photons\, i.e.\, for monochromatic irradiation."
comment: "In practice, the photonic yield spectrum is obtained by normalising the rate of the given process to a certain number of incident photons provided that the photoresponse is linear on the incident photon fluence."
comment: "Note that in photocatalysis the term yield is reserved for monochromatic irradiation, whereas the term efficiency is used for irradiation within a wavelength range. This is different from the use of efficiency as defined by IUPAC in photochemistry."
comment: "Should the fluence dependence of the photoresponse be nonlinear (as is often the case in catalysed photoresponses), a plot of the photoresponse vs. fluence should be made at several wavelengths and a standard response should be chosen (e.g., two-lg reduction). A plot of the inverse of the "standard response" level as a function of wavelength is then the action spectrum of the photoresponse."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14058

[Term]
id: goldbook:14060
name: photoradiation catalysis
def: "Radiation catalysis under conditions not allowing a distinction between the effect of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared and of ionising radiation as well as the case in which the ionising radiation alone generates emission of chemically active light \(e.g.\, the 'Cherenkov' light emission\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14067

[Term]
id: goldbook:14061
name: photosensitised reaction
def: "Reaction initiated by photosensitisation."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14000

[Term]
id: goldbook:14062
name: photothermocatalysis
def: "Catalysis occurring by the combined action of electronic excitation due to absorption of ultraviolet or visible radiation and thermal energy \(infrared radiation or exothermic reactions\) including catalytic phenomena."
comment: "This often occurs upon exposure of a heterogeneous photocatalyst to infrared-light, laser emission, or solar radiation."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14063
name: physical pathway
def: "Set of physical processes leading to the relaxation of the electronically excited system without chemical transformations."
comment: "Examples are radiative and nonradiative relaxations in molecules and surface complexes from the excited state to the ground state, recombination and trapping of free charge carriers and recombination decay of trapped carriers (particularly, recombination decay of carriers trapped by surface-active centres)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14064
name: radiant dose
def: "Radiant energy \(\\\(Q\\\)\, SI unit is \\\(\\pu\{J\}\\\)\) or amount of photons \(in \\\(\\pu\{mol\}\\\) or the non-SI unit einstein\) absorbed per area or volume by an object irradiated during a given exposure time."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14065
name: radiation-activation
def: "Increasing of catalytic activity or alteration of selectivity of a substance after its exposure to high-energy radiation."
synonym: "radioactivation" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:13999

[Term]
id: goldbook:14066
name: radiation adsorption
def: "The word combination may be misleading. Usually radiation is absorbed."
synonym: "radiation-induced adsorption" []
synonym: "radioadsorption" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14067
name: radiation catalysis
def: "Change in the rate of a chemical reaction under the action of ionising radiation in the presence of a radiation catalyst."
comment: "When using ionising radiation, from vacuum ultraviolet to higher energy, the phenomenon of photocatalysis cannot be distinguished from the phenomenon of radiation catalysis. Due to the nonselective absorption of ionising radiation, excitation of all reaction partners (both the reactants and the reaction catalysts) is possible. Thus, the phenomena globally designated as radiation catalysis include both direct radiation-induced and catalysed processes."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14068

[Term]
id: goldbook:14068
name: radiation catalyst
def: "Substance absorbing ionising radiation and able to produce chemical transformations of the reaction partners\, repeatedly producing intermediate transformations and regenerating its chemical composition after each cycle of such interactions."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14069
name: radiation-chemical yield
def: "Number of entities of a product formed \(alternatively\, the number of entities of a reactant disappearing\) generated per \\\(\\pu\{100 eV\}\\\) of energy absorbed from ionising radiation."
comment: "In SI units it refers to the amount of substance in \(\pu{\upmu mol}\) generated for each joule of radiation absorbed by the system. SI unit is \(\pu{\upmu mol J-1}\)."
comment: "It is convenient to use the amount of substance  \(\ce{B}\) in \(\pu{E-7 mol}\) of \(\ce{B}\), generated for each joule of radiation absorbed on the system. By choosing this (submultiple of) SI coherent unit, a number almost identical to the \(G\) value (within \(\pu{4\%}\)) in the obsolete unit \(\pu{(100 eV)-1}\) is obtained, as \(\pu{(100 eV)-1} = \pu{1.036E-7 mol J-1}\)."
comment: "Mathematical expression: \(G = 100 (N_{\ce{B}}/Q)\) where \(N_{\ce{B}}\) is the number of entities \(\ce{B}\) formed (or disappeared) and \(Q\) is the absorbed radiation energy in \(\pu{eV}\). Unit is \(\pu{(100 eV)-1}\)."
comment: "When the energy absorbed by the radiation catalyst cannot be calculated (as is often the case in radiation catalysis), the radiation chemical yield is calculated in terms of the energy absorbed by the system. In this case, it is an apparent yield."
synonym: "G value" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14007
consider: goldbook:14068

[Term]
id: goldbook:14070
name: radiation desorption
def: "Desorption from solid or liquid interfaces induced by absorption of radiation by the solid or liquid adsorbent."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14071
name: radiation-generated catalysis
def: "Initiation of chemical transformations through the formation\, by absorption of high-energy radiation\, of substances that are catalytically active even in the absence of the radiation."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14067

[Term]
id: goldbook:14072
name: radiation-induced adsorption
def: "Adsorption of a molecular entity at solid or liquid interfaces induced by absorption of high-energy radiation by the solid or the liquid adsorbent."
comment: "The word combination radiation adsorption is misleading. Usually, radiation is absorbed."
synonym: "radiation adsorption" []
synonym: "radioadsorption" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14073
name: radiation sensitisation
def: "Chemical change or physical process occurring in one entity \(e.g.\, biological molecules\, cells\, and living organisms\) as a result of initial absorption of high-energy radiation by the medium in the presence of another entity called the radiation sensitiser."
comment: "High-energy radiation is absorbed usually nonspecifically in the medium (e.g., solvent) and all chemical reactions arise from the products of this primary energy absorption."
synonym: "radiosensitisation" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14074

[Term]
id: goldbook:14074
name: radiation sensitiser
def: "Chemical substance that increases the radiation sensitivity of\, in particular\, biological molecules\, cells and biological organisms."
synonym: "radiosensitiser" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14073

[Term]
id: goldbook:14075
name: radiation-stimulated diffusion
def: "Increase of diffusion of an adsorbate under the action of ionising radiation absorbed by either the adsorbent and/or the adsorbate."
synonym: "radiostimulated diffusion" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14076
name: radiation thermal catalysis
def: "Radiation catalysis under the action of large radiation fluxes that simultaneously heat the system as a consequence of absorption of energy from radiationless deactivation."
synonym: "radiothermal catalysis" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14077
name: radiative transfer equation
def: "When a photon beam of radiance \\\(L_\{\\lambda\}\\\) and angular distribution \\\(\\mathit\{\\Omega\}\\\) passes along a single direction of propagation through a medium\, which is totally absorbing and/or scattering\, i.e.\, the processes occurring are described by the following equation\: \\\[\\begin\{array\} \\\\ \\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}L_\{\\Omega\,\\lambda\}\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\)\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}s\} + \\underbrace\{\\alpha_\{\\lambda\}\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\) L_\{\\mathit\{\\Omega\}\,\\lambda\}\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\)\}_\{\\rm\{adsorption\}\} + \\underbrace\{\\sigma_\{\\lambda\}\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\) L_\{\\mathit\{\\Omega\}\,\\lambda\}\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\)\}_\{\\rm\{out\\mbox\{-\}scattering\}\} = \\\\ \\underbrace\{j_\{\\lambda\}\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\)\}_\{\\rm\{emission\}\} + \\underbrace\{\\frac\{\\sigma_\{\\lambda\}\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\)\}\{4\\pi\}B_\{\\lambda\}\(\\mathit\{\\Omega\}' \\to \\mathit\{\\Omega\}\) L_\{\\mathit\{\\Omega\}\,\\lambda\}\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\)\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\mathit\{\\Omega\}'\}_\{\\rm\{in\\mbox\{-\}scattering\}\} \\end\{array\}\\\] where \\\(\\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}L_\{\\Omega\,\\lambda\}\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\)\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}s\}\\\) is the directional derivative along the direction \\\(s\\\) at a point \\\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\\\) in the reaction space of the incident radiance\, \\\(\\alpha_\{\\lambda\}\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\)\\\) is the space- and time-dependent spectral linear napierian absorption coefficient\, \\\(\\sigma_\{\\lambda\}\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\)\\\) is the space- and time-dependent spectral linear napierian scattering coefficient\, \\\(j_\{\\lambda\}\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\)\\\) represents the spontaneous and/or induced emission\, and \\\(B_\{\\lambda\}\\\) is the phase function. All quantities are a function of time. The RTE is written for the direction \\\(s\\\)."
comment: "Absorption and out-scattering reduce the radiance along the axis of propagation, whereas in-scattering and emission, increases it. Normally, emission can be safely neglected in photocatalysis."
comment: "Back scattering and forward scattering are global effects derived from the integration of absorption, in-scattering, out-scattering, and emission."
comment: "The RTE is a representation of the (radiation) energy conservation law and its solution in cases with complex geometry and in the presence of scattering remains a challenging issue."
synonym: "RTE" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14023
consider: goldbook:14028
consider: goldbook:14030
consider: goldbook:14031

[Term]
id: goldbook:14078
name: rate of generation
def: "Number of carriers \(or electron-hole pairs\, or excitons\) produced per time interval and per volume of a solid photocatalyst particle due to the absorption of light."
comment: "In kinetic studies of photocatalysed reactions, the rate of generation is typically considered as a constant \(g = \alpha(\lambda)\ E_{\rm{p}, \lambda}^{0}\) (uniform generation), which corresponds to the so-called weak light absorption case [\(\alpha(\lambda) x \ll 1\)]."
comment: "The rate of photogeneration in the bulk of optical uniform photocatalyst particles depends on the distance (\(x\)) from the irradiated surface."
comment: "The rate of photogeneration of carriers, \(g(x)\), has the general mathematical expression: \[g(x) = \alpha(\lambda)\ E_{\rm{p},\lambda}^{0}\ (1 - \rho) \exp[-\alpha(\lambda)x]\] where \(\alpha(\lambda)\) is the napierian (spectral) absorption coefficient, \(E_{\rm{p},\lambda}^{0}\) is the incident (superscript 0) spectral photon irradiance and \(\rho\) the reflectance. SI unit of \(g(x)\) is \(\pu{m-3 s-1}\) for \(q_{n,\rm{p},\lambda}^{0}\) in photon number basis and \(\pu{mol m-3 s-1}\) for \(q_{\rm{p},\lambda}^{0}\) in chemical amount basis."
comment: "\(g(x)\) is related to the number of photogenerated electrons (\(N_{\rm{e}}\)) and holes (\(N_{\rm{h}}\)) as \(N_{\rm{e}} = g(x) \tau_{\rm{e}}\) and \(N_{\rm{h}} = g(x) \tau_{\rm{h}}\), where \(\tau_{\rm{e}}\) and \(\tau_{\rm{h}}\) are the lifetimes of the free electrons and holes, respectively."
synonym: "rate of photogeneration" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14094

[Term]
id: goldbook:14079
name: rate of surface recombination
def: "Decay of carriers at the surface of a solid or interface between different phases\, e.g.\, at the crystal surface."
comment: "Inverse of the lifetime of free carriers in the bulk of the particle. The greater \(s\) is, the smaller is the stationary concentration of photocarriers at the surface (and vice versa). SI unit is \(\pu{s-1}\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14014
consider: goldbook:14040
consider: goldbook:14098

[Term]
id: goldbook:14080
name: recombination of photocarriers
def: "Two-step transition of an electron from the conduction band to a defect centre and then further to the valence band\, or from the defect centre to the valence band \(first step\) and a subsequent transition of the conduction band electron to the defect centre \(second step\)."
synonym: "recombination of photocarriers via defects" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14081

[Term]
id: goldbook:14081
name: recombination centre
def: "Defect in semiconductors and insulators capable of sequential capture of free charge carriers of both signs with high efficiency."
comment: "Provides a path for the recombination of free electrons and holes at high rates. Typically, in imperfect wide-bandgap energy solids, the rate of photoinduced carrier recombination through recombination centres at moderate photon irradiance is larger than the rate of band-to-band recombination."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14082
name: regular surface
def: "Perfect surface of a solid without heterogeneity or defect."
comment: "Rigorously speaking, this is a theoretical notion. In practice, however, this term is used to refer to local space regions of real relaxed and reconstructed surfaces if the perturbations caused by the nearest adjacent (surface) defects can be neglected."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14083
name: regular surface site
def: "Zero-dimension site \(atom\, ion\, or interstitial position\) at a regular surface."
comment: "It can be a photocatalytic centre (site) in the ground (not active) state in some particular cases of heterogeneous photocatalysis as, e.g., in the case when pre-physisorbed entities trap photocarriers in the course of the photocatalysed reaction."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14018
consider: goldbook:14040
consider: goldbook:14043
consider: goldbook:14082

[Term]
id: goldbook:14084
name: relative photonic efficiency
def: "Ratio of the photonic efficiency \(\\\(\\xi\\\)\) of a chemical process to the corresponding photonic efficiency of a standard process under otherwise identical conditions in the same reactor configuration and operation."
comment: "For measurements carried out at constant photon flux, \(\xi_{\rm{rel}}\) can be estimated as the ratio of the corresponding reaction initial rates (for \(t \to 0\)), determined under identical conditions."
comment: "Protocols for the determination of \(\xi_{\rm{rel}}\) have been reported."
comment: "See the restrictions about the determination of quantum yields in photocatalysis mentioned in the Introduction of the source IUPAC Recommendation."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14057

[Term]
id: goldbook:14085
name: scattering albedo
def: "Efficiency of scattering\, i.e.\, ratio of scattering coefficient\, \\\(\\sigma\(\\lambda_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\)\\\)\, to the total attenuance coefficient\, \\\(\\beta\(\\lambda_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\)\\\)\, at a particular wavelength \\\(\\lambda_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\\\)."
comment: "A scattering albedo of 1 indicates total scattering, whereas a scattering albedo of 0 indicates that all attenuance is due to absorption."
comment: "Mathematical expression: \(\sigma(\lambda_{\rm{i}})/\beta(\lambda_{\rm{i}})\). Dimension one."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14086
name: shallow trap
def: "Energy level \(of trapped carrier\) within the bandgap energy\, near the bottom of the conduction band \(electron shallow trap\) and near the top of the valence band \(hole shallow band\) at an energy such that \\\(E_\{\\rm\{trap\}\} \\approx kT\\\)\, with \\\(k\\\) the Boltzmann constant and \\\(T\\\) the thermodynamic temperature."
synonym: "shallow energy trap" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14087
name: Solonitsyn effect
def: "Adsorption \(or reaction\) after preliminary irradiation of a photocatalyst in vacuum \(or in the absence of reagent\)."
synonym: "post-adsorption" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14001

[Term]
id: goldbook:14088
name: sonophotocatalytic reactor
def: "Reactor in which a hybrid technique combining 'sonochemical' and photocatalytic reactions is used."
comment: "This type of reactor has been proposed for the treatment of wastewaters."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14089
name: space charge region
def: "Region formed within a semiconductor in contact with another phase in which there is a band bending that can be upward or downward."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14004

[Term]
id: goldbook:14090
name: specific photoadsorption number
def: "Maximum number of entities \(molecules\, atoms or radicals\) adsorbed as a result of irradiation divided by the surface area and by the mass of adsorbent."
comment: "This definition, per area and per mass, is misleading. It is included for historical reasons and was originally called specific photoadsorption capacity."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14034

[Term]
id: goldbook:14091
name: specific absorption coefficient
def: "Linear napierian\, \\\(\\alpha_\{\\lambda\}^\{\\ast\}\\\)\, or linear decadic\, \\\(a_\{\\lambda\}^\{\\ast\}\\\)\, absorption coefficient divided by the mass concentration\, \\\(\\rho\\\)."
comment: "Absorption coefficient is always a spectral property; the subscript \(\lambda\) may be omitted but the wavelength should be specified."
comment: "For reacting systems with mass fluctuations and high scattering, space- and time-dependent specific, napierian and decadic, absorption coefficients are necessary, i.e., \(\alpha_{\lambda}^{\ast}(\boldsymbol{x},t)\) and \(a_{\lambda}^{\ast}(\boldsymbol{x},t)\), respectively; \(\boldsymbol{x}\) defines the position in a three-dimensional space."
comment: "Mathematical definition: \[\alpha_{\lambda}^{\ast} = \frac{\alpha_{\lambda}}{\rho} \quad {\rm{or}} \quad a_{\lambda}^{\ast} = \frac{a_{\lambda}}{\rho}\] SI unit is \(\pu{m kg-1}\) , common unit is \(\pu{m g-1}\) or \(\pu{cm2 g-1 nm-1}\)."
comment: "The asterisk as superscript indicates that it is a specific property."
synonym: "specific linear Napierian spectral absorption coefficient" []
synonym: "specific linear decadic spectral absorption coefficient" []
synonym: "specific spectral absorption coefficient" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14092
name: specific napierian attenuance coefficient
def: "Attenuance \(napierian\)\, \\\(D_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\)\, divided by the absorption pathlength\, \\\(l\\\)\, and the mass concentration\, \\\(\\rho\\\)."
comment: "In common use for \(l\) in \(\pu{cm}\) and \(\rho\) in (\(\pu{g cm-3}\)), \(\beta_{\lambda}^{\ast}\) is in \(\pu{cm2 g-1}\)."
comment: "Mathematical expression: \[\beta_{\lambda}^{\ast} = \left(\frac{1}{\rho\,l}\right) \ln \left(\frac{P_{\lambda}^{0}}{P_{\lambda}}\right) = \frac{D_{\rm{e},\lambda}}{\rho\,l}\] where \(P_{\lambda}^{0}\) and \(P_{\lambda}\) are, respectively, the incident and transmitted spectral radiant power or the incident and transmitted spectral photon flux."
comment: "Sometimes called mass napierian (spectral) attenuance coefficient."
comment: "Strictly speaking, the spectral quantity should be used, which is the derivative of the specific napierian attenuance coefficient with respect to \(\lambda\), i.e., \({\rm{d}}\beta\!\ast\!(\lambda)/{\rm{d}}\lambda\) in common units \(\pu{cm2 g-1 nm-1}\) or in \(\pu{cm g-1}\). In practice, the subscript is omitted and the respective unit is not taken into account. The wavelength should be specified."
comment: "The specific napierian attenuance coefficient reduces to specific napierian absorption coefficient for a non-reflecting and non-scattering sample."
synonym: "specific Napierian extinction coefficient" []
synonym: "specific Napierian spectral extinction coefficient" []
synonym: "specific spectral Napierian attenuance coefficient" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14093
name: specific napierian scattering coefficient
def: "Fraction of attenuance \(napierian\) not due to absorbance\, divided by the pathlength\, \\\(l\\\)\, within the scattering medium and the mass concentration\, \\\(\\rho\\\)."
comment: "Mathematical expression: \[\sigma_{\lambda}^{\ast} = \sigma_{\lambda}/\rho = [D_{\rm{e},\lambda} - A_{\rm{e},\lambda}]/(l\,\rho)\], where \(\rho\) is the mass concentration. The absorbance should be subtracted from the total attenuance for the calculation of \(\rho_{\lambda}\) and \(\rho_{\lambda}^{\ast}\)."
comment: "Sometimes called mass napierian (spectral) scattering coefficient."
comment: "Strictly speaking, the spectral quantity should be used, which is the derivative of the specific napierian scattering coefficient with respect to \(\lambda\), i.e., \({\rm{d}}\sigma\!\ast\!(\lambda)/{\rm{d}}\lambda\) in common units \(\pu{cm2 g-1 nm-1}\) or in \(\pu{cm g-1}\). In practice, the subscript is omitted and the respective unit is not taken into account. The wavelength should be specified."
comment: "The superscript asterisk indicates that it is a specific property."
synonym: "specific spectral napierian scattering coefficient" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14094
name: spectral photon irradiance
def: "Derivative of photon irradiance\, \\\(E_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\)\, with respect to wavelength\, \\\(\\lambda\\\)."
comment: "All spectral terms may also be defined as derivatives with respect to frequency, \(\nu\), or wavenumber, \(\tilde{\nu}\) and are referred to, when necessary, as in terms of wavelength, or frequency or wavenumber, respectively."
comment: "Mathematical expression: \(E_{\rm{p},\lambda} = {\rm{d}}E_{\rm{p}}/{\rm{d}}\lambda\). SI unit is \(\pu{s-1 m-3}\); common unit is \(\pu{s-1 m-2 nm-1}\)."
comment: "This quantity can be expressed on a chemical amount basis by dividing \(E_{p,\lambda}\) by the Avogadro constant, the name then being "spectral photon irradiance, amount basis", the symbol \(E_{n,{\rm{p}},\lambda}\) and the SI unit \(\pu{mol s-1 m-3}\); common unit is \(\pu{einstein s-1 m-2 nm-1}\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14095
name: spectral photonic yield
def: "Dependence of the photonic yield on the photon energy \(wavelength\, frequency\, or wavenumber\) of the radiation for a given photoreaction obtained at a certain incident \(prior to absorption\) photon irradiance in a given spectral range."
comment: "In practice, it is normalised to a certain spectral photon flux provided that photoresponse scales linearly with it."
comment: "Information concerning the photoexcitation of a solid photocatalyst can be obtained from a comparison of the spectral quantum yield with the spectral photonic efficiency."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14057
consider: goldbook:14058
consider: goldbook:14096

[Term]
id: goldbook:14096
name: spectral quantum yield
def: "Dependence of the quantum yield on the photon energy \(wavelength\, frequency or wavenumber\) of the radiation absorbed by the reactant for a given photoreaction."
comment: "To obtain information on the spectral variation of activity of a photocatalyst, the measured photoresponse must scale linearly with the photon flux at all wavelengths."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14097
name: surface-active site
def: "Surface regular or defect site that can initiate the chemical transformation of reagents."
synonym: "surface-active centre" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14012
consider: goldbook:14102

[Term]
id: goldbook:14098
name: surface recombination
def: "Recombination of charge carriers at a solid surface or at an interface between different phases."
comment: "In theoretical models of heterogeneous photocatalysis, the photocarriers exchange between the solid and the reactants yielding products that may be considered as a specific type of surface recombination products."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14040
consider: goldbook:14079

[Term]
id: goldbook:14099
name: thermal bleaching
def: "Loss of absorption by the colour centres because of heating of the solid semiconductor or insulator."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14002

[Term]
id: goldbook:14100
name: thermophotocatalysis
def: "A photocatalytic phenomenon occurring when the system is also heated by the ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation."
comment: "In case of high photon flux, thermochemical processes may take place as a result of radiationless deactivation of excited species, in addition to photochemical processes. This often occurs upon exposure of a heterogeneous photocatalyst to pulsed IR-light or pulsed laser emission."
synonym: "light-initiated thermocatalytic phenomena" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14101
name: turnover number
def: "Number of times\, \\\(n\\\)\, that the overall reaction \(the photochemical transformation\) goes through a photocatalytic cycle."
comment: "In heterogeneous photocatalysis it is the ratio of the number of photoinduced transformations for a given period of time to the number of active sites or photocatalytic centres in their ground state. Should the number of active sites be known, TON could be expressed per number of sites."
comment: "In homogeneous photocatalysis it is the ratio of the number of photoinduced transformations for a given period to the number of ground state photocatalyst entities."
comment: "It can be equally applied to homogeneous and heterogeneous systems."
comment: "TON is a number, whereas in traditional catalysis, e.g., enzyme kinetics, it is defined per certain time period."
comment: "TON is a quantity of dimension one attributed in photocatalysis to the overall system that contains the photocatalyst, even if, in heterogeneous photocatalysis, part of the photocatalyst active centres appears to be not irradiated."
synonym: "TON" RELATED OMO:0003012 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14018
consider: goldbook:14020

[Term]
id: goldbook:14102
name: V centres
def: "Trapped holes in semiconductors."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14002

[Term]
id: goldbook:14103
name: volatile organic chemical/compounds
def: "Term commonly used to designate organic gas-phase pollutants frequently degraded by photocatalytic procedures."
comment: "Often used for any organic compound with a vapor pressure higher than \(\pu{10 Pa}\) at \(\pu{T} = \pu{293 K}\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-09-36
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14104
name: absorbance assay
def: "Assay in which response is determined by the detection of light absorption by an assay component."
comment: "The concentration of the absorbing species can be quantified using the Beer–Lambert law (or Beer–Lambert–Bouguer law) \[A = \varepsilon cl = \lg\left(\frac{P_{0}}{P}\right)\] where \(\varepsilon\) is the molar absorption coefficient of the absorbing species, \(l\) is the absorption path length, and \(c\) the amount concentration. A sample is irradiated with a specific wavelength of light, typically in the ultraviolet or visible spectral region; the spectral radiant power of incident light on the sample (\(P_{0}\)) and transmitted radiation after passing through the sample (\(P\)) are used to calculate absorbance (\(A\))."
synonym: "optical density assay (obsolete)" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14105
name: active
def: "Sample that produces a response or signal above a defined threshold at a tested concentration in a single assay or screen."
comment: "When the properties and identity of an active or hit are confirmed by subsequent experiment it becomes a confirmed hit."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14123
consider: goldbook:14150
consider: goldbook:14189

[Term]
id: goldbook:14106
name: activity distribution
def: "Graphical representation of the number of samples present in each activity range."
comment: "Often shown as a frequency distribution graph, it provides an overview of the screening results and typically allows the determination of the overall background signal and threshold for selection of actives."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14105

[Term]
id: goldbook:14107
name: allosteric site
def: "Binding site on a protein\, distinct from the site recognized by an endogenous agonist \(i.e.\, the orthosteric site\) in the case of a receptor\, or substrate in the case of an enzyme\, that when occupied by a ligand effects a conformational change in the protein\, thus altering its signaling\, catalytic properties\, or its capability to interact with other macromolecules."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14173

[Term]
id: goldbook:14108
name: artifact
def: "Observation\, effect\, or result that is inaccurate because it is produced by the methodology used in scientific investigation or by experimental error."
synonym: "artefact" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14109
name: assay validation
def: "Experiments conducted to verify that the output measurements of the assay are consistently reflective of the activity against the target."
comment: "Results are compared internally over multiple runs and externally (when available) to existing literature parameters such as \(K_{\rm{d}}\), \(K_{\rm{i}}\), \(K_{\rm{m}}\), or \(\pu{EC_{50}}\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14110
name: automated device
def: "Device that performs one or more functions without human intervention by means of direct control or by programmed operations."
comment: "Often referred to as a peripheral or peripheral device when it is an instrument incorporated into a module, a workstation, or a fully automated system."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14159
consider: goldbook:14164
consider: goldbook:14202

[Term]
id: goldbook:14111
name: binding assay
def: "Assay in which the specific physical association or interaction between two molecules \(e.g.\, ligand–receptor\, antibody–antigen\, protein–protein\, ligand–transport protein\) is measured."
comment: "The assay can be homogeneous or heterogeneous, competitive or noncompetitive, and may be run at equilibrium or in kinetic mode. Appropriate assay controls and/or standard reagents are often needed to determine the specific binding in contrast to nonspecific adsorption processes."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14112
name: binding isotherm
def: "Graphical representation of the relationship between the amount of ligand that is specifically bound to a receptor per mass of binding material and the amount of free ligand."
comment: "A Scatchard plot was formerly used to determine \(B_{\rm{max}}\) and \(K_{\rm{d}}\). It is now more common to determine these parameters by nonlinear least-squares fitting to the binding equation given above."
comment: "For a system containing a single binding site and lacking cooperative interactions, the binding isotherm is described by \[B = B_{\max}[\ce{L}]/(K_{\rm{d}} + [\ce{L}])\] where \(\ce{B}\) is the amount of ligand specifically bound, \([\ce{L}]\) is the amount concentration of free ligand, \(K_{\rm{d}}\) is the equilibrium dissociation constant of the ligand, and \(B_{\rm{max}}\) is the maximal specific binding of a ligand. The equation describes a hyperbola with \(B_{\rm{max}}\) as the asymptote. \(B_{\rm{max}}\) is defined quantitatively as the amount of ligand bound per mass of binding material, with units, e.g., \(\pu{pmol mg-1}\)."
comment: "The total number of receptors or binding sites in the system is proportional to \(B_{\rm{max}}\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14113
name: cell-based assay
def: "Assay in which the response of live cells to a test sample is measured."
comment: "Assay parameters are controlled with respect to conditions such as \(\ce{CO2}\), \(\ce{O2}\), temperature, and \(\pu{pH}\)."
comment: "The type of cells, parameters measured, and detection systems used vary widely."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14119
consider: goldbook:14125
consider: goldbook:14141

[Term]
id: goldbook:14114
name: cell membrane preparation
def: "Preparation from tissue or cells\, in which the membranous elements\, generally the cell membrane\, are enriched in relation to other cellular constituents."
comment: "Cell membrane preparations are the standard biological material for receptor binding assays. Cell membranes are typically lipid-rich."
comment: "These are obtained by disruption of the cells and differential centrifugation or other techniques to isolate the desired components."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14115
name: cell mortality
def: "Ratio of counted dead cells to the total number of observed cells."
comment: "Dead cells are often counted using apoptosis or nuclear markers."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14116
name: cell viability
def: "Ratio of counted live cells to the total number of observed cells."
comment: "Live cells are often counted using nuclear, membrane, or metabolic markers."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14117
name: channel blocker
def: "Compound that reduces or eliminates the conductance of an ion channel by impeding the movement of ions through that channel."
comment: "Dihydropyridine antagonists such as nifedipine, which also block the conductance of some L-type calcium channels, are not classified as channel blockers since their action is to inhibit channel gating."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14118
name: channel opener
def: "Compound that increases the conductance of an ion channel by a mechanism other than alteration of membrane potential or activation of the cognate receptor."
synonym: "channel activator" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14119
name: chemotaxis assay
def: "Cell-based assay in which the rate and/or direction of cell migration is measured."
comment: "Typically, the response to a test sample is detected by means of a fluorescent probe or with label-free methods. Classical chemotaxis assays utilized the Boyden chamber and required manual counting of migrating cells under the microscope."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14120
name: combinatorial library
def: "Set of compounds prepared by combinatorial chemistry. It may consist of a collection of pools\, or sublibraries. Its composition may be described by the chemset notation."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14121
name: compound collection
def: "Set of chemicals that has been assembled and annotated for easy storage and retrieval and that is available for screening."
comment: "Generally consists of compounds synthesized by combinatorial or standard synthetic methods or purchased from commercial sources, or of natural products as pure samples. Compounds may be stored in dry powder form, or dissolved at a fixed concentration in a solvent, such as dimethylsulfoxide."
synonym: "compound deck" []
synonym: "compound library" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14122
name: concentration–response relationship
def: "Association between compound concentration and the resultant magnitude of the continuously graded change produced\, in an assay."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14132

[Term]
id: goldbook:14123
name: confirmed hit
def: "Sample that produces activity above the hit threshold in an assay and whose structural identity has been confirmed."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14124
name: counter-screen
def: "Screen in which test samples are assessed against a target for unwanted activity."
comment: "This target may or may not be structurally or functionally related to the intended target."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14125
name: cytotoxicity assay
def: "Cell-based assay in which damage to cell structure or function caused by a test sample is determined."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14115
consider: goldbook:14116

[Term]
id: goldbook:14126
name: efficacy
def: "Extent to which a compound activates a receptor to produce a response in an assay under saturating conditions."
comment: "Due to the common overexpression of receptors in screening assays, it is not always possible to detect differences in efficacy among full agonists. A more accurate assessment of relative efficacy may require systems with lower receptor expression where it is often found that one agonist may show partial agonist character."
comment: "Usually compared to results with the positive and negative assay controls. When the compound produces a maximal signal that is \(\pu{100\%}\) of that of the positive control, it is said to be a full agonist and has high efficacy. When the effect plateaus with increasing concentration to reach an intermediary level of activity, the compound is said to be a partial agonist with lower efficacy."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14174

[Term]
id: goldbook:14127
name: electrochemiluminescence assay
def: "Assay in which light emitted by electrogenerated luminescence is detected and used to quantify a label\, e.g.\, ruthenium\(II\) tris-\(bipyridine\)\, on one of the two assay binding components."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14128
name: end-point assay
def: "Kinetic assay run for a set constant incubation time. Typically\, at the end of the set incubation time\, a reagent that stops the reaction is added to allow postponed measurement of the signal."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14130
consider: goldbook:14152

[Term]
id: goldbook:14129
name: epitope mapping
def: "Identification and localization of the specific regions of protein molecules that are recognized by the immune system."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14130
name: equilibrium assay
def: "Assay in which there is sufficient incubation time for the plateau phase of the signal to be reached and equilibrium has been established between the reactants."
comment: "At this point, the signal is time-independent."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14128
consider: goldbook:14152

[Term]
id: goldbook:14131
name: equilibrium association constant
def: "Ratio\, at equilibrium\, of \\\(\[\\ce\{AB\}\]/\[\\ce\{A\}\]\[\\ce\{B\}\]\\\) for the reversible binding interaction of \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\) to yield the complex \\\(\\ce\{AB\}\\\)."
comment: "Equal to \(1/K_{\rm{d}}\), or \(k_{\rm{on}}/k_{\rm{off}}\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14132
name: equilibrium dissociation constant
def: "Ratio\, at equilibrium\, of \\\(\[\\ce\{A\}\]\[\\ce\{B\}\]/\[\\ce\{AB\}\]\\\)\, for the reversible binding interaction of \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\) to yield the complex \\\(\\ce\{AB\}\\\)."
comment: "Reciprocal of \(K_{\rm{a}}\) (\(K_{\rm{d}} = 1/K_{\rm{a}}\)), equal to \(k_{\rm{off}}/k_{\rm{on}}\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14133
name: flow cytometry assay
def: "Cell-based assay in which the response is dependent on the detection of the phenotype of cells as determined through the light-scattering properties of each cell\, and\, typically\, the fluorescence intensity of a relevant probe."
comment: "Flow cytometry has been adapted to fluorescently labeled bead-based assays."
comment: "In fluorescence-activated cell sorting, single cells are rapidly passed through a channel where they are optically analyzed and separated based on the specific light-scattering and fluorescent characteristics of each individual cell."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14113

[Term]
id: goldbook:14134
name: fluorescence correlation assay
def: "Assay in which fluctuations in fluorescence intensity are detected and used to characterize a species that is labeled with a fluorescent probe."
comment: "The fluctuations are measured for a small volume of the total assay sample and are attributed to local diffusion of the probe in the detected volume or interactions that change the quantum yield of fluorescence."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14135
name: fluorescence polarisation
def: "Dimensionless value determined from the fluorescence intensities \(\\\(I\\\)\) measured parallel \(\\\(I_\{\\Vert\}\\\)\) and perpendicular \(\\\(I_\{\\perp\}\\\)\) to the excitation plane\: \\\[p = \\frac\{I_\{\\Vert\} - I_\{\\perp\}\}\{I_\{\\Vert\} + I_\{\\perp\}\}\\\]"
comment: "Fluorescence anisotropy (\(r\)) differs only in normalization and is determined from \[r = (I_{\Vert} - I_{\perp})/(I_{\Vert} + 2I_{\perp})\]"
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14136

[Term]
id: goldbook:14136
name: fluorescence polarisation assay
def: "Assay in which the association between an intrinsically fluorescent ligand\, or a ligand linked to a fluorescent probe\, and a larger macromolecule is detected."
comment: "The degree to which the labeled ligand depolarizes plane-polarized light is directly proportional to the rate at which it tumbles in solution. Tumbling will be slower when the ligand is associated with the larger macromolecule as compared to free in solution, consequently resulting in a lower degree of depolarization."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14135
consider: goldbook:14147

[Term]
id: goldbook:14137
name: fluorescence resonance energy transfer assay
def: "Assay based on the nonradiative energy transfer that occurs when the emission spectrum of a fluorescent donor molecule overlaps with the absorption spectrum of an energy acceptor molecule."
comment: "The donor and the acceptor fluorophores are linked directly or indirectly to two binding partners, and energy transfer occurs when the fluorophores are in close proximity with proper alignment of donor and acceptor electronic dipoles, i.e., when the binding partners interact."
comment: "The fluorophores can be either of small molecule or protein nature."
comment: "Use of a donor with a long fluorescence lifetime allows time-delayed (or time-resolved) fluorescence measurement (TR-FRET), providing conditions where other fluorophores in the assay medium are not detected."
synonym: "FRET" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
synonym: "FRET assay" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14138
name: functional assay
def: "Assay in which the biological or physiological activity of the target is measured."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14139
name: heterogeneous assay
def: "Assay in which the response is detected only after the physical separation by methods such as filtration or centrifugation of one or more assay components."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14140
consider: goldbook:14146

[Term]
id: goldbook:14140
name: heterogeneous binding assay
def: "Assay in which one binding component is immobilized to a surface \(e.g.\, cellular membrane\, glass\, gold\, bead\, or biopolymer layer\) while its binding partner is freely diffusible."
comment: "The extent of interaction between the binding partners is determined after their physical separation, which is usually achieved by filtration, centrifugation, magnetic field, or chromatography."
comment: "Used to qualitatively discriminate specific and non-specific interaction or quantitatively determine the concentration of a component part of a mixture."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14141
name: high-content screening assay
def: "A cell-based assay that produces multiple biological readouts."
comment: "Most commonly used in relation to the mathematical (quantitative) analysis of an image acquired using an automated microscope. Analysis algorithms are used to quantify cellular parameters (e.g., number, motility, neurite outgrowth, size, shape) and subcellular events (e.g., receptor internalization, protein translocation, protein expression nuclei shape)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14113

[Term]
id: goldbook:14142
name: high-performance liquid chromatography assay
def: "Assay in which response is determined after the physical separation of assay components using high-performance liquid chromatography \(HPLC\) with concurrent detection\, usually by monitoring absorbance at an appropriate wavelength."
comment: "Changes in the concentration of assay components at one or more times over the course of the assay can be determined by the relative area under the detection peaks as compared to assay controls and standards."
synonym: "HPLC" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
synonym: "HPLC Assay" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14143
name: high throughput
def: "Relative term\, applied to the generation of a large number of results in a short timeframe \(e.g.\, \\\(\\pu\{E5\}\\\) in a week or a month\)."
comment: "Usually achieved by employing some degree of automation."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14200

[Term]
id: goldbook:14144
name: hit rate
def: "Proportion of hits from a screen that displays confirmed activity beyond a minimum defined level\, the hit threshold. It is expressed as a percentage of the number of samples screened."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14145

[Term]
id: goldbook:14145
name: hit threshold
def: "Minimum activity that defines actives in a primary screen."
comment: "A widely used hit threshold is \(\pu{50\%}\) inhibition. A more statistically meaningful and equally common threshold is three standard deviations from the mean."
comment: "It is usually expressed as percentage of inhibition or stimulation relative to assay controls."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14105
consider: goldbook:14184

[Term]
id: goldbook:14146
name: homogeneous assay
def: "Assay in which all reagents and reactants are of a uniform phase \(typically\, liquid phase\) and assay response is detected without the need for physical separation of assay components."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14139

[Term]
id: goldbook:14147
name: homogeneous binding assay
def: "Single-phase system used to qualitatively discriminate between specific and nonspecific interactions or quantitatively determine the concentration of a component part of a mixture."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14136

[Term]
id: goldbook:14148
name: image-based assay
def: "Assay in which image analysis algorithms that determine the compartmentalization of fluorescent probes give topological as well as quantitative information."
comment: "The use of automated cellular imaging for screening has been made possible by the development of digital imaging technology."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14141

[Term]
id: goldbook:14149
name: immobilised metal ion affinity-based fluorescence polarisation assay
def: "Assay in which the interaction between nanoparticles coated with triply charged metal ions and phosphate groups on a substrate that carries a fluorescent tag is detected."
comment: "The phosphorylated fluorescent substrate bound to the nanoparticle will exhibit high fluorescence polarization relative to the unbound (unphosphorylated) peptide substrate."
comment: "Used for kinase assays where a phosphate group is added to a peptide substrate or for phosphatase assays where the phosphate is removed from a peptide substrate."
synonym: "IMAP™" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
synonym: "IMAP™ assay" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14135

[Term]
id: goldbook:14150
name: inactive
def: "Sample that\, at the tested concentration\, does not produce a response above the hit threshold in an assay."
comment: "A sample may also be designated as inactive when attempts to confirm an active fail."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14105
consider: goldbook:14108

[Term]
id: goldbook:14151
name: inverse agonist
def: "Ligand that decreases signaling through a receptor below the level of constitutive activity."
comment: "Several receptors, especially when expressed at high levels, exhibit constitutively active signaling. Demonstrating an inverse agonist effect is dependent upon the constitutive activity of the particular receptor which is manifested in the absence of a conventional agonist. Upwards of \(\pu{85\%}\) of antagonists identified in screens of GPCRs are actually inverse agonists."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14174

[Term]
id: goldbook:14152
name: kinetic assay
def: "Assay in which the time dependence of the signal intensity is measured. Signal values are typically acquired at several time intervals in order to calculate kinetic parameters."
comment: "Kinetic assays are generally designed such that the change in signal with time is linear throughout the experiment."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14128
consider: goldbook:14130

[Term]
id: goldbook:14153
name: label-free assay
def: "Assay in which there is no requirement to modify one of the interacting components in order to facilitate signal detection."
comment: "Used to monitor macromolecule/macromolecule or small-molecule/macromolecule binding, as well as cell adhesion or cell signaling."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14154
name: label-free detection
def: "Direct detection of compound activity on a target without the requirement for a labeled reagent."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14155
name: lead identification
def: "Process that is targeted toward the generation of at least one compound series that meets the requirements for progression to lead optimization."
comment: "Typically, it encompasses the steps from the detection of initial activity (via high-throughput screening and other lead-finding activities) through hit confirmation and hit-to-lead activities."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14156
name: lead optimisation
def: "Process in which the drug-like properties of an initial lead or lead series are improved."
comment: "Typically, biological activity will be enhanced, in vivo efficacy will be demonstrated, and compounds with a physicochemical, pharmacological, and toxicological profile consistent with progression to the clinic will be identified."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14157
name: library
def: "Collection of samples \(e.g.\, chemical compounds\, natural products\, over-expression library of a microbe\) available for screening. Set of compounds produced through combinatorial chemistry or other means that expands around a single core structure."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14158
name: ligand-activated channel
def: "Any ion channel that is gated \(i.e.\, opened or closed\) by a ligand."
comment: "Distinct from voltage-gated or stretch-activated ion channels."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14201

[Term]
id: goldbook:14159
name: liquid handler
def: "Automated device that accurately and precisely delivers programmed\, predefined quantities of liquid to a microplate."
comment: "It may be free-standing or incorporated as a workstation into a fully automated system."
synonym: "liquid handling machine" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14110
consider: goldbook:14161
consider: goldbook:14202

[Term]
id: goldbook:14160
name: luminescence assay
def: "Assay in which response is measured by the detection of light of a nonradiative origin\, such as bioluminescence or chemiluminescence."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14161
name: microplate
def: "Any of a number of plates containing a series of wells which may be used for storage or to perform individual assays."
comment: "Typically, these plates are constructed of a variety of clear and opaque plastics, and can contain from 6 to 3456 individual wells."
comment: "White plates are typically used for luminescence, assay black plates for fluorescence assays, and clear plates for absorbance assays."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14162
consider: goldbook:14176

[Term]
id: goldbook:14162
name: microplate standards
def: "Defined footprint\, height\, flanges\, and well positions for 96-\, 384-\, and 1536-well microplates."
comment: "These standards have been established by the Society for Biomolecular Sciences (SBS) and the American National Standards Institute (ANSI) and accredited by ANSI. They are numbered as ANSI-SBS-1 (2004) – Microplate footprint; ANSI-SBS-2 (2004) – Microplate Height; ANSI-SBS-3 (2004) – Microplate Flange; and ANSI-SBS-4 (2004) – Microplate Well Positions."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14161

[Term]
id: goldbook:14163
name: miniaturisation
def: "Experimental design aimed at decreasing the reaction volume of an assay and consequently the amount and cost of reagents."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14164
name: module
def: "Individual automated device within a fully automated assay system that usually performs a complete single assay step or procedure."
comment: "A fully enclosed module may allow for the control of temperature, humidity, and the gaseous environment."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14165
name: molecular imprinted polymers
def: "Synthetic polymers in which well-defined volume cavities \(imprints\) created by template molecules mimic a biomolecular specific ligand–receptor interaction."
comment: "MIPs have found uses as stationary phases in chromatography, as recognition elements in chemosensors, and as enzyme mimics in catalysis."
comment: "These artificially generated recognition sites have shapes, sizes, and functionalities complementary to the template molecule, and are capable of rebinding the template molecule in preference to other closely related structures."
synonym: "MIPs" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14166
name: multiplex assay
def: "Assay in which the activity of a single sample against more than one target is measured in a single test."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14167
name: natural product
def: "Complex mixture derived from natural \(biological\) sources used in screening as a resource for identification of lead compounds. Pure compound of natural \(biological\) origin \(whether obtained by purification of natural mixtures or by laboratory synthesis\)\, as opposed to compounds originating from synthetic chemistry.Note\: Typical origins include extracts from microbes and higher organisms from terrestrial or marine environments."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14168
name: negative assay control
def: "Experimental conditions designed to produce the signal reflective of the absence of the process under investigation."
comment: "For a binding or an activation mechanism, it is typically the signal measured in the absence of a test compound and is often relevant to the determination of the background signal. For an inhibitory or antagonist mechanism, it is the maximal signal, obtained in the absence of the reference inhibitor."
synonym: "low control" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14169
name: noncompetitive binding assay
def: "Assay in which the interaction of a ligand with a receptor is measured without a requirement for added competing agents. It can be homogeneous or heterogeneous."
synonym: "indirect binding assay" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14170
name: nuclease assay
def: "Method used to determine the presence or quantify the amount of specific target nucleotide sequences."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14171
name: off-rate
def: "Rate of dissociation of the enzyme-substrate \(\\\(\\ce\{ES\}\\\)\) complex to enzyme \(\\\(\\ce\{E\}\\\)\) and substrate \(\\\(\\ce\{S\}\\\)\) or of ligand–receptor \(\\\(\\ce\{LR\}\\\)\) to receptor \(\\\(\\ce\{R\}\\\)\) and ligand \(\\\(\\ce\{L\}\\\)\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14172
name: on-rate
def: "Rate of formation of the enzyme-substrate \(\\\(\\ce\{ES\}\\\)\) or ligand–receptor \(\\\(\\ce\{LR\}\\\)\) complex."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14173
name: orthosteric site
def: "Binding site recognized by the endogenous agonist of a receptor."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14107

[Term]
id: goldbook:14174
name: partial agonist
def: "Agonist that is unable to induce a maximal receptor response in a given biological assay\, regardless of the amount applied."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14126

[Term]
id: goldbook:14175
name: pipettor
def: "Device that aspirates and dispenses liquid \(\\\(\\pu\{nL\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{mL\}\\\) range\) for the purposes of transferring from a source to destination."
comment: "The device may be fully manual, electronic, single-channel, multi-channel (typically 8- or 12-channel), or part of a liquid handler."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14159

[Term]
id: goldbook:14176
name: plate format
def: "Number and configuration of wells on a microplate."
comment: "The most widely used formats are arrays of 96 wells (8 by 12) or 384 wells (16 by 24) or 1536 wells (32 by 48)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14161
consider: goldbook:14162

[Term]
id: goldbook:14177
name: plate gripper
def: "Handling device that positions microplates on a workstation."
comment: "A plate gripper may also be used to move microplates between independent modules of an automated platform. Often used in combination with plate stackers, washers, and readers."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14161

[Term]
id: goldbook:14178
name: plate map
def: "Layout of samples and controls configured on a plate during an assay."
comment: "For a primary screen in 384-well plates, columns 1, 2, and/or 23 and 24 are controls, and the remaining columns are for individual test compounds. For secondary screening, more complex layouts are used and each row may contain a single compound at varying concentrations."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14179
name: plate reader
def: "Automated device that uses optical and/or computer vision techniques to detect biological\, chemical\, or physical events in samples stored in microplates."
comment: "An absorbance plate reader measures color intensity in each well."
comment: "Often used in conjunction with a plate stacker."
comment: "Use of plate readers reduces or eliminates human subjectivity in the evaluation of plate contents."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14110
consider: goldbook:14180

[Term]
id: goldbook:14180
name: plate stacker
def: "Automated device that loads\, unloads\, and restacks microplates."
comment: "The device is usually part of system that integrates a plate reader, a liquid handler, or other device to minimize manual microplate handling."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14110

[Term]
id: goldbook:14181
name: plate washer
def: "Automated device used to wash the wells of microplates."
comment: "The device usually has functions that allow precise height adjustment for minimized residual volume, and digitally controlled aspiration or dispensing pumps to provide high accuracy and flow control."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14182
name: positive assay control
def: "Experimental conditions designed to produce the signal reflective of maximum biological effect in an assay."
comment: "For a binding or an activation mechanism, it is typically the signal measured in the presence of a test compound. For an inhibitory or antagonist mechanism, it is the minimum signal, obtained in the absence of the reference inhibitor."
synonym: "high control" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14183
name: potency
def: "Dose of drug required to produce a specific effect of given intensity as compared to a standard reference."
comment: "More potent compounds have lower \(\pu{IC_{50}}\) or \(\pu{EC_{50}}\) values implying that less is needed for an effect."
comment: "Potency is a comparative rather than an absolute expression of drug activity. Drug potency depends on both affinity and efficacy. Thus, two agonists can be equipotent, but have different intrinsic efficacies with compensating differences in affinity."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14126

[Term]
id: goldbook:14184
name: primary screen
def: "Initial screen applied to assess the activity of a collection of compounds and identify hits or actives against a biological target of interest."
comment: "This screen identifies hits or actives from a library."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14105
consider: goldbook:14157
consider: goldbook:14191

[Term]
id: goldbook:14185
name: radioligand
def: "Ligand into which a radioisotope has been incorporated as a label."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14186
name: robot
def: "Automated device that performs tasks \(i.e.\, screen functions\) that would normally be performed by a human."
comment: "A robot in laboratory automation is usually used to move microplates in an automated assay procedure and usually consists of either a robotic arm with at least three degrees of freedom or a plate gripper."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14161
consider: goldbook:14177
consider: goldbook:14189

[Term]
id: goldbook:14187
name: scheduling software
def: "Computer program used to organize the chronological sequence of experimental methods to be followed to execute an automated high-throughput screening campaign."
comment: "Based on the experimental needs of the campaign and constraints imposed by the programmer, this software uses an iterative process to determine the optimal order of the use of robots, liquid handlers, and modules to minimize screening time."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14188
name: scintillation proximity assay
def: "Homogeneous assay in which beads incorporated with scintillant emit light in the presence of radiolabeled molecules within the proximity of the bead’s scintillant detection."
comment: "An alternative form of the assay incorporates the scintillant into the base of a microplate instead of a bead (FlashPlate™)."
synonym: "SPA™" RELATED OMO:0003012 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14146

[Term]
id: goldbook:14189
name: screen
def: "Execution\, analysis\, and interpretation of a large number of assays to evaluate the activity of a collection of samples against a target."
comment: "A screen will often employ automation."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14190
name: screen validation
def: "Assay conditions\, as determined by assay validation\, are performed in the chosen plate format with an acceptable signal to background ratio as described by the Z′ factor."
comment: "Validation typically requires testing a number of samples a minimum of three times and allows for the estimation of false positives, false negatives, and the selection of statistically significant thresholds."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14109
consider: goldbook:14176

[Term]
id: goldbook:14191
name: secondary screen
def: "Screen applied to independently confirm actives from the primary screen."
comment: "A secondary screen may employ an assay that differs in type from the primary screen (e.g., biochemical assay vs. cell-based assay) or it may be of the same type with different readout."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14192
name: selectivity assay
def: "Assay used to determine the relative potency of active or lead compounds toward an alternative target."
comment: "A selectivity assay (or panel of assays) may include targets of the same family or unrelated targets."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14124

[Term]
id: goldbook:14193
name: stably transfected cells
def: "Eukaryotic cells into which recombinant DNA has been introduced and incorporated into the genome\, so that the cells replicate the new DNA in a stable fashion."
comment: "The biological material thus obtained is expected to show low variability and therefore increase assay reproducibility."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14194
name: standard error of the mean
def: "Sample standard deviation divided by the square root of the sample size."
synonym: "SEM" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14195
name: sublibrary
def: "Portion of a library that is grouped or selected based on similarity of structure or biological effect."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14157

[Term]
id: goldbook:14196
name: surface plasmon resonance assay
def: "Assay in which the binding of a ligand to a target immobilized on the gold surface of a chip is detected by changes in the angle of the light reflected from the surface."
comment: "This assay type is not usually used for high-throughput screening but has been expanded to multiple simultaneous detection channels and has improved sensitivity to detect low-molecular-weight binding molecules."
synonym: "SPR" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
synonym: "SPR Assay" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14197
name: targeted library
def: "Library designed\, on the basis of preexisting information\, to generate enhanced activity or hit rate against a particular biological target or target class."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14198
name: total internal reflection fluorescence
def: "Spectroscopic technique that discriminates between labeled molecules in the homogeneous phase and those interacting with binding partners immobilized at a surface."
comment: "TIRF measurements are restricted to a thin layer and are achieved by exciting the fluorophore with an evanescent wave, created by total internal reflection at the glass/aqueous interface of the wave guide. The goal of using TIRF in biological applications is to study events close to the interface of two different media."
synonym: "TIRF" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14199
name: transiently transfected cells
def: "Eukaryotic cells into which recombinant DNA has been introduced and expressed by cellular transcription\, but without incorporation into the stable genome."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14200
name: ultra-high throughput
def: "Relative term\, currently applied to the screening of \\\(\\pu\{E5\}\\\) test samples in a \\\(\\pu\{24\\!\\mbox\{-\}h\}\\\) period."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14143

[Term]
id: goldbook:14201
name: voltage-activated channel
def: "Ion channel that is specifically activated\, or gated\, by the surrounding potential difference across the cell membrane near the channel \(or near the cell\, neuron\, or synapse\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14202
name: workstation
def: "Programmable device used to automate a single operation \(such as liquid handling\) on a microplate supplied to the device by a robot \(automated stacker system or a robotic manipulator\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14161
consider: goldbook:14186

[Term]
id: goldbook:14203
name: Z factor
def: "Dimensionless statistical parameter that provides a practical assessment of assay performance in the presence of test compounds. \\\[Z = 1 - 3\(\\sigma_\{\\rm\{s\}\} + \\sigma_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\)/\\vert\\mu_\{\\rm\{s\}\} - \\mu_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\vert\\\] where \\\(\\mu_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\) denotes the mean of the library sample signal\, \\\(\\mu_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\) the mean of the control signal and \\\(\\sigma_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\)\, \\\(\\sigma_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\) the corresponding standard deviations. \\\(1 \\gt Z \\ge 0.5\\\) generally categorizes an excellent assay."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14204
name: Z′ factor
def: "Dimensionless statistical parameter used extensively in biomolecular screening\, and a characteristic of an assay without the intervention of test compounds. It provides a practical index of the quality and reliability of the assay\, and can be used as a guide to assay development and optimization. \\\[Z' = 1 - 3\(\\sigma_\{\\rm\{c^\{+\}\}\} + \\sigma_\{\\rm\{c^\{-\}\}\}\)/\(\\vert\\mu_\{\\rm\{c^\{+\}\}\} - \\mu_\{\\rm\{c^\{-\}\}\}\\vert\)\\\] where \\\(\\sigma_\{\\rm\{c^\{+\}\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\sigma_\{\\rm\{c^\{-\}\}\}\\\) are the standard deviations of the high and low controls and \\\(\\mu_\{\\rm\{c^\{+\}\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\mu_\{\\rm\{c^\{-\}\}\}\\\) are the means of the high and low controls\, respectively. A graphical illustration of the parameters used in the calculation of \\\(Z'\\\) is provided below."
comment: "The useful range of \(Z'\) values is from 0 (very poor) to +1 (excellent). In most cases, a \(Z'\) factor greater than \(\pu{0.5}\) is required for an assay to be accepted for high-throughput screening."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-09-05-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14205
name: two-phase model
def: "Model based on the assumption that the polymer sample consists only of domains of a crystalline phase and of an amorphous phase."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14206
name: axial advancement per constitutional repeating unit
def: "Projected length of a constitutional repeating unit \(CRU\) on the chain axis."
comment: "The axial advancement per CRU is characteristic of the conformation of a polymer chain. A large axial advancement per CRU is said to define a more extended conformation and a small axial advancement per CRU is said to define a more contracted conformation."
comment: "The value of \(h\) is obtained from the equation \[h = \frac{c}{M_{\rm{R}}}\] where \(c\) is the chain identity period and \(M_{\rm{R}}\) is the number of CRUs in \(c\). In cases where the equivalence postulate does not apply, \(h\) is an average value."
synonym: "unit height" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14207
name: directional chain
def: "Polymer chain that may be oriented in two distinguishable\, opposite directions along the chain axis."
comment: "The two possible chain orientations can be mutually interconverted through a rotation around a twofold axis orthogonal to the chain axis."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14208
name: directional stem
def: "Stem of a directional chain."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14207

[Term]
id: goldbook:14209
name: helix pitch
def: "Distance between successive helical windings projected on the chain axis."
comment: "The value of \(p\) is obtained from the equation \[p = \frac{c}{N}\] where \(c\) is the chain identity period and \(N\) is the number of helical windings in \(c\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14210
name: ideal crystalline chain conformation
def: "Minimum energy conformation of an isolated polymer chain constructed according to the equivalence postulate and approximating the conformation found in a crystalline phase."
comment: "If the symmetry elements present in the crystal do not correspond with those that characterize the ideal crystalline chain conformation, the chain conformation may be distorted in the crystal."
comment: "Symmetry elements characterizing the ideal crystalline chain conformation may be noncrystallographic (see rod group), i.e., they may not apply to the packing in certain crystal polymorphs. For example, the ideal crystalline chain conformation of isotactic polypropylene is \(3_{1}\) helix but, while the \(\ce{\upbeta\mbox{-}phase}\) crystal structure allows for the helical \(3_{1}\) symmetry operator, the \(\ce{\upalpha\mbox{-}}\) or the \(\ce{\upgamma\mbox{-}phase}\) of this polymer do not."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14214
consider: goldbook:14219

[Term]
id: goldbook:14211
name: left-handed helix
def: "Helix in which the continuous helical line\, approximating the sequence of bonds along the polymer main chain\, winds clockwise moving towards the observer\, i.e.\, seen from the positive axis direction \(see the figures in chain identity period\)."
comment: "In compliance with prevailing stereochemical terminology conventions the descriptor \(M\) (minus) is recommended rather than \(L\), presently widely used in polymer literature."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14213

[Term]
id: goldbook:14212
name: multiple helix
def: "Helical arrangement comprising two or more intertwined helical chains sharing the same axis."
comment: "The intertwined helical chains are usually identical and displaced along the chain axis direction by \(c/n\), where \(c\) is the chain identity period of the single helix and \(n\) the number of helices in the multiple helix."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14213
name: right-handed helix
def: "Helix in which the continuous helical line\, approximating the sequence of bonds along the polymer main chain\, winds anticlockwise moving towards the observer\, i.e.\, seen from the positive axis direction \(see the figures in chain identity period\)."
comment: "In compliance with prevailing stereochemical terminology conventions, the descriptor \(P\) (plus) is recommended rather than \(R\), presently widely used in polymer literature."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14211

[Term]
id: goldbook:14214
name: rod group
def: "Symmetry group of a three-dimensional array of atoms\, regularly repeating along one direction."
comment: "Details concerning the symbols used are explained in crystallographic literature can be found in the recommendation."
comment: "For reasons related to space filling, in the case of crystallographic rod groups, only two-, three-, four-, and sixfold screw axes are taken into consideration. Such limitations do not exist in the case of isolated chains for which any helical symmetry may, in principle, be acceptable. In other words, rod groups in are a subset of those applicable to ideal crystalline chain conformations."
comment: "The symmetry of isolated polymer chains, with translational periodicity along the chain axis direction, may be described by either the rod group, or the equivalent line-repetition-group approach. Whereas the line-repetition-group description has been developed in the polymer science community, rod groups originate in the crystallographic community and are more systematic. Table 1 shows the symmetry group symbols of some linear polymers according to both the rod-group and the line-repetitiongroup description."
synonym: "line repetition group" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14210

[Term]
id: goldbook:14215
name: rotation per helix residue
def: "Angle of rotation about the chain axis per helix residue."
comment: "The value of \(t\) is obtained from the equation \[t = \frac{2\pi N}{M}\] where \(N\) is the number of helix turns in a chain identity period \(c\) and \(M\) is the number of helix residues in \(c\). In cases where the equivalence postulate does not apply, \(t\) is an average value."
synonym: "unit twist" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14216
name: translational chain repeating unit
def: "Smallest structural unit of a polymer chain that is repeated by translation in the crystal lattice along the chain axis."
comment: "The translational chain repeating unit may differ for different crystal polymorphs of a given crystalline polymer."
synonym: "fiber repeat unit" []
synonym: "physical repeating unit" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14219

[Term]
id: goldbook:14217
name: anticlined stems
def: "Stems in a crystal having opposite side-group orientations with respect to the chain axis \(see \(a\) in figure\).\(a\) Pairs of anticlined stems and \(b\) pairs of isoclined stems of regioregular poly\(3-butylthiophene\)\, i.e.\, poly\(3-n-butylthiophene-2\,5-diyl\). Since regioregular poly\(3-n-butylthiophene-2\,5-diyl\) is also a directional chain\, \(a\) is an example of antidirectional packing of two chains\, while \(b\) is an example of isodirectional packing."
comment: "Anticlined stems occur in crystalline poly-α-olefins, and also in regioregular poly(3-alkylthiophenes), polyphosphazenes, and polysiloxanes."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14207
consider: goldbook:14218
consider: goldbook:14222
consider: goldbook:14223

[Term]
id: goldbook:14218
name: antidirectional packing
def: "In a parallel-chain crystal\, the crystalline packing of directional stems with opposite chain axis orientations."
comment: "Usually, stems of opposite orientations occur in equal numbers in parallel-chain crystals."
synonym: "antiparallel packing" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14208

[Term]
id: goldbook:14219
name: crystal polymorph
def: "One of the different crystalline phases in which a given substance is able to crystallize."
comment: "A given polymer in its different crystal polymorphs may display the same conformation and the same chain identity period, but possibly involve packing of isochiral only or of enantiomeric helices (e.g., isotactic polypropylene in its \(\ce{\upalpha\mbox{-}form}\), \(\ce{\upbeta\mbox{-}form}\), and \(\ce{\upgamma\mbox{-}form}\); syndiotactic polypropylene, forms I and II. Polymorphs are, in such cases, isoconformational, and polymorphism is determined only by different modes of packing."
comment: "Different polymer crystal polymorphs are characterized by different crystal lattices and different packing of stems."
comment: "In other cases, along with the packing, the local conformations of chains are different in its different crystal polymorphs. The chain identity period and the symmetry will also differ (e.g., isotactic poly(but-1-ene), crystal forms I, II, and III; syndiotactic polypropylene, crystalline forms I, III, and IV)."
synonym: "crystal modification" []
synonym: "crystalline form" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14220
name: frustration
def: "Feature of crystalline packing characterized by different crystalline arrangements of chain stems at two \(or more\) sites that would be expected to be equivalent."
comment: "The term "frustration" signifies that some chains can achieve the minimum energycconformation or an optimal packing arrangement, while others cannot."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14221
name: isochiral stem packing
def: "Crystalline packing of stems having the same helicity."
comment: "The term is applicable to macromolecules that are chiral or adopt a chiral conformation in the crystal (e.g., a helical conformation)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14222
name: isoclined stems
def: "Stems in a crystal having the same side-group orientation with respect to the chain axis \(see figure \(b\) in anticlined stems\)."
comment: "Isoclined stems occur, for example, in crystalline poly-α-olefins, regioregular poly(3-alkylthiophenes), polyphosphazenes, and polysiloxanes."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14217

[Term]
id: goldbook:14223
name: isodirectional packing
def: "In a parallel-chain crystal\, the crystalline packing of directional stems with the same chain axis orientation."
comment: "Crystal packings in which all chains are isodirectional are very unlikely in the case of synthetic polymers except in as-polymerized systems, provided a directional extendedchain crystallization process immediately follows polymerization."
comment: "The term "isodirectional packing" is less ambiguous than the more common "parallel packing". The latter is often used in the description of crystalline polymorphs of natural polymers (e.g., cellulose I)."
synonym: "parallel packing" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14208

[Term]
id: goldbook:14224
name: packing of chain stems
def: "Spatial arrangement of polymer chain stems in a polymer crystal."
comment: "Crystals require tridimensional translational periodicity in their packing."
comment: "The mode of packing is influenced by the kinetics and thermodynamics of the crystallization process. In some cases, pre-existing structural organization and chain folding may also play a role."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14225
name: polymorphic polymer crystal
def: "Polymer crystal formed by domains of different crystal polymorphs of the same polymer."
comment: "Isotactic polypropylene has been shown to form crystals containing \(\ce{\upalpha\mbox{-}}\) and \(\ce{\upgamma\mbox{-}phase}\) domains in which well-defined relationships between the lattices of the two phases in different domains are preserved."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14219

[Term]
id: goldbook:14226
name: racemic packing
def: "Packing in a polymer crystal in which stems of opposite helicity coexist in equal numbers."
comment: "Applicable to macromolecules that are chiral or adopt chiral conformations in the crystal. One example of the former case is the crystal structure in which poly(ʟ-lactide) and poly(ᴅ-lactide) stems coexist in equal numbers, i.e., the so-called polylactide stereocomplex. The second case is much more frequent: it applies to most isotactic vinyl polymer crystal polymorphs where the polymer chains are not intrinsically chiral but adopt helical, and thus chiral conformations. Left-handed helices and right-handed helices are, however, isoenergetic and equiprobable and coexist in equal numbers, often giving rise to centrosymmetric crystals."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14211
consider: goldbook:14213
consider: goldbook:14219

[Term]
id: goldbook:14227
name: symmetry breaking
def: "Vanishing\, in a specific crystal polymorph\, of selected symmetry elements present in other crystal polymorphs\, or in selected minimum energy conformations of isolated molecules of a given substance."
comment: "Crystal polymorphs affected by symmetry breaking are usually stable at low temperature under conditions that optimize packing energy."
comment: "The symmetry elements that vanish are frequently intramolecular. Symmetry breaking in polymers in such cases leads to deviations from the ideal crystalline chain conformation and from the equivalence postulate."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14210
consider: goldbook:14219

[Term]
id: goldbook:14228
name: orientational disorder
def: "Structural disorder at given crystallographic positions\, resulting from the statistical coexistence within crystals of directional stems with opposite orientations."
comment: "A typical example is provided by the statistical coexistence of chains with opposite orientations at each crystallographic position in the crystal structures of poly-α-olefins, for example, in the \(\ce{\upalpha\mbox{-}form}\) of polypropylene."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14208

[Term]
id: goldbook:14229
name: paracrystal
def: "Crystal model characterized by lattice distortions and hence by limited order along given lattice directions."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14230
name: paracrystalline disorder of the first kind
def: "Structural disorder in a paracrystal\, resulting from uncorrelated displacements of the atoms around their average positions."
comment: "Displacements are generally due to thermal vibrations."
synonym: "uncorrelated paracrystalline disorder" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14229

[Term]
id: goldbook:14231
name: paracrystalline disorder of the second kind
def: "Structural disorder in a paracrystal\, resulting from permanent atomic displacements from the ordered crystallographic positions."
synonym: "correlated paracrystalline disorder" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14229

[Term]
id: goldbook:14232
name: paracrystalline distortion factor
def: "Ratio of the statistical fluctuation \\\(\\Delta a_\{ik\}\\\) of a lattice vector \\\(a_\{i\}\\\) along the direction \\\(a_\{k\}\\\) \\\[g_\{ik\} = \\frac\{\\Delta a_\{ik\}\}\{a_\{k\}\}\\\]"
comment: "Values of \(g\) range from \(\pu{0}\) (distortion-free crystal) to \(\pu{0.03}\) (typical bulk polymer), to \(\pu{0.12}\) (\(\ce{SiO2}\) glass) to \(\pu{1}\) (Boltzmann gas)."
comment: "\(g\) can be determined from measurements of the integral breadth of successive orders of Bragg reflections [18b]."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14233
name: crystalline polymer-inclusion complex
def: "Crystal comprising a polymer guest within a low-molar-mass host."
comment: "A number of different crystalline polymer-inclusion complexes may be obtained, for example, by the crystallization of polyethylene, 1,4-polybutadiene, and other polymers within urea, perhydrotriphenylene, or cyclotriphosphazene crystals."
comment: "The stems in polymer inclusion complexes are generally isolated. They are often not conformationally ordered."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14234
name: crystalline-polymer molecular complex
def: "Crystalline molecular complex between different polymers."
comment: "Very few examples of polymer complexes are known. The best characterized is the complex of isotactic and syndiotactic poly(methyl methacrylate)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14235
name: isomorphous polymer co-crystal
def: "Crystal consisting of two or more structurally different polymers\, resulting in macromolecular isomorphism."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14236
name: copolymer isomorphism
def: "Macromolecular isomorphism involving copolymers."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14237
name: homopolymer isomorphism
def: "Macromolecular isomorphism involving two homopolymers with different constitutional repeating units."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14235

[Term]
id: goldbook:14238
name: nanoporous polymer crystal
def: "Polymer crystal presenting empty volume elements that can host low-molar-mass guest species."
comment: "A nanoporous polymer crystal is often obtained (e.g., in the case of syndiotactic polystyrene) by removing the low-molar-mass guest species from a polymer clathrate."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14239

[Term]
id: goldbook:14239
name: polymer clathrate
def: "Polymer co-crystal in which low-molar-mass guest species are hosted in molecular-sized cages or cavities containing more than one guest molecule."
comment: "Guest molecules are often packed isolated from each other or in columns."
comment: "Polymer clathrates are formed by many polymers with low-molar-mass compounds, particularly water. Examples are frequent among biological polymers (e.g., amylose) but also some synthetic polymers give polymer clathrates (e.g., poly(ethylene oxide) with various inorganic and organic species)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14240

[Term]
id: goldbook:14240
name: polymer co-crystal
def: "Crystal structure in which at least two substances coexist\, one of which is a polymer."
comment: "Types of polymer co-crystals are: (1) polymer clathrate, (2) isomorphous polymer co-crystal, (3) crystalline polymer-inclusion complex. Examples are provided under each separate entry."
synonym: "polymer mixed crystal" []
synonym: "polymer-based crystalline molecular complex" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14233
consider: goldbook:14235
consider: goldbook:14239
consider: goldbook:14271

[Term]
id: goldbook:14241
name: irregular adjacent re-entry model
def: "Adjacent re-entry model in which the fold period can vary."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14244

[Term]
id: goldbook:14242
name: regular adjacent re-entry model
def: "Idealized adjacent re-entry model in which the fold periods are assumed to have all the same length."
comment: "This model is probably only approached for isothermal growth at small supercooling from dilute solution."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14244
consider: goldbook:14273

[Term]
id: goldbook:14243
name: chain-extended crystal
def: "Folded-chain crystal in which each macromolecule makes one\, or\, at most\, a few folds."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14244
name: fold period
def: "Thickness of a lamellar parallel-chain crystal in the direction of its stems."
comment: "For lamellar crystals with chains whose axes are not normal to the lamellar surface, the fold period is longer than the long period."
comment: "In melt-crystallized polymers, consisting of lamellar crystals with the chain axis perpendicular to the lamellar surface, the fold period is shorter than the long period because the amorphous layer includes not only the folds but other non-crystallized components."
comment: "The fold period corresponds to the long period in the case of solution crystallized stacked lamellar crystals with the chain axis perpendicular to the lamellar surface."
comment: "The fold period is controlled by the crystallization temperature."
synonym: "fold length" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14251

[Term]
id: goldbook:14245
name: non-parallel-chain crystal
def: "Polymer crystal in which the stems are aligned along two or more different crystallographic directions."
comment: "So far, the only example of non-parallel-chain polymer crystal is the \(\ce{\upgamma\mbox{-}polymorph}\) of isotactic polypropylene, where double-layers of parallel chains alternate with identical double-layers rotated by approximately \(\pu{80\!^{\circ}}\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14246
name: solidification model
def: "Model of crystalline polymer formed by lamellar crystallization from the melt\, in which some stems of a macromolecule belong to one crystal\, whilst other stems of the same macromolecule are in different crystals."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14247
name: amorphous domain
def: "Phase domain consisting of matter in the amorphous state."
comment: "The linear dimensions of amorphous domains in crystalline polymers range from \(\pu{1 nm}\) upwards."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14248
name: bundle crystal
def: "Crystal in which the chains predominantly extend from the crystal surface\, resulting in a fringed-micelle model morphology."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14249
name: hedrite
def: "Polyhedral aggregate of lamellar crystals resulting in a morphology that resembles that of a polymer single crystal\, at least from one viewing direction."
comment: "A hedrite is a type of axialite."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14255

[Term]
id: goldbook:14250
name: lamellar crystal sector
def: "Portion of a lamellar crystal\, often bounded by a well-defined crystal face."
comment: "For some crystal morphologies, the fold surfaces of a sector make an angular discontinuity with the fold surfaces of contiguous sectors."
comment: "Lamellar crystal sectors in folded-chain crystals correspond to fold domains."
comment: "The fold plane prevailing in a sector is normally assumed to correspond to the crystal face bounding the sector."
comment: "The term generally applies to solution-crystallized lamellar crystals. Sectors are described using the indexing of the crystal face bounding it."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14251
name: long period
def: "Average periodicity \(repeating distance\) of the layers of lamellar crystals in a stacked assembly."
comment: "The long period is usually measured by SAXS (small-angle X-ray scattering), SANS (small-angle neutron scattering), atomic force microscopy (AFM), or transmission electron microscopy (TEM), and is the sum of the average thickness of the lamellar crystals and the average thickness of the amorphous inter-lamellar region."
synonym: "long spacing" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14252
name: polycrystalline polymer
def: "Crystalline polymer in which a large number of polymer crystallites coexist."
comment: "Polymer crystallites and amorphous domains in a specimen may be organized randomly or with some degree of order."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14247
consider: goldbook:14253

[Term]
id: goldbook:14253
name: polymer fibre
def: "Uniaxially oriented polymer filament with macroscopic cylindrical symmetry\, in which the average direction of the polymer chain axis coincides with the cylindrical symmetry axis."
comment: "A crystalline polymer fiber generally consists of lamellar crystals or fringed micelle-type crystals (see fringed micelle model) with an interconnecting amorphous phase, or, rarely, of extended-chain crystals."
comment: "The cylindrical symmetry shown by crystalline polymer fibers is not at the molecular scale but results from the random orientation of crystallites around the axis of the fiber."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14254
name: polymer fibril
def: "Small diameter polymer fiber consisting of a small number of polymer chains."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14253

[Term]
id: goldbook:14255
name: polymer single crystal
def: "Individually distinct polymer crystal whose lattice\, at crystallization\, is characterized by a single orientation."
comment: "Polymer single crystals obtained from dilute solutions are comparatively large, i.e, they have lateral dimensions typically of a few micrometers, which can reach tens of micrometers in some instances. They are normally delimited by well-defined boundary surfaces and may, in rare instances, be manipulated individually."
comment: "The thickness of individual polymer single crystals (inclusive of the fold surfaces thickness) is now measured by AFM (atomic force microscopy) along with transmission electron microscopy, while average values are derived using SAXS (small-angle X-ray scattering) and SANS (small-angle neutron scattering)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14260

[Term]
id: goldbook:14256
name: row structure
def: "Polycrystalline polymer morphology resulting from nucleation of polymer lamellar crystals by multiple fibrillar structures or by macroscopic linear objects such as fibers."
comment: "Row structures result frequently by heteroepitaxy."
comment: "Shish-kebab structures are the simplest form of row structures."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14252
consider: goldbook:14264

[Term]
id: goldbook:14257
name: dominant lamellae
def: "Lamellar crystals radiating from the center of the spherulite which\, by splaying\, constitute the initial skeleton of the spherulite."
comment: "Crystallization of dominant lamellae is largely associated with primary crystallization."
comment: "In the isothermal crystallization of crystalline polymers there is normally a single population of dominant lamellae, characterized by a single thickness."
comment: "The thickness and perfection of dominant lamellae is greater than for subsidiary lamellae, especially in non-isothermal crystallization."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14258
consider: goldbook:14266
consider: goldbook:14267

[Term]
id: goldbook:14258
name: subsidiary lamellae
def: "Lamellar crystals nucleated either epitaxially or incoherently on dominant lamellae."
comment: "Crystallization of subsidiary lamellae is generally associated with secondary crystallization, but may start before dominant lamellae meet at spherulite boundaries."
comment: "In typical crystalline polymers there may be populations of subsidiary lamellae characterized by bimodal thickness distributions."
comment: "Subsidiary lamellae can appear as pockets of lamellae in different locations within a spherulite, with no well-defined relation to dominant lamellae."
comment: "The thickness and perfection of subsidiary lamellae is reduced as compared to dominant lamellae, especially in non-isothermal crystallization."
synonym: "infilling lamellae" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14257
consider: goldbook:14267

[Term]
id: goldbook:14259
name: transcrystalline layer
def: "Polycrystalline layer nucleated and shaped by a pre-existing planar surface within or in contact with a polymer melt."
comment: "In growth from oriented melts, as in injection-molding, two transcrystalline layers may result. The first is the skin layer and is formed by individual lamellae, while the second consists of spherulites. Both grow preferentially in the direction perpendicular to the flow direction and the external surface of the molded articles."
comment: "In growth from unoriented melts, as in compression-molding, the transcrystalline layer consists of closely spaced, highly asymmetric spherulites, nucleated on the surface and elongated in the direction normal to the surface of the molded articles."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14256

[Term]
id: goldbook:14260
name: twinned polymer crystal
def: "Polymer crystal in which two or more single crystalline domains of the same crystal polymorph\, with different lattice orientations\, coexist."
comment: "Some of the lattice directions, very often the chain axis directions, remain parallel in the different domains of twinned crystals."
comment: "The lattice orientations in the different domains of twinned crystals are generally symmetry-related."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14219
consider: goldbook:14255

[Term]
id: goldbook:14261
name: cellulation
def: "Division of the growth front of individual or groups of dominant lamellae into divergent 'cells'\, with the impurities and the less crystallizable molecules segregated between the cells and at the spherulite boundaries."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14257

[Term]
id: goldbook:14262
name: confined-spaces crystallisation
def: "Polymer crystallization occurring inside elongated cavities\, spheres\, galleries\, or layers formed by a host material different from the polymer."
comment: "At least one dimension of the cavities must be of the order of a few nanometers, i.e., close to typical dimensions of a polymer crystallite and, in particular, to lamellar thickness."
comment: "Morphology and polymorphism may be significantly affected by confined crystallization."
comment: "Spherical structures may result from the crystallization of one of the two components in a heterogeneous polymer blend (e.g., in a polyethylene-polycarbonate blend) while lamellar, hexagonally packed cylindrical and inverse cylindrical morphologies are frequent in block copolymers with a crystallizable block (e.g., in poly(ethylene oxide)-block-polystyrene))."
comment: "Stems may aggregate in small, disordered bundle crystals or in relatively large lamellar crystals, typically within layered inorganic materials."
comment: "Stems may be isolated in the cavities of the frequently nonpolymeric host material, as in the cases of zeolites and the fully trans-form of perhydrotriphenylene in the crystalline state. In such cases, the polymer stems are usually highly disordered."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14248

[Term]
id: goldbook:14263
name: degree of supercooling
def: "Temperature difference between the equilibrium melting temperature and the temperature of a supercooled melt."
synonym: "degree of undercooling" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14272

[Term]
id: goldbook:14264
name: heteroepitaxy
def: "Epitaxy between crystals of different substances."
comment: "For some polymers, heteroepitaxy may be used to favor by nucleation the development of a crystalline phase that is not obtained normally."
comment: "Heteroepitaxy is often used to enhance nucleation and reduce spherulites’ size in commercial processes."
comment: "Heteroepitaxy may occur between crystals of different polymers. However, the heteroepitaxy of low-molecular-weight systems (e.g., alkanes) on polymer crystals (e.g., polyethylene), and of polymer crystals on crystals of low-molecular-weight substances can also occur. Heteroepitaxy has also been observed for polymer crystals on ionic substances and minerals like clay and mica."
comment: "Polymer crystals with unusual orientations with respect to the substrate may be obtained by epitaxial crystallization involving heteroepitaxy."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14265
name: homoepitaxy
def: "Epitaxy between crystals of the same substance."
comment: "A less frequent homoepitaxy may occur, as for the \(\ce{\upalpha\mbox{-}polymorph}\) of isotactic polypropylene, between crystals of the same crystal polymorph and morphology but with a different, well-defined orientation of the crystal lattice."
comment: "A new crystal layer, initiated by secondary nucleation, or the formation of a twinned polymer crystal, can be seen as a form of homoepitaxy."
comment: "Homoepitaxy may allow the development of crystals of the same polymer but in two distinct crystal polymorphs with well-defined mutual orientations, as in the case of \(\ce{\upgamma\mbox{-}isotactic}\) polypropylene grown on \(\ce{\upalpha\mbox{-}isotactic}\) polypropylene."
comment: "Homoepitaxy may occur between crystals of the same crystal polymorph but different morphology as in the crystallization of chain-folded crystals on fibril-like extended-chain crystals in shish-kebabs. Similarly, in non-isothermal crystallization, lamellar crystals of lower thickness are often nucleated on thicker crystals, preserving a unique orientation of the crystal lattice."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14219
consider: goldbook:14260
consider: goldbook:14267

[Term]
id: goldbook:14266
name: isothermal crystallisation
def: "Crystallization taking place at constant temperature."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14267
name: non-isothermal crystallisation
def: "Crystallization taking place under varying temperature conditions."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14268
name: primary nucleus
def: "The smallest crystalline entity\, the further growth of which is thermodynamically favored."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14269
name: secondary nucleation
def: "Nucleation of a new molecular layer on a growing crystal face.Note\: Secondary nucleation refers to the further growth of an already-existing crystal."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14270
name: self-nucleation
def: "Heterogeneous nucleation by crystals of the same polymer."
synonym: "self-seeding" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14271
name: polymer co-crystallisation
def: "Crystallization\, within the same crystal\, of a polymer and at least one other component."
comment: "The following possibilities need to be considered: (1) a polymer and a low-molar-mass species forming a polymer clathrate; (2) a molecular complex of different polymers (see polymer co-crystal); and (3) different polymers displaying some degree of isomorphism (isomorphous polymer co-crystal)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14235
consider: goldbook:14239
consider: goldbook:14240

[Term]
id: goldbook:14272
name: supercooled melt
def: "Metastable melt of a crystallizable polymer at temperatures below its equilibrium melting temperature \\\(T^\{\\circ\}_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\)."
comment: "The term is not applicable to the amorphous component of a crystalline polymer, i.e., after crystallization has occurred."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14273
name: supercooling
def: "The process of cooling a melt to a temperature below its equilibrium melting point\, without crystallization occurring."
synonym: "undercooling" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-11-13
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14274
name: polymer particle
def: "Particle of polymer of any shape."
comment: "For the description of a particle, the expression "size" is often used. However, because this expression does not have a sufficiently precise meaning its usage is not recommended."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-06-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14275
name: polymer bead
def: "Sphere of polymer\, usually with a diameter in the range from one-tenth to a few millimeters."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-06-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14276
name: polymer microparticle
def: "Particle of polymer of any shape with an equivalent diameter from approximately \\\(\\pu\{0.1 \\upmu m\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{100 \\upmu m\}\\\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-06-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14290

[Term]
id: goldbook:14277
name: polymer microsphere
def: "Polymer microparticle of spherical shape."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-06-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14276

[Term]
id: goldbook:14278
name: polymer nanoparticle
def: "Particle of polymer of any shape and an equivalent diameter from approximately \\\(\\pu\{1 nm\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{100 nm\}\\\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-06-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14290

[Term]
id: goldbook:14279
name: polymer nanosphere
def: "Polymer nanoparticle of spherical shape."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-06-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:14278

[Term]
id: goldbook:14280
name: polymer gel particle
def: "Particle of gel in which the network component is a polymer."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-06-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:14281
name: gel microparticle
def: "Particle of gel of any shape with an equivalent diameter of approximately \\\(\\pu\{0.1 \\upmu m\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{100 \\upmu m\}\\\)."
synonym: "microgel" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-10-06-03
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
