[Term]
id: goldbook:15178
name: drug-eluting stent
def: "Refers to a stent with an active drug that is intended to produce a therapeutic effect \(e.g.\, reduction of restenosis\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:15236

[Term]
id: goldbook:15179
name: dusting powder
def: "Usually intended for external use."
comment: "Examples are antibacterial and antifungal products."
comment: "Normally dispensed in containers with perforated lids."
comment: "The powder must flow well so that it can be dusted over the required area."
comment: "Usually contains ingredients used for therapeutic, prophylactic, or lubricant purposes."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:15227

[Term]
id: goldbook:15180
name: effervescent tablet
def: "Solid preparation that on contact with water breaks apart by the effect of gas \(usually \\\(\\ce\{CO2\}\\\)\) evolution\, resulting commonly from the reaction of hydrogen carbonate with citric or tartaric acid\, in order to facilitate dissolution or dispersion of the active ingredient before ingestion."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:15181
name: efflorescent
def: "Substance that loses water to form a lower hydrate or becomes anhydrous spontaneously."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:15182
name: elixir
def: "Sweet \(often colored\) dilute alcohol-based\, 'hydroalcoholic'\, liquid used in the compounding of drugs to be taken by mouth in order to improve palatability."
comment: "Elixirs are among the most common types of medicinal preparations taken orally in liquid form."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:15183
name: embedding
def: "Technological process\, which consists of mixing or inclusion of the therapeutic substance with an excipient or their mixtures\, typically as a matrix dosage form\, in order to change the rate of release."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:15175
consider: goldbook:15207

[Term]
id: goldbook:15184
name: enemas
def: "Solutions \(aqueous or oily\)\, emulsions\, or suspensions for rectal administration of medicaments for cleansing\, diagnostic\, or therapeutic purposes."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:15147

[Term]
id: goldbook:15185
name: enteric coating
def: "Used on tablets\, granules\, pellets\, and capsules to make them resistant to gastric fluids but designed to disintegrate\, disrupt\, or dissolve when the preparation enters the duodenum."
comment: "Enteric coating is used for one of the following reasons: To protect the drug from degradation by the acid in the stomach (e.g., erythromycin).To protect the stomach from the irritant effect of the drug (e.g., aspirin).To facilitate absorption of a drug distally to the stomach."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:15159
consider: goldbook:15167
consider: goldbook:15193
consider: goldbook:15221
consider: goldbook:15241

[Term]
id: goldbook:15186
name: equilibrium moisture content
def: "Final stage reached after drying of a solid\, beyond the critical moisture content."
synonym: "EMC" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:15187
name: fast-dissolving tablet
def: "A tablet formulation intended for a rapid release of its active agent."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:15198
consider: goldbook:15239
consider: goldbook:15241

[Term]
id: goldbook:15188
name: formulation
def: "Summary of operations carried out to convert a pharmacologically active compound into a dosage form suitable for administration."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:15143
consider: goldbook:15175
consider: goldbook:15177
consider: goldbook:15233

[Term]
id: goldbook:15189
name: gargle
def: "Aqueous solution used for the prevention and treatment of mouth and throat infections."
comment: "May contain antiseptics, antibacterials, analgesics, and/or astringents."
comment: "Usually diluted with water before use."
synonym: "mouthwash" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:15190
name: gastric emptying rate
def: "Pace at which a drug along with the stomach content leaves and enters the duodenum."
comment: "Gastric emptying rate (or time) is expressed typically in units of time or \(t_{1/2}\). It is also expressed as the amount of a given substance emptied per the total mass in the stomach, at a given time; or as the amount emptied, at a given time, of a given substance."
comment: "Often, gastric emptying rate is also expressed as gastric emptying time."
comment: "Since most drugs are optimally absorbed from the small intestine, the onset of drug action depends on the gastric emptying rate. Thus, the rate of gastric emptying determines the timing but not the extent of oral drug absorption."
comment: "The gastric emptying rate depends on several factors, e.g., stomach calorie content, \(\rm{pH}\), hunger, anxiety, the nature of the drugs and body posture. High-calorie foods (e.g., fats) usually retard gastric emptying and delay drug absorption."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:15145

[Term]
id: goldbook:15191
name: gene therapy
def: "Use of products containing genetic material \(e.g.\, pDNA\, antisense DNA\, siRNA\) to treat a disease or condition\, or to modify or manipulate the expression of genetic material or to alter the biological properties of living cells."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:15192
name: generic
def: "Drug\(s\) or formulation\(s\) of drug\(s\) or dosage forms\, which no longer have patent protection."
comment: "Generic or nonproprietary drugs that may enter the market after the expiry of the basic patent covering the original drugs."
comment: "Generic products may themselves bear brand names."
comment: "Nonproprietary drugs are required to meet the same bioequivalency test as the original brand name drugs."
synonym: "generics" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:15175

[Term]
id: goldbook:15193
name: granules
def: "Powder particles\, which have been aggregated to form larger irregular particles\, usually of \\\(\\pu\{0.5\\!\{-\}\\! 2 mm\}\\\) diameter."
comment: "Granules are also used as intermediates in tableting. These are typically of smaller sizes."
comment: "May also be used rarely as independent dosage form for oral administrations."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:15145
consider: goldbook:15175
consider: goldbook:15227
consider: goldbook:15241

[Term]
id: goldbook:15194
name: granulation
def: "Process in which powder particles are made to aggregate to larger particles called granules."
comment: "Granulation is preceded by mixing the necessary powdered ingredients to assure their uniform distribution in the granules."
comment: "Granulation may be carried out by two methods: wet granulations which utilize nontoxic volatile liquids, like water or low alcohols; or dry methods in which high pressure is applied."
comment: "In the majority of cases, granulation is required in the production of tablets or capsules, when granules are made as an intermediate product."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:15159
consider: goldbook:15193
consider: goldbook:15227
consider: goldbook:15241

[Term]
id: goldbook:15195
name: hydrate
def: "Crystalline form of a compound in which water molecules are part of the crystal structure."
comment: "Association of water molecule(s) in a crystal can be of different strength."
comment: "The term "hydrate" may mean different things in different contexts. Common to all contexts is the content of water, which may be part of crystal structure or part of a molecule to which water has been added reversibly (e.g., chloral hydrate) or the elements of water incorporated covalently (e.g., carbohydrates)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:15166
consider: goldbook:15234

[Term]
id: goldbook:15196
name: hydrophile-lipophile balance system
def: "Empirical scale \(of \\\(0\\!-\\!20\\\)\) used to classify surfactants and emulsifying agents."
comment: "An HLB value of \(0\) corresponds to a completely hydrophobic molecule, while a value of \(20\) corresponds to a molecule made up completely of hydrophilic components."
comment: "The more hydrophilic the surfactant the more it favors the formation of o/w over w/o emulsions."
comment: "The numerical value is determined by the expression (\({\rm{HLB}} = 20 M_{\rm{h}}/M\)) where \(M_{\rm{h}}\) is the molecular mass of the hydrophilic portion of the molecule, and \(M\) is the molecular mass of the whole molecule, giving a result on an arbitrary scale of \(0\!-\!20\)."
synonym: "HLB" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
synonym: "HLB System" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:15197
name: hygroscopicity
def: "Tendency of a substance to absorb water from the atmosphere."
comment: "A substance that absorbs moisture from the atmosphere is called hygroscopic."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:15198
name: immediate-release tablet
def: "Dosage form that releases the drug immediately."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:15175
consider: goldbook:15187
consider: goldbook:15239

[Term]
id: goldbook:15199
name: inactivation factor
def: "Number that expresses the reduction in the numbers of a microorganism\, brought about by a sterilization process."
comment: "The IF value is specific for a microorganism and a sterilization process."
synonym: "IF" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:15200
name: inhalation therapy
def: "Administration of drugs directly to the respiratory tract\, mostly by aerosols."
comment: "Since a drug is delivered directly to the site of action, lower dose is needed by this route than by other routes, e.g., the gastrointestinal or parenteral routes."
comment: "The incidence and the intensity of side effects are generally lower when this route is used as compared to other routes of drug administration."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:15143
consider: goldbook:15146

[Term]
id: goldbook:15201
name: injection
def: "Delivery of a generally sterile liquid medication into the body\, or a vessel\, tissue\, or organ via syringe and needle."
comment: "Epidural injections are given into the epidural space of the spinal cord."
comment: "Intra-articular injections are made into the synovial fluid, which lubricates the articulating ends of bones in a joint."
comment: "Intrabursal injections are given into the bursae, which are small sacks of fluids between the tendons and bones."
comment: "Intracardial injections are given directly into the heart in emergencies."
comment: "Intracutaneous or intradermal injections are made into the skin between the inner layer (dermis) and the outer layer (epidermis)."
comment: "Intramuscular injections are made by inserting the needle across the skin, subcutaneous tissue, and membrane enclosing the muscle."
comment: "Intraspinal injections are made into or around the spinal cord."
comment: "Intrathecal injection is the introduction of material for diffusion throughout the subarachnoid space by means of lumbar puncture."
comment: "Intravascular injections (intra-arterial and -venous) are made directly into the blood stream for rapid effect."
comment: "Ophthalmic injections include a variety of sites within the eye."
comment: "Subcutaneous or hypodermic injections are made under the skin into the subcutaneous tissue."
comment: "The same formulation cannot be used for all routes."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:15202
name: liniment
def: "Liquid intended for massaging into the skin."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:15203
name: loading dose
def: "Initial\, typically larger than the maintenance dose of a drug given to a patient at the start of pharmacotherapy."
comment: "The objective is to reach quickly the therapeutically beneficial plasma level. This is followed by smaller (maintenance) doses in order to maintain the plasma concentration."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:15204
name: lotion
def: "Solution\, emulsion\, or suspension to be applied to the skin."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:15205
name: lozenge
def: "Tablet\, which does not contain a disintegrant and which is sucked to dissolve in the mouth to produce either a local \(e.g.\, antiseptic\) or systemic \(e.g.\, vitamins\) effect."
comment: "Lozenges must be palatable and slowly soluble."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:15241
consider: goldbook:15243

[Term]
id: goldbook:15206
name: lubricant
def: "Used as processing aid in tablet and capsule manufacturing\, to facilitate the movement of the formulation into the dye and punch and to reduce the energy of compression."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:15159
consider: goldbook:15241

[Term]
id: goldbook:15207
name: matrix formulation
def: "Specific case of drug embedding in insoluble excipients \(typically in a polymer\) in order to achieve extended release."
comment: "Matrices can be monolithic or heterogeneous, dissolved or dispersed, or both."
comment: "This term also applies to a matrix made of hydrophilic substances, which, in contact with water, form a gel of high viscosity."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:15183
consider: goldbook:15239

[Term]
id: goldbook:15208
name: maximum additive concentration
def: "Maximum concentration of a drug\, which will form a clear solution with a given concentration of surfactant."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:15209
name: maximum safe concentration
def: "Concentration of a drug in the plasma\, above which side effects are likely to occur in a patient."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:15210
name: minimum effective plasma concentration
def: "Concentration of a drug that must be achieved in the plasma before any desired therapeutic or pharmacological effect can be obtained."
synonym: "minimum therapeutic plasma concentration" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:15211
name: minimum inhibitory concentration
def: "Lowest concentration of an antibacterial drug necessary to inhibit the growth of a microorganism."
synonym: "MIC" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:15212
name: modified release
def: "Release of a drug from a dosage form that it is not immediate \(e.g.\, sustained release\, retarded release\, delayed-action preparations\, controlled release\, extended release\, etc.\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/PAC-REC-04-10-14
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
