consider: goldbook:16268

[Term]
id: goldbook:15354
name: retrograde solubility
def: "Solubility that decreases with an increase in conditions such as temperature or added component\, where an increase is expected as the usual case. In ternary liquid systems with coexisting phases\, where the critical solution point is not a maximum on a ternary diagram\, passing from a homogeneous mixture in which the composition of a given component is greater than that of the critical mixture to a heterogeneous mixture and back to a homogeneous mixture by altering the relative amounts of the two other components."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200880020233
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:15365
consider: goldbook:16268

[Term]
id: goldbook:15355
name: saturating solute
def: "Solute that forms a saturated solution."
comment: "Together with the term non-saturating solute, used to distinguish among solutes in ternary and higher-order systems."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200880020233
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:15332
consider: goldbook:15343
consider: goldbook:15366

[Term]
id: goldbook:15356
name: scaling equation
def: "Dimensionless equation representing mutual solubilities in a number of chemically related liquid systems. Each mutual solubility is subtracted from the solubility at the critical solution point\, and the temperature is divided by the critical solution temperature."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200880020233
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:15342

[Term]
id: goldbook:15357
name: Sechenov equation
def: "Expression of the salt effect that relates the change in solubility of a nonelectrolyte \(e.g.\, gas or organic liquid\) to changing ionic strength of aqueous solutions\, \\\[\\lg \(s_\{\\rm\{o\}\}/s\) = K_\{s\} I_\{s\}\\\] where \\\(s_\{o\}\\\)\, \\\(s\\\) are the solubilities of nonelectrolyte in pure water and saline solution\, respectively\, \\\(K_\{s\}\\\) the Sechenov parameter \(an empirical proportionality constant\)\, and \\\(I_\{s\}\\\) the ionic strength of the saline solution."
comment: "Positive values of the Sechenov parameter correspond to the commonly observed salting-out effect; negative values to the less common salting-in effect."
comment: "Sechenov is the international and Setschenow the German transliteration of Сеченоь."
synonym: "Setschenow equation" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200880020233
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:15358
name: Sieverts’ law
def: "Solubility of a diatomic gas in a molten metal is proportional to the square root of the partial pressure."
comment: "Other forms of the solubility constant are common, especially in terms of amount concentrations or mass fractions (especially in metallurgical papers) instead of mole fractions."
comment: "The law follows from the solubility constant for equilibrium dissolution of an ideal diatomic gas \(\ce{X2}\) in a metal \(\ce{M}\) to form an ideal solution of dissociated atoms: \[\ce{X2(g)  2X(M)}\] for which, and for dilute solutions of the gas, \[K_{{\rm{s}},x} = a(\ce{X},\ce{M})^{2}/a(\ce{X2},{\rm{g}}) \approx x(\ce{X},\ce{M})^{2} p^{_{^⦵\!}}/p (\ce{X2},\rm{g})\] where \(x(\ce{X},\ce{M})\) is the solubility of the gas in the molten metal expressed as a mole fraction of \(\ce{H\mbox{-}atoms}\). The solubility constant \(K_{\rm{s},x}\) is called the Sieverts constant."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200880020233
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:15359

[Term]
id: goldbook:15359
name: solubility constant
def: "Generalization of solubility product to include\, for example\, undissociated salt or uncharged cation-anion pair as solute\, reaction between solution species and the cation or anion of the solid salt\, and gas-solution equilibria."
comment: "The solubility constant can also be considered in many cases as relating to a solubility equilibrium as described under solubility product plus one or more simultaneous homogeneous equilibria in solution."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200880020233
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:15360
name: solubilisation
def: "Process by which an agent increases the solubility or the rate of dissolution of a solid or liquid solute.Modified from entry 'micellar solubilization' in."
comment: "Solubilization is sometimes used loosely to include processes caused by agents such as surfactants which hold a component in micelles or colloidal suspension rather than in solution."
comment: "The solute that is solubilized is called the solubilizate."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200880020233
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:16268

[Term]
id: goldbook:15361
name: solvent mole fraction
def: "Mole or mass fraction of a solvent in a solution containing \\\(s\\\) solute constituents \(\\\(i = 1\, 2\, \\ldots\, s\\\)\) and \\\(p\\\) solvent constituents \(\\\(i = s + 1\, s + 2\, \\ldots\, p\\\)\). For a solvent \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\)\, \\\[x_\{\{\\rm\{v\}\}\,\{\\ce\{A\}\}\} = x_\{\\ce\{A\}\}/\\sum \\limits_\{i=s+1\}^\{p\} x_\{i\}\\\] where \\\[\\sum \\limits_\{i=1\}^\{p\} x_\{i\} = \\sum \\limits_\{i=1\}^\{s\} x_\{i\} + \\sum \\limits_\{i=s+1\}^\{p\} x_\{i\} = 1\\\] and analogously for \\\(w_\{\{\\rm\{v\}\}\,\{\\ce\{A\}\}\}\\\)\, where the first and last summations are over all solvent constituents."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200880020233
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:15373
consider: goldbook:15376
consider: goldbook:16269

[Term]
id: goldbook:15362
name: solvent volume fraction
def: "For a solvent \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\)\, volume fraction of \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\) divided by the sum of the volume fractions of solvent in a solution containing both \\\(s\\\) solutes \(\\\(i = 1\, 2\, \\ldots\, s\\\)\) and \\\(p\\\) solvents \(\\\(i = s + 1\, s + 2\, \\ldots\, p\\\)\) \\\[\\varphi_\{\{\\rm\{v\}\}\,\{\\ce\{A\}\}\} = \\varphi_\{\\ce\{A\}\}/\\sum \\limits_\{i=s+1\}^\{p\} \\varphi_\{i\}\\\] where the summation is over the \\\(p\\\) solvent components only."
comment: "Used to express solvent composition for a system containing a solute in a mixed solvent."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200880020233
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:16268

[Term]
id: goldbook:15363
name: standard amount concentration
def: "Chosen value of amount concentration."
comment: "In principle, one may choose any value for the standard concentration, although the choice must be specified. The most common choice for standard concentration is \(c^{_{^⦵\!}} = \pu{1 mol dm-3}\), which is universally accepted."
synonym: "standard concentration" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200880020233
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:15364
name: synthetic method
def: "Class of experimental procedures for solubility determination in which a solution of known composition is prepared. Two major subclasses are recognized. In the isothermal method\, one liquid component is titrated with a second liquid component at constant temperature until persistent turbidity is observed \(i.e.\, to the cloud point\). In the polythermal method\, a mixture of known composition is heated above its solution temperature and monitored visually during cooling until turbidity \(the cloud point\) is observed."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200880020233
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:16268
consider: goldbook:16270

[Term]
id: goldbook:15365
name: ternary diagram
def: "Triangular plot \(usually an equilateral or right-angled triangle\) whose vertices represent the pure components of a ternary system at constant temperature and pressure. Any point within the triangle represents the composition in terms of the two independent mole \(or mass\) fractions. Lines parallel to the sides of the triangle represent constant proportions of the respective components \(isopleths\)."
comment: "In addition, any triangle of any shape, including any triangle within the main triangle, can be used to obtain the composition of the system represented by a point within the triangle in terms of the compositions represented by its vertices."
comment: "In an isobaric diagram, an axis perpendicular to the plane of the triangle may be added to represent temperature."
synonym: "Gibbs’ triangular representation" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200880020233
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:15336
consider: goldbook:15366
consider: goldbook:16268

[Term]
id: goldbook:15366
name: ternary system
def: "System containing three components."
