[Term]
id: goldbook:15381
name: azeotropic copolymerisation
def: "Binary copolymerization in which the two monomer reactivity ratios\, \\\(r_\{12\}\\\) and \\\(r_\{21\}\\\) are not both equal to unity yet the copolymerization forms a copolymer with its molar ratio of monomer units equal to the molar ratio of monomers in the monomer feed."
comment: "Although, in its equality of copolymer and monomer-feed compositions, an azeotropic polymerization has the same feature as an ideal binary copolymerization in which \(r_{12} = r_{21} = 1\), it is not an ideal copolymerization as it has \(r_{12} r_{21} \neq 1\)."
comment: "An azeotropic polymerization occurs at only one specific molar ratio of monomers in the feed."
