[Term]
id: goldbook:15937
name: ocular
def: "Pertaining to the eye."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200779071153
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:15938
name: odds
def: "Ratio of the probability of occurrence of an event to that of non-occurrence\, or the ratio of the probability that something is so\, to the probability that it is not so."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200779071153
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:15939
name: odds ratio
def: "Quotient obtained by dividing one set of odds by another. The terms 'odds' or 'odds ratio' are defined differently according to the situation under discussion. Consider the following notation for the distribution of a binary exposure and a disease in a population or a sample. \\\[\\begin\{array\}\{l c c\} & Exposed & Nonexposed \\\\ \\rm\{Disease\} & a & b \\\\ \\rm\{No\\ disease\} & c & d \\\\ \\end\{array\}\\\] The odds ratio \(cross-product ratio\) is \\\(ad/bc\\\)."
comment: "The disease-odds (rate-odds) ratio for a cohort or cross-section is the ratio of the odds in favor of disease among the exposed population (\(a/c\)) to the odds in favor of disease among the unexposed (\(b/d\)), which is equal to \(ad/bc\) and hence is equal to the exposure-odds ratio for the cohort or cross-section."
comment: "The exposure-odds ratio for a set of case control data is the ratio of the odds in favor of exposure among the cases (\(a/b\)) to the odds in favor of exposure among non-cases (\(c/d\)), which is equal to \(ad/(bc)\). With incident cases, unbiased subject selection, and a "rare" disease (say, under \(\pu{2\%}\) cumulative incidence rate over the study period), \(ad/bc\) is an approximate estimate of the risk ratio. With incident cases, unbiased subject selection, and density sampling of controls, \(ad/bc\) is an estimate of the ratio of the person-time incidence rates (force of morbidity) in the exposed and unexposed. No rarity assumption is required for this."
comment: "The prevalence-odds ratio refers to an odds ratio derived cross-sectionally, as, for example, an odds ratio derived from studies of prevalent (rather than incident) cases."
comment: "The risk-odds ratio is the ratio of the odds in favor of getting disease, if exposed, to the odds in favor of getting disease if not exposed. The odds ratio derived from a cohort study is an estimate of this."
synonym: "OR" RELATED OMO:0003012 []
synonym: "cross-product ratio" []
synonym: "relative odds" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200779071153
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:15695

[Term]
id: goldbook:15940
name: odor threshold
def: "In principle\, the lowest concentration of an odorant in the air that can be detected by a human being."
comment: "In practice, a panel of "sniffers" is often used, and the threshold taken as the concentration at which $\pu{50\%}$ of the panel can detect the odorant (although some workers have also used $\pu{100\%}$ thresholds). The odor concentration at the detection threshold may be defined as one odor unit."
synonym: "odor detection threshold" []
synonym: "odour threshold" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200779071153
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:16136

[Term]
id: goldbook:15941
name: olf
def: "Unit used to measure scent emission of people and objects; one olf is defined as the scent emission of an 'average person'\, a sitting adult who takes an average of \\\(\\pu\{0.7\}\\\) baths per day and whose skin has a total area of \\\(\\pu\{1.8 m2\}\\\); the scent emission of an object or person is measured by specially trained personnel comparing it to normed scents."
comment: "The olf should not be confused with the of unit of scent immission (as opposed to emission), the decipol, which also takes into account the ventilation system’s air volume flow."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200779071153
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
