[Term]
id: goldbook:16198
name: zoocide
def: "Substance intended to kill animals."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200779071153
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:16199
name: caged compound
def: "Molecular species that can rapidly be converted from inactive into active form by light. Typically\, photocaged compounds have a covalently attached group that can be photocleaved by electromagnetic radiation of a specific wavelength."
comment: "An example is the photorelease of nucleotides from their attachment to 1-(4,5-dimethoxy-2-nitrophenyl) ethyl ester."
comment: "Electromagnetic radiation activated caged compounds (photocages) are widely used in biochemistry for a rapid photoinduced introduction of a variety of compounds to biological systems with spatial and temporal control, allowing the time-resolved study of the ensuing events."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200779030293
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:16214

[Term]
id: goldbook:16200
name: charge-transfer absorption
def: "Electronic absorption corresponding to a charge-transfer transition."
comment: "In some cases, the charge-transfer absorption band(s) may be strongly obscured by the local absorptions of the donor and acceptor systems."
synonym: "CT" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
synonym: "CT absorption" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200779030293
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:16201
name: cis/trans photoisomerisation
def: "Photoinduced geometric isomerization about a double bond."
comment: "Geometrical isomerization of a \(\ce{C-C}\) double bond is called a cis/trans isomerization in 1,2-disubstituted alkenes. \(E/Z\) isomerization is a more general designation applying also to higher substituted alkenes."
comment: "In a \(\ce{C-C}\) double bond, a geometrical cis/trans (or \(E/Z\)) isomerization implies a \(\pu{180\!^{\circ}}\) rotation (except when modified by steric interactions), whereas in other bonds the mechanism might be different, e.g., in azocompounds, a cis/trans photoisomerization may occur through an inversion at a \(\ce{N}\) center."
comment: "Photochemical pathways for cis/trans isomerization have the advantage over thermal and catalytic methods of giving equilibrium cis/trans mixtures (photostationary states) rich in thermodynamically unstable isomers."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200779030293
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:16202
name: external quantum efficiency
def: "Efficiency of light emission for solid-state light-emitting devices\, such as organic light-emitting devices \(OLEDs\). Analogous to emission quantum yield."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200779030293
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:16203
name: Franck–Condon factor
def: "Squared modulus of the overlap integral between the vibrational wave functions \(\\\(\\mathit\{\\Theta\}\\\)\) for the initial \(0\) and final \(\\\(\\rm\{e\}\\\)\) state of a given electronic transition. \\\[\\left\\vert \\int \\mathit\{\\Theta\} _\{\\rm\{v'\}\}^\{\(\\rm\{e\}\)\} \\mathit\{\\Theta\} _\{\\rm\{v\}\}^\{\(0\)\} dQ \\right\\vert^\{2\}\\\] where the integration is over all nuclear coordinates."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200779030293
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:16205
name: Lippert–Mataga equation
def: "Correlation of the Stokes-shift\, \\\(\\Delta \\tilde\{ u\}\\\)\, between the absorption and emission maxima\, and the orientation polarizability \\\(f\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\,n\)\\\) of the solvent\,\\\[\\Delta \\tilde\{ u\} = 2 \\frac\{\(\\boldsymbol\{\\mu\}_\{\\rm\{exc\}\} - \\boldsymbol\{\\mu\}_\{\\rm\{gr\}\}\)^\{2\}\}\{c h r^\{3\}\} f\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\,n\) + \{\\rm\{const.\}\}\\\]with \\\(\\boldsymbol\{\\mu\}_\{\\rm\{exc\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\boldsymbol\{\\mu\}_\{\\rm\{gr\}\}\\\) the excited- and ground-state dipole moments of the dissolved molecule\, respectively\, \\\(c\\\) the speed of light\, \\\(h\\\) the Planck constant\, \\\(r\\\) the radius of the cavity in which the solute resides\, and \\\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\) the 'relative static permittivity' \(formerly called dielectric constant\). The expression of the Stokes shift depends only on the absolute magnitude of the difference between dipole moments and not on the angle between the dipoles. The above equation is sometimes called the Lippert equation. The orientation polarizability is given by\\\[f\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\,n\) = \\frac\{\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\} - 1\)\}\{\(2\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\} + 1\)\} - \\frac\{\(n^\{2\} - 1\)\}\{\(2n^\{2\} + 1\)\}\\\]with \\\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\) the relative static permittivity and \\\(n\\\) the refractive index of the solvent\, \\\(f\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\,n\)\\\) ranges from \\\(\\pu\{0.001\}\\\) in cyclohexane to \\\(\\pu\{0.320\}\\\) in water."
comment: "The assumptions made in theories of solvatochromic shifts, together with the uncertainty over the size and shape of the cavity radius \(r\), explain the inaccuracies in the determined excited-state dipole moments."
comment: "\(\Delta \tilde{\nu}\) as a function of \(f(\varepsilon_{\rm{r}},n)\) (Lippert plot), is used to determine the difference in dipole moment (\(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{\rm{exc}} - \boldsymbol{\mu}_{\rm{gr}}\)) between a molecule in the equilibrated excited state and in the ground state, as a measure of the change in charge distribution brought about by light absorption. In this method, it is assumed that the dipole moments do not depend on solvent polarity. A linear variation is not always observed because only dipole-dipole interactions are taken into account and the solute polarizability is neglected."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200779030293
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

