[Term]
id: goldbook:A00485
name: asymmetric transformation
def: "The conversion of a racemate into a pure enantiomer or into a mixture in which one enantiomer is present in excess\, or of a diastereoisomeric mixture into a single diastereoisomer or into a mixture in which one diastereoisomer predominates. This is sometimes called deracemization. If the two enantiomers of a chiral substrate A are freely interconvertible and if an equal amount or excess of a non-racemizing second enantiomerically pure chemical species\, say \($R$\)-B\, is added to a solution of racemic A\, then the resulting equilibrium mixture of adducts A·B will\, in general\, contain unequal amounts of the diastereoisomers \($R$\)-A$\\cdot$\($R$\)-B and \($S$\)-A$\\cdot$\($R$\)-B. The result of this equilibration is called asymmetric transformation of the first kind. If\, in such a system\, the two diastereoisomeric adducts differ considerably in solubility so that only one of them\, say \($R$\)-A$\\cdot$\($R$\)-B\, crystallizes from the solution\, then the equilibration of diastereoisomers in solution and concurrent crystallization will continue so that all \(or most\) of the substrate A can be isolated as the crystalline diastereoisomer \($R$\)-A$\\cdot$\($R$\)-B. Such a 'crystallization-induced asymmetric transformation' is called an asymmetric transformation of the second kind."
synonym: "deracemization" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199668122193
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
