consider: goldbook:N04266

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00544
name: avoided crossing
def: "Frequently\, two Born–Oppenheimer electronic states \(A\, B\) change their energy order as molecular geometry \(x\) is changed continuously along a path. In the process their energies may become equal at some points \(the surfaces are said to cross\, dotted lines in the figure\)\, or only come relatively close \(the crossing of the surfaces is said to be avoided\). If the electronic states are of the same symmetry\, the surface crossing is always avoided in diatomics and usually avoided in polyatomics.   Synonymous with intended crossing."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199668122223
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:I03071
consider: goldbook:S06166

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00546
name: axial
def: "In the chair form of cyclohexane ring bonds to ring atoms \(and molecular entities attached to such bonds\) are termed axial or equatorial according to whether the bonds make a relatively large or small angle\, respectively\, with the plane containing or passing closest to a majority of the ring atoms. Thus the axial bonds are approximately parallel to the $C_\{3\}$ axis and the equatorial bonds approximately parallel to two of the ring bonds. These terms are also used for the chair form of other saturated six-membered rings. The corresponding bonds occurring at the allylic positions in mono-unsaturated six-membered rings are termed pseudo-axial \(or quasi-axial\) and pseudo-equatorial \(or quasi-equatorial\). The terms axial and equatorial have similarly been used in relation to the puckered conformation of cyclobutane\, crown conformer of cyclooctane\, etc. and the terms pseudo-axial and pseudo-equatorial in the context of the non-planar structures of cyclopentane and cycloheptane.  See apical for an alternative use of axial and equatorial with bipyramidal structures."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199668122193
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:A00346
consider: goldbook:A00415
consider: goldbook:C01258
consider: goldbook:C01262
consider: goldbook:C01421
consider: goldbook:E02174

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00547
name: axial chirality
def: "Term used to refer to stereoisomerism resulting from the non-planar arrangement of four groups in pairs about a chirality axis. It is exemplified by allenes $\\ce\{abC=C=Ccd\}$ \(or $\\ce\{abC=C=Cab\}$\) and by the atropisomerism of ortho-substituted biphenyls. The configuration in molecular entities possessing axial chirality is specified by the stereodescriptors $R_\{\\rm\{a\}\}$ and $S_\{\\rm\{a\}\}$ \(or by $P$ or $M$\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199668122193
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:A00238
consider: goldbook:A00511
consider: goldbook:C01058
consider: goldbook:C01059
consider: goldbook:C01249
