[Term]
id: goldbook:BT06998
name: basis function
def: "A one-electron function used in the expansion of the molecular orbital function. Basis functions are commonly represented by atomic orbitals \(see also Slater-type orbital or Gaussian-type orbital\) centered on each atom of the molecule."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:M03996
consider: goldbook:S05710

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT06999
name: basis set
def: "A set of basis functions employed for the representation of molecular orbitals. One may distinguish the minimal basis set \(includes one basis function for each SCF occupied atomic orbital with distinct principal and angular momentum quantum numbers\); split valence basis set \(includes two or more sizes of basis function for each valence orbital\); double zeta \(DZ\) basis set \(a split valence basis set that includes exactly twice as many functions as the minimal basis set\); extended basis set \(the set larger than the double zeta basis set\); polarized basis set \(incorporates basis functions of higher angular quantum number beyond what is required by the atom in its electronic ground state; allows orbitals to change not only a size\, but also a shape\); basis set with diffuse functions and others."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:A00353
consider: goldbook:BT06998
consider: goldbook:G02704
consider: goldbook:V06588
consider: goldbook:Z06745

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07000
name: Bent's rule
def: "In a molecule\, smaller bond angles are formed between electronegative ligands since the central atom\, to which the ligands are attached\, tends to direct bonding hybrid orbitals of greater p character towards its more electronegative substituents."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:C00930
consider: goldbook:L03518

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07001
name: binding energy
def: "The difference between the total energy of a molecular system and the sum of the energies of its isolated $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$ and $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bonds\}$. The value of binding energy depends upon the geometrical arrangement of the isolated subunits \(molecules\). According to another definition\, the term to be subtracted from the total energy is the sum of the energies of the separate atoms in the corresponding valence states\, which compose the molecule."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:V06588

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07002
name: bond energy
def: "The energy required to break a given type of bond between atoms in certain valence states. An averaged bond energy is commonly derived by dissecting the heat of atomization of a molecule into contributions of individual bonds. For molecules with localized bonds\, the heats of atomization \(formation\) are usually well approximated by the sum of pertinent averaged bond energies."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:V06588

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07003
name: bond length
def: "The distance between atomic centers involved in a chemical bond. The notion of bond length is defined differently in various experimental methods of determination of molecular geometry; this leads to small \(usually \\\(0.01\{-\}0.02\\ \\mathring\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\)\) differences in bond lengths obtained by different techniques. For example\, in gas-phase electron-diffraction experiments\, the bond length is the interatomic distance averaged over all occupied vibrational states at a given temperature. In an X-ray crystal structural method\, the bond length is associated with the distance between the centroids of electron densities around the nuclei. In gas-phase microwave spectroscopy\, the bond length is an effective interatomic distance derived from measurements on a number of isotopic molecules\, etc. A number of empirical relationships between bond lengths and bond orders in polyatomic molecules were suggested\, see\, for example\, fractional bond number \(the Pauling's bond order\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:B00705
consider: goldbook:B00707
consider: goldbook:CT07009
consider: goldbook:D01711
consider: goldbook:S05848

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07004
name: bond orbital
def: "A localized molecular orbital related to a certain $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$\, $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$\, or $\\ce\{\\updelta\\!\\mbox\{-\}bond\}$."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:M03996

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07005
name: bond order
def: "The electron population in the region between atoms A and B of a molecular entity at the expense of electron density in the immediate vicinity of the individual atomic centers. Different schemes of partitioning electron density give rise to different definitions of bond orders. In the framework of the Mulliken population analysis\, bond order is associated with the total overlap population\, \\\[\\rm\{q\}_\{\\rm\{AB\}\}=2\\ \\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}  \\end\{array\}\}^\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}  u  \\end\{array\}\}^\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\rm\{P\}_\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BD\} \}\\ \\rm\{S\}_\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BD\} \}\\\] where \\\(\\rm\{P\}_\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BD\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\rm\{S\}_\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BD\}\}\\\) are respectively the elements of the density matrix and overlap matrix \(see overlap integral\). A large positive value of bond order signifies strong bonding between the atoms of the molecular entity\, whereas negative values of \\\(\\rm\{q\}_\{\\rm\{AB\}\}\\\) imply that electrons are displaced away from the inter-atomic region and point to an anti-bonding interaction. In valence bond theory\, bond order is given by a weighted average of the formal bond orders \(i.e. by the number of electron pairs in a given Lewis structure\) between the atoms in the resonance structures \(see resonance hybrid\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:A00381
consider: goldbook:E01986
consider: goldbook:O04357
consider: goldbook:R05326
consider: goldbook:RT07094
consider: goldbook:V06588

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07006
name: bond-dissociation energy
def: "For a diatomic molecule\, the maximum vibrational energy that a molecule can have prior to its decomposition into the ground electronic states of the constituent atoms \(spectroscopic bond-dissociation energy\, \\\(D_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\)\). The \\\(D_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\) value is related to the chemical dissociation energy\: \\\(D_\{\\rm\{0\}\}=D_\{\\rm\{e\}\}- \\mathrm\{E\}_\{\\rm\{vib\}\}\\left\(0\\right\)\\\)\, where \\\(\\mathrm\{E\}_\{\\rm\{vib\}\}\\left\(0\\right\)\\\) is zero-point vibrational energy. This definition is usually extended to the dissociation of polyatomic molecules into certain molecular fragments through homolytic or heterolytic bond cleavages."
synonym: "BDE" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:B00699
consider: goldbook:C01281
consider: goldbook:D01547
consider: goldbook:D01801

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07007
name: bonding molecular orbital
def: "The molecular orbital whose occupation by electrons increases the total bonding \(usually\, lowers the total energy\) of a molecule. Generally\, the energy level of a bonding MO lies lower than the average of the valence orbitals of the atoms constituting the molecule."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:M03996
consider: goldbook:V06588

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07008
name: Born–Oppenheimer approximation
def: "Representation of the complete wavefunction as a product of an electronic and a nuclear part\, \\\[\\Psi \\left\(\\rm\{r\,R\}\\right\)=\\Psi _\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\left\(\\rm\{r\,R\}\\right\)\\ \\Psi _\{\\rm\{N\}\}\\left\(\\rm\{R\}\\right\)\\\] where the two wave-functions may be determined separately by solving two different Schroedinger equations. The validity of the Born–Oppenheimer approximation is founded on the fact that the ratio of electronic to nuclear mass \(\\\(\\frac\{m\}\{M\}\\cong 5\\times 10^\{-4\}\\\)\) is sufficiently small and the nuclei\, as compared to the rapidly moving electrons\, appear to be fixed. The approximation breaks down near a point where two electronic states acquire the same energy \(see Jahn–Teller effect\). The BO approximation is often considered as being synonymous with the adiabatic approximation. More precisely\, the latter term denotes the case when \\\(\\Psi _\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\) diagonalize the electronic Hamiltonian. Thus\, the adiabatic approximation is an application of the BO approximation."
synonym: "BO" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
synonym: "BO approximation" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:A00141
consider: goldbook:J03361
consider: goldbook:W06657

