[Term]
id: goldbook:C01246
name: confidence level
def: "The probability of covering the expected value of an estimated parameter with an interval estimated for the parameter \(symbol \\\(1 - \\alpha\\\)\). The confidence level can be expressed as a number between 0 and 1\, or in percent. The complementary quantity is known as the significance level."
comment: "In some cases the confidence level is dictated by the needs of the situation. In all other instances, use of \(1 - \alpha = 0.95\) is recommended."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466030595
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:M03796
consider: goldbook:P04483
consider: goldbook:P04855

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01247
name: confidence limits
def: "Symmetric confidence limits \(\\\(\\pm C\\\)\) about the estimated mean\, which cover the population mean with probability \\\(1 - \\alpha \\\). The quantity \\\(C\\\) is calculated by the formula\: \\\[C = \\frac\{t_\{\\rm\{p\}\,v^\{S\}\}\}\{\\sqrt\{n\}\}\\\] Here \\\(t_\{\\rm\{p\}\,v\}\\\)\, is the critical value from the \\\(t\\\)- \(or Student\) distribution function corresponding to the confidence level \\\(1 - \\alpha \\\) and degrees of freedom \\\(v\\\). The symbol \\\(p\\\) represents the percentile \(or percentage point\) of the \\\(t\\\)-distribution. For 1-sided intervals\, \\\(p=1- \\alpha \\\); for 2-sided intervals\, \\\(p=1- \\frac\{\\alpha \}\{2\}\\\). In each case\, the confidence level is \\\(1 - \\alpha \\\). The confidence interval is given as \\\(\\overline\{x\}\\ \\pm \\ C\\\)."
comment: "If the population standard deviation \(σ\) is known, confidence limits about a single result may be calculated with the formula: \[C = t_{\rm{p},\infty}\sigma\] The coefficient \(t_{p,\infty}\), is the limiting value of the \(t\)-distribution function for \(\nu = \infty\) at confidence level \(1 - \alpha\). This is identical to \(z_{\rm{p}}\), the \(p\)th percentage point of the standard normal variate."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466030595
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:C01124
consider: goldbook:C01246
consider: goldbook:D01572
consider: goldbook:P04855
consider: goldbook:S05911

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01248
name: configuration
def: "A distribution of the electrons of an atom or a molecular entity over a set of one-electron wavefunctions called orbitals\, according to the Pauli principle. From one configuration several states with different multiplicities may result. For example\, the ground electronic configuration of the oxygen molecule \($\\ce\{O2\}$\) is\: \\\(1\\sigma _\{\\rm\{g\}\}^\{2\}\\\)\, \\\(1\\sigma _\{\\rm\{u\}\}^\{2\}\\\)\, \\\(2\\sigma _\{\\rm\{g\}\}^\{2\}\\\)\, \\\(2\\sigma _\{\\rm\{u\}\}^\{2\}\\\)\, \\\(1\\pi _\{\\rm\{u\}\}^\{4\}\\\)\, \\\(3\\sigma _\{\\rm\{g\}\}^\{2\}\\\)\, \\\(1\\pi _\{\\rm\{g\}\}^\{2\}\\\) resulting in the \\\(^\{3\}\\Sigma_\{\\rm\{g\}\}\\\)\, \\\(^\{1\}\\Delta_\{\\rm\{g\}\}\\\) and \\\(^\{1\}\\Sigma_\{\\rm\{g\}\}^\{+\}\\\) multiplets."
synonym: "electron configuration" []
synonym: "electronic configuration" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466051077
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199668122223
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
