[Term]
id: goldbook:D01733
name: dilational transition
def: "A transition in which the crystal structure is dilated \(or compressed\) along one \(or more\) crystallographic direction\(s\) while the symmetry about that direction is retained. Examples\:The transition of a $\\ce\{CsCl\}$-type structure to a rock salt structure in which dilation occurs along the three-fold axis.The transition at \\\(T_\{\\rm\{D\}\}\\\) in quenched $\\ce\{NiS\}$ in which volume expansion occurs without change of symmetry on going from a metallic state \(\\\(T > T_\{\\rm\{D\}\}\\\)\) to a semiconducting state \(\\\(T < T_\{\\rm\{D\}\}\\\)\)."
synonym: "dilatational transition" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466030577
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:S05447

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01735
name: diluent
def: "The liquid or homogeneous mixture of liquids in which extractant\(s\) and possible modifier\(s\) may be dissolved to form the solvent phase."
comment: "Although the diluent may well be a single liquid or even the major portion of the extracting phase, the term solvent should not be used in this sense as it has a much wider meaning in the context of liquid-liquid extraction, although the term cosolvent may be used in certain circumstances."
comment: "The diluent by itself does not extract the main (extractable) solute appreciably."
comment: "The term carrier, which implies an inert diluent is not recommended."
synonym: "cosolvent" []
