[Term]
id: goldbook:D01780
name: discontinuous measuring cell
def: "A measuring cell which operates intermittently and not necessarily at fixed time intervals."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199062112167
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01782
name: discontinuous precipitation
def: "A diffusional reaction in a multi-component system in which structural and compositional changes occur in regions immediately adjacent to the advancing interface. The parent phase remains unchanged until swept over by the interface; the transition is complete in regions over which the interface has passed. Synonymous with nucleation and growth."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466030577
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:I03082
consider: goldbook:N04244

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01785
name: discriminator
def: "A basic function unit comprising an electronic circuit which gives an output pulse for each input pulse whose amplitude lies above a given threshold value."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac198254081533
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01790
name: disjoining pressure
def: "The force \\\(\\mathit\{\\Pi\} _\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\\) per unit area which can be obtained as the derivative of \\\(-g_\{\\rm\{t\}\}\\\) with respect to the distance\, where \\\(g_\{\\rm\{t\}\}\\\) is the total Gibbs energy of interaction per unit area of each of the two flat and parallel plates."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac197231040577
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01792
name: dispenser
def: "A device used to deliver a measured amount of material."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac198961091657
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:S05459

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01794
name: dispersion
def: "The dilution of a pollutant by spreading in the atmosphere due to diffusion or turbulent action \(eddy diffusion\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199062112167
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:D01716
consider: goldbook:E01888

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01797
name: displacement chromatography
def: "A procedure in which the mobile phase contains a compound \(the displacer\) more strongly retained than the components of the sample under examination. The sample is fed into the system as a finite slug."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199365040819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01798
name: displacive transition
def: "A transition in which a displacement of one or more kinds of atoms or ions in a crystal structure changes the lengths and/or directions of bonds\, without severing the primary bonds. Examples\: The transitions of the low-temperature polymorphs of $\\ce\{SiO2\}$ \(quartz\, tridymite and cristobalite\) to their respective high-temperature polymorphs\, which involve distortions or rotations of the $\\ce\{SiO4\}$ tetrahedra. Also Jahn–Teller and ferroic transitions."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466030577
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:F02348
consider: goldbook:J03362

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01799
name: disproportionation
def: "Any chemical reaction of the type \, where A\, A' and A'' are different chemical species. For example\:  The reverse of disproportionation is called comproportionation. A special case of disproportionation \(or 'dismutation'\) is 'radical disproportionation'\, exemplified by\:  Reactions of the more general type\:  are also loosely described as 'radical disproportionations'."
synonym: "dismutation" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466051077
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:C01217

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01801
name: dissociation
def: "The separation of a molecular entity into two or more molecular entities \(or any similar separation within a polyatomic molecular entity\). Examples include unimolecular heterolysis and homolysis\, and the separation of the constituents of an ion pair into free ions.   The separation of the constituents of any aggregate of molecular entities.   In both senses dissociation is the reverse of association."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466051077
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:A00472
consider: goldbook:H02851
consider: goldbook:I03231
consider: goldbook:M03986
consider: goldbook:M03989

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01803
name: dissociative adsorption
def: "Adsorption with dissociation into two or more fragments\, both or all of which are bound to the surface of the adsorbent. The process may be homolytic\, as in the chemisorption of hydrogen\: \\\[\\rm\{H\}_\{2\}+2\\rm\{*\}\\rightarrow 2\\rm\{H\}^\{\\rm\{*\}\}\\\] where * represents a surface site\, or heterolytic\, as in\: \\\[\\rm\{H\}_\{2\}+\\rm\{M\}^\{n+\}+\\rm\{O\}^\{2-\}\\rightarrow \\rm\{H\}^\{-\}\\rm\{M\}^\{n+\}+\\rm\{HO\}^\{-\}\\\] where \\\(\\rm\{M\}^\{n+\}\\\) and \\\(\\rm\{O\}^\{2- \}\\\) are surface sites in which the ions are of lower coordination than the ions in the bulk phase. Associative desorption is the reverse of dissociative adsorption."
synonym: "dissociative chemisorption" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac197646010071
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:A00153
consider: goldbook:A00155
consider: goldbook:C01329
consider: goldbook:D01801

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01804
name: dissociative ionization
def: "An ionization process in which a gaseous molecule decomposes to form products\, one of which is an ion."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199163101541
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:I03183

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01805
name: dissociative surface reaction
def: "Dissociative surface reaction and its reverse\, associative surface reaction\, are defined by\: \\\[2^\{\\rm\{*\}\}+\\rm\{C\}_\{2\}\\rm\{H\}_\{5\}^\{\\rm\{*\}\}\\rightleftharpoons \\rm\{H\}^\{\\rm\{*\}\}+\\\,^\{\\rm\{*\}\}\{\\rm\{CH\}_\{2\}\\rm\{CH\}_\{2\}\}^\{\\rm\{*\}\}\\\] where * represents a surface site."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac197646010071
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01806
name: dissolution
def: "The mixing of two phases with the formation of one new homogeneous phase \(i.e. the solution\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466030577
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01809
name: distonic radical cation
def: "A radical cation in which charge and radical sites are separated."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466051077
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:R05073

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01811
name: distribution
def: "The apportionment of a solute between two phases. The term partition or extraction may also be used in this sense where appropriate."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199365112373
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:E02301
consider: goldbook:P04435

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01814
name: distribution constant
def: "The concentration of a component in or on the stationary phase divided by the concentration of the component in the mobile phase. Since in chromatography a component may be present in more than one form \(e.g. associated and dissociated forms\)\, the analytical condition used here refers to the total amount present without regard to the existence of various forms. These terms are also called the distribution coefficients. However\, the present term conforms more closely to the general usage in science."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199365040819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:C01075

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01817
name: distribution ratio
def: "The ratio of the total analytical concentration of a solute in the extract \(regardless of its chemical form\) to its total analytical concentration in the other phase."
comment: "If there is possible confusion with the extraction factor or (mass) distribution ratio the term concentration distribution ratio (symbol&nbsp;\(D_\rm{c}\)) should be used, but this is not common usage. This is reasonably compatible with chromatographic nomenclature."
comment: "In equations relating to aqueous/organic systems the organic phase concentration is, by convention, the numerator and the aqueous phase concentration the denominator. In the case of stripping ratio the opposite convention is sometimes used but should then be clearly specified."
comment: "In the past there has been much confusion between the distribution ratio as defined above, the value of which varies with experimental conditions, e.g. \(\rm{pH}\), presence of complexing agents, extent of achievement of equilibrium etc. and the true partition coefficient which is by definition invariable or the partition coefficient or distribution constant which apply to a particular chemical species under specified conditions. For this reason the terms distribution constant, partition constant, partition coefficient, partition ratio and extraction constant should not be used in this context."
comment: "The distribution ratio is an experimental parameter and its value does not necessarily imply that distribution equilibrium between the phases has been achieved."
comment: "The terms distribution coefficient, extraction coefficient and, where appropriate, scrubbing coefficient, stripping coefficient are widely used alternatives but are not recommended. If they must be used in a given situation the term ratio is preferable to coefficient."
comment: "The use of the ratio: light phase concentration to heavy phase concentration is ambiguous and is not recommended."
synonym: "distribution coefficient" []
synonym: "extraction coefficient" []
synonym: "scrubbing coefficient" []
synonym: "stripping coefficient" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199365112373
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199668040957
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
