[Term]
id: goldbook:E01952
name: electrode current density
def: "If the charging current is negligible\, in the case of a single electrode reaction\, the electrode current density \(\\\(\\rm\{c.d.\}\\\)\) of the electric current flowing through the electrode is related to the flux density of a species B by the equation\: \\\[j = n\\  u _\{\\rm\{B\}\}^\{-1\}\\ F\\ \\left\(N_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\right\)_\{e\}\\\] where \\\(\\left\(N_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\right\)_\{e\}\\\) is the normal component of the vector \\\(N_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) at the electrode-solution interface\, \\\(n\\\) is the charge number of the electrode reaction and \\\( u _\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the stoichiometric number of species B. The ratio \\\(\\frac\{n\}\{ u _\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\\\) is to be taken as positive if the species B is consumed in a cathodic reaction or produced in an anodic reaction. Otherwise it is to be taken as negative. With the convention that the normal distance vector points into the electrolytic solution\, a cathodic current is then negative\, an anodic current positive."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac198153101827
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
