[Term]
id: goldbook:E02302
name: extraction
def: "In connection with processes\, this term often refers to the initial transfer step whereby the main solute\, often together with impurities\, is transferred from feed to solvent."
comment: "Partition and distribution are not synonymous in this specific instance."
comment: "The term extraction may be used in a more general sense."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199365112373
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:E02301
consider: goldbook:L03599
consider: goldbook:P04435

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02304
name: extraction constant
def: "The extraction constant at zero ionic strength\, \\\(K_\{\\rm\{ex\}\}^\{\\rm\{0\}\}\\\)\, is the equilibrium constant of the distribution reaction expressed in terms of the reacting species. Thus\, for the gross reaction\: \\\[\\rm\{M\}_\{\\rm\{aq\}\}^\{n+\} + n\\rm\{HL\}_\{\\rm\{org\}\} \\rightleftharpoons  \\rm\{ML\}_\{n\,\\rm\{org\}\} + n\\rm\{H\}_\{\\rm\{aq\}\}^\{+\}\\\] in which the reagent $\\ce\{HL\}$ initially dissolved in an organic phase reacts with a metal ion $\\ce\{M^\{n\}\}$ in aqueous solution to form a product $\\ce\{ML_\{n\}\}$ which is more soluble in the organic phase than in water\, \\\[K_\{\\rm\{ex\}\}^\{\\rm\{0\}\} = \\frac\{a_\{\\rm\{ML \} n\,\\rm\{org\}\}\\hspace\{2pt\}a_\{\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\,\\rm\{aq\}\}^\{n\}\}\{a_\{\\rm\{M\}^\{n+\}\,\\rm\{aq\}\}\\hspace\{2pt\}a_\{\\rm\{HL\}\,\\rm\{org\}\}^\{n\}\}\\\]"
comment: "In actual practice, it may be necessary to include other terms to take into account other complexes formed by auxiliary reagents and the solvation and/or polymerization of the various species. In such cases, \(K_{\rm{ex}}\) must be defined with reference to the relevant explicit chemical equation. An example is complex formation between the metal ion and an uncharged crown ether or cryptand molecule followed by ion-pair extraction: \[\rm{M}_{\rm{aq}}^{n+} + \rm{L}_{\rm{org}} + n\rm{A}_{\rm{aq}}^{-} \rightleftharpoons  \left ( \rm{ML}^{n+}.\rm{A}_{n}^{n-} \right )_{\rm{org}}\] \[K_{\rm{ex}} = \frac{[\ce{ML^{n+}} \ce{A_{n}^{n-}}]_{\rm{org}}}
{[\ce{M_{aq}^{n+}}] [\ce{L}]_{\rm{org}} [\ce{A^{-}}]_{\rm{aq}}^{n}}
\]"
comment: "In distribution equilibria involving non-aqueous systems, e.g. liquid $\ce{SO2}$, molten salts and metals, the mass action equilibrium constant for the relevant extraction process can be identified with \(K_{\rm{ex}}\) which should be explicitly defined in this context."
comment: "The extraction constant is related to other terms relevant to such systems by: \[K_{\rm{ex}} = \frac{D_{\rm{ML},n}\,\beta_{n}\,K_{a}^{n}}{D_{\rm{HL}}}\] where \(\beta_{n}\) is the overall formation constant of $\ce{ML_{n}}$ and \(K_{a}\) is the dissociation constant of $\ce{HL}$. Where the reagent $\ce{HL}$ is more soluble in water than the other immiscible phase it may be more convenient to define a special equilibrium constant in terms of \(\rm{HL}_{\rm{aq}}\): \[K_{\rm{ex}}^{0} = D_{\rm{ML},n}\,\beta_{n}\,K_{a}^{n}\]"
comment: "The phases can also be specified by the formula of the solvent or by other symbols (preferably Roman numerals) or by overlining formulae referring to one phase, usually the less polar one. The subscript aq (or w) is often omitted; aq is preferable to w as the latter is appropriate only in English and German."
comment: "The qualification 'equilibrium' is often omitted."
comment: "The terms partition constant and distribution constant must not be used in this sense."
comment: "Use of Ringbom's 'conditional extraction constant', \[K_{\rm{ex}}^{\rm{eff}} = \frac{{a_{\rm{H}^{+}}^{n}}^{n} [ \rm{ML}_{n}' ]_{\rm{org}}}{ [ \rm{M}' ]_{\rm{aq}} [ {\rm{HL}'  ]_{\rm{org}}}^{n}}\] in conjunction with alpha coefficients is useful."
comment: "When concentrations are used instead of activities or mixed terms are employed as when $\ce{H^{+}}$ and/or $\ce{M^{n}}$ are measured with an electrode, the appropriate name is extraction constant, symbol \(K_{\rm{ex}}\), accompanied by a careful definition. \(K_{\rm{ex}}^{\rm{0}}\) may be termed the thermodynamic extraction constant."
synonym: "extraction equilibrium constant" []
