[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07028
name: energy gradient
def: "First derivatives of the total energy with respect to nuclear coordinates\, i.e.\, negative values of forces on the nuclei. Evaluation of energy gradient plays a central role in searching potential energy surfaces for stationary points. A widespread technique is based on the minimization of the gradient norm \(the square of energy gradient\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:G02669

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07029
name: energy hypersurface
def: "The notion of hypersurface is used to stress the multidimensionality of PESs. In a molecular system consisting of \\\(N\\\) atomic nuclei\, the number of the independent coordinates that fully determine the PES is equal to \\\(3\\ N-6\\\) \(or \\\(3\\ N-5\\\) if the system is linear\)."
synonym: "PES" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
synonym: "potential energy surface" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07030
name: equalization of electronegativity\, principle of
def: "The postulate that in a molecule all the constituent atoms should have the same electronegativity value\, which would be the geometric mean of the electronegativities of isolated atoms."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:C01281
consider: goldbook:E01990

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07031
name: equilibrium geometry
def: "Molecular geometry that corresponds to the true minimum on the respective potential energy surface. While information relating to the equilibrium geometry is provided by calculations within the adiabatic approximation \(minimization of the total energy with respect to any independent geometrical parameter\)\, various experiments yield some effective geometries for the molecule which are averaged over molecular vibrations."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:A00141

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07032
name: extended Hückel MO method
def: "A semi-empirical all-valence electron quantum mechanical method which uses the same approximations\, apart from $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}approximation\}$ and neglect of overlap integrals\, as those of the Hückel molecular orbital theory. The method reproduces relatively well the shapes and the order of energy levels of molecular orbitals. The account for overlap makes it possible to describe the net destabilization caused by interaction of two doubly occupied orbitals\, which effect is not reproduced by HMO theory."
synonym: "EHMO" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
synonym: "EHMO method" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:M03996
consider: goldbook:V06588

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07034
name: exchange repulsion
def: "The correction to the Coulomb repulsion between two electrons in orbitals \\\(\\Psi _\{i\}\\\) and \\\(\\Psi _\{j\}\\\) for the case when the electrons possess parallel spins. It is to be subtracted from the Coulomb repulsion to give the total energy of the electron–electron interaction. In the Hartree–Fock theory the magnitude of the exchange repulsion is given by the exchange integral \\\[K_\{ij\}=\\int \\int \\Psi _\{i\}^\{\\rm\{*\}\}\\left\(\\mathbf\{r\}_\{1\}\\right\)\\ \\Psi _\{j\}^\{\\rm\{*\}\}\\left\(\\mathbf\{r\}_\{1\}\\right\)\\ \\left\(\\frac\{e^\{2\}\}\{r_\{12\}\}\\right\)\\ \\Psi _\{i\}\\left\(\\mathbf\{r\}_\{2\}\\right\)\\ \\Psi _\{j\}\\left\(\\mathbf\{r\}_\{2\}\\right\)  \\ \\mathrm\{d\}\\mathbf\{r\}_\{1\}  \\ \\mathrm\{d\}\\mathbf\{r\}_\{2\}=\\lt ij|ji \\gt\\\] For the case of electrons with opposite spins \\\(K_\{ij\}\\\) vanishes."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:CT07013
consider: goldbook:H02747

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07171
name: electroluminescent polymer
def: "Polymeric material that shows luminescence when an electric current passes through it such that charge carriers can combine at luminescent sites to give rise to electronically excited states of luminescent groups or molecules."
comment: "Electrogenerated chemiluminescence directly connected with electrode reactions may also be called electroluminescence."
comment: "Electroluminescent polymers are often made by incorporating luminescent groups or dyes into conducting polymers."
