[Term]
id: goldbook:H02867
name: Hückel \(4<em>n</em> + 2\) rule
def: "Monocyclic planar \(or almost planar\) systems of trigonally \(or sometimes digonally\) hybridized atoms that contain $\\ce\{\(4n + 2\) \\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electrons\}$ \(where $n$ is a non-negative integer\) will exhibit aromatic character. The rule is generally limited to $n = 0 – 5$. This rule is derived from the Hückel MO calculation on planar monocyclic conjugated hydrocarbons $\\ce\{\(CH\)_\{m\}\}$ where $m$ is an integer equal to or greater than 3 according to which $\\ce\{\(4n + 2\) \\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electrons\}$ are contained in a closed-shell system. Examples of systems that obey the Hückel rule include\:  Systems containing $\\ce\{4n\\;\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electrons\}$ \(such as cyclobutadiene and the cyclopentadienyl cation\) are 'antiaromatic'."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466051077
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
