[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07050
name: hydrogen bond
def: "A particular type of multicenter \(three center - four electron\) $\\ce\{X–H...Y\}$ in which the central hydrogen atom covalently linked to an electronegative atom $\\ce\{X\}$ \($\\ce\{C\}$\, $\\ce\{N\}$\, $\\ce\{O\}$\, $\\ce\{S\}$...\) forms an additional weaker bond with atom $\\ce\{Y\}$ \($\\ce\{N\}$\, $\\ce\{O\}$\, $\\ce\{S\}$...\) in the direction of its lone electron pair orbital. The energy of hydrogen bonds\, which is usually in the range of \\\(\\pu\{3\\!\{-\}\\! 15 kcal/mol\}\\\) \(\\\(\\pu\{12\\!\{-\}\\! 65 kJ/mol\}\\\)\)\, results from the electrostatic interaction and also from the orbital interaction of the antibonding $\\upsigma^\{\\ast\}\(\\ce\{XH\}\)MO$ of the molecule acting as the hydrogen donor and the non-bonding lone electron pair $MO\{\\rm\{n\}\}_\{\\rm\{Y\}\}$ of the hydrogen acceptor molecule."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07051
name: hypercoordination
def: "A property of main-group atoms in molecular entities to acquire coordination numbers greater than four \(which would comply with the Lewis octet rule\). Hypercoordination may be associated with hypervalency\, but usually is referred to peculiar atomic centres in the electron-deficient species with multicentre $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bonding\}$\, in which the bonding power of a pair of electrons is spread over more than two atoms. An example of a hypercoordinated atom is the five-coordinate carbon atom in the methanium cation\, where three $\\ce\{C–H\}$ bonds may be regarded as normal two center - two electron bonds and the bonding in the remaining $\\ce\{CH2\}$ fragment is governed by the three-centre\, two-electron bond. A particular case of a hypercoordinated atom is the hydrogen atom included into a hydrogen bond."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:C00907
consider: goldbook:C01329
consider: goldbook:H02899
consider: goldbook:HT07054
consider: goldbook:LT07065
consider: goldbook:P04792

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07052
name: hyperfine coupling
def: "The interaction between the spin magnetic moment of an unpaired electron and the nuclear spin magnetic moments resulting in the splitting of the $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$ \(spin up\) and $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\}$ \(spin down\) energy levels in an external magnetic field and\, thus\, in the multiplet pattern of the ESR spectra of radical-like species and transition metal compounds. Two main contributions to the hyperfine coupling are usually considered\, Fermi contact and dipolar interactions. The contact interaction is isotropic and related to the unpaired spin density at the nucleus\, \\\(|\\psi _\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3C3\}\}|^\{2\}\\\). The dipolar interaction is anisotropic\, and related to \\\(r^\{-3\}\\\)\, where \\\(r\\\) is the distance between the atom holding the unpaired electron and the nucleus with non-zero spin."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:C01025
consider: goldbook:F02339
consider: goldbook:I03353
consider: goldbook:N04256
consider: goldbook:S05864

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07053
name: hyperpolarizability
def: "The energy of a molecule in an external electrostatic field can be expanded as \\\[E=E^\{\\rm\{o\}\}- \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}_\{i\}\\ F_\{i\}- \\frac\{1\}\{2\}\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} _\{ij\}\\ F_\{i\}\\ F_\{j\}- \\frac\{1\}\{6\}\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} _\{ijk\}\\ F_\{i\}\\ F_\{j\}\\ F_\{k\}- \\frac\{1\}\{24\}\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B3\} _\{ijkl\}\\ F_\{i\}\\ F_\{j\}\\ F_\{k\}\\ F_\{l\}- \\rm\{...\}\\\] where \\\(E^\{\\rm\{o\}\}\\\) is the unperturbed energy\, \\\(F_\{i\}\\\) is the component of the field in the i direction\, \\\(\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}_\{i\}\\\) is the permanent dipole moment\, \\\(\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\}_\{ij\}\\\) is the polarizability tensor\, and \\\(\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\}_\{ijk\}\\\) and \\\(\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B3\}_\{ijkl\}\\\) are the first and second hyperpolarizability tensors\, respectively. \\\(\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\}\\\) is a third order symmetric tensor that measures the second order response of the molecular electric dipole moment to the action of an external electric field and is thus often referred to as dipole hyperpolarizability."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:D01761
consider: goldbook:E01929
consider: goldbook:P04711

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07054
name: hypervalency
def: "The ability of an atom in a molecular entity to expand its valence shell beyond the limits of the Lewis octet rule. Hypervalent compounds are common for the second and subsequent row elements in groups 15–18 of the periodic table. A description of the hypervalent bonding implies a transfer of the electrons from the central \(hypervalent\) atom to the nonbonding molecular orbitals which it forms with \(usually more electronegative\) ligands. A typical example of the hypervalent bond is a linear three-centre\, four-electron bond\, e.g. that of $\\ce\{F_\{ap\}–P–F_\{ap\}\}$ fragment of $\\ce\{PF5\}$."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
