[Term]
id: goldbook:I03142
name: inverse isotope effect
def: "A kinetic isotope effect in which \\\(\\frac\{k^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\}\{k^\{\\rm\{h\}\}\}1\\\). The isotope effect will normally be 'normal' when the frequency differences between the isotopic transition state are smaller than in the reactants. Conversely\, an inverse isotope effect can be taken as evidence for an increase in the corresponding force constants on passing from the reactant to the transition state."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466051077
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:F02481
consider: goldbook:I03327
consider: goldbook:K03405
consider: goldbook:T06468

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03145
name: inverse square law
def: "The intensity of radiation from a point source in free space is inversely proportional to the square of the distance from the source. Presence of absorbers necessitates corrections to this law."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac198254081533
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:A00034

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03146
name: inversion
def: "A symmetry operation involving a centre of inversion \(\\\(i\\\)\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199668122193
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:P04956
consider: goldbook:R05397
consider: goldbook:W06653

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03147
name: inversion height
def: "The height above ground level at which there is change in sign of the normal temperature 'lapse rate'\, \\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}T\}\{\\rm\{d\}z\}\\\) \(the rate of change of the temperature with height\). Several temperature inversions may be present in the air over a given site at different altitudes as result of various meteorological factors."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199062112167
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03149
name: inversion point
def: "The temperature \(pressure\) at which one polymorph of a solid phase changes into another at constant pressure \(temperature\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466030577
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:P04748

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03151
name: inverted micelle
def: "The reversible formation of association colloids from surfactants in non-polar solvents leads to aggregates termed inverted \(or inverse\, reverse or reversed\) micelle. Such association is often of the type\: \\\[\\rm\{Monomer\}\\rightleftarrows \\rm\{Dimer\}\\rightleftarrows \\rm\{Trimer\}\\rightleftarrows \\rm\{ ... \}n\\rm\{-mer\}\\\] and the phenomenon of critical micelle concentration \(or an analogous effect\) is consequently not observed. In an inverted micelle the polar groups of the surfactants are concentrated in the interior and the lipophilic groups extend towards and into the non-polar solvent."
synonym: "inverse micelle" []
synonym: "reverse micelle (or reversed micelle)" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466051077
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:A00472
consider: goldbook:C01395
consider: goldbook:L03572
consider: goldbook:M03889
consider: goldbook:P04717

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03152
name: inverted region
def: "In plots relating rate constants to charges in standard Gibbs energy \(\\\(\\Delta G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\)\) for electron transfer a region may occur in which the rate constants decrease as the exergonicity of the reaction increases. This region is often referred to as the inverted region and its presence is predicted by the theory developed for outer sphere electron transfer for the case \\\(-\\Delta G^\{\\circ\} \\gt \\lambda \\\) in the Marcus equation\, \\\(\\lambda\\\) being the reorganization energy. Note the similarity to the energy gap law for radiationless conversion of an excited state."
synonym: "Marcus inverted region" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199668122223
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
