[Term]
id: goldbook:I03199
name: ionization energy
def: "Minimum energy required to eject an electron out of a neutral atom or molecule in its ground state. The adiabatic ionization energy refers to the formation of the molecular ion in its ground vibrational state and the vertical ionization energy applies to the transition to the molecular ion without change in geometry. This quantity was formerly called 'ionization potential'. The second ionization energy of an atom is the energy required to eject the second electron from the atom \(energy for the process $\\ce\{X^\{+\} -> X^\{2+\} + e^\{-\}\}$\)."
synonym: "ionization potential" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466051077
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:A00141
consider: goldbook:G02704
consider: goldbook:I03183
consider: goldbook:V06610

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03214
name: ionizing collision
def: "An ion/neutral species interaction in which an electron or electrons are stripped from the ion and/or the neutral species in the collision. Generally\, this term has come to be used to describe collisions of fast-moving ions with a neutral species in which the neutral species is ionized with no change in the number of charges carried by the ion. Care should be taken when this term is used to emphasize if charge stripping of the ion has taken place."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199163101541
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:S06054

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03215
name: ionizing power
def: "A term to denote the tendency of a particular solvent to promote ionization of an uncharged or\, less often\, charged solute. The term has been used both in a kinetic and in a thermodynamic context."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466051077
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:D01746
consider: goldbook:G02710
consider: goldbook:I03183
consider: goldbook:Z06737

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03216
name: ionizing radiation
def: "Any radiation consisting of directly or indirectly ionizing particles or a mixture of both\, or photons with energy higher than the energy of photons of ultraviolet light or a mixture of both such particles and photons."
