[Term]
id: goldbook:J03361
name: Jahn–Teller effect
def: "Molecular distortions due to an electronically degenerate @G02704-1@ state. For non-linear molecular entities in a geometry described by a point symmetry group possessing degenerate irreducible representations there always exists at least one non-totally symmetric vibration that makes electronically degenerate states unstable at this geometry. The nuclei are displaced to new equilibrium positions of lower symmetry causing a splitting of the originally degenerate states \(first-order Jahn–Teller effect\)."
comment: "Effect due to the odd terms in the vibronic perturbation expansion. In the case of molecules with a non-degenerate @G02704-2@ but with a low-lying degenerate excited state, distortions of proper symmetry arise that result in mixing of the ground and excited states, thereby lowering the ground-state energy (pseudo Jahn–Teller effect). The pseudo Jahn–Teller effect manifests itself in fluxional behaviour (see fluxional) and stereochemical non-rigidity of molecules and ions. The Jahn–Teller effect generates a surface crossing (e.g., a conical intersection) whereas a pseudo Jahn–Teller effect generates an avoided crossing."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200779030293
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
