[Term]
id: goldbook:L03515
name: lifetime
def: "Lifetime of a molecular entity\, which decays by first-order kinetics\, is the time needed for a concentration of the entity to decrease to \\\(1/\\rm\{e\}\\\) of its original value\, i.e.\, \\\(c\(t = \\tau\) = \\frac\{c\(t\\\,=\\\,0\)\}\{e\}\\\). Statistically\, it represents the life expectation of the entity. It is equal to the reciprocal of the sum of the first-order rate constants of all processes causing the decay of the molecular entity."
comment: "Lifetime is used sometimes for processes, which are not first order. However, in such cases, the lifetime depends on the initial concentration of the entity, or of a quencher and, therefore, only an initial or a mean lifetime can be defined. In this case it should be called 'decay time' (also 'apparent lifetime')."
comment: "Mathematical definition: \(\tau = \frac{1}{k} = \frac{1}{\sum_{i}k_{i} }\) with \(k_{i}\) the first-order rate constants for all decay processes of the decaying state."
comment: "Occasionally, the term half life (\(\tau_{1/2}\)) is used, representing the time needed for the concentration of an entity to decrease to one half of its original value, i.e., \(c(t = \tau_{1/2}) = \frac{c(t\,=\,0)}{2}\). For first-order reactions, \(\tau_{1/2} = \rm{ln}2\, \tau\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200779030293
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:H02716
consider: goldbook:Q05006

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03516
name: lifetime
def: "The time required for the luminescence intensity to decay from some initial value to \\\(\\frac\{1\}\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\) of that value \(\\\(\\rm\{e\} = 2.718\\\,...\\\)\). Lifetimes can be measured by 'phase fluorimetry' \(phosphorimetry\) where the phase shift between the sinusoidally modulated exciting light and the emitted light is measured."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac198456020231
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:F02416
consider: goldbook:L03641

