[Term]
id: goldbook:M03892
name: Michaelis–Menten kinetics
def: "The dependence of an initial rate of reaction upon the concentration of a substrate S that is present in large excess over the concentration of an enzyme or other catalyst \(or reagent\) E with the appearance of saturation behaviour following the Michaelis-Menten equation\: \\\[ u=\\frac\{V\\ \\left\[\\rm\{S\}\\right\]\}\{K_\{\\rm\{m\}\}+\\left\[\\rm\{S\}\\right\]\}\\\] where \\\( u\\\) is the observed initial rate\, \\\(V\\\) is its limiting value at substrate saturation \(i.e. \\\(\\left\[\\rm\{S\}\\right\]\\gg K_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\)\)\, and \\\(K_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\) the substrate concentration when \\\( u = \\frac\{V\}\{2\}\\\). The definition is experimental\, i.e. it applies to any reaction that follows an equation of this general form. The symbols \\\(V_\{\\max \}\\\) or \\\( u_\{\\max \}\\\) are sometimes used for \\\(V\\\). The parameters \\\(V\\\) and \\\(K_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\) \(the 'Michaelis constant'\) of the equation can be evaluated from the slope and intercept of a linear plot of \\\( u^\{-1\}\\\) vs. \\\(\\left\[\\rm\{S\}\\right\]^\{-1\}\\\) \(a 'Lineweaver–Burk plot'\) or from slope and intercept of a linear plot of \\\( u\\\) vs. \\\(\\frac\{ u\}\{\\left\[\\rm\{S\}\\right\]\}\\\) \('Eadie–Hofstee plot'\). A Michaelis–Menten equation is also applicable to the condition where E is present in large excess\, in which case the concentration \\\(\\left\[\\rm\{E\}\\right\]\\\) appears in the equation instead of \\\(\\left\[\\rm\{S\}\\right\]\\\). The term has sometimes been used to describe reactions that proceed according to the scheme\: \\\[\\rm\{E\}+\\rm\{S\}\\overset\{k_\{1\}\}\{\\underset\{k_\{-1\}\}\\rightleftarrows \}\\rm\{ES\}\\overset\{k_\{\\rm\{cat\}\}\}\{\\rightarrow \}\\rm\{Products\}\\\] in which case \\\(K_\{\\rm\{m\}\} = \\frac\{k_\{-1\}+k_\{\\rm\{cat\}\}\}\{k_\{1\}\}\\\) \(Briggs–Haldane conditions\). It has more usually been applied only to the special case in which \\\(k_\{-1\}\\gg k_\{\\rm\{cat\}\}\\\) and \\\(K_\{\\rm\{m\}\} = \\frac\{k_\{-1\}\}\{k_\{1\}\}=K_\{s\}\\\); in this case \\\(K_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\) is a true dissociation constant \(Michaelis–Menten conditions\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466051077
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199668010149
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
