[Term]
id: goldbook:M04030
name: More O'Ferrall–Jencks diagram
def: "Visualization of the potential energy surfaces for a reacting system\, as a function of two chosen coordinates. It is particularly useful to discuss structural effects on the transition state geometry for processes occurring either by stepwise or concerted routes. The use of such diagrams\, first suggested for elimination reactions\, was later extended to acid–base catalysis and to certain other reactions."
comment: "Structural changes influencing vibrational modes of the transition states cause changes in transition state geometry. Changes in the direction of the reaction coordinate (reactant or product stabilizing- or destabilizing factors) cause changes according to the Hammond principle. Structural changes perpendicular to the reaction coordinate (anti-Hammond effects, perpendicular effects) cause changes opposite to the Hammond behavior, i.e., the easier the process related to the structural change, the more advanced it will be at the transition state."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466051077
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
