[Term]
id: goldbook:N04187
name: non-crystalline electrodes
def: "Electrodes in which a support matrix\, containing an ion exchanger \(either cationic or anionic\)\, a plasticizer solvent\, and possibly an uncharged\, selectivity-enhancing species\, forms the ion-selective membrane which is usually interposed between two aqueous solutions. The support used can be either macroporous \[e.g. poly\(propylene carbonate\) filter\, glass frit\, etc.\] or microporous \(e.g. 'thirsty' glass or inert polymeric material such as PVC\) yielding with the ion-exchanger and the solvent a 'solidified' homogeneous mixture. These electrodes exhibit a response due to the presence of the ion-exchange material in the membrane. The solvent-polymeric-membrane is an example."
