[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07076
name: natural atomic orbital
def: "A valence-shell atomic orbital whose derivation involves diagonalising the localized block of the full density matrix of a given molecule associated with basis functions \\\(\\chi _\{i\}\\left\(\\rm\{A\}\\right\)\\\) on that atom. A distinguishing feature of NAOs is that they meet the simultaneous requirement of orthonormality and maximum occupancy. For isolated atoms\, NAOs coincide with natural orbitals. In a polyatomic molecule the NAOs \(in contrast to natural orbitals that become delocalised over all nuclear centres\) mostly retain one-centre character\, and thus are optimal for describing the molecular electron density around each atomic centre."
synonym: "NAO" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:B00682
consider: goldbook:E01986
consider: goldbook:V06588

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07077
name: natural bond orbital
def: "The orbital which is formed from natural hybrid orbitals. For a localized $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bond\}$ between atoms $\\ce\{A\}$ and $\\ce\{B\}$\, the NBO is\: \\\[\\sigma _\{\\rm\{AB\}\}=c_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\ \\rm\{h\}_\{\\rm\{A\}\}+c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ \\mathrm\{h\}_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\rm\{h\}_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\rm\{h\}_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) are the natural hybrids centered on atoms $\\ce\{A\}$ and $\\ce\{B\}$. NBOs closely correspond to the picture of localized bonds and lone pairs as basic units of molecular structure\, so that is possible to conveniently interpret ab initio wave-functions in terms of the classical Lewis structure concepts by transforming these functions to NBO form."
synonym: "NBO" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07078
name: natural hybrid orbital
def: "Symmetrically orthogonalised directed hybrid orbital derived through unitary transformation of natural atomic orbitals centred on a particular atom."
synonym: "NHO" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:HT07049
consider: goldbook:T06446

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07079
name: natural orbital
def: "The orbitals defined \(P. Lowdin\) as the eigenfunctions of the spinless one-particle electron density matrix. For a configuration interaction \(CI\) wave-function constructed from orbitals \\\(\\varPhi \\\)\, the electron density function\, \\\(\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3C1\}\\\)\, is of the form\: \\\[\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3C1\} = \\sum_\{i\}\\sum _\{j\}a_\{ij\}\\\,\\varPhi_\{i\}^\{*\}\\\,\\varPhi_\{j\}\\\] where the coefficients \\\(a_\{ij\}\\\) are a set of numbers which form the density matrix. The NOs reduce the density matrix \\\(\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3C1\}\\\) to a diagonal form\: \\\[\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3C1\} = \\sum _\{k\}b_\{k\}\\mathit\{\\Phi\}_\{k\}^\{*\}\\mathit\{\\Phi\}_\{k\}\\\] where the coefficients \\\(b_\{k\}\\\) are occupation numbers of each orbital. The importance of natural orbitals is in the fact that CI expansions based on these orbitals have generally the fastest convergence. If a CI calculation was carried out in terms of an arbitrary basis set and the subsequent diagonalisation of the density matrix \\\(\\rm\{a\}_\{ij\}\\\) gave the natural orbitals\, the same calculation repeated in terms of the natural orbitals thus obtained would lead to the wave-function for which only those configurations built up from natural orbitals with large occupation numbers were important."
synonym: "NO" RELATED OMO:0003012 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:BT06999
consider: goldbook:E01986
consider: goldbook:ET07024

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07080
name: natural population analysis
def: "The analysis of the electron density distribution in a molecular system based on the orthonormal natural atomic orbitals. Natural populations\, \\\(n_\{i\}\\left\(\\rm\{A\}\\right\)\\\) are the occupancies of the natural atomic orbitals. These rigorously satisfy the Pauli exclusion principle\: \\\(0 < n_\{i\}\\left\(\\rm\{A\}\\right\) < 2\\\). The population of an atom \\\(n\\left\(\\rm\{A\}\\right\)\\\) is the sum of natural populations \\\(n\\left\(\\rm\{A\}\\right\)=\\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\} \\rm\{A\} \\end\{array\}\}n_\{i\}\\left\(\\rm\{A\}\\right\)\\\). A distinguished feature of the NPA method is that it largely resolves the basis set dependence problem encountered in the Mulliken population analysis method."
synonym: "NPA" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:BT06999
consider: goldbook:E01986
consider: goldbook:PT07089

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07081
name: negative hyper-conjugation
def: "The effect of donation of electron density from filled $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\}$ or $\\ce\{n_\{\\uppi\\!\}\\mbox\{-\}orbitals\}$ to the symmetry adapted $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!^\{\\ast\}\\mbox\{-\}orbital\(s\)\}$ of $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!^\{\\ast\}\\mbox\{-\}bond\(s\)\}$ of a molecular entity resulting in building $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}character\}$ into bonds that nominally possess only $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}character\}$. The consequences of the effect are\, for example\, in elongation of the $\\ce\{C–F\}$ bond in the $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox\{-\}fluoroethyl\}$ anion and stabilization of the anti-periplanar conformation of fluoromethylamine."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199971101919
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
