[Term]
id: goldbook:P04524
name: pH
def: "The quantity pH is defined in terms of the activity of hydrogen\(1+\) ions \(hydrogen ions\) in solution\: \\\[\\rm\{pH\} = -\\rm\{lg\}\[a\(\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\)\] = -\\rm\{lg\}\[\\rm\{m\}\(\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\)\\gamma_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\(\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\)/m^\{\\unicode\{x29B5\} \}\]\\\] where \\\(a\(\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\)\\\) is the activity of hydrogen ion \(hydrogen 1+\) in aqueous solution\, \\\(\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\\rm\{\(aq\)\}\\\)\, \\\(\\gamma_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\(\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\)\\\) is the activity coefficient of \\\(\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\\rm\{\(aq\)\}\\\) \(molality basis\) at molality \\\(m\(\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\)\\\)\, and \\\(m^\{⦵\} = \\pu\{1 mol kg-1\}\\\) is the standard molality."
comment: "Practical pH measurements generally use cells with liquid junctions in which, consequently, liquid junction potentials, \(E_{\rm{j}}\), are present. Measurements of pH are not normally performed using the $\ce{Pt}$|$\ce{H2}$ electrode, but rather the glass (or other $\ce{H^{+}}$- selective) electrode, whose response factor (\(\rm{d}E/\rm{dpH}\)) usually deviates from the Nernst slope. The associated uncertainties are significantly larger than those associated with fundamental measurements using the Harned cell. Nonetheless, incorporation of the uncertainties for the primary method, and for all subsequent measurements, permits the uncertainties for all procedures to be linked to the primary standards by an unbroken chain of comparisons."
comment: "Reference values for standards in $\ce{D2O}$ and aqueous-organic solvent mixtures exist."
comment: "The establishment of primary pH standards requires the application of the concept of 'primary method of measurement', assuring full traceability of the results of all measurements and their uncertainties. Any limitation in the theory of determination of experimental variables must be included in the estimated uncertainty of the method."
comment: "The primary method for measurement of pH involves the use of a cell without transference, known as the Harned cell: $\ce{Pt(s)}$|$\ce{H2(g)}$|Buffer S, $\ce{Cl^{-}(aq)}$|$\ce{AgCl(s)}$|$\ce{Ag(s)}$ The equation for this cell can be rearranged to give: \[-\rm{lg}[a(\rm{H}^{+})\gamma(\rm{Cl}^{-})] = \frac{E - E^{\unicode{x29B5} }}{(R\:T\:\rm{ln}10)/F}+ \rm{lg}[m(\rm{Cl}^{-})/m^{\unicode{x29B5}}]\] where \(E\) is the potential difference of the cell and \(E^{\unicode{x29B5}}\) is the known standard potential of the $\ce{AgCl(s)}$|$\ce{Ag(s)}$ electrode. Measurements of \(E^{\unicode{x29B5}}\) as a function of \(m(\rm{Cl}^{-})\) are made and the quantity \(a(\rm{H}^{+})\gamma(\rm{Cl}^{-})\) (called the acidity function) is found by extrapolation to \(m(\rm{Cl}^{-})/m^{\unicode{x29B5}} = 0\). The value of \(\gamma(\rm{Cl}^{-})\) is calculated using the Bates–Guggenheim convention based on Debye–Hückel theory. Then \(\rm{lg}[a(\rm{H}^{+})]\) is calculated and identified as \(\rm{pH(PS)}\), where \(\rm{PS}\) signifies primary standard. The uncertainties in the two estimates are typically \(0.001\) in \(\rm{lg}[a(\rm{H}^{+})\gamma(\rm{Cl}^{-})]\) and \(0.003\) in pH. Materials for primary standard buffers must also meet the appropriate requirements for reference materials, including chemical purity and stability, and applicability of the Bates–Guggenheim convention for the estimation of \(-\rm{lg}[\gamma(\rm{Cl}^{-})]\). This convention requires that the ionic strength be \(\pu{\leq 0.1 mol kg-1}\). Primary standard buffers should also lead to small liquid junction potentials when used in cells with liquid junctions. Secondary standards, \(\rm{pH(SS)}\), are also available, but carry a greater uncertainty in measured values."
comment: "pH cannot be measured independently because calculation of the activity involves the activity coefficient of a single ion. Thus it can be regarded only as a notional definition."
