property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:A00543
consider: goldbook:C01019
consider: goldbook:Q04991

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04637
name: photon irradiance
def: "Number of photons \(quanta of radiation\, \\\(N_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\)\) per time interval \(photon flux\)\, \\\(q_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\)\, incident from all upward directions on a small element of surface containing the point under consideration divided by the area of the element. SI unit is \\\(\\rm\{m\}^\{-2\}\\ \\rm\{s\}^\{-1\}\\\). Equivalent definition\: Integral\, taken over the hemisphere visible from the given point\, of the expression \\\(L_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\, \\rm\{cos\}\\\:\\theta\\\, \\rm\{d\}\\varOmega\\\) the photon radiance at the given point in the various directions of the incident beam of solid angle \\\(\\varOmega\\\) and \\\(\\theta\\\) the angle between any of these beams and the normal to the surface at the given point."
comment: "Mathematical definition: \(E_{\rm{p}} = \rm{d}q_{\rm{p}}/\rm{d}S\). If the photon flux is constant over the surface considered, \(E_{\rm{p}} = q_{\rm{p}}/S\). Equivalent definition: \(E_{\rm{p}} = \int_{2\pi} L_{\rm{p}}\, \rm{cos}\:\theta\, \rm{d}\varOmega\)."
comment: "This quantity can be used on a chemical amount basis by dividing \(E_{\rm{p}}\) by the Avogadro constant, the symbol then being \(E_{n\rm{,p}}\), the name 'photon irradiance, amount basis', SI unit is \(\pu{mol m-2 s-1}\); common unit is \(\pu{einstein m-2 s-1}\)."
comment: "This term refers to a beam not scattered or reflected by the target or its surroundings. For a beam incident from all directions photon fluence rate (\(E_{\rm{p,o}}\)) is an equivalent term."
synonym: "SI" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200779030293
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:A00346
consider: goldbook:A00543
consider: goldbook:C01019
consider: goldbook:P04635
consider: goldbook:P04639
consider: goldbook:VT07496

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04639
name: photon radiance
def: "Number of photons \(quanta of radiation\, \\\(N_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\)\) per time interval \(photon flux\)\, \\\(q_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\)\, leaving or passing through a small transparent element of surface in a given direction from the source about the solid angle \\\(\\varOmega\\\)\, divided by the solid angle and by the orthogonally projected area of the element in a plane normal to the given beam direction\, \\\(\\rm\{d\}S_\{\\perp \} = \\rm\{d\}S\\\, cos\\\,\\theta\\\)\, with \\\(\\theta\\\) the angle between the normal to the surface and the direction of the beam. Equivalent definition\: Integral taken over the hemisphere visible from the given point\, of the expression \\\(L_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\, cos\\\,\\theta\\\,\\rm\{d\}\\varOmega\\\)\, with \\\(L_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\) the photon radiance at the given point in the various directions of the incident beam of solid angle \\\(\\varOmega\\\) and \\\(\\theta\\\) the angle between any of these beams and the normal to the surface at the given point."
comment: "For a parallel beam it is the number of photons (quanta of radiation, \(N_{\rm{p}}\)) per time interval (photon flux), \(q_{\rm{p}}\), leaving or passing through a small element of surface in a given direction from the source divided by the orthogonally projected area of the element in a plane normal to the given direction of the beam, \(\theta\). Mathematical definition in this case: \(L_{\rm{p}} = \rm{d}q_{\rm{p}}/\left ( \rm{d}S\, cos\,\theta \right )\) If \(q_{\rm{p}}\) is constant over the surface area considered, \(L_{\rm{p}} = q_{\rm{p}}/\left ( S\, cos\,\theta \right )\), SI unit is \(\rm{m}^{-2}\ \rm{s}^{-1}\)."
comment: "Mathematical definition: \[L_{\rm{p}} = \frac{\rm{d}^{2}q_{p}}{\rm{d}\varOmega \, \rm{d}S_{\perp }} = \frac{\rm{d}^{2}q_{p}}{\rm{d}\varOmega \, \rm{d}S\, cos\,\theta}\] for a divergent beam propagating in an elementary cone of the solid angle \(\varOmega\) containing the direction \(\theta\). SI unit is \(\rm{m}^{-2}\ \rm{s}^{-1}\ \rm{sr}^{-1}\)."
comment: "This quantity can be used on a chemical amount basis by dividing \(L_{\rm{p}}\) by the Avogadro constant, the symbol then being \(L_{n\rm{,p}}\), the name 'photon radiance, amount basis'. For a divergent beam SI unit is \(\pu{mol m-2 s-1 sr-1}\); common unit is \(\pu{einstein m-2 s-1 sr-1}\). For a parallel beam SI unit is \(\pu{mol m-2 s-1}\); common unit is \(\pu{einstein m-2 s-1}\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200779030293
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
