[Term]
id: goldbook:P04756
name: polytypic transition
def: "A transition of a crystalline structure into one or more forms which differ in the way identical layers of atoms are stacked. Example\: $\\ce\{ZnS\}$ consists of two identical close packings\, one of $\\ce\{Zn\}$ atoms\, the other $\\ce\{S\}$ atoms\, with the one displaced to the other along the c-axis through one-quarter of the layer spacing. In sphalerite-type $\\ce\{ZnS\}$ the layers have the face-centred-cubic \(ABC ABC\) sequence\, in wurtzite-type $\\ce\{ZnS\}$ they have the hexagonal-close-packed \(AB AB\) sequence. The transition of sphalerite-type $\\ce\{ZnS\}$ to wurtzite-type $\\ce\{ZnS\}$ occurs at \\\(1297\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466030577
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
