[Term]
id: goldbook:P04930
name: pseudohalogens
def: "Compounds that resemble the halogen elements\, $\\ce\{X2\}$\, in their chemistry\, e.g. $\\ce\{\(CN\)2\}$ cyanogen\, $\\ce\{\(SCN\)2\}$ thiocyanogen\, $\\ce\{ICN\}$ iodine cyanide. Certain ions that have sufficient resemblance to halide ions are sometimes referred to as pseudohalide ions\, e.g. $\\ce\{N3^\{−\}\}$\, $\\ce\{SCN^\{−\}\}$\, $\\ce\{CN^\{−\}\}$."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199567081307
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04932
name: pseudopericyclic
def: "A concerted transformation is pseudopericyclic if the primary changes in bonding occur within a cyclic array of atoms at one \(or more\) of which nonbonding and bonding atomic orbitals interchange roles. A formal example is the enol \\\(\\rightarrow \\\) enol prototropy of pentane-2\,4-dione \(acetylacetone\).  Because the \\\(\\pi \\\)- and \\\(\\sigma \\\)-atomic orbitals that interchange roles are orthogonal\, such a reaction does not proceed through a fully conjugated transition state and is thus not a pericyclic reaction and therefore not governed by the rules that express orbital symmetry restrictions applicable to pericyclic reactions."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466051077
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:C01234
consider: goldbook:C01267
consider: goldbook:O04320
consider: goldbook:P04491

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04933
name: pseudo rate constant
def: "A term sometimes used for a rate coefficient. For example\, if the rate equation is\: \\\[v=k\\ \\rm\{\[A\]\}\\ \\rm\{\[B\]\}\\\] the function \\\(k\\ \\rm\{\[A\]\}\\\) is the pseudo first-order rate constant \(or first-order rate coefficient\) with respect to B. Similarly\, \\\(k\\ \\rm\{\[B\]\}\\\) is the pseudo first-order rate constant \(or first-order rate coefficient\) with respect to A. The term pseudo rate constant is particularly useful when the concentration of one reactant is much greater than that of another."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199668010149
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:O04322
consider: goldbook:R05137

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04934
name: pseudorotation
def: "Stereoisomerization resulting in a structure that appears to have been produced by rotation of the entire initial molecule and is superposable on the initial one\, unless different positions are distinguished by substitution\, including isotopic substitution. One example of pseudorotation is a facile interconversion between the many envelope and twist conformers of a cyclopentane due to the out of plane motion of carbon atoms. Another example of pseudorotation \(Berry pseudorotation\) is a polytopal rearrangement that provides an intramolecular mechanism for the isomerization of trigonal bipyramidal compounds \(e.g. \\\(\\lambda ^\{5\}\\\)-phosphanes\)\, the five bonds to the central atom E being represented as \\\(\\rm\{e\}^\{1\}\\\)\, \\\(\\rm\{e\}^\{2\}\\\)\, \\\(\\rm\{e\}^\{3\}\\\)\, \\\(\\rm\{a\}^\{1\}\\\) and \\\(\\rm\{a\}^\{2\}\\\). Two equatorial bonds move apart and become apical bonds at the same time as the apical bonds move together to become equatorial.  A related conformational change of a trigonal bipyramidal structure is described as turnstile rotation. The process may be visualized as follows.  An apical and an equatorial bond rotate as a pair ca. 120° relative to the other three bonds. \(Doubts have been expressed about the distinct physical reality of this mechanism.\)"
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199668122193
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
