consider: goldbook:ST06775

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05179
name: reaction time
def: "The period of time that elapses between the start of the reaction and the attainment of a given extent of a reaction."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199365102291
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05180
name: reactive
def: "As applied to a chemical species\, the term expresses a kinetic property. A species is said to be more reactive or to have a higher reactivity in some given context than some other \(reference\) species if it has a larger rate constant for a specified elementary reaction. The term has meaning only by reference to some explicitly stated or implicitly assumed set of conditions. It is not to be used for reactions or reaction patterns of compounds in general. The term is also more loosely used as a phenomenological description not restricted to elementary reactions. When applied in this sense the property under consideration may reflect not only rate\, but also equilibrium\, constants."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466051077
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:CT01038
consider: goldbook:E02035
consider: goldbook:O04322
consider: goldbook:S05900
consider: goldbook:U06567
consider: goldbook:U06569

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05181
name: reactive adsorption
def: "Reactive adsorption \(and its reverse\, reactive desorption\) resembles dissociative adsorption \(and its reverse\, dissociative adsorption\) but one fragment adds to an adsorbate rather than to a surface site."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac197646010071
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:A00152
consider: goldbook:D01803

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05182
name: reactive complex
def: "A species of very short life that occurs as an intermediate in a chemical reaction. An activated complex \(or transition state\) is a special case."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199668010149
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:A00092
consider: goldbook:T06468

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05184
name: reactive scattering
def: "If in a molecular-beam experiment a chemical reaction occurs there is said to be reactive scattering."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199668010149
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:M03982
consider: goldbook:S05487

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05185
name: reactivity index
def: "Any numerical index derived from quantum mechanical model calculations that permits the prediction of relative reactivities of different molecular sites. Many indices are in use\, based on a variety of theories and relating to various types of reaction. The more successful applications have been to the substitution reactions of conjugated systems where relative reactivities are determined largely by changes of $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electron\}$ energy."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466051077
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:C01267
consider: goldbook:S06078

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05186
name: reactivity–selectivity principle
def: "This idea may be expressed loosely as\: the more reactive a reagent is\, the less selective it is. Consider two substrates \\\(\\rm\{S\}^\{1\}\\\) and \\\(\\rm\{S\}^\{2\}\\\) undergoing the same type of reaction with two reagents \\\(\\rm\{R\}^\{1\}\\\) and \\\(\\rm\{R\}^\{2\}\\\)\, \\\(\\rm\{S\}^\{2\}\\\) being more reactive than \\\(\\rm\{S\}^\{1\}\\\)\, and \\\(\\rm\{R\}^\{2\}\\\) more reactive than \\\(\\rm\{R\}^\{1\}\\\) in the given type of reaction. The relative reactivities \(in log units\) for the four possible reactions may notionally be represented as shown in the diagram.  With the positions of \(\\\(\\rm\{S\}^\{1\}+\\rm\{R\}^\{1\}\\\)\)\, \(\\\(\\rm\{S\}^\{2\}+\\rm\{R\}^\{1\}\\\)\) and \(\\\(\\rm\{S\}^\{1\}+\\rm\{R\}^\{2\}\\\)\) fixed\, there are three types of positions for \(\\\(\\rm\{S\}^\{2\}+\\rm\{R\}^\{2\}\\\)\). In position \(i\) the selectivity of \\\(\\rm\{R\}^\{2\}\\\) for the two substrates\, measured by \\\(a\\\)\, is the same as the selectivity of \\\(\\rm\{R\}^\{1\}\\\) for the two substrates\, also \\\(a\\\). In position \(ii\) the selectivity of \\\(\\rm\{R\}^\{2\}\\\) for the two substrates\, measured by \\\(b\\\)\, is less than the selectivity of \\\(\\rm\{R\}^\{1\}\\\) for the two substrates\, i.e. \\\(b < a\\\). It is this situation which is in accord with the RSP. In position \(iii\) the selectivity of \\\(\\rm\{R\}^\{2\}\\\) for the two substrates\, measured by \\\(c\\\)\, is greater than the selectivity of \\\(\\rm\{R\}^\{1\}\\\) for the two substrates\, i.e. \\\(c > a\\\). This situation may be described as anti-RSP. There are many examples in which the RSP is followed\, but there are also many examples corresponding to situations \(i\) and \(iii\). The RSP is in accord with intuitive feeling and certainly holds in the limiting case when reactivity is controlled by diffusion. However\, the validity of the RSP is a matter of great controversy."
synonym: "RSP" RELATED OMO:0003000 []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466051077
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:A00381
consider: goldbook:D01716
consider: goldbook:R05180
consider: goldbook:R05190
consider: goldbook:S05563

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05189
name: reading
def: "Datum provided by an instrument."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac198961091657
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05192
name: real potential of a species in a phase
def: "Defined for species $\\ce\{B\}$ in phase $\\upbeta\\!$ as \\\[\\alpha _\{\\rm\{B\}\}^\{\\beta \} = \\mu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}^\{\\beta \}\\\,-\\\,z_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ F\\ \\psi ^\{\\beta \}\\\] where \\\(\\mu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}^\{\\beta \}\\\) is the electrochemical potential of species $\\ce\{B\}$ in phase $\\upbeta\\!$\, \\\(z_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the charge number of species $\\ce\{B\}$\, \\\(F\\\) is the Faraday constant\, and \\\(\\psi ^\{\\beta \}\\\) is the outer electric potential of phase $\\upbeta\\!$. Since \\\(\\psi ^\{\\beta \}\\\) is zero when the charge on the phase $\\upbeta\\!$ is zero\, the real potential may be regarded as the value of the electrochemical potential of the uncharged phase."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac197437040499
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:C00993
consider: goldbook:E01945
consider: goldbook:F02325
consider: goldbook:O04349

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05195
name: rearrangement ion
def: "An electrically charged species\, involving a molecular or parent ion\, in which atoms or groups of atoms have transferred from one portion of a molecule or molecular moiety to another during the ionization fragmentation processes."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199163101541
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
