[Term]
id: goldbook:R05212
name: reduced adsorption
def: "Of component \\\(i\\\)\, defined by the equation \\\[\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{i\}^\{\(n\)\} = \\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{i\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3C3\} \}- \\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{i\}\\ \\frac\{c_\{i\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \} - c_\{i\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\}\{c^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}- c^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\}\\\] where \\\(\\mathit\{\\Gamma \}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3C3\} \}\\\)\, \\\(c^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\\\) and \\\(c^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\}\}\\\) are\, respectively\, the total Gibbs surface concentration and the total concentrations in the bulk phases $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$ and $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\}$\: \\\[\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3C3\} \}=\\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}  i  \\end\{array\}\}\\mathit\{\\Gamma \}_\{i\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3C3\} \}\\\] \\\[c^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}=\\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}  i  \\end\{array\}\}c_\{i\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\\\] \\\[c^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}=\\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}  i  \\end\{array\}\}c_\{i\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\}\}\\\] The reduced adsorption is invariant to the location of the Gibbs surface. Alternatively\, the reduced adsorption may be regarded as the Gibbs surface concentration of \\\(i\\\) when the Gibbs surface is chosen so that \\\(\\mathit\{\\Gamma \}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3C3\} \}\\\) is zero\, i.e. the Gibbs surface is chosen so that the reference system has not only the same volume\, but also contains the same total amount of substance\(\\\(n\\\)\) as the real system."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac197231040577
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:A00297
consider: goldbook:G02635

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05215
name: reduced mobile phase velocity
def: "A term used mainly in liquid chromatography. It compares the mobile phase velocity with the velocity of diffusion into the pores of the particles \(the so-called diffusion velocity\, \\\(u_\{\\rm\{D\}\}\\\)\)\: \\\[ u=\\frac\{\\overline\{u\}\}\{u_\{\\rm\{D\}\}\}=\\frac\{\\overline\{u\}\\ d_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\}\{D_\{\\rm\{M\}\}\}\\\] In open-tubular chromatography\: \\\[ u=\\frac\{\\overline\{u\}\\ d_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\}\{D_\{\\rm\{M\}\}\}\\\]"
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199365040819
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:C01075
consider: goldbook:D01716

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05216
name: reduced osmotic pressure
def: "The osmotic pressure divided by mass concentration."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac197231040577
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:O04344

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05217
name: reduced sample
def: "A representative part of the primary \(composited or gross\) sample obtained by a division and reduction process. Typically the mass approximates that of the final laboratory sample."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199062061193
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:L03433

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05219
name: reduced species
def: "A term used to characterize the degree of reduction \(or oxidation\) in atoms\, molecules and ions. An atom in a molecule or an ion which has a low oxidation state. An element or atom in a compound can be reduced by the reaction of an element or compound with hydrogen\, while it can be oxidized by reaction with oxygen. A reduced species can be formed also through the gain of electrons \(either at the negative electrode in a cell or through transfer from another atom\, ion or group of atoms in a chemical reaction\). For example\, the carbon atom in $\\ce\{CH4\}$ and other hydrocarbons is in a reduced state\, while the carbon in $\\ce\{CO2\}$ is in an oxidized state. Similarly the sulfur atom in $\\ce\{H2S\}$ is in a reduced state while that in sulfuric acid \($\\ce\{H2SO4\}$\) is in an oxidized state."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199062112167
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:H02889
consider: goldbook:O04362
consider: goldbook:O04365

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05221
name: reducing
def: "Decreasing the size of the laboratory sample or individual particles\, or both. Division of the size of the laboratory sample may be accomplished manually by coning and quartering or by riffling or mechanically by rotary dividers. Reduction of particle size may be accomplished by milling or grinding. Simultaneous division and reduction may also be achieved with mills having stream diverters."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199062061193
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:L03433

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05222
name: reduction
def: "The complete transfer of one or more electrons to a molecular entity \(also called 'electronation'\)\, and\, more generally\, the reverse of the processes described under oxidation \(2\) and \(3\)."
synonym: "electronation" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466051077
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:M03986
consider: goldbook:O04362

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05223
name: reductive elimination
def: "The reverse of oxidative addition."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466051077
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:O04367

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05224
name: reductones
def: "Compounds containing an enediol structure stabilized by conjugation and hydrogen bonding with an adjacent carbonyl group\, $\\ce\{RC\(OH\)=C\(OH\)C\(=O\)R\}$. They are strong reducing agents\, fairly strong acids and commonly derived from saccharides by oxidation at the carbon atom alpha to the carbonyl function\, e.g. ascorbic acid."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199567081307
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:C01267
consider: goldbook:O04362
consider: goldbook:S05441

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05225
name: re-extraction
def: "Since the prefix 're' can signify 'back' as well as 'again' this term is ambiguous and should be avoided\, except where the process of extraction \(e.g. from aqueous solution to an organic phase\) in a single direction is repeated \(following stripping\). It should not be used as a synonym for stripping or back-extraction."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199365112373
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:E02301
consider: goldbook:S06054

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05227
name: reference atom
def: "The atom to which a primitive change is nucleophilic\, electrophilic\, nucleofugal or homolytic."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac198961010023
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
