id: goldbook:S05449
name: salt form of an ion exchanger
def: "The ionic form of an ion exchanger in which the counter-ions are neither hydrogen nor hydroxide ions. When only one valence is possible for the counter-ion\, or its exact form or charge is not known\, the symbol or the name of the counter-ion without charge is used\, e.g. sodium-form or $\\ce\{Na\}$-form\, tetramethylammonium-form\, orthophosphate-form. When one of two or more possible forms is exclusively present\, the oxidation state may be indicated by a Roman numeral\, e.g. $\\ce\{Fe^\{II\}\}$ form\, $\\ce\{Fe^\{III\}\}$ form."
