[Term]
id: goldbook:S05660
name: sigmatropic rearrangement
def: "A molecular rearrangement that involves both the creation of a new $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bond\}$ between atoms previously not directly linked and the breaking of an existing $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bond\}$. There is normally a concurrent relocation of $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}bonds\}$ in the molecule concerned\, but the total number of $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$ and $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bonds\}$ does not change. The term was originally restricted to intramolecular pericyclic reactions\, and many authors use it with this connotation. It is\, however\, also applied in a more general\, purely structural\, sense. If such reactions are intramolecular\, their transition state may be visualized as an association of two fragments connected at their termini by two partial $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bonds\}$\, one being broken and the other being formed as\, for example\, the two allyl fragments in \($\\ce\{a'\}$\). Considering only atoms within the \(real or hypothetical\) cyclic array undergoing reorganization\, if the numbers of these in the two fragments are designated \\\(i\\\) and \\\(j\\\)\, then the rearrangement is said to be a sigmatropic change of order \[\\\(i\\\)\, \\\(j\\\)\] \(conventionally \\\(\\rm\{\[\}i\\rm\{\]\}\\leq \\rm\{\[\}j\\rm\{\]\}\\\)\). Thus the rearrangement \(a\) is of order\, whilst reaction \(b\) is asigmatropic shift of hydrogen. \(N.B. By convention square brackets here refer to numbers of atoms\, in contrast with current usage in the context of cycloaddition.\)   The descriptors a and s \(antarafacial and antarafacial\) may also be annexed to the numbers \\\(i\\\) and \\\(j\\\); \(b\) is then described as a \[1s\,5s\] sigmatropic rearrangement\, since it is suprafacial with respect both to the hydrogen atom and to the pentadienyl system\:  The prefix 'homo' \(meaning one extra atom\, interrupting conjugation — cf. 'homoaromaticity'\) has frequently been applied to sigmatropic rearrangements\, but is misleading."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466051077
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
