[Term]
id: goldbook:S06023
name: stoichiometric concentration
def: "The stoichiometric concentration\, \\\(c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\)\, of a component B in a system is given by the expression\: \\\[c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}=\\frac\{n_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\{V\}\\\] where \\\(n_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the stoichiometric amount of substance of component B in the system and \\\(V\\\) is the volume of the system. The component added to the system may dissociate or react with other components to form a series of derived components and only a fraction of the original component may actually exist in a free form in the system. It is therefore essential to distinguish between the stoichiometric concentration and the amount-of-substance concentration of the free form of the component in the system. Sometimes stoichiometric quantities are indicated by a subscript \(\\\(_\{\\circ\}\\\)\)\, e.g. \\\(n_\{\\rm\{o\,B\}\}\\\)\, \\\(c_\{\\rm\{o\,B\}\}\\\). In clinical chemistry\, the term stoichiometric concentration is rarely employed. Instead\, the name of the component is modified to indicate inclusion of the various derived forms\, e.g. mixtures of a defined chemical component and its derivatives may be denoted by the plural form of the name of the pure unchanged substance\, or to indicate the sum of components specified in individual quantities the specification 'total' may be employed."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac198456050567
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:A00297
consider: goldbook:A00298
consider: goldbook:S06021

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06024
name: stoichiometric mean molal
def: "The practical activity coefficient of electrolyte B is given by \\\[\\gamma _\{\\pm \}=\\frac\{a_\{\\pm \}\}\{\\sqrt\{ u _\{\\rm\{+\}\}^\{ u _\{\\rm\{+\}\}\}\\  u _\{\\rm\{-\}\}^\{ u _\{\\rm\{-\}\}\}\}\\ \\frac\{m_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\{m^\{\\rm\{+\}\}\}\}\\\] where \\\(a_\{\\pm \}\\\) is the mean activity of B in solution\, \\\(m_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the molality of B\, \\\(m^\{+\} = \\pu\{1 mol kg-1\}\\\)\, \\\( u _\{\\rm\{+\}\}\\\) is the number of cations and \\\( u _\{\\unicode\{x2013\}\}\\\) the number of anions in the chosen group B which is taken as the electrolyte. \\\[ u =  u _\{\\rm\{+\}\}+ u _\{\\rm\{-\}\}\\\]"
synonym: "practical activity coefficient" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac197437040499
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:C01124
consider: goldbook:M03970

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06025
name: stoichiometric number
def: "A chemical reaction of known stoichiometry can be written in general as\: \\\[a\\rm\{A\}\\\,+\\\,b\\rm\{B\}\\\,+\\\,...\\rightarrow\\\,...\\\,+y\\rm\{Y\}\\\,+\\\,z\\rm\{Z\}\\\] For the reaction products Y and Z the numbers \\\(y\\\) and \\\(z\\\) are known as the stoichiometric numbers\, \\\( u _\{\\rm\{Y\}\}\\\) and \\\( u _\{\\rm\{Z\}\}\\\)\, for Y and Z respectively. For the reactants the stoichiometric numbers are the negatives of the numbers appearing in the equation; for example the stoichiometric number \\\( u _\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\) for the reactant A is \\\(-a\\\). In other words\, the stoichiometric numbers are positive for products and negative for reactants."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199668010149
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199668040957
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:S06021
consider: goldbook:S06026

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06026
name: stoichiometry
def: "The term refers to the relationship between the amounts of substances that react together in a particular chemical reaction\, and the amounts of products that are formed. The general stoichiometric equation\: \\\[a\\rm\{A\}\\\,+\\\,b\\rm\{B\}\\\,+\\\,...\\\,\\rightarrow\\\,...\\\,+\\\,y\\rm\{Y\}\\\,+\\\,z\\rm\{Z\}\\\] provides the information that a moles of A reacts with b moles of B to produce y moles of Y and z moles of Z. The stoichiometry of a reaction may be unknown\, or may be very complex. For example\, the thermal decomposition of acetaldehyde yields mainly methane and carbon monoxide\, but also a variety of minor products such as ethane\, acetone and diacetyl. The stoichiometric equation\:  is therefore only an approximate one. Even when the overall stoichiometry of a reaction is well defined\, it may be time-dependent in that it varies during the course of a reaction. Thus if a reaction occurs by the mechanism \\\(\\rm\{A\}\\rightarrow \\rm\{X\}\\rightarrow \\rm\{Y\}\\\)\, and X is formed in substantial amounts during the course of the process\, the relationship between the amounts of A\, X and Y will vary with time\, and no one stoichiometric equation can represent the reaction at all times."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199668010149
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:D01547
consider: goldbook:S06021

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06027
name: stokes
def: "cgs unit of kinematic viscosity\, \\\(\\rm\{St\} = 10^\{-4\}\\ \\rm\{m\}^\{2\}\\ \\rm\{s\}^\{-1\}\\\)."
