property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06169
name: surface dipole layer
def: "Particles in the surface region of a phase are subjected to orienting forces as a result of the anisotropic force field. Polar molecules \(e.g. permanent dipoles\) may thus be preferentially oriented in the surface region\, while polarizable molecules may be polarized \(induced dipoles\). The array of oriented polar and/or polarized molecules is called the surface dipole layer with which an electric potential drop is associated\, called the surface potential of the phase."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac198355081251
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:E01935
consider: goldbook:S06187

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06170
name: surface electric potential
def: "Given by \\\[\\chi ^\{\\beta\} = \\mathit\{\\Phi \}^\{\\beta\} - \\psi ^\{\\beta\}\\\] where \\\(\\psi ^\{\\beta \}\\\) and \\\(\\mathit\{\\Phi\} ^\{\\beta\}\\\) are the outer and inner electric potentials of phase $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\}$\, respectively."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac197437040499
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06171
name: surface excess
def: "For an interface\, the adsorption or surface excess of a given component is defined as the difference between the amount of component actually present in the system\, and that which would be present \(in a reference system\) if the bulk concentration in the adjoining phases were maintained up to a chosen geometrical dividing surface \(Gibbs dividing surface\). For a solid/liquid interface in which no component of the liquid phase penetrates into the solid\, the surface excess \(or adsorption\) of component i is defined as\: \\\[n_\{i\}^\{\\sigma \}=n_\{i\}- V^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\ c_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\] where \\\(n_\{i\}\\\) is the total amount of i in the system\, \\\(V^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\) is the volume of an arbitrarily chosen amount of bulk liquid \(in the framework of the so-called algebraic method\) and \\\(c_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\) is its bulk concentration in the liquid."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac198658070967
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:D01823
consider: goldbook:G02627
consider: goldbook:G02635
consider: goldbook:I03082

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06172
name: surface excess concentration
def: "A quantity defined by \\\[\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{i\} = \\frac\{n_\{i\}^\{\\sigma \}\}\{A_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\}\\\] where \\\(n_\{i\}^\{\\sigma\}\\\) is the surface excess \(or adsorption\) of component \\\(i\\\) and \\\(A_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\) is the area of the dividing surface or interface."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac198658070967
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:A00155
consider: goldbook:D01823
consider: goldbook:I03082
consider: goldbook:S06171

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06173
name: surface excess energy
def: "Defined by\: \\\[U^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3C3\} \} = U - U^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}- U^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}= U - V^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\\ \\frac\{U_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\}\{V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\} - V^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\\ \\frac\{U_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\}\{V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\}\\\] where \\\(V^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\\\) and \\\(V^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\\\) satisfy the condition \\\(V^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \} + V^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \} = V\\\)\, the total volume of the system. \(\\\(\\frac\{U_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\}\{V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\}\\\)\) and \(\\\(\\frac\{U_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\}\{V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\}\\\)\) are the energy densities in the two bulk phases where \\\(U_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\\\) and \\\(U_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\\\) are the mean molar energies and \\\(V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\\\) and \\\(V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\\\) are the mean molar volumes of the two phases."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac197231040577
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06174
name: surface excess enthalpy
def: "Defined by\: \\\[H^\{\\sigma \}=U^\{\\sigma \}- \\gamma \\ A_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\] where \\\(U^\{\\sigma \}\\\) is the surface excess energy\, \\\(\\gamma \\\) is the surface tension\, and \\\(A_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\) is the area relative to a Gibbs surface."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac197231040577
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:G02635
consider: goldbook:S06173
consider: goldbook:S06192

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06175
name: surface excess entropy
def: "Defined by\: \\\[S^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3C3\}\} = S - S^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}- S^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}=S- V^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\\ \\frac\{S_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\}\{V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\}- V^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\\ \\frac\{S_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\}\{V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\}\\\] \(\\\(\\frac\{S_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\}\{V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\}\\\)\) and \(\\\(\\frac\{S_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\}\{V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\}\\\)\) are the entropy densities in the two bulk phases\, where \\\(S_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\\\) and \\\(S_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\\\) are the mean molar entropies and \\\(V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\\\) and \\\(V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\\\) are the mean molar volumes of the two phases."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac197231040577
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:E02149

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06176
name: surface excess Gibbs energy
def: "Defined by \\\[G^\{\\sigma \}=H^\{\\sigma \}- T\\ S^\{\\sigma \}=A^\{\\sigma \}- \\gamma \\ A_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\] where \\\(H^\{\\sigma \}\\\) is the surface excess enthalpy\, \\\(T\\\) is the thermodynamic temperature\, \\\(S^\{\\sigma \}\\\) is the surface excess entropy\, \\\(A^\{\\sigma \}\\\) is the surface excess Helmholtz energy\, \\\(\\gamma \\\) is the surface tension\, and \\\(A_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\) is the area relative to a Gibbs surface."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac197231040577
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:G02635
consider: goldbook:S06174
consider: goldbook:S06175
consider: goldbook:S06177
consider: goldbook:S06192
consider: goldbook:T06321

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06177
name: surface excess Helmholtz energy
def: "Defined by\: \\\[A^\{\\sigma \}=U^\{\\sigma \}- T\\ S^\{\\sigma \}\\\] where \\\(U^\{\\sigma \}\\\) is the surface excess energy\, \\\(T\\\) is the thermodynamic temperature\, and \\\(S^\{\\sigma \}\\\) is the surface excess entropy."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac197231040577
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:S06173
consider: goldbook:S06175
consider: goldbook:T06321

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06178
name: surface excess isotherm
def: "The function relating\, at constant temperature and pressure\, \\\(\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{i\}^\{\\left\(\\rm\{n\}\\right\)\}\\\)\, \\\(\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{i\}^\{\\left\(\\rm\{l\}\\right\)\}\\\) or \\\(\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{i\}^\{\\left\(\\rm\{v\}\\right\)\}\\\)\, or the respective specific quantities \\\(\\frac\{n^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\ \\Delta x_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\}\{m\}\\\)\, \\\(\\frac\{A_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\ \\mathit\{\\Gamma \}_\{i\}^\{\\left\(\\rm\{l\}\\right\)\}\}\{m\}\\\) or \\\(\\frac\{V^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\ \\Delta c_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\}\{m\}\\\) to the mole fraction \(or concentration\) of component \\\(i\\\) in the equilibrium liquid phase. With solutions of more than two components such isotherms are unequivocal functions only when the ratios of the mole fractions \(or concentrations\) of all other components except \\\(i\\\) are kept constant. \(\\\(A_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\) is the area of interface\, \\\(\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{i\}^\{\\left\(\\rm\{n\}\\right\)\}\\\)\, \\\(\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{i\}^\{\\left\(\\rm\{l\}\\right\)\}\\\)\, \\\(\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{i\}^\{\\left\(\\rm\{v\}\\right\)\}\\\) are relative adsorptions\, \\\(\\Delta x_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\) is the change in mole fraction of \\\(i\\\) resulting from bringing a specified mass \\\(m\\\) of solid into contact with a specified amount of solution \\\(n^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\)\, \\\(\\Delta c_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\) is the change in concentration resulting from bringing a specified mass of solid into contact with a specified amount of solution of volume \\\(V^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\).\)"
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac197231040577
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:A00296
consider: goldbook:A00430
consider: goldbook:M03980

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06179
name: surface ionization
def: "Takes place when an atom or molecule is ionized when it interacts with a solid surface. ionization occurs only when the work function of the surface\, the temperature of the surface\, and the ionization energy of the atom or molecule have an appropriate relationship."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199163101541
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:I03183
consider: goldbook:I03199

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06180
name: surface ions
def: "Ions that are constituents of the surface or which have a particularly high affinity for the surface or surface sites."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199163060895
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06181
name: surface layer
def: "The region of space comprising and adjoining the phase boundary within which the properties of matter are significantly different from the values in the adjoining bulk phases."
synonym: "interfacial layer" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac197231040577
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06182
name: surface of a phase
def: "The plane ideally marking the boundary between a phase and its environment."
synonym: "free surface" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac198355081251
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06183
name: surface of tension
def: "The mechanical properties of an interfacial layer between two fluid phases may be expressed in terms of those of a geometrical surface of uniform tension called the surface of tension\, whose location is dependent on the distribution of the stress tensor within the interfacial layer."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac197231040577
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:I03085

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06185
name: surface pressure
def: "The change of interfacial tension caused by addition of a given species to a base solution. When an area of liquid covered with a spread substance is separated from a clean area of surface by a mechanical barrier\, the force acting on unit length of the barrier is called the surface pressure\, \\\(\\pi \\\) or \\\(\\pi _\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\)\, and is equal to \\\(\\gamma ^\{0\} - \\gamma \\\) where \\\(\\gamma ^\{0\}\\\) is the surface tension of the clean surface and \\\(\\gamma \\\) that of the covered surface."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac197231010049
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac198355081251
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:C01099
consider: goldbook:I03088
consider: goldbook:S06192

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06187
name: surface region
def: "The tridimensional region\, extending from the free surface of a condensed phase towards the interior\, where the properties differ from the bulk. The term surface may also be used in this sense."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac198355081251
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:S06154

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06189
name: surface shear viscosity
def: "For steady state deformations a surface shear viscosity \\\(\\eta ^\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\)\, and an area viscosity or surface dilatational viscosity \\\(\\zeta ^\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\) can be defined. In a Cartesian system with the x-axis normal to the surface\, they are defined by the equations\: \\\[\\eta ^\{\\rm\{s\}\} = \\frac\{\\sigma _\{xy\}\}\{\\frac\{\\partial  u_\{y\}\}\{\\partial  u_\{x\}\}\}\\\] \\\[\\zeta ^\{\\rm\{s\}\}=\\frac\{\\Delta \\gamma \}\{\\frac\{\\mathrm\{d\}\(\\ln A\)\}\{\\mathrm\{d\}t\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\sigma _\{xy\}\\\) is the shear component of the surface stress tensor\, \\\( u_\{x\}\\\) and \\\( u_\{y\}\\\) are the \\\(x\\\) and \\\(y\\\) components of the surface velocity vector\, respectively\, \\\(A\\\) is the surface area\, \\\(t\\\) is the time\, and \\\(\\Delta \\gamma \\\) is the difference between the \(steady state\) dynamic surface tension and the equilibrium surface tension."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac197951051213
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:D01875
consider: goldbook:S05642
consider: goldbook:S06191

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06190
name: surface states
def: "Energy levels localized in the surface region of semiconductors\, which do not bear any direct relation to the bulk energy distribution\, but which can exchange electrons with the bulk. In such a case a space charge may arise even when \\\(\\sigma \\\) \(the free charge density\) is zero\, i.e. \\\(\\sigma =\\sigma _\{\\mathrm\{sc\}\}+\\sigma _\{\\mathrm\{ss\}\}\\\)\, where \\\(\\sigma _\{\\mathrm\{sc\}\}\\\) is the space charge density and \\\(\\sigma _\{\\mathrm\{ss\}\}\\\) is the charge density associated with the surface states."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac198658030437
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:C00988
consider: goldbook:S05776
consider: goldbook:S06187

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06191
name: surface stress
def: "The work required to form unit area of new surface by stretching under equilibrium conditions. It is numerically equal to the force acting in the jth direction per unit length of exposed edge\, the edge being normal to the ith direction\, that must be applied to a terminating surface to keep it in equilibrium\, the ith and jth directions lying in the plane of the surface."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac198658030437
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06192
name: surface tension
def: "Work required to increase a surface area divided by that area. When two phases are studied it is often called interfacial tension."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199668040957
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:I03088

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06193
name: surface work
def: "Surface tension is the intensive factor in the differential expression for the work required to increase the area of the surface of tension. Measured under reversible conditions at constant temperature \(and normally constant pressure\) and referred to unit area\, this work\, the so-called \(differential\) surface work\, is equal to the static surface tension."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac197231040577
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:S06183
consider: goldbook:S06192

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06194
name: surfactant
def: "A substance which lowers the surface tension of the medium in which it is dissolved\, and/or the interfacial tension with other phases\, and\, accordingly\, is positively adsorbed at the liquid/vapour and/or at other interfaces."
synonym: "surface active agent" []
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac197231040577
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:I03088
consider: goldbook:S06192

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06195
name: surprisal
def: "A function relating the prior distribution \\\(P_\{0\}\\\) of product states to an observed or calculated product distribution P. It is defined by\: \\\[s=- \\ln \(\\frac\{P\}\{P_\{0\}\}\)\\\]"
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199668010149
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:P04852

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06196
name: surprisal analysis
def: "The study of product state distributions in terms of surprisal has been referred to as surprisal analysis."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199668010149
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:S06195

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06197
name: suspended matter
def: "All particulate material which persists in the atmosphere or in a flue gas stream for lengthy periods because the particles are too small in size to have an appreciable falling velocity."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199062112167
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06198
name: suspension
def: "A liquid in which solid particles are dispersed."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac197231040577
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06199
name: suspension effect
def: "An effect which occurs when ion-selective electrodes are used in concentrated\, space-filled suspensions while the external reference electrode remains in the supernatant \(suspension-free\) solution. The suspensions are specifically solvent-swollen ion exchangers or other materials\, like soils and clays\, that concentrate ions by adsorption and absorption. Space-filled\, gravity-packed suspensions act like a second phase and form apparently an interfacial potential difference \(PD\) with respect to the supernatant. The measured ion activity in the suspension differs from the value in the supernatant by the interfacial PD\, and corresponds to a higher value approximating the activity inside the ion exchanger gel. The effect nearly disappears when the outer reference electrode is placed in the same region of the suspension as the sensor electrode. There are some changes in the junction potential differences of the reference electrode\, between suspension and supernatant."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466122527
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:G02600
consider: goldbook:I03171
consider: goldbook:R05229
consider: goldbook:S06198

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06200
name: Swain–Lupton equation
def: "A dual parameter approach to the correlation analysis of substituent effects\, which involves a field constant \(\\\(F\\\)\) and a resonance constant \(\\\(R\\\)\). The original treatment was modified later. The procedure has been considerably applied\, but also much criticized."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466051077
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:C01346
consider: goldbook:R05326

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06201
name: Swain–Scott equation
def: "The linear free-energy relation of the form\: \\\[\\log _\{10\}\(\\frac\{k\}\{k_\{0\}\}\)=s\\ n\\\] applied to the variation of reactivity of a given electrophilic substrate towards a series of nucleophilic reagents. \\\(n\\\) is characteristic of the reagent \(i.e. a measure of its nucleophilicity\) and \\\(s\\\) is characteristic of the substrate \(i.e. a measure of its sensitivity to the nucleophilicity of the reagent\). A scale of \\\(n\\\) values is based on the rate coefficients \\\(k\\\) for the reaction of methyl bromide with nucleophiles in water at \\\(25\\ ^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\rm\{C\}\\\)\, \\\(s\\\) being defined as 1.00 for these reactions and \\\(n\\\) being defined as 0.00 for the hydrolysis of methyl bromide. \(Other scales have been devised.\)"
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466051077
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:E02020
consider: goldbook:H02902
consider: goldbook:L03551
consider: goldbook:N04251
consider: goldbook:R05180
consider: goldbook:R05190
consider: goldbook:S06082

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06202
name: swelling
def: "The increase in volume of a gel or solid associated with the uptake of a liquid or gas."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac197231040577
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:G02600
consider: goldbook:UT06806

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06203
name: swelling pressure
def: "That pressure difference \\\(\\mathit\{\\Pi\} _\{\\rm\{sw\}\}\\\) or \\\(\\mathit\{\\Pi\}\\\) which has to be established between a gel and its equilibrium liquid\, to prevent further swelling of the gel."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac197231040577
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:G02600
consider: goldbook:S06202

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06205
name: switching transition
def: "A transition in semiconductive glasses in which\, beyond a critical applied voltage\, there is an avalanche breakdown of conducting electrons that causes local melting and hence local crystallization that gives metallic conductivity."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466030577
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:C01434
consider: goldbook:M03821

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06206
name: sydnone imines
def: "Imines of sydnones \(i.e. having an imino group in place of the exocyclic oxygen atom\).  \(These structures represent only two canonical forms.\)"
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199567081307
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:I02957
consider: goldbook:S06207

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06207
name: sydnones
def: "Mesoionic compounds having the 1\,2\,3-oxadiazole skeleton bearing an oxygen atom attached to the 5 position.  \(These structures represent only two canonical forms\)."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199567081307
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:M03842

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06208
name: <i>sym</i>-
def: "An affix used in names to denote symmetrical."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1515/pac-2014-0718
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06209
name: symbiosis
def: "The term was originally applied to describe the maximum flocking of either hard or soft ligands in the same complexes. For hydrocarbon molecules\, symbiosis implies that those containing a maximum number of $\\ce\{C–H\}$ bonds \(e.g. $\\ce\{CH4\}$\) or $\\ce\{C–C\}$ bonds \(e.g. $\\ce\{Me4C\}$\) are the most stable."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466051077
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:L03518
consider: goldbook:S05900

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06210
name: symbol
def: "Conventional sign designating a quantity or a unit of measurement."
comment: "Symbols for quantities are denoted by letters of the Latin or Greek alphabet printed in italic (sloping) type; \(\rm{pH}\) is an exception."
comment: "Symbols for units are denoted by letters of the Latin or Greek alphabet printed in roman (upright) type."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199668040957
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06211
name: symmetrical films
def: "The properties of fluid films depend on the nature of the film phase and that of each of the two neighbouring bulk phases. These films should be described\, where appropriate\, by three capital letters such as A for air\, W for water\, O for oil\, and S for solid\, separated by solidi\, the middle letter indicating the film phase. For symmetrical films the first and last symbols are the same\, e.g. A/W/A\: water film in air\, or W/O/W\: oil film in water\, whereas for unsymmetrical films these are different\, e.g. W/O/A\: oil film between water and air."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac197231040577
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:A00482
consider: goldbook:F02366

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06213
name: symmetry-conserving transition
def: "A transition in which the cell dimensions and/or angles of the one phase differ from those in the other phase\, but where the space-group symmetry is conserved. Example\: The transition of face-centred cubic $\\ce\{Ce\}$\, upon cooling\, to a face-centred cubic phase that is $\\pu\{10\\%\}$ denser. Upon cooling\, enough contraction takes place to allow an overlap of the \\\(\\rm\{fsp\}^\{2\}\\\) configuration and the change from an isolated non-bonding magnetic f electron to a bonding non-magnetic electron pair."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466030577
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06214
name: symmetry number
def: "The symmetry number of a molecule is obtained by imagining all identical atoms to be labelled\, and then counting the number of different but equivalent arrangements that can be obtained by rotating \(but not reflecting\) the molecule. In the statistical-mechanical treatment of chemical equilibrium\, the partition function for each molecular species must be divided by its symmetry number."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199668010149
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:C01023
consider: goldbook:P04439

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06216
name: <i>syn</i>
def: "See\: torsion angleSee\: endo\, exo\, syn\, antiSee\: antiFormerly used to describe the stereochemistry of oximes and related systems.See\: E\, Z"
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199668122193
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
consider: goldbook:A00381
consider: goldbook:E01882
consider: goldbook:E02094
consider: goldbook:O04372
consider: goldbook:T06406

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06218
name: synchronization
def: "This principle applies to reactions in which there is a lack of synchronization between bond formation or bond rupture and other primitive changes that affect the stability of products and reactants\, such as resonance\, solvation\, electrostatic\, hydrogen bonding and polarizability effects. The principle states that a product-stabilizing factor whose development lags behind bond changes at the transition state\, or a reactant-stabilizing factor whose loss is ahead of bond changes at the transition state\, increases the intrinsic barrier and decreases the 'intrinsic rate constant' of a reaction. For a product-stabilizing factor whose development is ahead of bond changes\, or reactant factors whose loss lags behind bond changes\, the opposite relations hold. The reverse effects are observable for factors that destabilize a reactant or product."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac199466051077
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
