[Term]
id: goldbook:W06671
name: Weller correlation
def: "Empirical correlation for the energy of full charge-transfer exciplex relative to the ground state in n-hexane as a function of the electrochemical one electron standard reduction potential of the cation radical produced upon electron donation and standard reduction potential of the acceptor measured in a polar solvent for the donor \(D\) and the acceptor \(A\) involved \\\[\\Delta H\(\\rm\{D\}^\{+\}\\rm\{A\}^\{-\}\,\\rm\{hexane\}\) = e\\left \[ E^\{0\}\(\\rm\{D\}^\{+\\bullet \}/\\rm\{D\}\) - E^\{0\}\(\\rm\{A\}/\\rm\{A\}^\{-\\bullet \}\) \\right \]+e\\Delta E\\\] with \\\(E^\{0\}\(D^\{+\\bullet \}/D\)\\\) and \\\(E^\{0\}\(A/D^\{-\\bullet \}\)\\\) the standard electrode potentials of the donor and acceptor\, respectively."
comment: "Although not complying with the IUPAC recommended nomenclature for the standard electrode potentials, traditionally the equation has been written as: \[\Delta H(\rm{D}^{+}\rm{A}^{-},\rm{hexane}) = e\left [ E_{\rm{ox}}^{0} - E_{\rm{red}}^{0} \right ]+(0.15 \pm 0.10)\ \rm{eV}\] with \(E_{\rm{ox}}^{0}\) the standard electrode potential at which the oxidation occurs, and \(E_{\rm{red}}^{0}\) the standard electrode potential at which the reduction occurs. This writing of the first term within the square brackets is misleading and not recommended."
comment: "In the case diethylaniline as donor and aromatic hydrocarbons as acceptors, the last term on the right, i.e., \(e\Delta E = (0.15 \pm 0.10)\ \rm{eV}\). This equation assumes a constant Coulomb term and a constant entropy change within a particular series of partners. \(e\) is the elementary charge."
comment: "The IUPAC recommendations for the sign and symbols of standard potentials are used in the equation as written above."
property_value: mito:isMentionedBy doi:10.1351/pac200779030293
property_value: hasStatus "current" xsd:string
