[Term]
id: goldbook:Z06751
name: Zucker–Hammett hypothesis
def: "This hypothesis states that\, if in an acid catalysed reaction\, \\\(\\log _\{10\}k_\{1\}\\\) \(first-order rate constant of the reaction\) is linear in \\\(H_\{0\}\\\) \(Hammett acidity function\)\, water is not involved in the transition state of the rate-controlling step. However\, if \\\(\\log _\{10\}k_\{1\}\\\) is linear in \\\(\\log _\{10\}\\left\[\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\\right\]\\\) then water is involved. This has been shown to be incorrect by Hammett himself."
